"hadiths[1148]{hadithnumber,text}:"
18372,"(18366) Yahyā narrated to us, Isrāʾīl narrated to us, from Jābir, from ʿĀmir, who said: ""The people of al-Sawād have no covenant; they only came down under a ruling (ḥukm)."" [Authentic]"
18373,"(18367) He said: And al-Ṣalt ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Zubaydī narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Qays al-Asadī, from al-Shaʿbī, that he was asked during the time of ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz about the people of al-Sawād: ""Do they have a covenant?"" He said: ""They had no covenant. But when the kharāj was accepted from them, the covenant came into effect for them."" [Authentic]"
18374,"(18368) Yahyā narrated to us, Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us, from Ibn Abī Laylā, who said: ""ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb restored their lands to them and made a settlement with them regarding the kharāj."" [Weak]"
18375,"(18369) Abū Saʿīd informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, al-Ḥasan narrated to us, Yahyā narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us, from Ibn Lahīʿah, from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb, who said: ""ʿUmar wrote to Saʿd — may Allah be pleased with both of them — when he conquered Iraq: 'To proceed: Your letter has reached me in which you mention that the people have asked you to divide their spoils and what Allah has granted as fayʾ among them. When this letter of mine reaches you, look at what the people have brought to the camp in the way of livestock or wealth, and divide it among the Muslims who are present. But leave the lands and the rivers for their cultivators, so that they may serve as stipends for the Muslims. For if you divide them among those who are present, there will remain nothing for those who come after them.'"" [Weak]"
18376,"(18370) Yahyā narrated to us, Isrāʾīl narrated to us, from Abū Isḥāq, from Ḥārithah ibn Muḍarrib, from ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — that he intended to divide the people of al-Sawād among the Muslims and ordered that they be counted. They found that one Muslim man was allotted three cultivators (i.e., the ʿulūj). He consulted the Companions of the Prophet — peace and blessings be upon him — regarding that. ʿAlī — may Allah be pleased with him — said: ""Leave them; let them be a resource (māddah) for the Muslims."" So he sent ʿUthmān ibn Ḥunayf, who assessed upon them [the following]: forty-eight, forty-three, and twelve. [Weak]"
18377,"(18371) Yahyā narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Walīd ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maʿqil, ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Ḥarrah narrated to me, from his father, who said: ""ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb — may Allah be pleased with him — set aside ten categories from this al-Sawād: the land of those who were killed in battle, the land of those who fled to the Muslims (i.e., from the enemy), every land belonging to Kisrā, every land that belonged to any of its inhabitants, every water source (maghīḍ māʾ), and every postal station (dīr barīd)."" He said: ""And I have forgotten four."" He said: ""The total kharāj of what he set aside was seven hundred thousand. When the civil wars (jamājim) occurred, the people burned the register (dīwān), and each group took what was in their possession."" [Weak]"
18378,"(18372) Yahyā narrated to us, Qays ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, from a man of Banū Asad, from his father, who said: ""Ḥudhayfah set aside for himself the land of Kisrā, the land of the family of Kisrā, and the land of whoever Kisrā had set aside for himself, the land of those who were killed, the land of those who fled, the thickets (ājām), and the water sources (maghīḍ al-māʾ)."" [Weak]"
18379,"(18373) Yahyā narrated to us, Qays ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, from Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit, from Thaʿlabah al-Ḥimānī, who said: ""We entered upon ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib — may Allah be pleased with him — at al-Raḥbah, and he said: 'Were it not that some of you would strike the faces of others, I would divide al-Sawād among you.'"" [Weak]"
18380,"(18374) Yahyā narrated to us, ʿAmr ibn Abī al-Miqdām narrated to us, from Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit, from Thaʿlabah ibn Yazīd al-Ḥimānī, from ʿAlī — may Allah be pleased with him — a similar report. And Yahyā narrated to us, from Qurʾān al-Asadī, from Abū Sinān al-Shaybānī, from ʿUmayrah, from ʿAlī — may Allah be pleased with him — who said: ""I had intended to divide al-Sawād, [but I feared that] one of you would settle in a village and say: 'This is my village — leave me alone,' or 'Leave me,' or 'I will surely divide it.'"" [Authentic]"
18381,(18375) Abū Saʿīd informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us; al-Rabīʿ said: al-Shāfiʿī said: And they say that Jarīr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Bajalī — and this is the most firmly established hadith according to them regarding it.
18382,"(18376) It was informed to us by the trustworthy one, from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid, from Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim, from Jarīr ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: Banū Bajīlah constituted a quarter of the people. A quarter of al-Sawād was apportioned for them, and they collected its produce for three or four years — I am uncertain — then I came to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with me, and with me was Fulānah bint Fulān, a woman from among them — she mentioned her name but I do not recall it. ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Were it not that I am a distributor who will be questioned, I would leave you upon what was apportioned for you, but I think that you should return it to the people. Al-Shāfiʿī said: And in his hadith there is: He compensated me for my share therein with approximately eighty dinars. And in his hadith there is: Fulānah said: My father witnessed the Battle of al-Qādisiyyah, and his share is established, and I will not relinquish it until you give me such-and-such and give me such-and-such. So he gave it to her.

And Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated it from Ismāʿīl, mentioning the account of Jarīr. And Hushaym narrated it from Ismāʿīl, mentioning it and mentioning the account of the woman, and he mentioned that she was Umm Kurz, and that she said: I will not relinquish it until you mount me upon a tractable she-camel upon which is a red velvet cloak, and fill my two palms with gold. So he did that. And the dinars were approximately eighty. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18383,"(18377) It was informed to us by Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Qatādah; Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Khamīruwayh informed us; Aḥmad ibn Najdah informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn al-Rabīʿ informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak, from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid, from Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim, who said: When Jarīr ibn ʿAbd Allāh came as a delegate to ʿUmar, along with ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir and some Muslims, ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to Jarīr: O Jarīr, by Allah, were it not that I am a distributor who will be questioned, you would remain upon what was apportioned for you, but I see that I should return it to the Muslims. So he returned it. He had assigned a quarter of al-Sawād to Banū Bajīlah, and they collected the kharāj for three years. He returned it and gave them eighty dinars. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18384,"(18378) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Ādam informed us; Ibn Abī Zāʾidah, from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid, from Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim, who said: We were a quarter of the people on the Day of al-Qādisiyyah. ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, gave us a quarter of al-Sawād. We collected its produce for three years. Then Jarīr came as a delegate to ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, after that. He said: By Allah, were it not that I am a distributor who will be questioned, you would remain upon what was apportioned for you. But I think that you should return it to the Muslims. So he did so, and he granted him eighty dinars. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18385,"(18379) Abū Saʿīd informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us; al-Ḥasan informed us; Yaḥyā informed us; ʿAbd al-Salām ibn Ḥarb, from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid, from Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim, who said: ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, gave Jarīr and his people a quarter of al-Sawād. He collected its produce for two or three years. Then Jarīr came as a delegate to ʿUmar along with ʿAmmār, may Allah be pleased with them. ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: O Jarīr, were it not that I am a distributor who will be questioned, you would remain upon what you are upon. But I think that you should return it to the Muslims. So he returned it to them, and ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, gave him eighty dinars. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was narrated before it.]"
18386,"(18380) Abū Saʿīd informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us; al-Ḥasan informed us; Yaḥyā informed us; Ibn al-Mubārak, from Ḥammād ibn Salamah, from Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind, from al-Shaʿbī, who said: ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to Jarīr: Would you come to Iraq, and for you is a quarter or a third after the khums from every land and thing?

This is munqaṭiʿ, and what precedes it is mawṣūl. And in the narrations that we have transmitted — and we did not transmit it regarding al-Sawād of Iraq — there is nothing more authentic than it, as al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, said.

Abū Saʿīd informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us; al-Rabīʿ said: al-Shāfiʿī said: In this hadith there is an indication that when he gave Jarīr al-Bajalī compensation for his share, and the woman compensation for her father's share, it was with the pleasure of those who had charged upon it (i.e., the conquerors). They relinquished their rights from it, and he made it a waqf for the Muslims. And this is permissible for the Imam: if he conquers a land of coercion today, and enumerates those who conquered it, and they willingly relinquish their rights from it, the Imam may make it a waqf. Their rights from it are the four-fifths, and the people of the khums should be given their due, unless the adults among them relinquish their rights, and that is for him. The ruling regarding land is like the ruling regarding wealth. The Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, captured the people of Hawāzin and distributed the four-fifths among those who charged on horseback. Then the delegations of Hawāzin came as Muslims and asked him to bestow upon them by returning what he had taken from them. He gave them the choice between wealth and captives. They said: You have given us a choice between our lineage and our wealth, and we choose our lineage. So the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, relinquished his right and the right of his household. When the Muhājirūn heard of that, they relinquished their rights for him. And when the Anṣār heard of that, they relinquished their rights for him. There remained a group of the later Muhājirūn and the conquerors. He appointed over every ten men one man, then he said: Bring me the willing consent of those who remain. Whoever dislikes it, I will give him such-and-such camels, up to a time he mentioned. So they came with their willing consent, except al-Aqraʿ ibn Ḥābis and ʿUyaynah ibn Badr, for they refused in order to humiliate Hawāzin. The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, did not compel them upon that, until they both later relinquished. ʿUyaynah was deceived out of his right, and the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, gave the right of the one who willingly consented regarding his right.

Al-Shāfiʿī said: And this is the most deserving course for ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding al-Sawād and its conquests, if they were by coercion. And what al-Shāfiʿī mentioned regarding the affair of Hawāzin has already passed in the hadith of al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and in the narration of ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb from his father from his grandfather. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18387,"(18381) Abū Manṣūr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Dāmghānī informed us in Bayhaq; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; Abū Aḥmad Hārūn ibn Yūsuf al-Qaṭīʿī informed us; Ibn Abī ʿUmar informed us; Sufyān, from Ibn Abī Khālid, from Qays, from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: Al-Ḥīrah was presented to me like the carcasses of dogs, and you will conquer it. A man stood and said: O Messenger of Allah, grant me the daughter of Buqaylah. He said: She is yours. So they gave her to him. Her father came and said: Will you sell her? He said: Yes. He said: For how much? Judge as you wish. He said: One thousand dirhams. He said: I have taken her.

They said to him: Had you said thirty thousand, he would have taken her. He said: Is there any number greater than one thousand?

Ibn Abī ʿUmar is alone in narrating it from Sufyān in this manner. Others narrated from him from ʿAlī ibn Zayd ibn Judʿān, and the well-known version of this hadith is from Khuraym ibn Aws, and he is the one to whom the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, gave this woman. We have narrated it in the book Dalāʾil al-Nubuwwah at the end of the expedition of Tabūk. [Mawḍūʿ]"
18388,"(18382) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh al-Narsī informed us; Rūḥ informed us; Ibn Abī ʿArūbah, from Qatādah, from Lāḥiq ibn Ḥumayd, who said: When ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, sent ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, and ʿUthmān ibn Ḥunayf, may Allah be pleased with them, to al-Kūfah, he appointed ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir over the prayer and the armies, and he appointed Ibn Masʿūd over the judiciary and the bayt al-māl, and he appointed ʿUthmān ibn Ḥunayf over the surveying of the land. He allotted them each day one sheep — half of it and its offal for ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir, and the half shared between the other two. Then he said: I have placed you and myself regarding this money in the position of a guardian of an orphan's wealth. ""And test the orphans until they reach marriageable age; then if you perceive in them sound judgment, deliver to their possessions to them, and do not consume them extravagantly and hastily lest they grow up. And whoever is wealthy, let him abstain, and whoever is poor, let him consume reasonably."" (Al-Nisāʾ 4:6) And I do not think that a village from which one sheep is taken every day will escape rapid ruin.

He said: ʿUthmān ibn ḿunayf imposed upon every jarīb of vines ten dirhams, and upon every jarīb of date palms — I think he said eight — and upon every jarīb of qaḍb six dirhams, and upon every jarīb of wheat four dirhams, and upon every jarīb of barley two dirhams. And from every adult male, twenty-four dirhams every year. He exempted from that the women and the children. And regarding their various trades, there was half the ʿushr (one-twentieth). Then he wrote that to ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, and he approved it and was pleased with it.

It was said to ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him: How do we take from the traders of the enemy when they come to us? ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: How do they take from you when you go to their lands? They said: The tenth. He said: Then likewise take from them.

And Yazīd ibn Zurayʿ narrated it from Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah, and he said: And upon every jarīb of date palms eight, and upon every jarīb of qaḍb six, without doubt. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18389,"(18383) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; Saʿdān ibn Naṣr informed us; Wakīʿ, from Ibn Abī Laylā, from al-Ḥakam, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, sent ʿUthmān ibn Ḥunayf, and he surveyed al-Sawād. He imposed upon every jarīb of cultivated or uncultivated land, wherever water reaches, one qafīz and one dirham. Wakīʿ said: Meaning wheat and barley. And he imposed upon every jarīb of vines ten dirhams, and upon every jarīb of fresh dates five dirhams. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18390,"(18384) He said: And Wakīʿ narrated to us from ʿAlī ibn Ṣāliḥ, from Abān ibn Taghlib, from a man, from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, that he imposed upon date palms: upon the daqlatayn one dirham, and upon the Persian variety one dirham. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18391,"(18385) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, and Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr — informed us: they said, Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affan narrated to us: Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Iraq has withheld its dirham and its qafiz; Sham has withheld its mudd and its dinar; and Egypt has withheld its irdab and its dinar. You shall return to where you began, you shall return to where you began, you shall return to where you began."" The flesh and blood of Abu Hurayrah bear witness to that. Yahya said: He means by this hadith that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, mentioned the qafiz and the dirham before 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, placed them upon the land.

Narrated by Muslim in al-Sahih from 'Ubayd ibn Ya'ish and Ishaq ibn Rahawayh, from Yahya ibn Adam.

[Sahih. Muslim 2896]"
18392,"(18386) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, and Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi — informed us: they said, Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us: Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Fazari, from Malik ibn Anas, who said: Thawr narrated to me: Salim, the mawla of ibn Mutyi', narrated to me that he heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: ""We conquered Khaybar and acquired no gold or silver; we acquired only camels, cattle, orchards, and household goods. Then we departed with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, toward Wadi al-Quray, and with him was a slave called Mid'am, who had been given to him by one of the Banu al-Dibab. While he was unloading the mount of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, a stray arrow came and struck that slave, killing him. The people said, 'Congratulations to him for martyrdom!' But the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: 'No, by the One in whose Hand is my soul! The cloak he took on the day of Khaybar from the war spoils — before the shares were allocated — shall blaze with fire upon him.' A man came, having heard that from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, with a sandal or two sandals, and said, 'This is something I had acquired.' The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: 'A sandal or two sandals of fire.'""

Narrated by al-Bukhari in al-Sahih from 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad, from Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr. [Sahih. Muttafaq 'alayh]"
18393,"(18387) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' informed us: al-Hasan Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi narrated to us: 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghayath narrated to us: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us: 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar — in what Abu Salamah considered — narrated from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, fought the people of Khaybar until he forced them into their fortress. He gained control of the land, the crops, and the date palms. They made a settlement with him that they would evacuate it, carrying what their mounts could bear, and that the yellow and the white (i.e., gold and silver) would belong to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him. They would leave, and he stipulated upon them that they must not conceal or hide anything; if they did, they would have no protection and no pact."" But they hid a skin (misk) containing wealth and ornaments belonging to Hayy ibn Akhtab, who had carried it with him to Khaybar when the Banu al-Nadir were expelled. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""What has become of the skin of Hayy that he brought from al-Nadir?"" He replied: ""Expenditures and wars consumed it."" He said: ""The pact was near, and the wealth was greater than that."" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, handed it over to al-Zubayr, and he punished it (i.e., Hayy). Before that, Hayy had entered a ruin and said: 'I saw Hayy circling about in this ruin.' They went and circled about and found the skin in the ruin. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, killed the two sons of Huqayq — one of them being the husband of Safiyyah bint Hayy ibn Akhtab — and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, took captive their women and children, and divided their wealth on account of the treachery they had committed. He intended to expel them from it. They said: 'O Muhammad, allow us to remain in this land, to cultivate it and tend it. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and his Companions have no servants to maintain it, and they themselves have no leisure to do so.' So he gave them Khaybar on the condition that they would have half of every crop, date palm, and produce, as the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, willed. 'Abd Allah ibn Rawahah would come to them each year, estimate the yield, and collect their half. They complained to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, of the severity of his estimation and sought to bribe him. He said: 'O enemies of Allah! You would feed me haram? By Allah, I have come to you from the one most beloved to me, and you are more hateful to me than a number of monkeys and swine. My hatred of you and my love for him do not prevent me from being just between you.' They said: 'By this the heavens and the earth stand.' The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, noticed a green mark in the eye of Safiyyah and said: 'O Safiyyah, what is this greenness?' She said: 'My head was in the lap of Ibn Huqayq and I was asleep, and I saw as though a moon had fallen into my lap. I told him of that, and he struck me and said: ""Do you desire the king of Yathrib?""' She said: 'The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, was the most hated of people to me, for he had killed my husband and my father.' He continued to apologize to me and say: 'Your father incited the Arabs against me, and he did such and such,' until that left my heart. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, used to give each of his wives eighty wasqs of dates each year and twenty wasqs of barley. In the time of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, the Muslims were beset with troubles, and they threw Ibn 'Umar from the roof of a house and dislocated his hands. 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: 'Whoever has a share in Khaybar, let us come so that we may divide it among them.' He divided it among them. Their chief said: 'Do not expel us; allow us to remain as the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, had allowed us.' 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to their chief: 'Do you think I have forgotten the words of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him? What will become of you when your mount dances you toward Sham one day, then another, then another?' Then 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, divided it among those who had witnessed Khaybar from among the people of al-Hudaybiyyah. [Sahih]"
18394,"(18388) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas, al-Asamm, narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affan narrated to us: Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us: Abu Shahab narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Bishr ibn Yasar, who said he heard a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, saying: ""When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, conquered Khaybar, he divided it into thirty-six shares, each share comprising one hundred portions. Half was for the Muslims, and one share was for the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and he set aside half for the Muslims for whatever matters and calamities might befall.""

The Sheikh said: This is because he conquered part of Khaybar by force and part by peace treaty. What he divided among them was what was taken by force, and what he set aside for calamities was what Allah had bestowed upon His Messenger — land that was not reached by horse or mount. [Sahih]"
18395,"(18389) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Faris narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Juwayriyah, from Malik, from al-Zuhri, that Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib informed him: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, conquered part of Khaybar by force."" [Da'if]"
18396,"(18390) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us: Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn Ja'far — narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to me: my father narrated to me: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us, from Malik, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""Were it not for the later Muslims, no village would have been conquered except that we would have divided it just as the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, divided Khaybar.""

Narrated by al-Bukhari in al-Sahih from Sadaqah, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi. [Sahih. al-Bukhari 2334-3125]"
18397,"(18391) And Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzaki and Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi — informed us: they said, Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Hisham ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, who said: I heard 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: ""Were it not that I am leaving the people with nothing, no village would have been conquered except that I would have divided it just as the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, divided Khaybar."" The Sheikh said: And this, as we understand it — and Allah knows best — is that he would seek to win their hearts and then dedicate it as a waqf for the Muslims, out of consideration for them. [Sahih. Muttafaq 'alayh]"
18398,"(18392) And Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah narrated to us: Abu al-Fadl ibn Khumayruwayh informed us: Ahmad ibn Najdah narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Jarir ibn Hazim, who said: I heard Nafi', the mawla of Ibn 'Umar, saying: ""The people acquired a conquest in Sham, among them being Bilal — and I think he mentioned Mu'adh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with them both — and they wrote to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: 'This fay' that we have acquired — its fifth is yours and the remainder is for us, and none of it belongs to anyone else, just as the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, did with Khaybar.' 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote back: 'It is not as you say. Rather, I shall make it a waqf for the Muslims.' They exchanged letters — refusing and refusing — and when they refused, 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, stood and invoked against them, saying: 'O Allah, suffice me against Bilal and the companions of Bilal.' Before a year had passed, they had all died."" The Sheikh, may Allah have mercy upon him, said: His statement, may Allah be pleased with him, ""It is not as you say"" — he did not intend by it a denial of their argument regarding the division of Khaybar, for we have narrated it from 'Umar from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him. Rather, it is as though he meant: ""The maslahah (public interest) is not in what you propose; rather, the maslahah lies in my making it a waqf for the Muslims."" He made ""refusing"" (ya'bawna) regarding the distribution that he hoped would win their hearts for him, and they made ""refusing"" (ya'bawna) regarding what was their right. When they refused, he did not enforce the judgment by removing it from their hands and making it a waqf; rather, he invoked against them when they opposed him regarding what he considered to be the maslahah. Had they agreed, he and the common people and his followers would have agreed. And the hadith is mursal (disconnected in its chain), and Allah knows best. And we have narrated in the Book of Division regarding the conquest of Egypt that he held this view, and that al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam, may Allah be pleased with him, held the view that it should be divided just as the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, divided Khaybar. [Da'if]"
18399,"(18393) Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi — narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-'Abbas Muhammad al-Dawri narrated to us: Qurad Abu Nuh narrated to us: al-Murajja ibn Raja' narrated to us, from Abu Salamah, from Qatadah, from Abu Rafi', from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Whatever village Allah and His Messenger conquer, it belongs to Allah and His Messenger; and whatever village the Muslims conquer by force, its fifth belongs to Allah and His Messenger, and the remainder belongs to those who fought for it."" Abu al-Fadl al-Dawri said: Abu Salamah — this one, as far as I am concerned, is the companion of food, or Hammad ibn Salamah. The Sheikh said: We have narrated it in the Book of Division from the hadith of Hammam ibn Munabbih from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, with the same meaning. [Da'if]"
18400,"(18394) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn Bakr — narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Harun ibn Muhammad ibn Bakkar ibn Bilal informed us: Muhammad ibn 'Isa ibn Sumay' narrated to us: Zayd ibn Waqid narrated to me: Abu 'Abd Allah narrated to us, from Mu'adh, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: ""Whoever has the jizyah placed upon his neck, then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, is free of responsibility for what is upon him."" [Da'if]"
18401,"(18395) Abū ʿAlīyy informed us, Abū Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Ḥaywah ibn Sharīḥ al-Ḥaḍramī narrated to us, Baqiyyah narrated to me, ʿUmārah ibn Abī al-Shaʾthāʾ narrated to me, Sinān ibn Qays narrated to me, Shabīb ibn Nuʿaym narrated to me, Yazīd ibn Khumayr narrated to me, and Abū al-Dardāʾ (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Whoever takes a land upon which jizyah is due has nullified his migration (hijrah), and whoever removes the humiliation (ṣaghār) from the neck of a disbeliever and places it upon his own neck has turned his back on Islam.""

Sinān said: Khālid ibn Maʿdān heard this hadith from me and said to me: ""Did Shabīb narrate it to you?"" I said: ""Yes."" He said: ""When I arrive, ask him to write the hadith to me."" So he wrote it to him. When I arrived, Ibn Maʿdān asked me for the parchment, and I gave it to him. When he read it, he abandoned the land that was in his possession upon hearing that.

Abū Dāwūd said: This is Yazīd ibn Khumayr al-Yazanī, not the one who was a companion of Shuʿbah.

The Sheikh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: These two hadiths have a Shāmī chain of narration, and al-Bukhārī and Muslim did not use such narrators as evidence. And Allah knows best. [Weak]"
18402,"(18396) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Abū al-Walīd and Ḥajjāj both said: Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Ḥabīb — meaning Ibn Abī Thābit — who said: I heard Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), when a man asked him and said: ""I reside in al-Sawād and I accept [the arrangement], but I do not wish to increase [my wealth]; I only wish to protect myself."" He recited this verse: ""Fight those who do not believe in Allah nor in the Last Day…"" [al-Tawbah: 29] until His words: ""…until they pay the jizyah with their own hands while they are humbled"" [al-Tawbah: 29]. He said: ""Do not remove the humiliation (ṣaghār) from their necks and place it upon your own neck."" [Authentic]"
18403,"(18397) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Zakariyyāʾ ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us, Ibn Wahb informed me, ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar narrated from Nāfiʿ that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), when asked about a Muslim man who takes land from the People of the Dhimmah upon which kharāj is due, would say: ""It is not permissible for a Muslim, or it is not befitting for a Muslim, to impose humiliation (dhull) and subjugation (ṣaghār) upon himself."" [Weak]"
18404,"(18398) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Burqān, from Maymūn ibn Mihrān, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: ""It would not please me that all the land be mine in exchange for a jizyah of five dirhams, while I remain therein in a state of humiliation upon myself."" [Good]"
18405,"(18399) Abū Saʿīd informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us, al-Ḥasan informed us, Yaḥyā informed us, Sufyān ibn Saʿīd narrated from Jābir, from al-Qāsim, from ʿAbdullāh — meaning Ibn Masʿūd — who said: ""Whoever acknowledges the payment of the ṭasq (land tax) has acknowledged humiliation (ṣaghār)."""
18406,"(18400) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Kārizī narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated from Abī ʿUbayd, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated from Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah, from Qatādah, from Sufyān al-ʿUqaylī, from Abī ʿIyāḍ, from ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""Do not purchase the slaves of the People of the Dhimmah, for they are a people of kharāj — some of them paying on behalf of others — and do not purchase their lands. And let none of you remain in a state of humiliation (ṣaghār) after Allah has delivered him from it.""

Abū ʿUbayd said: He intended, as we understand, that when a person possesses slaves, land, and visible wealth, his jizyah becomes greater. And this was the practice of ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding them — he would assess the jizyah according to ease and hardship. For this reason, he disliked their slaves being purchased. As for the purchase of land, he considered it in terms of the kharāj, and he disliked that this should fall upon the Muslims. Do you not see him saying: ""And let none of you remain in a state of humiliation after Allah has delivered him from it""?

Abū ʿUbayd said: After ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), some of the eminent Companions of Muḥammad (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) permitted this, among them ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, who owned land in Rādhān, and Khabbāb ibn al-Aratt, and others besides them. [Weak]"
18407,"(18401) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, ʿAbadah narrated from Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah, from Qatādah, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), that he used to dislike purchasing anything from kharāj land, saying: ""Upon it is the kharāj of the Muslims."" [Weak]"
18408,"(18402) Abū Saʿīd informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us, al-Ḥasan informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Muʿāwiyah narrated from Kulayb ibn Wāʾil, who said: I said to Ibn ʿUmar: ""I have purchased land."" He said: ""The purchase is good."" I said: ""I give for every jarīb of land one dirham and a qafīz of food."" He said: ""Then do not place humiliation (ṣaghār) upon your own neck."" [Good]"
18409,"(18403) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us in Baghdad, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us, Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from al-Ḥajjāj, from al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, who said: ʿAbdullāh purchased land from kharāj land. Its owner — meaning its dihqān — said to him: ""I will take care of paying its kharāj and managing it on your behalf."" [Weak]"
18410,"(18404) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, Ḥafṣ narrated from Mujāld, from al-Shaʿbī, who said: ʿAbdullāh purchased kharāj land from a dihqān on the condition that the latter would take care of its kharāj on his behalf. [Weak]"
18411,"(18405) Abū Saʿīd informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, al-Ḥasan narrated to us, Yaḥyā narrated to me, Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from Ibn Abī Laylā, who said: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with them both) purchased a salt mine or salt, and al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) purchased two parcels of kharāj land. He said: ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) had returned their land to them and made a settlement with them regarding the kharāj he had imposed upon them. [Weak]"
18412,"(18406) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad narrated to us — he said: I heard Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn — ʿAbbād ibn al-ʿAwwām narrated from al-Ḥajjāj, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ḥasan, that al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn (may Allah be pleased with them both) purchased a parcel of kharāj land. [Weak]"
18413,"(18407) He said, and Yaḥyā narrated to us, ʿAbbād narrated from Ḥajjāj, who said: It reached us that Ḥudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) purchased a parcel of kharāj land. [Weak]"
18414,"(18408) Abū Saʿīd informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to me, ʿAbd al-Raḥīm narrated from Ashʿath, from al-Ḥakam, from Sharīḥ, that he purchased a tract of land from the lands of al-Ḥīrah called Zabbā. He said, and al-Ḥakam said: They used to permit the purchase of the land of al-Ḥīrah because it was land obtained through a treaty (ṣulḥ).

Yaḥyā said: I asked Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ, and he disliked the purchase of kharāj land that had been taken by force upon which kharāj was imposed, but he saw no harm in purchasing the land of the people of treaty (ahl al-ṣulḥ). [Weak]"
18415,"(18409) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Aṣbahānī informed us, Abū ʿAmr ibn Ḥamdān informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ṭalḥah narrated from Dāwūd ibn Sulaymān, who said: ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz (may Allah have mercy on him) wrote to ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, mentioning it, and said in it: ""There is no kharāj upon anyone from the people of the land who embraces Islam."" A musnad ḥadīth has been narrated concerning this: nothing is upon them except ṣadaqah. This has already been discussed along with other matters in the Book of Zakāh. [Weak]"
18416,"(18410) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Salām — who is ibn Ḥarb — narrated from Bukayr ibn ʿĀmir, from ʿĀmir, who said: ʿUtbah ibn Farqad purchased a tract of kharāj land, then he came to ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) and informed him. He said: ""From whom did you purchase it?"" He said: ""From its people."" He said: ""These are its people — the Muslims. Did you buy anything from them?"" They said: ""No."" He said: ""Go and seek your wealth (back)."" [Weak]"
18417,"(18411) Abū Saʿīd informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, al-Ḥasan narrated to us, Yaḥyā narrated to us, Qays narrated from Abū Ismāʿīl, from al-Shaʿbī, from ʿUtbah ibn Farqad, who said: I purchased ten ajribah of land from the Sawād on the bank of the Euphrates for fodder for livestock. That was mentioned to ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: ""Did you purchase it from its owners?"" I said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Come to me."" So I went to him, and he said: ""O people, did you sell him anything?"" They said: ""No."" He said: ""Seek your wealth wherever you placed it."" [Weak]"
18418,"(18412) Abū Saʿīd informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, al-Ḥasan narrated to us, Yaḥyā narrated to us, Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from Qays ibn Muslim, from Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb, who said: A woman from the people of Nahr al-Malik embraced Islam. He said: ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote, or it was written by ʿUmar: ""If she chooses her land and pays what is due upon her land, then leave her and her land alone; otherwise, leave the Muslims and their land alone."" [Good]"
18419,"(18413) Abū Saʿīd informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, al-Ḥasan narrated to us, Yaḥyā narrated to us, Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated from Muḥammad ibn Qays al-Asadī, from Abū ʿAwn al-Thaqafī, who said: ʿUmar and ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with them both) used to leave a man from the people of the Sawād who embraced Islam to remain on his land, paying its kharāj. [Good]"
18420,"(18414) He said, and Yaḥyā narrated to us, Sharīk and Qays narrated from Jābir, from ʿĀmir, who said: al-Rufayl embraced Islam, so ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) gave him his land with its kharāj and assigned him a stipend of two thousand. [Weak]"
18421,"(18415) He said: And Yaḥyā narrated to us — Qays ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us — from Ibrāhīm ibn Muhājir — from a shaykh of Banū Zuhrah — from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāḍ (may Allah be pleased with him): That he wrote to Saʿd, instructing him to grant Saʿīd ibn Zayd a parcel of land. So he granted him the land of Banū al-Rufayl. Then the son of al-Rufayl came to ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, on what terms did you make peace with us?"" He replied: ""On the condition that you pay the jizyah to us, while your land, your wealth, and your children remain yours."" The man said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, have you given my land to Saʿīd ibn Zayd?"" He said: ""So he wrote to Saʿd: 'Return his land to him.' Then he called him to Islam, and he accepted Islam. So ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) assigned him seven hundred [as jizyah] and placed his ʿaṭāʾ among the Khathʿam tribe. And he said: 'If you remain in your land, you shall pay for it what you used to pay.'""

And there is weakness in its isnād. If it is established, then his statement ""while your land remains yours"" would be understood as meaning: ""the land which you used to cultivate and from which you used to pay the kharāj"" — that being among the lands taken by force (ʿanwah). Do you not see that its kharāj was not dropped when he accepted Islam, whereas through a peace treaty it would be dropped. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18422,"(18416) Abū Saʿīd informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us — al-Ḥasan narrated to us — Yaḥyā narrated to us — Ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us — from Maʿmar — from ʿAlī al-Ḥakam — from Muḥammad ibn Zayd, who said: I heard Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī saying: A man came to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: ""I have indeed accepted Islam, so remove the kharāj from my land."" He said: ""No, for your land was taken by force (ʿanwah)."" He said: ""And a man came to him and said: 'Such-and-such people are capable of paying more kharāj than what is upon them.'"" He said: ""There is no way regarding them; rather, we made peace with them upon [specific] terms."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18423,"(18417) He said: And Yaḥyā narrated to us — Hushaym narrated to us — from Sayyār, Abū al-Ḥakam — from al-Zubayr ibn ʿAdī, who said: A dihqān from the people of al-Sawād accepted Islam during the caliphate of ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him). So ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: ""If you remain in your land, we shall remove the jizyah from you and take [the kharāj] from your land. But if you relocate from it, then we have the greater right to it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18424,"(18418) He said: And Yaḥyā narrated to us — Wakīʿ narrated to us — from al-Masʿūdī — from Abū ʿAwn, who said: A dihqān from the people of ʿAyn al-Tamr accepted Islam. So ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: ""As for the jizyah upon your person, we shall remove it. As for your land, it belongs to the Muslims. If you wish, we shall assign [a stipend] for you, and if you wish, we shall make you a qahramān for us, and whatever Allah brings forth from it, you shall bring to us."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18425,"(18419) The shaykh said: And this is from what Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us — they both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us —

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Dāwūd al-Razzāz informed us in Baghdad — Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us — they both said: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us — Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us — Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid narrated to us — from Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim — from Jarīr ibn ʿAbd Allāh (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent a sariyyah to Khathʿam. A number of them sought protection by prostrating [in sajdah], but the fighters made haste to kill them. This reached the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family), so he commanded that half the blood-money (ʿaql) be paid for them, and he said: ""I am innocent of every Muslim who resides among the backs of the polytheists."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, why?"" He said: ""Do you not see that they cannot see each other's fire."" [Ḍaʿīf; previously cited as no. 16470]"
18426,"(18420) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us — Isḥāq ibn Idrīs narrated to us — Hammām narrated to us — from Qatādah — from al-Ḥasan — from Samurah (may Allah be pleased with him) — from the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family), who said: ""Do not reside alongside the polytheists, nor intermingle with them. Whoever resides alongside them or intermingles with them is not of us."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18427,"(18421) And Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak narrated to us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us — Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Shuʿbah narrated to us — from al-Aʿmash, who said: I heard Abū Wāʾil narrating from ʿAbd Allāh (may Allah be pleased with him) — from the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family), who said: ""For every traitor (ghādir) there shall be a banner (liwāʾ) on the Day of Resurrection, and it shall be said: 'This is the treachery of so-and-so.'""

They both [al-Bukhārī and Muslim] recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ; agreed upon (muttafaq ʿalayh)]"
18428,"(18422) And Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us — Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Abān narrated to us — from al-Suddī — from Rifāʿah ibn Shaddād (may Allah be pleased with him) — ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥamiq al-Khuzāʿī (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""When a man grants a man safety (amānah) over his life and then kills him, we are innocent of the killer, even if the slain were a disbeliever."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18429,"(18423) And Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us — Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Qurrah ibn Khālid narrated to us — from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr — from Rifāʿah ibn Shaddād, who said: ""I had concealed something for al-Mukhtār — meaning 'the Liar.' He said: 'One day I entered upon him and he said: ""You have come, when Jibrīl had already risen from this chair."" So I reached for the hilt of the sword and said: ""I will not wait to walk between this one's head and his body"" — until I remembered a ḥadīth that ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥamiq al-Khuzāʿī (may Allah be pleased with him) had narrated to me, that the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: 'When a man grants a man safety over his blood and then kills him, the banner of treachery shall be raised for him on the Day of Resurrection.' So I held back from him."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18430,"(18424) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAnazī informed me — ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us — Ibn Wahb narrated to me — Ḥaywah ibn Sharīḥ narrated to me — from Ibn al-Hād — from Sharḥabīl ibn Saʿd — from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) during the expedition of Khaybar. A sariyyah went out and captured a man who had sheep that he was tending. They brought him to the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family). The Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) spoke to him as Allah willed. The man said: ""I have believed in you and in what you have brought. But what about the sheep, O Messenger of Allah? They are a trust, and they belong to the people — one sheep, two, and even more than that."" He said: ""Pelt their faces, and they will return to their owners."" So he took a handful of pebbles or dust and threw it at their faces. They went running back until every sheep entered its owner's home. Then he advanced to the battle line and a stray arrow struck him and killed him. He had never once prostrated to Allah [in sajdah]. The Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Take him into the tent."" So he was taken into the tent of the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family). When the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) had finished, he went in to him and then came out and said: ""Indeed, the Islam of your companion has been excellent. I entered upon him, and he had with him two wives from al-Ḥūr al-ʿĪn.""

I have not recorded it as connected (mawṣūl) except from the ḥadīth of Sharḥabīl ibn Saʿd, and they have spoken critically regarding it. (T) And it has been narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Yasār from his father as a mursal [report].

And a similar story has been narrated from Abū al-ʿĀṣ ibn al-Rabīʿ, except that it is with a mursal isnād. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18431,"(18425) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us; Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Ishaq; 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm narrated to me, who said: Abu al-'As ibn al-Rabi' set out as a merchant to Syria. He was a trustworthy man, and he was carrying merchandise belonging to Quraysh. When he was returning, a detachment of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) encountered them and drove off his caravan. He escaped, and they brought what they had seized to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family). He divided it among them. Then Abu al-'As came to Zaynab (may Allah be pleased with her) and sought her protection, asking her to request the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) to return to him his property and the property of other people that was with him. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) summoned the detachment and asked them, and they returned it. Then he departed until he came to Makkah and repaid to the people whatever merchandise of theirs he had, until when he finished he said: ""O company of Quraysh, is there anyone among you who still has property with me that I have not returned to him?"" They said: ""No. May Allah reward you with good. We have found you faithful and noble."" He said: ""By Allah, nothing prevented me from accepting Islam before I came to you except my fear that you would think I had only accepted Islam in order to take your wealth. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger.""

Al-Shafi'i said regarding the Muslim who is captured and is not given a guarantee of safety, nor is it taken from them that they are safe from him: he is permitted to take whatever he can of their property, to destroy it, and to escape from them.

The Sheikh said: We have narrated the hadith of 'Imran ibn Husayn (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding the Muslim woman who took a she-camel and escaped on it. [Da'if]"
18432,"(18426) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us; Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Ishaq; al-Zuhri narrated to me, from Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, from Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her), the wife of the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family), that she said: When Makkah became straitened for us, she mentioned the hadith of their migration to the land of Abyssinia, and what transpired regarding Quraysh's sending of 'Amr ibn al-'As and 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Rabi'ah to the Negus to expel them from their land and return them to Quraysh, and what transpired regarding Ja'far ibn Abi Talib and his companions (may Allah be pleased with them) entering upon the Negus. He said: The Negus asked: ""Do you have anything with which he came?"" Ja'far said: ""Yes."" He then recited to him the opening portion of {Kaf-Ha-Ya-'Ayn-Sad} [Surah Maryam, 1]. By Allah, the Negus wept until his beard became wet, and his bishops wept until their scriptures became wet. Then he said: ""Indeed, this speech issues from the same niche from which what came to Moses issued. Go forth, rightly guided."" Then he mentioned the hadith regarding their description to him of what they say about 'Isa ibn Maryam (peace be upon him), that he is a servant. They came to him while his courtiers were with him, and he asked: ""What do you say about 'Isa ibn Maryam (peace be upon him)?"" Ja'far said to him: ""We say he is the servant of Allah, His Messenger, His Word, and His Spirit which He cast into Maryam, the chaste virgin."" The Negus placed his hand to the ground and took up a small stick between his fingers and said: ""'Isa ibn Maryam is no more than what you have said, by this stick."" Then he mentioned the hadith. She said: It was not long before a man from the Abyssinians came out disputing with him over his kingdom. By Allah, we have never known grief more severe than that, out of fear that that king would prevail over him and a king would come who would not recognize our rights as this one recognized them. We began supplicating Allah and seeking His help for the Negus. He went out against him marching, and the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) said to one another: ""Who is a man who will go out and be present at the battle to see how it turns out?"" Al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) said — and he was the youngest of them in age — ""I will."" They blew air into a water-skin and placed it on his chest, and he went out swimming on it across the Nile until he reached the other side to where the people had gathered. He was present at the battle. Allah routed that king and killed him, and the Negus prevailed over him. Al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) came to us, waving his cloak at us and saying: ""Rejoice! Allah has granted victory to the Negus."" By Allah, we have never rejoiced at anything as we rejoiced at the victory of the Negus. [Sahih]"
18433,"(18427) The Sheikh said: Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed him; Ahmad ibn Ja'far al-Qati'i narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to me; my father narrated to us; 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated from Ma'mar; al-Zuhri said: 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr narrated to me from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Marwan ibn al-Hakam, and they mentioned the hadith of the Treaty of Hudaybiyyah, and mentioned in it the story of Abu Jandal and Abu Basir, in a similar but more complete version.

The Sheikh said: The Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) only returned Abu Jandal to them because there was no fear for him in the return on account of his father's standing. Similarly, he advised Abu Basir to return to them at the outset for that reason. And Allah knows best. Al-Shafi'i's statement on this matter, if Allah wills, will be presented in the Book of Jizyah. [Sahih. Bukhari 28342]"
18434,"(18428) In a similar vein, Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz, Abu Muhammad ibn Abi Hamid al-Muqri', Abu Bakr al-Qadi, and Abu Sadiq al-'Attar informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas, al-Asamm, narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us; 'Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed me; 'Amr ibn al-Harith narrated from Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj that al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn Abi Rafi' narrated to him that Abu Rafi' (may Allah be pleased with him) informed him: He came with a letter from Quraysh to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family). He said: When I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family), Islam was cast into my heart, so I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, I will never return to them."" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) said: ""I do not betray a trust, nor do withhold the couriers. But return, and if what is in your heart now is still there, then return."" He said: I returned to them, then I came to the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) and accepted Islam. Bukayr said: And he informed me that Abu Rafi' was a Copt. [Sahih]"
18435,"(18429) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me; Ahmad ibn Ja'far al-Qati'i narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to me; my father narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad narrated — 'Abd Allah said: I heard it myself from 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Shaybah — Abu Usamah narrated from al-Walid ibn Jami'; Abu al-Tufayl narrated to us; Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Nothing prevented me from being present at Badr except that I went out with my father, Husayl. He said: The disbelievers of Quraysh seized us and said: ""You intend to go to Muhammad."" We said: ""We do not intend him; we only intend to go to Madinah."" They took from us a covenant of Allah and a pledge that we would go to Madinah and not fight alongside him. We came to the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) and informed him of the news. He said: ""Go back. We will fulfill their covenant for them, and we will seek help from Allah against them.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Bakr 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Shaybah.

The Sheikh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: This was because their withdrawal did not entail the abandonment of an obligation, since their going out was not obligatory upon them, nor did it entail committing a prohibited act. And returning to them and residing among them is not permissible when one fears tribulation upon himself in returning. And Allah knows best. [Sahih. Muslim 1787]"
18436,"(18430) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; some of the people of Madinah informed us from Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah from al-Zuhri: that Musrif presented Yazid ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Zam'ah on the Day of Harrah to have his neck struck. He divorced his wife without having consummated the marriage. They asked the people of knowledge, and they said to her: she is entitled to half the dower, and she has no inheritance. [Da'if]"
18437,"(18431) With his chain of narration, al-Shafi'i informed us; some of the people of knowledge informed us from Hisham from his father: that the general charitable bequests of al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) were given as charity, and he performed certain matters while mounted on the back of his horse on the Day of the Camel.

Al-Shafi'i (may Allah be pleased with him) said: It has been narrated from 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz (may Allah have mercy on him) and Ibn al-Musayyib (may Allah have mercy on him) that they both said: When a man is on the back of his horse fighting, whatever he does is permissible. And it has been narrated from 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz (may Allah have mercy on him): The gift of a pregnant woman is permissible until she sits among the midwives. And al-Qasim ibn Muhammad and Ibn al-Musayyib said: The gift of a pregnant woman is permissible. Al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on him) said: We hold all of this as our position.

The Sheikh said: The hadith of al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) — we have narrated it in full in the Book of Wills. [Da'if]"
18438,"(18432) Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak informed us; 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far al-Isbahani narrated to us; Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us; Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated from al-Zuhri; from 'Umar ibn Asid ibn Jarah, an ally of Banu Zuhrah, and he was one of the companions of Abu Hurayrah; from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) sent out ten men as a scouting party and appointed over them 'Asim ibn Thabit ibn Abi al-Aqlah — and he was the grandfather of 'Asim, meaning Ibn 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him). They set out until they were at al-Haddah, between 'Usfan and Makkah. They were mentioned to a tribe of Hudhayl called Banu Lihyan, and they mustered against them one hundred archers. They followed their tracks until they found their provisions of dates and said: ""These are the dates of Yathrib."" When 'Asim and his companions (may Allah be pleased with them) became aware of them, they took refuge in a hilltop. The people surrounded them and said: ""Come down, and you have our covenant and pledge that none of you will be killed."" 'Asim said: ""As for me, by Allah, I will not come down under the protection of a disbeliever today. O Allah, convey greetings of peace from us to Your Prophet."" They fought them, and seven of them were killed. Three came down on the covenant and pledge. When they gained power over them, they loosened the strings of their bows and tied their arms behind their backs. When one of the three saw that, he said: ""By Allah, this is the first treachery."" They struggled with him and killed him. They took Khubayb ibn 'Adiy and Zayd ibn al-Dathinnah and brought them to Makkah and sold them. That was after the Battle of Badr. Banu al-Harith purchased Khubayb, and he had killed al-Harith on the Day of Badr. The daughter of al-Harith said: Khubayb was a prisoner with us, and by Allah, I have never seen a prisoner better than Khubayb. By Allah, I saw him eating a cluster of grapes, and there was no fruit in Makkah at that time. It was nothing but a provision that Allah had bestowed upon Khubayb. She said: He asked me for a razor to shave with for the execution. I said: I lent it to him. A small child of mine crawled while I was unaware, and I saw him sitting on his chest. She said: I was startled by a fright that Khubayb recognized. She said: He became aware of my state and said: ""Do you think I would kill him? I would never do that."" She said: When they resolved to kill him, he said to them: ""Let me pray two rak'ahs."" She said: He prayed two rak'ahs and then said: ""Were it not that you would think I was afraid, I would have added more."" Khubayb was the first to establish the practice of prayer for one who is killed in captivity. Then he said: ""O Allah, count them as a number, kill them one by one, and leave none of them alive."" And he began to recite:

I care not when I am killed as a Muslim,
In what state I am in, in the cause of my Lord, my place of death.
And that is in the side of the Almighty, and if He wills,
He will bless the limbs of a torn-apart body.

He said: The polytheists sent to 'Asim ibn Thabit to be given some of his flesh, for he had killed one of their nobles. Allah sent upon them something like a canopy of wasps, which protected him from their envoys, so they were unable to take any of his flesh. [Sahih. Bukhari 3045]"
18439,"(18433) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us; Ibrahim, meaning Ibn Sa'd, informed us; Ibn Shihab informed me; 'Umar ibn Jarah al-Thaqafi, an ally of Banu Zuhrah, narrated from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), and he mentioned it with the same meaning in abridged form, without the poetry and without the story of 'Asim at the end.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Musa ibn Isma'il in full. He said: And Ibn Asid ibn Jarah informed me — and he is 'Amr ibn Abi Sufyan ibn Asid ibn Jarah al-Thaqafi, and it is also said 'Umar ibn Asid. Al-Bukhari said: The first is more correct, meaning 'Amr ibn Abi Sufyan ibn Asid is more correct. Shu'ayb ibn Abi Hamzah, Ma'mar, Yunus, and others said the same from al-Zuhri. [Sahih]"
18440,"(18434) Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Dawud al-'Alawi (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us; 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn al-Sharqi narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Hashim ibn Hayyan al-Tusi narrated to us; Sufyan ibn 'Uyyaynah narrated.

(And) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz, Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan, and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman al-Muradi informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; Sufyan ibn 'Uyyaynah narrated from 'Amr ibn Dinar from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad from 'Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Rafi', who said: I heard 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) sent me, al-Zubayr, and al-Miqdad and said: ""Go out until you reach the meadow of Khakh, for there is a woman there with a letter."" We set out, our horses racing us, and there we found a woman. We said: ""Produce the letter."" She said: ""I have no letter."" We said to her: ""You must produce the letter, or we will strip you."" She produced it from her braids. We brought it to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family), and in it was a letter from Hatib ibn Abi Balta'ah to certain polytheists among the people of Makkah, informing them of some of the affairs of the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family). He said: ""What is this, O Hatib?"" He said: ""Do not be hasty with me. I was a man attached to Quraysh but not one of their own, and those of the emigrants who were with you had relatives through whom they protected their kin, but I had no relative in Makkah. So I desired, when I lacked that, to establish a favor with them. By Allah, I did not do it out of doubt in my religion, nor out of satisfaction with disbelief after Islam."" The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) said: ""He has spoken the truth."" 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""O Messenger of Allah, let me strike the neck of this hypocrite."" The Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) said: ""He witnessed Badr, and what do you know — perhaps Allah looked upon the people of Badr and said: 'Do as you wish, for I have forgiven you.'"" And there was revealed: {O you who believe, do not take My enemy and your enemy as allies, casting affection toward them} [Al-Mumtahinah, 1].

Al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it in their two Sahihs from a group from Sufyan. [Sahih. Agreed upon]"
18441,"(18435) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us that Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah informed us that Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us that Hushaym informed us, from Ḥuṣayn, from Saʿd ibn ʿUbaydah, from Abū ʿAbd al-Rahmān al-Sulamī and Ḥayyān ibn ʿAṭiyyah al-Sulamī, that the two of them used to dispute regarding ʿAlī and ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with them both). Ḥayyān used to love ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), and Abū ʿAbd al-Rahmān used to love ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him). Abū ʿAbd al-Rahmān said: ""I heard him narrate"" — meaning ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) — who said: ""Ḥāṭib ibn Abī Baltaʿah wrote to the people of Makkah that Muhammad intended to attack them with his Companions, so take your precautions. He handed his letter to a woman called Sārah, and she placed it in her garment, tucking it into one of her braids. She set out, and Allah informed His Messenger (peace and blessings be upon him) of that."" ʿAlī said: ""He sent me, and with me were al-Zubayr ibn al-ʿAwwām and Abū Murthad al-Ghanawī, and all of us were horsemen. He said: 'Go forth, for you will meet her at such-and-so meadow. Search her, for she has with her a letter from Ḥāṭib to the people of Makkah.' So we set out and came upon her. We said: 'Produce the letter you have with you to the people of Makkah.' She said: 'I have no letter.' I said: 'I have not lied nor been lied to. You will produce it, or I will strip you.' When she realized that I would indeed do it, she produced the letter. We took it and brought it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). He opened it and read it, and in it was: 'From Ḥāṭib to the people of Makkah. To proceed: Muhammad intends to attack you, so take your precautions and make ready' — or as he said. When he read the letter, he sent for Ḥāṭib and said to him: 'Did you write this letter?' He said: 'Yes.' He said: 'What compelled you to do this?' He said: 'O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, I have not disbelieved since I embraced Islam, and I am a believer in Allah and His Messenger. What compelled me to write this letter to the people of Makkah was that none of your Companions had anyone there in Makkah to defend his family and his property, whereas I had no one there to defend my family and my property. So I wished to place a debt of gratitude with the people, and I knew that Allah would grant victory to His Messenger over them.' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) believed him and accepted his excuse."" He said: ""Then ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) stood and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, permit me to strike his neck, for he has betrayed Allah and the believers.' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'O ʿUmar, he is among the people of Badr, and what do you know — perhaps Allah looked upon them and said: ""Do as you wish, for I have forgiven you.""'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ḥawshab, from Hushaym, and both he and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Idrīs and others, from Ḥuṣayn. Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: ""It has been narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he said: 'Overlook the ḥudūd in cases involving people of standing.' And it has been said in the ḥadīth: 'So long as it is not a ḥadd (prescribed punishment).' If this (offence) comes from a person of standing and is committed in a state of ignorance, as was the case with Ḥāṭib, who acted out of ignorance, and he is not under suspicion, then I would prefer that he be shown leniency. But if it comes from one who is not of standing, then it is for the Imam to impose discretionary punishment (taʿzīr), and Allah knows best."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon.]"
18442,"(18436) Abū al-Qāsim ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Yaʿqūb al-Iyādī informed us in Baghdād that Abū Bakr al-Shāfiʿī informed us that Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥarbī informed us that Abū Nuʿaym informed us that Abū ʿUmays informed us, from Ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ, from his father, who said: ""A spy from among the polytheists came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) while he was on a journey. He sat and conversed with his Companions, then slipped away. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'Seek him out and kill him.' He said: 'I outran them to him, killed him, and took his spoils.'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Nuʿaym. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon.]"
18443,"(18437) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dīnār informed us that al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah informed us that Abū Hammām al-Dallāl informed us in the mosque of al-Baṣrah that Sufyān al-Thawrī informed us, from Abī Isḥāq, from Ḥārithah ibn Muḍarrib, from al-Furāt ibn Ḥayyān — and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) had ordered his killing, and he had been a spy for Abū Sufyān and an ally. I think he said to a man from the Anṣār — that he passed by a gathering of the Anṣār and said: 'I am a Muslim.' A man from among them stood and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, he says: ""I am a Muslim.""' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'Among them are men whom cowardice drives to their faith — among them is al-Furāt ibn Ḥayyān.'"" [Ḍaʿīf — previously cited under no. 116831.]"
18444,"(18438) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAnazī informed us that ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī informed us that Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl informed us that Ḥammād ibn Salamah informed us. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us that Abū Dāwūd informed us that Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl informed us that Ḥammād informed us, from Thābit, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) urged his Companions to set out and they departed for Badr. They came upon the watering camels of Quraysh, among which was a black slave belonging to Banī al-Ḥajjāj. The Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) seized him and began asking him: 'Where is Abū Sufyān?' He said: 'By Allah, I have no knowledge of any of his affairs. But these are Quraysh — among them are Abū Jahl, ʿUtbah, Shaybah the sons of Rabīʿah, and Umayyah ibn Khalaf.' When he told them that, they would beat him, and he would say: 'Leave me, leave me, I will tell you.' When they left him, he said: 'By Allah, I have no knowledge of Abū Sufyān. But these are Quraysh — among them are Abū Jahl, ʿUtbah, Shaybah the sons of Rabīʿah, and Umayyah ibn Khalaf — they have come.' The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) was praying and heard that. When he finished, he said: 'By Him in whose Hand is my soul, you beat him when he tells the truth and you leave him when he lies. These Quraysh have come to protect Abū Sufyān.'"" Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'This is the place where so-and-so will fall tomorrow' — and he placed his hand on the ground. 'And this is the place where so-and-so will fall tomorrow' — and he placed his hand on the ground. 'And this is the place where so-and-so will fall tomorrow' — and he placed his hand on the ground. He said: 'By Him in whose Hand is my soul, not one of them went beyond the place where the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) had placed his hand.' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) ordered concerning them, and they were seized by their feet, dragged, and thrown into the well of Badr."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from another chain from Ḥammād. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 1779.]"
18445,"(18439) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr al-Qāḍī both said that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us that Abū al-Naḍr informed us that Sulaymān — meaning Ibn al-Mughīrah — informed us, from Thābit, from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) sent Bussaysah as a spy to see what the caravan of Abū Sufyān had done. He came, and there was no one in the house besides me and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and he related the report to him."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Abī al-Naḍr as previously cited. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 1901.]"
18446,"(18440) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us that Abū al-Qāsim Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Lakhmī informed us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam informed us that al-Firyābī informed us. (Ḥ) And ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us that Abū Nuʿaym informed us. Both said: Sufyān informed us, from Ibn al-Munkadir, from Jābir (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said on the day of al-Aḥzāb: 'Who will bring me news of the people?' al-Zubayr said: 'I will.' Then he said: 'Who will bring me news of the people?' al-Zubayr said: 'I will.' Then he said: 'Who will bring me news of the people?' al-Zubayr said: 'I will.' The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'Every prophet has a ḥawārī (disciple), and my ḥawārī is al-Zubayr.'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Nuʿaym, and Muslim narrated it from another chain from al-Thawrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon.]"
18447,"(18441) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs informed us that ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd informed us that ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī informed us that Sufyān informed us that Ibn al-Munkadir informed us. He said: ""I heard Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh (may Allah be pleased with him) say: 'The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) called the people to action on the day of al-Khandaq, and al-Zubayr responded. Then he called them again, and al-Zubayr responded. Then he called them again, and al-Zubayr responded. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Every prophet has a ḥawārī, and my ḥawārī is al-Zubayr.""'"" Sufyān said: ""Hishām ibn ʿUrwah added in it: 'And my ḥawārī is al-Zubayr, the son of my paternal aunt.'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn al-Madīnī, and Muslim narrated it from ʿAmr al-Nāqid from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon.]"
18448,"(18442) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb and Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm both said that Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us that Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us that Jarīr informed us, from al-Aʿmash, from Ibrāhīm al-Taymī, from his father, who said: ""We were with Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān (may Allah be pleased with him) when a man said: 'Had I caught the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), I would have fought alongside him and worn myself out (in his cause).' Ḥudhayfah said to him: 'Would you have done that? I saw us with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) on the night of al-Aḥzāb, on a night of intense bitter cold and wind. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Is there a man who will bring me news of the people and be with me on the Day of Resurrection?"" None of us answered him. Then he said the same a second time. Then he said: ""O Ḥudhayfah, go and bring us news of the people."" I found no escape when he called me by my name but to stand. He said: ""Bring me news of the people, and do not alarm them against me."" He said: ""I set out as though I was walking in a bathhouse until I reached them. There was Abū Sufyān warming his back by the fire. I placed my arrow in the belly of my bow and wanted to shoot him, but I remembered the words of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him): 'Do not alarm them against me.' Had I shot him, I would have hit him."" He said: ""I returned as though I was walking in a bathhouse until I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). Then the cold struck me when I had finished and I shivered. I informed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) clothed me from the excess of a cloak he had in which he was praying. I did not cease sleeping until morning. When it was morning, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'Get up, O sleeper!'"" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 1788.]"
18449,"(18443) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us that Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us that Abū al-Azhar informed us that Marwān ibn Muḥammad informed us that Muʿāwiyah ibn Salām informed us. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — and the wording is his — that Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Salamah al-ʿAnazī informed us that ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī informed us that Abū Tawbah al-Rabīʿ ibn Nāfiʿ al-Ḥalabī informed us that Muʿāwiyah ibn Salām informed us that Zayd ibn Salām informed me that Abū Kabshah al-Salūlī informed me that he heard Sahl ibn al-Ḥanẓalīyah (may Allah be pleased with him) mention that they set out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) on the day of Ḥunayn and they pressed on with the journey until it was evening. The prayer was due with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). A horseman came and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I went ahead of you until I climbed such-and-such mountain, and there I found Hawāzin, with their families, their cattle, and their sheep, gathered at Ḥunayn.' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) smiled and said: 'That will be the spoils of the Muslims tomorrow, if Allah wills.' Then he said: 'Who will guard us tonight?' Anas ibn Abī Murthad al-Ghanawī (may Allah be pleased with him) said: 'I will, O Messenger of Allah.' He said: 'Mount.' So he mounted his horse and came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said to him: 'Face this mountain pass and be at its highest point, and let us not be taken unawares from your direction tonight.' When we woke, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) went to his place of prayer and performed two rakʿahs. Then he said: 'Have you sensed your horseman?' A man said: 'We have not sensed him.' The call to prayer was given, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) kept turning toward the mountain pass until he completed his prayer and gave the salām. He said: 'Rejoice, for your horseman has come.' He said: ""We began looking through the gaps in the trees in the mountain pass, and there he was — he had come and stood before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and gave salām. He said: 'I set out until I was at the highest point of this mountain pass, as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded me. When morning came, I looked down upon both sides of the pass and saw no one.' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said to him: 'Did you dismount during the night?' He said: 'No, except for one praying or one relieving himself.' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said to him: 'You have made Paradise obligatory for yourself; there is no blame upon you if you do not work after this.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
18450,"(18444) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us that Musaddad informed us that Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd informed us that Thawr ibn Yazīd informed us, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿĀʾidh, from Mujāhid, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Shall I not inform you of a night better than the Night of Decree? A guard who keeps watch in a land of fear, perhaps he will not return to his family."" Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān raised it (as a marfūʿ ḥadīth), and Wakīʿ stopped it (as a mawqūf ḥadīth). [Ḍaʿīf.]"
18451,"(18445) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us, Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam narrated to us, Ibn Wahb narrated to us, 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Shurayh narrated to me from Muhammad ibn Sumayr from Abu 'Ali al-Janbi from Abu Rayhanah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) on a military expedition. He led us to an elevated place, and intense cold afflicted us, to the point that each of us would dig a trench, enter it, and cover himself with his leather shield. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) saw that state of the people, he said: ""Is there a man who will guard us this night, that I may supplicate Allah for him with a supplication through which he will attain merit?"" A man from the Ansar stood and said: ""I will, O Messenger of Allah."" So he supplicated for him. Abu Rayhanah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Then I [also offered], and he supplicated for me with a supplication lesser than that which he had supplicated for the Ansari."" Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""The Fire is forbidden upon an eye that weeps out of fear of Allah; the Fire is forbidden upon an eye that keeps watch in the cause of Allah."" He said: ""And I have forgotten the third."" Abu Shurayh, who is 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Shurayh, said: ""And I heard him afterwards say: 'The Fire is forbidden upon an eye that is lowered from the prohibitions of Allah, or an eye that is gouged out in the cause of Allah.'"" [Da'if]"
18452,"(18446) Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-'Alawi informed us, Abu Nasr Muhammad ibn Hamdawayh ibn Sahl al-Marwazi informed us, 'Abd Allah ibn Hammad al-Amuli narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us, Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Jamil al-Jumahi narrated to us, Salih ibn Muhammad narrated to us from 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz from Qays ibn al-Harith, who reported that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""May Allah have mercy upon the guard of the watch."" [Da'if]"
18453,"(18447) It is narrated from al-Darawardi from Salih from 'Umar from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). It was reported to us by 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan, Ahmad ibn 'Ubaid narrated to us, Sa'id ibn 'Uthman al-Ahwazi narrated to us, 'Ali ibn Bahr narrated to us, al-Darawardi narrated it."
18454,"(18448) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us, Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Sadaqah ibn Yasar narrated to me from Ibn Jabir from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) on the expedition of Dhat al-Riqa' from Nakhl. He mentioned the hadith and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) halted at a place and said: ""Who is the man who will guard us this night?"" A man from the Muhajirun and a man from the Ansar volunteered and said: ""We will, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""Then be at the mouth of the mountain pass."" When they went out to the mouth of the pass, the Ansari said to the Muhajir: ""Which part of the night would you prefer that I cover for you—its first part or its last part?"" He said: ""Rather, cover the first part for me."" So the Muhajir lay down and slept, and the Ansari stood up to pray. He mentioned the hadith. [Da'if]"
18455,"(18449) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us, Ahmad ibn 'Ubaid al-Saffar informed us, 'Ubayd ibn Sharik narrated to us. And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us, Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us, 'Ubayd ibn Sharik narrated to us, Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us, al-Layth narrated from 'Uqayl from Ibn Shahab from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, that 'Abd Allah ibn Ka'b said: I heard Ka'b ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrating about when he stayed behind from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). He mentioned the hadith and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) would rarely intend a military expedition without alluding to another [to conceal its true direction]. Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Yahya ibn Bukayr, and Muslim narrated it from another route from al-Layth. [Sahih. Muttafaq 'alayh]"
18456,"(18450) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us, Abu al-'Abbas al-Sayyari informed us, Ibrahim ibn Hilal informed us, 'Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq informed us, 'Abd Allah informed us, Yunus narrated from al-Zuhri, 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik narrated to me, who said: I heard Ka'b ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) would rarely intend a military expedition without alluding to another, until the expedition of Tabuk came. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) undertook it in intense heat, facing a long journey and a vast desert, and facing a numerous enemy. So he made the matter clear to the Muslims so that they might prepare the preparation for their enemy, and he informed them of the direction he intended. Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Ahmad ibn Muhammad from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak, and Muslim narrated it from another route from Yunus, similar to the isnad of 'Uqayl. [Sahih. Muttafaq 'alayh]"
18457,"(18451) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us, Abu Bakr ibn Dasah informed us, Abu Dawud narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us, Ibn Thawr narrated from Ma'mar from al-Zuhri from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik from his father, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) would, when he intended a military expedition, allude to another, and he would say: ""War is deception."" [Sahih]"
18458,"(18452) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us, Abu Hamid Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Bilal al-Bazzaz narrated to us, 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Bishr and Yahya ibn al-Rabi' al-Makki said: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated to us from 'Amr ibn Dinar from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""War is deception."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Sadaqah ibn al-Fadl, and Muslim narrated it from 'Ali ibn Hajr and Zuhayr, all of them from Ibn 'Uyaynah. [Sahih. Muttafaq 'alayh]"
18459,"(18453) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us, Abu Bakr al-Qattan informed us, Ahmad ibn Yusuf narrated to us, 'Abd al-Razzak informed us, Ma'mar narrated from Hammam ibn Munabbih, who said: This is what Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to us from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family): that he named war ""deception."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad from 'Abd al-Razzak, and both of them narrated it from the hadith of Ibn al-Mubarak from Ma'mar. [Sahih. Muttafaq 'alayh]"
18460,"(18454) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr al-Qadi said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us, Mahmud ibn Ghaylan narrated to us, 'Abd al-Razzak informed us, Ma'mar said: I heard Thabit al-Bunani narrating from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) conquered Khaybar, al-Hajjaj ibn 'Illat said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have wealth in Makkah and I have family there, and I wish to go to them. If I obtain anything from you [by way of permission to speak], then I am free to do so."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) gave him permission to say what he wished. He said: So he came to his wife when he arrived and said: ""Gather for me whatever you have, for I wish to purchase from the war gains of Muhammad and his companions, for they have been plundered and their wealth has been seized."" He said: That news spread throughout Makkah. The Muslims withdrew and the polytheists showed joy and delight. The news reached 'Abd al-Muttalib, and he collapsed and could not stand. Ma'mar said: 'Uthman al-Jazari informed me from Miqsam, who said: So 'Abbas took a son of his called Qutham, laid down, and placed him upon his chest, saying:

""My beloved Qutham, the likeness of the one of the noble nose,
My Prophet of blessings, despite whoever shows spite.""

Ma'mar said: Thabit said: Anas said in his hadith: Then 'Abbas ibn 'Abd al-Muttalib sent a young servant of his to al-Hajjaj ibn 'Illat, saying: ""Woe to you! What have you brought and what are you saying? What Allah has promised is better than what you have brought."" Al-Hajjaj ibn 'Illat said to his young servant: ""Greet Abu al-Fadl with salam and tell him to set aside for me one of his houses, for I will come to him, for the news is as he would love it to be."" His young servant came and when he reached the door of the house, he said: ""Good tidings, O Abu al-Fadl!"" 'Abbas leapt up in joy and kissed between his eyes, and he informed him of what al-Hajjaj had said. So 'Abbas freed him. Then al-Hajjaj came and informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) had conquered Khaybar, seized their wealth, and that the shares of Allah had been distributed among their properties. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) had chosen Safiyyah bint Huyayy for himself and gave her the choice between being freed and becoming his wife, or joining her family. She chose to be freed and become his wife. ""But I have come for wealth I had here, which I wished to collect and take away. I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) for permission to say what I wished, and he gave me permission. So conceal my matter for three days, then mention what you will."" His wife gathered whatever she had of jewelry or goods and handed them to him, and he departed with them. After three days, 'Abbas came to al-Hajjaj's wife and said: ""What has your husband done?"" She informed him that he had left on such-and-such a day, and she said: ""May Allah not grieve you, O Abu al-Fadl! What has reached us has been distressing."" He said: ""No, may Allah not grieve me. It has been, by the praise of Allah, only what we had hoped for. Allah has granted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) victory at Khaybar, and the shares of Allah were distributed therein, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) chose Safiyyah for himself. If you have need of your husband, then go to him."" She said: ""I think, by Allah, you are truthful."" He said: ""I am truthful, and the matter is as I have told you."" Then he went until he came to the assembly of Quraysh, who were saying when he passed by them: ""Nothing but good has befallen you, O Abu al-Fadl!"" He said: ""Nothing but good has befalled me, by the praise of Allah. Al-Hajjaj ibn 'Illat has informed me that Allah has granted His Messenger (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) victory at Khaybar, and the shares of Allah were distributed therein, and he chose Safiyyah for himself. He asked me to conceal his matter for three days. He has only come to take his wealth and whatever he had here, and then depart."" So Allah turned back the grief that had been in the Muslims upon the polytheists. The Muslims who had entered their houses in sorrow came out to 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), and he informed them. The Muslims rejoiced, and Allah removed from them what had been of anger and grief. [Sahih. Narrated by 'Abd al-Razzak (9771), Ahmad (12001)]"
18461,"(18455) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-'Abbas al-Sayyari narrated to us — Ibrahim ibn Hilal narrated to us — 'Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq narrated to us — 'Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us — Yunus informed us — from al-Zuhri — 'Abd Rahman ibn Ka'b ibn Malik informed me that Ka'b ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, used to say: ""It was rare that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, would set out on a journey when he did so except on a Thursday.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Ahmad ibn Muhammad from Ibn al-Mubarak. [Sahih. Bukhari 2949]"
18462,"(18455) Abu Muhammad ibn Yusuf narrated to us — Abu Bakr al-Qattan informed us — Ibrahim ibn al-Harith narrated to us — Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us — (h) — and Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Fawak informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us — Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — both saying: Shu'bah narrated to us — Ya'la ibn Ata' informed me — I heard 'Umarah ibn Hadid narrating from Sakhr al-Ghamidi, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""O Allah, bless my Ummah in their early mornings."" He said: ""And the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, when he sent a military detachment, he would send it at the beginning of the day. And Sakhr was a merchant who would send his servants at the beginning of the day, so his wealth increased until he did not know where to place it.""

The wording is from the hadith of Abu Dawud. [Da'if]"
18463,"(18457) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Muhammad ibn Salih ibn Hani' narrated to me — Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Mihran narrated to us — 'Amr ibn 'Uthman al-Himsi narrated to us — al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us — from 'Abd Allah ibn al-A'la ibn Zabr — that he heard Muslim ibn Mishkam, Abu 'Ubayd Allah — or he said Abu 'Abd Allah — saying: Abu Tha'labah al-Khushani, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated: ""When the people would arrive at a place where the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, was staying, they would disperse into the mountain passes and valleys. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: 'Your dispersing into these mountain passes and valleys — that is only from Satan.' After that, they would not stop at any place without some of them gathering together, to the point that it was said: 'If a cloth were spread over them, it would cover them all.'""

[Sahih]"
18464,"(18458) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Dasah informed us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us — Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us — from Asid ibn 'Abd Rahman al-Khath'ami — from Farwah ibn Mujahid al-Lakhmi — from Sahl ibn Mu'adh al-Juhani — from his father, who said: ""I went on a campaign with the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, in such-and-such expedition. The people narrowed the encampments and blocked the road. So the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sent a caller to announce among the people: 'Whoever narrows an encampment or blocks a road — there is no jihad for him.'""

[Da'if]"
18465,"(18459) Abu 'Abd Allah Ishaq ibn Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Susi informed it to us — Abu al-'Abbas al-Asam narrated to us — Muhammad ibn 'Awf narrated to us — Abu al-Mughirah narrated to us — al-Awza'i narrated to us — Asid ibn 'Abd Rahman narrated to us — from a man from Juhaynah — from his father — from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him — with its like (meaning a narration similar to the preceding one)."
18466,"(18460) And Baqiyyah narrated it from al-Awza'i — from Asid — from Ibn Mujahid — from Sahl ibn Mu'adh — from his father, who said: ""We went on a campaign with the Prophet of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him"" — with its meaning. Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed it to us — Abu Bakr ibn Dasah informed us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — 'Amr ibn 'Uthman narrated to us — Baqiyyah narrated from al-Awza'i — and he mentioned it."
18467,"(18461) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-Walid narrated to me — Ibrahim ibn Jabalah narrated to us — al-Hasan ibn 'Ali al-Hulwani narrated to us — Abu 'Amir narrated to us — al-Mughirah ibn 'Abd Rahman narrated to us — from Abu al-Zinad — from al-A'raj — from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Do not wish for the encounter with the enemy. And when you do meet them, be patient.""

Al-Bukhari extracted it in his Sahih, and he said: ""And Abu 'Amir narrated it."" And Muslim narrated it from al-Hulwani. [Sahih]"
18468,"(18462) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi both informed us — saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us — Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr narrated to us — from Abu Ishaq — from Musa ibn 'Uqbah — from Salim Abu al-Nadr, the freedman of 'Umar ibn 'Ubayd Allah, who was his scribe. He said: ""When 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Awfah, may Allah be pleased with him, set out toward the Haruriyyah, I read his letter, and in it was: 'The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, on some of the days in which he met the enemy, would wait until the sun had declined. Then he would stand among the people and say: ""O people, do not wish for the encounter with the enemy, and ask Allah for well-being. And when you meet them, be patient, and know that Paradise is beneath the shadows of the swords."" Then he would say: ""O Allah, the Sender of the Book, the Driver of the clouds, the Defeater of the parties — defeat them and grant us victory over them.""'""

He said: ""And Abu al-Nadr said — and it reached us — that the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, supplicated with something similar, saying: 'You are our Lord and their Lord, and we are Your servants and they are Your servants, and the forelocks of our heads and the forelocks of their heads are in Your Hand — so defeat them and grant us victory over them.'""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad from Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr. And Muslim extracted it from the hadith of Ibn Jurayj from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, without the reaching of Abu al-Nadr. [Sahih. Muttafaq 'alayh]"
18469,"(18463) Abu Tahir al-Husayn ibn 'Ali ibn Salamah al-Hamdani informed us therein — Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Ibrahim ibn Masi al-Matwathi informed us — Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah narrated to us — 'Amr ibn Marzuq informed us — 'Imran narrated to us — from Qatadah — from Abu Burdah — from Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, when he feared a people, would say: ""O Allah, I place You before their chests, and I seek refuge in You from their evils.""

[Sahih]"
18470,"(18464) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us — Ibn Abi Qumash, meaning Muhammad ibn 'Isa, informed us — Sa'id ibn Sulayman narrated to us — from Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah — (h) — and Muhammad narrated to us — Sulayman ibn Harb and Ibn A'ishah narrated to us — from Hammad ibn Salmah — both of them from Thabit al-Bunani — from 'Abd Rahman ibn Abi Layla — from Suhayb, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, used to move his lips with something we could not understand. We said: 'O Messenger of Allah, you are moving your lips with something we do not understand.' He said: 'A prophet from among the prophets was amazed by the great number of his people. He said: ""Who will suffice for these people, or who will stand for these people?"" It was said to him: ""Choose for your companions between an enemy being set upon them who will annihilate their stronghold, or hunger, or death."" So he chose for them, and they chose death. Seventy thousand of them died within three days.'""

He said: ""And the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, used to say: 'I say: O Allah, by You I fight, by You I scheme, and by You I battle. There is no power and no strength except through You.'""

And the remainder of the supplications that have come in this regard have already been mentioned in the Book of Hajj and in the Book of Supplications. [Sahih]"
18471,"(18465) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; Ḥammād narrated to us; Abū ʿImrān al-Jawnī narrated from ʿAlqamah ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Muzanī, from Maʿqil ibn Yasār, that al-Nuʿmān, meaning Ibn Muqarrin (may Allah be pleased with him), said: I was present with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). When he did not fight in the first part of the day, he would delay fighting until the sun passed its zenith, the winds blew, and victory descended. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Sijistānī, 2655]"
18472,"(18466) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us; Abū Usāmah narrated to us; Hishām ibn Abī ʿAbd Allāh al-Dustawāʾī narrated from Qatādah, from al-Ḥasan, from Qays ibn ʿUbād, who said: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) used to dislike raising the voice at three occasions: during fighting, during funerals, and during remembrance (of Allah). [Ḍaʿīf]"
18473,"(18467) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Hishām narrated to us; Qatādah narrated from al-Ḥasan, from Qays ibn ʿUbād, who said: The Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) used to dislike raising the voice during fighting. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18474,"(18468) He said, and Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated from Hammām, who said: Maṭar narrated to me from Qatādah, from Abū Burdah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), the same as that."
18475,"(18469) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr al-Qāḍī both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ziyād ibn Anʿum informed me; Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ḥublī narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Do not wish for encounter with the enemy; rather, ask Allah for safety. But if you meet them, stand firm and remember Allah abundantly. If they raise a clamour and shout, then upon you is silence."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18476,"(18470) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn Sahl al-Faqīh in Bukhārī informed us; Ṣāliḥ ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm, Abū Maʿmar al-Hudhalī, narrated to us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Ayyūb, from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) reached Khaybar at dawn, and its people had come out with their spades. When they saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), they rushed toward the fortress, crying out: ""Muḥammad and the army!"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) raised his hands and then said: ""Allāhu Akbar"" three times. ""Khaybar is destroyed! When we descend upon the courtyard of a people, evil is the morning of those who have been warned."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad and others, from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
18477,"(18471) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Abū ʿĀmir al-ʿAqadī narrated to us; ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār al-Yamāmī narrated from Iyās ibn Salamah, from his father, who said: We went on a campaign with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). He mentioned the hadith at length, and in it is that when they raided the livestock of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), he said: Then I stood upon a mountain pass, faced Madīnah, and called out three times: ""Yā ṣabāḥāh!"" Then I went out pursuing the people, shooting them with arrows and reciting rajaz verses:

""I am the son of al-Akwʿ, and today is the day of destruction.""

And in it he said: We went to Khaybar, and my uncle ʿĀmir was saying:

""By Allah, were it not for Allah, we would not have been guided, nor would we have given charity, nor would we have prayed. And we are in need of Your bounty. So make our feet firm if we meet the enemy, and send down tranquillity upon us.""

The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Who is this?"" They said: ""ʿĀmir."" He said: ""May your Lord forgive you."" And in it: When we arrived at Khaybar, Marḥāb came out brandishing his sword, saying:

""Khaybar knows that I am Marḥāb, a warrior armed with weapons, a proven hero, when wars flare up.""

My uncle came forward to face him and said:

""Khaybar knows that I am ʿĀmir, a warrior armed with weapons, a daring hero.""

Then he mentioned the hadith about ʿĀmir's sword turning back upon himself, and ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) going out, his rajaz verses, and his killing of Marḥāb. It has been narrated previously. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
18478,"(18472) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us; Sufyān narrated. And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Kathīr and Abū Ḥudhayfah both said: Sufyān narrated to us from Abū Isḥāq. He said: I heard al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib (may Allah be pleased with him) say: A man came to him and said: ""O Abū ʿUmarah, did you flee on the day of Ḥunayn?"" He said: ""As for me, I bear witness that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) did not flee. But the swiftest among the people rushed ahead, and Hawāzin pelted them with arrows. And Abū Sufyān ibn al-Ḥārith was holding the head of his white mule, and he (the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was saying:

'I am the Prophet, no lie; I am the son of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Kathīr, and both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān, from Sufyān."
18479,"(18473) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq regarding the story of Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) and his fighting in the expedition of Muʾtah. He said, and he (Jaʿfar) was reciting:

""How excellent is Paradise and its nearness! Its drink is pure and cool. And the Romans are a people whose punishment has drawn near. Upon me, if I meet them, is their combat.""

And from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr ibn Ḥazm narrated to me that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rāwāḥah said when he took the banner that day:

""I have sworn, O soul, that you shall descend into battle—willingly or by compulsion. Even if the people gather and raise a clamour, why do you see me disliking Paradise? You have long lived in comfort. Are you anything but a drop of fluid in a skin?""

Ibn Isḥāq said, and he also said:

""O soul, if you are not killed, you will die. This is the pool of death, which you have reached. And what you wished for has been given to you. If you do as they did, you will be guided. But if you hold back, you will be wretched.""

He meant Jaʿfar and Zayd (may Allah be pleased with them both). Then he drew his sword, advanced, and fought until he was killed. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18480,"(18474) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn Marzūq narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from Abū Isḥāq. He said: I heard Hunaydah, a man from Khuzāʿah, say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Who will take this sword and fulfil its right?"" A man said: ""I will."" So he took it. When he met the enemy, he began to say:

""I am a man whose intimate companion pledged me, while we were at the foot of the palm trees, that I would never stand in the rear ranks. I strike with the sword of Allah and the Messenger.""

Others added in it that he fought until he was killed (may Allah be pleased with him). [Ḍaʿīf]"
18481,"(18475) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā informed me; Abū Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥīm narrated to us; Abū Aḥmad al-Zubayrī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ghasīl narrated from Ḥamzah ibn Abī Usayd and al-Mundhir ibn Abī Usayd.

(Ḥ) Ibrāhīm said, and Hārūn ibn ʿAbdillāh narrated to us; al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn narrated to us; Ibn al-Ghasīl narrated from Ḥamzah ibn Abī Usayd from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said on the day of Badr, when we drew up ranks against Quraysh and they drew up ranks against us: ""When they draw close to you, pelt them with arrows."" This is the wording of al-Faḍl's ḥadīth. And Abū Aḥmad said in his ḥadīth: ""When they draw near to you"" — meaning, when they outnumber you — ""pelt them with arrows and conserve your arrows."" Abū Bakr said: The correct wording is ""When they draw close to you.""

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Nuʿaym al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn, and from Abū Yaḥyā Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥīm from Abū Aḥmad. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2900, 3984]"
18482,"(18476) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Najīḥ — not al-Malaṭī — narrated from Mālik ibn Ḥamzah ibn Abī Usayd al-Sāʿidī from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said on the day of Badr: ""When they draw close to you, pelt them with arrows, and do not unsheathe your swords until they overwhelm you."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18483,"(18477) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us; Abū Khaythamah narrated from Abū Isḥāq, who said: A man said to al-Barāʾ (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu): ""O Abū ʿUmārah, did you flee on the day of Ḥunayn?"" He said: ""No, by Allah, the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) did not turn back. But the young men among his Companions and their light-armed ones — upon whom there was little or no weaponry — went forth and encountered a people of archers whose arrows rarely missed, the gathered forces of Hawāzin and Banī Naṣr. They showered them with arrows that scarcely erred, and so they turned back toward the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) was upon his white mule, and Abū Sufyān ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib was leading it. He dismounted, sought victory, and said:

'I am the Prophet, no lie —
I am the son of ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib.'

Then he arranged them in ranks.""

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr ibn Khālid from Zuhayr, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18484,"(18478) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Abān narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm from Ibn Jābir ibn ʿAtīk from Jābir ibn ʿAtīk (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""Among forms of jealousy (ghayrah) are those that Allah loves and those that He hates. As for the jealousy that Allah loves, it is jealousy regarding a matter of suspicion (rībah). And as for the jealousy that Allah hates, it is jealousy where there is no suspicion. And among forms of swagger (khuyalāʾ) that Allah loves is a man's swaggering during battle and his swaggering when giving charity. And among forms of swagger that Allah hates is a man's swaggering in pride and vainglory."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18485,"(18479) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Burshān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿUbaydillāh ibn Yazīd narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Yūsuf al-Azraq narrated to us; Zakariyyā —

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; ʿAmr ibn Tamīm ibn Sayyār al-Ṭabarī narrated to us; Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us; Zakariyyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah narrated from ʿĀmir from ʿUrwah al-Bāriqī that the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""Goodness is tied to the forelocks of horses until the Day of Resurrection — reward and booty."" This is the wording of Abū Nuʿaym's ḥadīth. The narration of al-Azraq does not contain ""reward and booty.""

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Nuʿaym, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Zakariyyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
18486,"(18480) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us —

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — both said: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn Burqān narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī Nushbah from Anas ibn Mālik (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""Three things are of the foundation of faith: refraining from harming the one who says 'There is no god but Allah' — not declaring him a disbeliever on account of a sin, nor expelling him from Islam on account of an act; jihād, which has continued since Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, sent me, until the last of my Ummah fights the Dajjāl — it is not invalidated by the oppression of an oppressor or the justice of a just ruler; and faith in divine decree (al-qadar).""

And the ḥadīth of Makhūl from Abū Hurayrah from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam): ""Jihād is obligatory upon you with every ruler, whether he is righteous or wicked."" This has been mentioned in the chapter of Imāmah and the book of Funerals. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18487,"(18481) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us —

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Maḥbūr al-Dahhān narrated to us; Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz narrated to us — both said: Abū al-Azhar Aḥmad ibn al-Azhar narrated to us; Wahb ibn Jarīr narrated to us; my father narrated to me, he said: I heard Yūnus ibn Yazīd from al-Zuhrī from ʿUbaydillāh ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿUtbah from Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""The best of companions are four; the best of detachments (sarāyā) are four hundred; and the best of armies are four thousand. And twelve thousand will not be overcome due to small numbers.""

Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim is the only one who narrated it in connected form. And ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated it from Yūnus from ʿUqayl from al-Zuhrī from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) in disconnected form. Abū Dāwūd said: Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim chains it, and it is an error. [Munkar]"
18488,"(18482) Abū Abī Naṣr ibn Qatādah and Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fārisī both said: Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us; a man from the people of Shām narrated from Ḥuyayy ibn Mikhmir al-Waṣṣābī, who said: I heard Abā ʿAbdillāh, a man from the people of Damascus, narrate from Aktham ibn al-Jawn al-Khuzāʿī, then al-Kaʿbī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""O Aktham ibn al-Jawn, go forth to battle with other than your people — your character will improve and you will honor your companions. O Aktham ibn al-Jawn, the best of companions are four; the best of vanguards are forty; the best of detachments are four hundred; and the best of armies are four thousand. And twelve thousand will not be overcome due to small numbers. O Aktham ibn al-Jawn, do not accompany two hundred."" [Very Ḍaʿīf]"
18489,"(18483) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to us; Ibn Shihāb narrated from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab from Abū Hurayrah (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) was asked: ""Which deed is best?"" He said: ""Faith in Allah and His Messenger."" It was said: ""Then what?"" He said: ""Then jihād in the cause of Allah."" It was said: ""Then what?"" He said: ""Then an accepted Ḥajj (ḥajj mabrūr).""

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus and others, and Muslim narrated it from Manṣūr ibn Abī Mazāḥim from Ibrāhīm. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
18490,"(18484) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād narrated to us; ʿUmārah ibn al-Qaʿqaʿ narrated from Abū Zurʿah ibn ʿAmr ibn Jarīr from Abū Hurayrah (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""Allah has pledged for the one who goes forth as a mujāhid in His cause — nothing drives him forth except faith in Me and belief in My Messenger — that I guarantee him entry into Paradise, or his return to the house from which he departed, attaining whatever reward and booty he has gained.""

And the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""There is no one wounded in the cause of Allah but that he will come on the Day of Resurrection with his wound flowing blood — its color the color of blood, and its fragrance the fragrance of musk.""

And the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""By the One in whose Hand is my soul, were it not for the hardship it would cause my Ummah, I would not remain behind any detachment that goes forth to fight in the cause of Allah. But I do not find mounts for them, nor do they find the means to follow me, and their souls would not be content to remain behind me.""

And the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""By the One in whose Hand is my soul, I wish I could fight in the cause of Allah and be killed, then fight and be killed, then fight and be killed.""

The ḥadīth of the wound (al-kalm) was narrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and he narrated the rest from Ḥarmiyy ibn Ḥafṣ from ʿAbd al-Wāḥid. Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd from ʿUmārah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
18491,"(18485) Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ghālib al-Khwārizmī al-Ḥāfiẓ in Baghdād — informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Naysābūrī — narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Naḍr — informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā — informed us: al-Mughīrah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān — from Abū al-Zinād — from al-Aʿraj — from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) — from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), who said:

Allah has taken it upon Himself to guarantee the one who strives in His cause — who leaves his home for nothing other than jihād in the path of Allah and belief in His Word — that He will admit him into Paradise, or return him to his dwelling from which he set out, together with whatever reward or spoils he may have obtained. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
18492,"(18486) And from the Prophet (ṣallallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), who said:

By the One in whose hand is the soul of Muḥammad, were it not that I would hardship the believers — if I were to stay behind from a sariyyah raiding party that goes forth in the cause of Allah — but I cannot find the means to mount them, nor do they find the means to follow me, and their souls would not be content to remain behind after me. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
18493,"(18487) And from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), who said:

By the One in whose hand is the soul of Muḥammad, I would love to be fought in the cause of Allah and be killed, then be brought back to life and be killed, then be brought back to life and be killed, then be brought back to life and be killed, then be brought back to life.

Abū Hurayrah used to say, three times: ""I bear witness to Allah."" The first ḥadīth was narrated by Muslim from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated the remainder through other chains. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
18494,"(18488) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān — informed us: Abū Jaʿfar — Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz — narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Shākir — narrated to us: ʿAffān —

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī — narrated to us: ʿAffān — narrated to us: Hammām — narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Juḥādah — that Abū Ḥaṣīn narrated to him — that Dhakwān narrated to him — that Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to him, saying:

A man came to the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, teach me a deed equal to jihād."" He said: ""I cannot find it."" Then he said: ""Are you able, when the mujāhid sets out, to enter the mosque and stand in prayer without flagging and to fast without breaking your fast?"" He said: ""I cannot do that."" Abū Hurayrah said: ""The horse of the mujāhid prances along its tether, and for him good deeds are recorded.""

The wording is from the ḥadīth of Jaʿfar.

Narrated by al-Bukhārī in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq from ʿAffān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
18495,"(18489) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh — informed us: Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad ibn Sufyān al-Ṭūsī — narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥīm ibn Munīb — narrated to us: Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd —

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq — narrated to us by dictation: Aḥmad ibn Salamah — narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm — informed us: Jarīr — from Suhayl — from his father — from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said:

They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, tell us what is equal to jihād in the cause of Allah."" He said: ""You are not able to do it."" We said: ""Yes, indeed."" He said: ""You are not able to do it."" He said: ""I do not know whether it was the third or the fourth time: The likeness of the mujāhid in the cause of Allah is like that of one who fasts and stands in prayer, reciting the verses of Allah, never ceasing from prayer or fasting until the mujāhid returns to his family.""

Narrated by Muslim in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb from Jarīr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
18496,"(18490) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī and Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — both said: Abū ʿAbd Allāh — al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Ayyūb — narrated to us: Abū Ḥātim al-Rāzī —

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh — informed me: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs — narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī — both said: Abū Tawbah — narrated to us: Muʿāwiyah, i.e. ibn Salām — from Zayd, i.e. ibn Salām — that he heard Abū Salām say: al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me, saying:

I was at the minbar of the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) when a man said: ""I do not care if I do no deed after Islam except populating the Masjid al-Ḥarām."" And another said: ""Jihād in the cause of Allah is better than what you have said."" ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) rebuked them and said: ""Do not raise your voices near the minbar of the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) on the day of Jumuʿah. Rather, when I have prayed Jumuʿah, I will enter and ask him about that in which you differ."" So Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: *""Do you consider the providing of water to the pilgrims and the maintenance of al-Masjid al-Ḥarām equal to the worth of one who believes in Allah and the Last Day and strives in the cause of Allah? They are not equal in the sight of Allah.""* [al-Tawbah: 19]

Narrated by Muslim in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥulwānī from Abū Tawbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1789.]"
18497,"(18491) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed me: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh — narrated to us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh — Muḥammad ibn Naṣr — narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā — informed us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Ḥāzim — from his father — from Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Sāʿidī (may Allah be pleased with him) — from the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), that he said:

A space in Paradise the size of a whip is better than the world and all that is in it. A morning expedition or an evening expedition that the servant undertakes in the cause of Allah is better than the world and all that is in it.

Narrated by Muslim in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and narrated by al-Bukhārī from al-Qaʿnabī from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz. In this chapter there are also narrations from Abū Ayyūb al-Anṣārī, Abū Hurayrah, and Anas ibn Mālik from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
18498,"(18492) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Umawī — informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam — narrated to us: Ibn Wahb — informed me: ʿUmar ibn Mālik al-Sharʿabī — from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Jaʿfar — from Ṣafwān ibn Sulaym — from Salmān al-Agharr — from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) ordered a sariyyah to set out. They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, shall we go out tonight or wait until morning?"" He said: ""Do you not love to spend the night among the gardens of Paradise?""

(Al-kharīf means al-ḥadīqah — a garden.) [Ḥasan.]"
18499,"(18493) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam — informed us: Ibn Wahb — informed me: Abū Hānīʾ al-Khawlānī — from Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ḥubulī — from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said:

""O Abū Saʿīd, whoever is pleased with Allah as Lord, with Islam as religion, and with Muḥammad as Prophet, Paradise becomes obligatory for him."" Abū Saʿīd was astonished by this and said: ""Repeat it for me, O Messenger of Allah."" So he repeated it. Then the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""And another [deed] by which the servant is raised a hundred degrees in Paradise, the distance between every two degrees being like that between the heaven and the earth."" He said: ""What is it, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Jihād in the cause of Allah, jihād in the cause of Allah, jihād in the cause of Allah.""

Narrated by Muslim in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1884.]"
18500,"(18494) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh — informed us: Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān — narrated to us: Abū al-Azhar — narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Muḥammad — narrated to us: Fulayḥ — from Hilāl ibn ʿAlī — from ʬAṭāʾ ibn Yasār or Ibn Abī ʿAmrah — from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said:

Whoever believes in Allah and His Messenger, establishes the prayer, gives the zakāh, and fasts Ramaḍān, it becomes a right upon Allah to admit him into Paradise — whether he emigrates in the cause of Allah or dies in the land in which he was born. They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, shall we not inform the people of this?"" He said: ""In Paradise there are a hundred degrees, the distance between every two degrees being like that between the heaven and the earth, which Allah has prepared for those who strive in His cause. When you ask Allah, ask Him for al-Firdaws, for it is the middle of Paradise and the highest part of Paradise, and from it the rivers of Paradise spring forth, and above it is the Throne of al-Raḥmān, the Blessed and Most High."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī.]"
18501,"(18495) He said: And Abu al-Azhar narrated to us; Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: So Fulayh narrated this hadith to us a second time, and he mentioned it from Hilal ibn 'Ali from 'Atā' ibn Yasār from Abū Hurayrah, similarly, without doubt.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Ṣāliḥ from Fulayh, without doubt."
18502,"(18496) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us; Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; 'Abd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham narrated to us; Abū al-Yamān informed us; Shu'ayb informed us from al-Zuhrī; 'Atā' ibn Yazīd informed me that Abū Sa'īd al-Khudrī — may Allah be pleased with him — informed him that it was said: ""O Messenger of Allah, which people are the most excellent?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""A believer who strives in the cause of Allah with his life and his wealth."" He was asked: ""Then who?"" He said: ""A believer in a mountain valley, who fears Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and leaves people from his harm.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān, and both he and Muslim reported it through other chains from al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
18503,"(18497) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan al-'Anazī narrated to us; 'Uthmān ibn Sa'īd al-Dārimī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah and Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn 'Abd Allāh al-'Aṭṭār al-Ḥīrī and Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fārisī informed us; they said: Abū 'Amr ibn Maṭar narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn 'Alī al-Dhuhlī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us; 'Abd al-'Azīz ibn Abī Ḥāzim informed us from his father, from Ba'jah, from Abū Hurayrah — may Allah be pleased with him — from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), that he said: ""Among the best livelihoods of people is a man holding the reins of his horse in the cause of Allah, flying upon its back; whenever he hears a call to arms or an alarm, he flies upon it, seeking death and killing wherever they may be expected. Or a man among a small flock of sheep at the summit of a mountain peak or in the bottom of a valley, who establishes the prayer, gives the zakāh, and worships his Lord until certainty (death) comes to him, and he is among people only in goodness.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. And he reported it from Qutaybah from 'Abd al-'Azīz ibn Abī Ḥāzim and Ya'qūb ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān, both of them from Abī Ḥāzim, with this same chain, similarly. And he said: from Ba'jah ibn 'Abd Allāh ibn Badr, and he said: ""in a mountain pass among these mountain passes."""
18504,"(18498) Abū 'Abd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us; Aḥmad ibn Ja'far ibn Ḥamdān al-Qaṭī'ī narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn 'Abd Allāh — and he is Abū Muslim — narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan 'Alī ibn Aḥmad ibn 'Abdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Abū Muslim narrated to us; 'Amr ibn Marzūq narrated to us; 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn 'Abd Allāh ibn Dīnār narrated to us from his father, from Abī Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah — may Allah be pleased with him — from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), who said: ""Wretched is the slave of the dīnār, the slave of the dirham, and the slave of the khameesah (garment). If he is given, he is pleased; and if he is withheld, he is angry. Wretched and overturned! And if he is pricked by a thorn, may he not extract it. Blessed is the one who holds the reins of his horse in the cause of Allah, his head disheveled and his feet dusty. If he is placed in the rear guard, he remains in the rear guard; and if he is placed in the front guard, he remains in the front guard. If he seeks permission, he is not granted permission; and if he intercedes, his intercession is not accepted. Blessed is he, then blessed is he!""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from 'Amr ibn Marzūq. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 4635, 2887.]"
18505,"(18499) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū 'Abd Allāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī informed us; they said: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us; al-'Abbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Mazīd informed me; my father informed me; al-Awzā'ī informed me; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr informed me; Abū Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān informed me; 'Abd Allāh ibn Salām informed me: that some of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""If only we were to send a messenger to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) to ask him about the most beloved deeds to Allah."" He said: ""So none of us went to him, and we held back from asking him about that."" He said: ""Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) called those men, one by one, until he gathered them, and this Sūrah was revealed concerning them: {Whatever is in the heavens and the earth glorifies Allah} [al-Ḥadīd: 1]."" 'Abd Allāh ibn Salām said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) recited it to us in its entirety."" Abū Salamah said: ""'Abd Allāh ibn Salām recited it to us in its entirety."" Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr said: ""And Abū Salamah recited it to us in its entirety."" Al-Awzā'ī said: ""And Yaḥyā recited it to us in its entirety."" Al-'Abbās said: ""My father said: 'And al-Awzā'ī recited it to us in its entirety.'""

[Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
18506,"(18500) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū 'Abd Allāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī informed us; they said: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; Mu'āwiyah ibn 'Amr narrated to us; Abū Isḥāq narrated to us from al-Awzā'ī from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr; Abū Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān informed me from 'Abd Allāh ibn Salām, who said: ""We gathered and discussed among ourselves, and we said: 'Which one of you will go to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and ask him which deeds are most beloved to Allah?' Then we dispersed, and we held back that any one of us should go to him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent for us and gathered us, and he gestured some of us toward others. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) recited to us: {Whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on the earth glorifies Allah, and He is the Exalted in Might, the Wise} [al-Ḥashr: 1] to the end of the Sūrah."" Yaḥyā said: ""Abū Salamah recited it to us from its beginning to its end."" Abū Salamah said: ""'Abd Allāh ibn Salām recited it to us from its beginning to its end."" Al-Awzā'ī said: ""Yaḥyā recited it to us from its beginning to its end."" Abū Isḥāq said: ""Al-Awzā'ī recited it to us from its beginning to its end."" Mu'āwiyah said: ""Abū Isḥāq recited it to us from its beginning to its end."" Abū Bakr al-Ṣaghānī said: ""Mu'āwiyah recited it to us from its beginning to its end."" Abū al-'Abbās said: ""Al-Ṣaghānī did not recite the Sūrah to us in its entirety."" Abū al-'Abbās recited something from its beginning, and al-Qāḍī recited something from its beginning, and Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ recited the Sūrah to us from its beginning to its end, and the Shaykh recited it from its beginning to its end.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded before it.]"
18507,"(18501) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak — may Allah have mercy on him — informed us; 'Abd Allāh ibn Ja'far narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; al-Aswad ibn Shaybān narrated to us from Yazīd ibn 'Abd Allāh ibn al-Shikhkhīr from Muṭarrif ibn 'Abd Allāh ibn al-Shikhkhīr, who said: ""A hadith used to reach me from Abū Dharr — may Allah be pleased with him — and I longed to meet him. So I met him and said: 'O Abū Dharr! A hadith used to reach me from you, and I longed to meet you.' He said: 'May your father be ruined! You have indeed met me, so speak.' I said: 'A hadith reached me in which you narrate that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) told you: ""Indeed, Allah, the Most High, loves three and dislikes three.""' He said: 'I do not think I would lie upon my intimate friend (peace and blessings be upon him and his family).' I said: 'So who are the three whom Allah loves?' He said: 'A man who meets the enemy and fights — and you will find that in your Book with you: {Indeed, Allah loves those who fight in His cause in a row} [al-Ṣaff: 4].' I said: 'And who?' He said: 'A man who has a bad neighbor who harms him, yet he bears his harm with patience, and Allah suffices him against him in life or death.' He said: 'And who?' He said: 'A man who was with a people on a journey; they stopped and made camp, and sleep and drowsiness overcame them, and they laid down their heads and slept. But he rose, performed wuḍū', and prayed out of fear of Allah and desire for Him.' I said: 'So who are the three whom He dislikes?' He said: 'The miser who reminds of his favors, and the arrogant boaster — and you will find that in the Book of Allah: {Indeed, Allah does not love every arrogant boaster} [Luqmān: 18].' He said: 'And who is the third?' He said: 'The swearing merchant, or the swearing seller.'""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Ṭayālisī 468.]"
18508,"(18502) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-'Abbās — he is al-Aṣamm — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn 'Abd Allāh ibn 'Abd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; al-Layth ibn Sa'd informed me from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb from Abī al-Khayr from Abī al-Khaṭṭāb from Abī Sa'īd al-Khudrī — may Allah be pleased with him — that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said in the year of Tabūk, when he addressed the people, leaning his back against a palm tree: ""Shall I not inform you of the best of people and the worst of people? Among the best of people is a man who strives in the cause of Allah on the back of his horse, or on the back of his camel, or on his feet, until death comes to him. And among the worst of people is a wicked, audacious man who recites the Book of Allah but does not heed any of it.""

[Ḍa'īf.]"
18509,"(18503) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Abū al-Azhar narrated to us; Abū 'Āmir al-'Aqadī narrated to us from Hishām ibn Sa'd.

(Ḥ) And Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-'Abbās al-Umawī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn 'Abd Allāh ibn 'Abd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Hishām ibn Sa'd informed us from Sa'īd ibn Abī Hilāl from Ibn Abī Dhubāb from Abū Hurayrah — may Allah be pleased with him — that a man from among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) passed by a mountain pass in which there was a spring of sweet water, and he was delighted by its pleasantness and beauty. He said: ""If only I were to withdraw from the people and settle in this mountain pass."" Then he said: ""I shall not do so until I seek the permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family)."" So he mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), and he said: ""Do not do so, for the standing of one of you in the cause of Allah is better than his prayer in his family for sixty years. Do you not love that Allah forgive you and admit you to Paradise? Strive in the cause of Allah. Whoever fights in the cause of Allah for the time it takes to milk a camel, Paradise becomes obligatory for him.""

[Ḥasan.]"
18510,"(18504) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Abū al-Azhar narrated to us; 'Abd Allāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrated to us from Hishām ibn Ḥassān from al-Ḥasan from 'Imrān ibn Ḥuṣayn — may Allah be pleased with him — that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""The standing of a man in the rank in the cause of Allah is better than the worship of a man for sixty years.""

[Ḥasan for other than it.]"
18511,"(18505) Abu Bakr ibn Furak informed us that 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us that Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us that Abu Dawud narrated to us that Ibn al-Mubarak narrated from Abu Ma'n from Abu Salih, the freedman of 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, who said: 'Uthman ibn 'Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) said in the Mosque of al-Khayf: ""O people, I heard a hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) which I had concealed from you, out of concern for you. Now it has occurred to me to disclose it as an act of sincere counsel to you. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: 'A day spent striving in the cause of Allah is equal to a thousand days spent in anything else.' So let each one of you consider his own affair."" [Da'if]"
18512,"(18506) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Faqih informed us that 'Ubayd ibn Sharik narrated to us that Abu al-Jumahir Muhammad ibn 'Uthman al-Tanukhi narrated to us that al-Haytham ibn Humayd narrated to us that al-'Ala' ibn al-Harith narrated to us that al-Qasim Abu 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us from Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him) that a man said: ""O Messenger of Allah, grant me permission for spiritual wandering (siyahah)."" He said: ""The spiritual wandering of my Ummah is jihad in the cause of Allah."" [Hasan]"
18513,"(18507) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us that Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us that 'Ubayd ibn Sharik narrated to us that Abu al-Jumahir narrated to us that al-Haytham, meaning Ibn Humayd, narrated to us that al-'Ala' ibn al-Harith narrated to us that al-Qasim narrated from Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him) that a man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and said: ""Grant me permission to commit adultery (zina)."" Those who were near the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) moved to seize him, but the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Leave him."" Then the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said to him: ""Come near. Would you like that to be done to your sister?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Or to your daughter?"" He said: ""No."" He continued asking about various close relations, and each time the man said: ""No."" Then the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Then detest what Allah detests, and love for your brother what you love for yourself."" The man said: ""O Messenger of Allah, then supplicate to Allah that He make me averse to women."" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""O Allah, make women averse to him."" The narrator said: ""The man departed, but returned after several nights and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, there is nothing more averse to me than women. Grant me permission for spiritual wandering (siyahah).'"" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""The spiritual wandering of my Ummah is jihad in the cause of Allah."" [Hasan]"
18514,"(18508) Imam Abu al-Tayyib Sahl ibn Muhammad ibn Sulayman (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated to us that Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub ibn Yusuf informed us that Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us that my father and Shu'ayb ibn al-Layth both narrated to us that al-Layth ibn Sa'd narrated to us from Ibn al-Had from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih from Safwan ibn Abi Yazid from al-Qa'qa' ibn Abi al-Lajlaj from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: ""Dust in the cause of Allah and the smoke of Hellfire shall never coexist in the belly of a servant, ever; and miserliness and faith shall never coexist in the heart of a servant, ever."" [Sahih]"
18515,"(18509) Abu al-Husayn 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn Bishran al-'Adl and Abu 'Abdullah al-Husayn ibn al-Hasan al-Ghadairi informed us in Baghdad, both saying: Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Amr narrated to us that Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Munadi narrated to us that Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us that Shayban narrated from Qatadah who narrated to us that Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd narrated to us that Ma'dan ibn Abi Talhah narrated to us from Abu Najih al-Sulami (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""I was present with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) at the fortress of Ta'if, and I heard the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: 'Whoever shoots an arrow and it reaches its target, he shall have a degree in Paradise.' A man said: 'O Prophet of Allah, if I shoot and my arrow reaches its target, shall I have a degree in Paradise?' He said: 'Yes.' So he shot and his arrow reached its target."" He said: ""I shot sixteen arrows that day."" He said: ""And I heard the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: 'Whoever is granted a grey hair in the cause of Allah, it shall be a light for him on the Day of Resurrection. And whoever shoots an arrow in the cause of Allah, it shall be a light for him on the Day of Resurrection. And any Muslim man who frees a Muslim slave, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall make every bone of his bones a protection for every bone of his body from the Fire. And any Muslim woman who frees a Muslim woman, Allah shall make every bone of her bones a protection for every bone of her freed woman from the Fire.'""

It was also narrated by Asad ibn Wada'ah from Abu Najih 'Amr ibn 'Abasah. [Sahih]"
18516,"(18510) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us that Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us that Ibn Wahb informed me that several scholars among them 'Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to me that Sulayman ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us that al-Qasim, the freedman of 'Abd al-Rahman, narrated to us from 'Amr ibn 'Abasah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: 'Whoever shoots an arrow at the enemy and his arrow reaches its mark, whether it hits or misses, it is equal to freeing a slave.'"" [Sahih]"
18517,"(18511) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed me that 'Ali ibn 'Isa al-Hiri narrated to us that Musaddad ibn Qattan narrated to us that 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us that Jarir narrated from al-A'mash from 'Amr ibn Murrah from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd from Shurayhbil ibn al-Samt, who said: ""We said to Ka'b ibn Murrah al-Sulami (may Allah be pleased with him): 'Narrate to us, and be cautious.' He said: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: ""Whoever is granted a grey hair in Islam, it shall be a light for him on the Day of Resurrection. And whoever shoots an arrow in the cause of Allah, it is as if he has freed a slave.""'"" [Sahih]"
18518,"(18512) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran, Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari, Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Qattan, and Abu 'Abdullah al-Husayn ibn 'Umar ibn Burhan, and Abu Muhammad 'Abdullah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar all said: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us that al-Hasan ibn 'Arafah narrated to us that Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us from Hashim ibn Hashim al-Zuhri, who said: ""I heard Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib say: 'I heard Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) handed me his quiver.""' Al-Hasan ibn 'Arafah said, meaning he emptied his quiver on the day of Uhud, and said: 'Shoot, may my father and mother be your ransom.'""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad from Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah. [Sahih. Agreed upon.]"
18519,"(18513) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us that Abu al-Qasim Sulayman ibn Ahmad al-Tabrani narrated to us that Hafs ibn 'Umar narrated to us that Qabisah narrated to us.

(And in another chain) Sulayman informed us that Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us that Musaddad narrated to us that Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us. Both said: Sufyan narrated from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim from 'Abdullah ibn Shaddad ibn al-Had from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""I never heard the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) mention both his parents together for anyone except Sa'd, for he said: 'Shoot, may my father and mother be your ransom.'""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Qabisah and from Musaddad, from Yahya, from al-Thawri. Muslim also narrated it through other chains from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim. [Sahih. Agreed upon.]"
18520,"(18514) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu Muhammad al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Halim informed us that Abu al-Muwajjah informed us that 'Abdan informed us that 'Abdullah informed us that al-Awza'i narrated from Ishaq ibn Abi Talhah from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""Abu Talhah used to shield himself and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) with a single shield. Abu Talhah was an excellent archer, and when he shot, the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) would look to see where his arrow landed.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Ahmad ibn Muhammad from 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak. [Sahih. Agreed upon.]"
18521,"(18515) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu al-Hasan — Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdus — narrated to me; 'Uthman ibn Sa'id al-Darimi narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Uthman al-Tanukhi, Abu al-Jamahir, narrated to us; Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us.

(Chain variant) And Abu 'Amr al-Adib narrated to us; Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Ziyad al-Mawsili narrated to us; Ishaq ibn Zaid al-Khattabi narrated to us — and he resided in Harran — Muhammad ibn al-Mubarak al-Suri narrated to us; Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to us; Yazid ibn Abi Maryam narrated to me; 'Abayah ibn Rifa'ah ibn Rafi' narrated to me; Abu 'Abs, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""No two feet of a servant become dusty in the path of Allah but that the Fire shall never touch them, ever."" The wording of both narrations is the same.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Ishaq from Muhammad ibn al-Mubarak. [Sahih. Bukhari 2541]"
18522,"(18516) Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak — narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far ibn Ahmad narrated to us; Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us; 'Utbah ibn Hakim narrated to us; from Harmalah; from Abu al-Musabbih al-Himsi, who said: We were travelling in a military expedition, and over the people was Malik ibn 'Abd Allah al-Khath'ami. He came upon Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him, who was walking, leading his mule. He said to him: ""Will you not ride, when Allah has provided you for you?"" Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: 'Whosever two feet become dusty in the path of Allah, Allah has forbidden them for the Fire.' I am tending to my mount and I am independent of my people."" So the people dismounted from their mounts, and I did not see a day with more people on foot than that day. [Da'if]"
18523,"(18517) Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak — narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us; Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated to us.

(Chain variant) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us; Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated to us; from Qatadah; from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""No one enters Paradise and then wishes to leave it, even if he were given everything on the face of the earth, except the martyr. For he wishes to return and be killed ten times, on account of the honour he has witnessed.""

The wording is that of al-'Aqadi's narration. In the narration of al-Tayalisi it reads: ""There is no servant who has any good with Allah who would love to return to the world, except the martyr. For he would wish to return and be killed ten times, on account of the virtue of martyrdom he has witnessed.""

Al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Ghundar from Shu'bah. [Sahih. Agreed upon]"
18524,"(18518) Abu al-Hasan — 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' — narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us; Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Abu Musa narrated to us; Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us; al-A'mash narrated to us.

(Chain variant) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu al-Fadl ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; Jarir and 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us; from al-A'mash; from 'Abd Allah ibn Murrah; from Masruq; from 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, that he was asked about the spirits of the martyrs. He said: ""We asked about that, and he (the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: 'Their spirits are like green birds with lanterns suspended from the Throne. They roam wherever they wish, then return to their lanterns. While they are in that state, your Lord looks upon them and says: ""What do you desire?"" They say: ""What should we desire, when we are in Paradise, roaming wherever we wish?"" When they see that they must ask, they say: ""Return our spirits to our bodies so that we may fight in the path of Allah and be killed once again."" When He sees that they have no need of anything, He leaves them.'""

The wording is that of Abu 'Abd Allah's narration. In the narration of al-Muqri' it reads: ""We asked 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, about this verse: {And never think of those who have been killed in the path of Allah as dead. Rather, they are alive with their Lord, receiving provision, rejoicing} [Al 'Imran 169–170]."" He said: ""Indeed, we asked about that,"" then he mentioned its meaning.

Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya from Abu Mu'awiyah, and from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim. [Sahih. Agreed upon]"
18525,"(18519) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu 'Abd Allah — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub al-Hafiz — narrated to us; Yahya ibn Mansur al-Harawi narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us; Asbat and Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us; al-A'mash narrated to us; from 'Abd Allah ibn Murrah; from Masruq, who said: ""We asked 'Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him, about this verse,"" and he mentioned it, saying: ""Their spirits are within the bellies of green birds.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Numayr. [Sahih. Muslim 1887]"
18526,"(18520) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Idris narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us; Isma'il ibn Umayyah narrated to us; Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us; Sa'id ibn Jubayr narrated to us; from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""When your brothers were struck down at Uhud, Allah placed their spirits within the bellies of green birds. They come to the rivers of Paradise, eat from its fruits, and return to golden lanterns suspended beneath the shade of the Throne. When they found the goodness of their food, drink, and resting place, they said: 'Who will convey to our brothers on our behalf that we are alive in Paradise, receiving provision, so that they may not lose interest in jihad and not falter at the time of battle?' Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, said: 'I shall convey on your behalf to them.' And Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: {And never think of those who have been killed in the path of Allah as dead. Rather, they are alive with their Lord} [Al 'Imran 169] to the end of the verses."" [Hasan]"
18527,"(18521) Abu al-Hasan — 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' — narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us; Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Yazid ibn Zurai' narrated to us; 'Awf narrated to us; Hasna' bint Mu'awiyah narrated to us; my uncle narrated to us, who said: I said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family): ""Who is in Paradise?"" He said: ""The Prophet is in Paradise, the martyr, the newborn child, and the stillborn girl."" [Da'if]"
18528,"(18522) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Hakam narrated to us; Ibn Wahb narrated to us; 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Sa'd narrated to us; Sahl ibn Abi Umaymah ibn Sahl ibn Hunaif narrated to us; from his father; from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Indeed, the first drop of blood of the martyr to be shed, his sins are forgiven."" [Sahih]"
18529,"(18523) Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak — narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us; Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us; Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us; Safwan ibn 'Amr al-Saksaki narrated to us; from Abu al-Muthanna al-Mulayki; from 'Utbah ibn 'Abd al-Sulami, may Allah be pleased with him, who had been a Companion, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""The slain are of three types: A believing man who went out with his life and his wealth, met the enemy, and fought until he was killed — he is the one tested in the tent of Allah beneath His Throne. The Prophets do not surpass him except by the rank of Prophethood. And a believing man who burdened himself with sins and transgressions, met the enemy, and fought until he was killed — that is a cleansing that erased his sins and transgressions. Indeed, the sword is an eraser of sins. And it is said to him: 'Enter through any of the eight gates of Paradise you wish.' For Paradise has eight gates, and Hell has seven gates, some of them more excellent than others — meaning the gates of Paradise. And a hypocrite who went out with his life and his wealth, fought until he was killed — he is in the Fire. Indeed, the sword does not erase hypocrisy."" [Da'if]"
18530,"(18524) Abu al-Hasan — 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' — narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us; Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ghayyath narrated to us; Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us; from 'Atā' ibn al-Sa'ib; from Murrah al-Hamdani; from 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Our Lord marvels at two men: A man who rises from his bedding and his cover, from among his beloved ones and his family, to his prayer, desiring what is with Me and fearing what is with Me. And a man who went out to fight in the path of Allah, was defeated, and knew what was upon him in defeat and what was in his returning. So he returned until his blood was shed. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says to His angels: 'Look at My servant — he returned desiring what is with Me and fearing what is with Me, until his blood was shed.'""

Something similar in meaning has been narrated from Abu al-Darda', raised to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). [Saduq]"
18531,"(18525) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us — Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Qattan narrated to us — Ali ibn al-Hasan al-Darabadiji narrated to us — Abdullah ibn Yazid al-Muqri' narrated to us — Sa'id narrated to me — Muhammad ibn 'Ajlan narrated from al-Qa'qa' ibn Hakim — from Abu Salih — from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""The martyr does not feel the pain of being killed except as one of you feels the pain of a sting."" [Hasan]"
18532,"(18526) Abu al-Husayn ibn Burhan al-'Adl in Baghdad informed us — Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Amr al-Razzaz narrated to us — Ahmad ibn al-Walid narrated to us — Hajjaj said: Ibn Jurayj said — Uthman ibn Abi Sulayman narrated to me — 'Ali al-Azdi narrated — from 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr — from Abdullah ibn Hubshiyy, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, was asked: ""Which deed is most virtuous?"" He said: ""Faith without doubt, jihad without treachery, and an accepted pilgrimage."" It was asked: ""Which prayer is most virtuous?"" He said: ""Lengthy standing (in prayer)."" It was asked: ""Which charity is most virtuous?"" He said: ""The effort of a poor person."" It was asked: ""Which emigration is most virtuous?"" He said: ""The one that which Allah has forbidden."" It was asked: ""Which jihad is most virtuous?"" He said: ""The one who strives against the polytheists with his wealth and his life."" It was asked: ""Which death is most noble?"" He said: ""The one whose blood is shed and whose steed is hamstrung."" [Hasan]"
18533,"(18527) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Dasah narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us — Yahya ibn Hasssan narrated to us — al-Walid ibn Rabbah al-Dhimari narrated to me — my uncle Nimran ibn 'Utbah al-Dhimari said: We entered upon Umm al-Darda' while we were orphans, and she said: ""Rejoice, for I heard Abu al-Darda' saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: 'The martyr will intercede for seventy members of his household.'"" Abu Dawud said: The correct name is Rabbah ibn al-Walid. [Sahih by other than its chain. Without the story of entering upon Umm al-Darda'.]"
18534,"(18528) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf informed us — Abu Sa'id Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Basri in Makkah narrated to us — al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'farani narrated to us — Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated — from Abu al-Zinad and Ibn 'Ajlan — from al-A'raj — from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""No one is wounded in the cause of Allah — and Allah knows best who is wounded in His cause — except that he will come on the Day of Resurrection with his wound gushing blood; its color is the color of blood, and its fragrance is the fragrance of musk."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Ibn Yusuf from Malik from Abu al-Zinad, and Muslim narrated it from al-Naqid and Zuhayr from Sufyan from Abu al-Zinad. [Agreed upon]"
18535,"(18529) Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Dawud al-'Alawi informed us — Abu al-Qasim 'Ubayd Allah ibn Ibrahim ibn Balawayh al-Muzakki narrated to us — Ahmad ibn Yusuf al-Sulami narrated to us — 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us — Ma'mar narrated — from Hammam ibn Munabbih, who said: This is what Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, and the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Every wound that a Muslim receives in the cause of Allah will be in its original state on the Day of Resurrection — when it is pierced, it will gush forth blood; its color is the color of blood, and its fragrance is the fragrance of musk."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn Rafi' from 'Abd al-Razzaq. [Sahih. Previously cited.]"
18536,"(18530) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us — Mu'awiyah ibn 'Amr narrated to us — (H) And Abu 'Abd Allah informed me — Abu 'Amr al-Hayri and Abu Bakr al-Warraq informed us, saying: al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us — 'Abd Allah ibn 'Awn narrated to us — they both said: Abu Ishaq al-Fazari narrated — from Suhayl ibn Abi Salih — from his father — from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Two people will not be gathered together in the Fire in a gathering that harms either of them."" It was asked: ""Who are they, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""A believer who kills a disbeliever and then remains steadfast (on the straight path)."" This is the wording of the hadith of 'Abd Allah. Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Awn. [Sahih. Muslim 1891]"
18537,"(18531) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me — Abu al-Nadr narrated to us — Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Nasr narrated to us — Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us — Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated — from al-'Ala' — from his father — from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""A disbeliever and his killer will never be gathered together in the Fire."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Qutaybah. [Sahih. Previously cited.]"
18538,"(18532) Abu 'Ali al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Rudhbari and Abu al-Husayn 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Burhan informed us, saying: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us — Ahmad ibn Mansur al-Ramadi narrated to us — 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us — Ma'mar narrated — from Hammam ibn Munabbih, that he heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Allah laughs at two men, one of whom kills the other — both of them enter Paradise."" They said: ""How is that, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""This one is killed and enters Paradise; then Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, grants repentance to the other and guides him to Islam, and he strives in the cause of Allah and is martyred."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn Rafi' from 'Abd al-Razzaq, and al-Bukhari reported it from another chain from Ma'mar. [Agreed upon]"
18539,"(18533) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us — Ibn Wahb informed me — Malik ibn Anas narrated — from Abu al-Zinad — from al-A'raj — from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Allah looks down upon two men, one of whom kills the other — both of them enter Paradise. This one fights in the cause of Allah and is killed; then Allah grants repentance to the killer, and he fights and is martyred."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf from Malik, and Muslim reported it from the hadith of Sufyan from Abu al-Zinad. [Sahih. Agreed upon]"
18540,"(18534) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me — Abu al-Nadr al-Faqih informed us — Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Nasr al-Imam narrated to us — (H) And Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah al-Ansari and Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far al-'Attar al-Hayri and Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Farisi informed us, saying: Abu 'Amr ibn Matar narrated to us — Ibrahim ibn 'Ali narrated to us — they both said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, he said: I read to Malik ibn Anas — from Ishaq ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Talhah — from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, used to visit Umm Haram bint Milhan and she would feed him. Umm Haram was under the marriage of 'Ubadah ibn al-Samit. One day the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, came to her and she fed him, then she sat down to delouse his head. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, fell asleep, then he awoke smiling. She said: ""What makes you smile, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Some people from my Ummah were shown to me, raiders in the cause of Allah, riding upon the surface of this sea like kings upon thrones — or like kings upon thrones."" He (the narrator) said: ""She said: 'O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah to make me one of them.' So he supplicated for her. Then the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, laid his head down and slept, then awoke smiling. She said: 'What makes you smile, O Messenger of Allah?' He said: 'Some people from my Ummah were shown to me, raiders in the cause of Allah,' as he had said the first time. She said: 'O Messenger of Allah, supplicate to Allah to make me one of them.' He said: 'You are among the first.' So Umm Haram bint Milhan rode the sea in the era of Mu'awiyah, and when she came out of the sea she fell from her mount and perished."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya, and al-Bukhari narrated it from Isma'il and others from Malik."
18541,"(18535) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us.

(Ḥ) He said: And Abū al-Walīd informed me: Abū al-Qāsim al-Baghawī narrated to us: Khalaf ibn Hishām narrated to us. Both said: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān narrated from Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Umm Ḥarām bint Milḥān, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated to me: that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was in her house one day, then he awoke smiling. She said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what has made you smile?"" He said: ""People from my Ummah were presented to me, riding upon the back of this sea like kings upon couches."" She said: ""Supplicate Allah to make me among them."" So he supplicated for her. Then he slept, then he arose and said the same thing. She said: ""Supplicate Allah to make me among them."" He said: ""You are among the first ones."" So ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit married her, and he took her on a military expedition by sea. When they returned, a mule was brought near for her to ride, but it threw her down, breaking her neck, and she died.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī al-Nuʿmān from Ḥammād, and Muslim narrated it from Khalaf ibn Hishām.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh.]"
18542,"(18536) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Umawī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Taymī from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAtīk, the brother of Banī Salimah, from his father, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: ""Whoever leaves his house striving in the cause of Allah"" — he then closed his three fingers together — ""and where are the strivers in the cause of Allah? Whoever goes out in the cause of Allah and falls from his mount and dies, his reward rests upon Allah. And if he is stung by a creature and dies, his reward rests upon Allah. And whoever dies a natural death"" — he said: ""Indeed, it is a word I have not heard from any Arab before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family)"" — meaning by 'a natural death' upon his bed — ""his reward rests upon Allah. And whoever is killed on the spot has earned Paradise."""
18543,"(18537) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn Najdah narrated to us: Baqiyyah narrated from Ibn Thawbān from his father, tracing it back to Makḥūl, to Ibn Ghanm al-Ashʿarī, that Abū Mālik al-Ashʿarī, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: ""Indeed, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has said: Whoever volunteers to go out in the cause of Allah, seeking His Countenance, believing in His promise, and having faith in His Messengers — upon Allah is the guarantee. Either Allah causes him to die in the army by any manner of death He wills and admits him into Paradise, or he remains under Allah's guarantee, even if his absence is long, then Allah returns him to his family in safety, along with whatever reward and spoils of war he has obtained."" He said: ""And whoever sets out in the cause of Allah and dies or is killed — meaning he is a martyr — or his horse or camel throws him, or a venomous creature stings him, or he dies upon his bed by any manner of death Allah wills — then he is a martyr, and for him is Paradise."""
18544,"(18538) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Bazzāz in Baghdād narrated to us: Simāk ibn ʿAbd al-Ṣamad narrated to us: Abū Muhir ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn Muhir al-Ghassānī narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to me: al-Awzāʿī narrated to me: Sulaymān ibn Ḥabīb narrated from Abī Umāmah al-Bāhilī, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), who said: ""Three persons — all of them are under the guarantee of Allah, Mighty and Majestic: a man who goes out as a warrior in the cause of Allah — he is under the guarantee of Allah until He causes him to die and admits him into Paradise, or returns him with whatever reward or spoils of war he has obtained; and a man who goes to the mosque — he is under the guarantee of Allah until He causes him to die and admits him into Paradise, or returns him with whatever reward or spoils of war he has obtained; and a man who enters his house in peace — he is under the guarantee of Allah.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
18545,"(18539) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh al-ʿAdl narrated to us: ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk al-Bazzār narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Layth ibn Saʿd narrated from al-Ḥārith ibn Yaʿqūb from Qays ibn Rāfiʿ al-Qaysī from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jubayr from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with both of them, who said: He passed by Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, while he was sitting at his door, gesturing with his hand as though he was talking to himself. ʿAbd Allāh said to him: ""What is the matter with you, O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, that you are talking to yourself?"" He said: ""What is it to me? The enemy of Allah wants to distract me from words I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family)."" He said: ""Are you struggling all your time now in your house? Why do not you go out to the gathering and narrate, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: 'Whoever strives in the cause of Allah is under the guarantee of Allah; and whoever sits in his house and does not backbite anyone with evil is under the guarantee of Allah; and whoever visits a sick person is under the guarantee of Allah; and whoever goes early to the mosque or returns is under the guarantee of Allah; and whoever comes to a leader to support him is under the guarantee of Allah.'"" He said: ""The enemy of Allah wants to drive me out from my house to the gathering.""

[Ḍaʿīf.]"
18546,"(18540) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Maymūn narrated to us: Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Shaybān narrated from Qatādah: Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated that Umm al-Rabīʿ bint al-Barāʾ — who was Umm Ḥārithah ibn Surāqah — came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and said: ""O Prophet of Allah, will you not tell me about Ḥārithah? He was killed on the day of Badr, struck by a stray arrow. If he is in Paradise, I will be patient, and if it is otherwise, I will weep over him with all my effort."" He said: ""O Umm Ḥārithah, indeed there are gardens in Paradise, and your son has attained al-Firdaws al-Aʿlā.""

Qatādah said: ""Al-Firdaws is the highest, most central, and most excellent part of Paradise.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh from Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2809, 2983.]"
18547,"(18541) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ghālib al-Ḥāfiẓ in Baghdād informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥamdān al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā informed us: ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus narrated to us: Zakariyyā narrated from Abī Isḥāq from al-Barāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and said: ""I bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that You are His slave and Messenger."" Then he advanced and fought until he was killed. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""He did a little and was rewarded abundantly.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Janāb from ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh.]"
18548,"(18542) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rajāʾ informed us: Isrāʾīl narrated from Abī Isḥāq from al-Barāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), his head covered in iron (i.e., wearing armor), and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, should I fight or should I embrace Islam?"" He said: ""No, rather embrace Islam, then fight."" So he embraced Islam, then fought and was killed. He said: ""This one did little and was rewarded abundantly.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥīm from Shabābah from Isrāʾīl.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh.]"
18549,"(18543) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAnazī narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Salamah informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr narrated from Abī Salamah from Abī Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that ʿAmr ibn Uqaysh had usurious interest (ribā) from the Days of Ignorance, and he disliked embracing Islam until he could collect it. He came on the day of Uḥud and said: ""Where are my paternal cousins?"" They said: ""At Uḥud."" He said: ""Where is so-and-so?"" They said: ""At Uḥud."" He said: ""Where is so-and-so?"" They said: ""At Uḥud."" So he put on his armor, mounted his horse, and set out towards them. When the Muslims saw him, they said: ""Keep away from us, O ʿAmr!"" He said: ""Indeed, I have believed."" He fought until he was wounded. He was carried to his family in a wounded state. Saʿd ibn Muʿādh came and said to his sister: ""Ask him: was it out of zeal for your people, or anger for them, or anger for Allah and His Messenger?"" He said: ""Rather, anger for Allah and His Messenger."" He died and entered Paradise, and he had never performed a single prayer for Allah."
18550,"(18544) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī and Ṣāliḥ ibn Muḥammad al-Rāzī both said: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us: Shuʿbah ibn al-Ḥajjāj narrated from ʿAmr ibn Murrah from Abī Wāʾil from Abī Mūsā al-Ashʿarī, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and said: ""A man fights for the sake of spoils of war, and a man fights for fame, and a man fight to be recognized — which one is in the cause of Allah?"" He said: ""Whoever fights so that the Word of Allah may be the highest — he is in the cause of Allah.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb, and he as well as Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ghundar from Shuʿbah.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh.]"
18551,"(18545) Abu Muhammad — Abd Allah ibn Yusuf al-Ashbahani — informed us that Abu Saeed — Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Basri — in Makkah — narrated to us that Sadan ibn Nast al-Mukharrimi — narrated to us that Abu Muawiyah al-Darir — from al-A'mash — from Shuqayq — from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, may Allah be pleased with him — who said: A man came to the Prophet, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, a man fights out of bravery, and fights out of zeal, and fights for show — which of that is in the way of Allah?"" The Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, said: ""Whoever fights so that the Word of Allah may be the highest, then he is in the way of Allah."" [Authenticated. Previously referenced.]"
18552,"(18546) And Abu al-Hasan — Ali ibn Ahmad ibn Abdan — informed us that Ahmad ibn Ubayd al-Saffar — narrated to us that Uthman ibn Umar al-Dabbi — narrated to us that Ibn Kathir — narrated to us that Sufyan — narrated to us that al-A'mash — from Abu Wail — from Abu Musa al-Ash'ari, may Allah be pleased with him — who said: A man came to the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, and mentioned something similar to it. Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn Kathir, and Muslim narrated it from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah and others from Abu Muawiyah. [Authenticated. Agreed upon (by al-Bukhari and Muslim).]"
18553,"(18547) Ali ibn Ahmad ibn Abdan — informed us that Ahmad ibn Ubayd al-Saffar — narrated to us that Ubayd ibn Sharik — narrated to us that Muhammad ibn Wahb — narrated to us that Baqiyyah — (H) And Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz — informed us that Abu Ahmad — Bakr ibn Muhammad al-Sayrafi — in Marw — narrated to us that Abu al-Ahwas — Muhammad ibn al-Haytham al-Qadi — narrated to us that Haywah ibn Shurayh al-Hadrami — narrated to us that Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid — narrated to me that Bahir ibn Sa'd — from Khalid ibn Ma'dan — from Abu Bahriyyah — from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him — from the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, that he said: ""Military expeditions are of two kinds: as for the one who seeks the Countenance of Allah, the Almighty, the Majestic, obeys the Imam, spends what is precious, associates with his companion, and avoids corruption — then his sleep and his waking are all reward. And as for the one who goes out for pride, for show, and for fame, disobeys the Imam, and spreads corruption in the land — then he will not return empty-handed."" This is the wording of the hadith of al-Hadrami. And in the narration of Muhammad ibn Wahb, he said: from Abu Bahriyyah, Abd Allah ibn Qays, and at its end: ""and disobeys the Imam and does not spend what is precious — he will not return empty-handed."" [Weak.]"
18554,"(18548) Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz — informed us that Ahmad ibn Ja'far al-Qati'i — in Baghdad — narrated to us that Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal — narrated to me that my father — narrated to us that Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi — narrated to me that Muhammad ibn Abi al-Waddah — from al-Ala' ibn Abd Allah ibn Rafi' — from Hanan ibn Kharijah — from Abd Allah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both — that he said: ""O Messenger of Allah, inform me about jihad and military expeditions."" He said: ""O Abd Allah ibn Amr, if you fight patiently and with expectation of reward, Allah will resurrect you as patient and expecting reward. And if you fight for show and for amassing, Allah will resurrect you as a show-off and an amasser. O Abd Allah ibn Amr, in whatever state you fight or are killed, Allah will resurrect you upon that state."" [Weak.]"
18555,"(18549) Abu al-Qasim — Ali ibn Muhammad ibn Ali al-Iyadi — in Baghdad — informed us that Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn Yusuf ibn Khallad al-Nasibi — narrated to us that al-Harith ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Usamah — narrated to us that Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata — informed us that Abd al-Malik ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Jurayj — narrated to me that Yunus ibn Yusuf — from Sulayman ibn Yasar — who said: The people dispersed from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and Natil, the brother of the people of Sham, said to him: ""O Abu Hurayrah, narrate to us a hadith you heard from the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family."" He said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, saying: 'The first people to be judged on the Day of Resurrection will be three: a man who was martyred — he will be brought, and his blessings will be made known to him, and he will recognize them. It will be said: ""What did you do with them?"" He will say: ""I fought in Your cause until I was martyred."" It will be said: ""You have lied. Rather, you wanted it to be said: 'So-and-so is a brave man.' And it was said."" Then he will be commanded, and he will be dragged on his face until he is cast into the Fire. And a man who acquired knowledge and recited the Qur'an — he will be brought, and his blessings will be made known to him, and he will recognize them. It will be said: ""What did you do with them?"" He will say: ""I acquired knowledge and recited the Qur'an and taught it for Your sake."" It will be said: ""You have lied. Rather, you wanted it to be said: 'So-and-so is a scholar and so-and-so is a reciter.' And it was said."" Then he will be commanded, and he will be dragged on his face to the Fire. And a man to whom Allah gave various kinds of wealth — he will be brought, and his blessings will be made known to him, and he will recognize them. It will be said: ""What did you do with them?"" He will say: ""I did not leave any way in which You love wealth to be spent except that I spent it for Your sake."" It will be said: ""You have lied. Rather, you wanted it to be said: 'So-and-so is a generous man.' And it was said."" Then he will be commanded, and he will be dragged on his face until he is cast into the Fire.'"" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from two other routes from Ibn Jurayj. [Authenticated. Muslim 1905.]"
18556,"(18550) Abu al-Hasan — Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri — informed us that al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq — narrated to us that Yusuf ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us that Sulayman ibn Harb — narrated to us that Hammad ibn Zayd — from Ayyub — from Muhammad, meaning Ibn Sirin — from Abu al-Ajfa' — who said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed the people and said: ""And you say about those killed in your military expeditions: 'So-and-so was martyred and so-and-so died a martyr's death' — when perhaps he loaded his mount with gold or silver, seeking the world, or pursuing trade. Do not say that. Rather, say as the Prophet, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, said: 'Whoever is killed in the way of Allah or dies, he is in Paradise.'"" [Hasan.]"
18557,"(18551) Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz — informed us that Abu al-Abbas — al-Qasim ibn al-Qasim al-Sayyari — narrated to us that Abd Allah ibn Ali al-Ghazal — narrated to us that Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Shaqiq — narrated to us that Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak — (H) And Abu Ali al-Rudhbari — informed us that Abu Bakr ibn Dasah — narrated to us that Abu Dawud — narrated to us that Abu Tawbah — al-Rabi' ibn Nafi' — from Ibn al-Mubarak — from Ibn Abi Dhi'b — from al-Qasim, who is Ibn Abbas — from Bukayr ibn Abd Allah ibn al-Ashajj — from Ibn Mikzan, a man from the people of Sham — and in the narration of Ibn Shaqiq: from Ayyub ibn Mikzan — from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him — that a man said: ""O Messenger of Allah, a man intends jihad in the way of Allah while seeking some worldly gain."" The Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, said: ""He has no reward."" He asked a second time and a third time, and the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, said: ""He has no reward."" This is the wording of the hadith of Ibn Shaqiq. The Sheikh said: These narrations and those similar to them may apply to the one who does not intend by his military expedition except the world and what pertains to its means. [Weak.]"
18558,"(18552) As for the one who seeks reward and hopes to obtain spoils of war — Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan — in Baghdad — informed us that Abd Allah ibn Ja'far ibn Durustawayh — narrated to us that Ya'qub ibn Sufyan — narrated to us that Abu Salih — narrated to me that Mu'awiyah ibn Salih — that Damrah ibn Habib — narrated to him — from Ibn Zughb al-Iyadi — who said: Abd Allah ibn Hawalah, the companion of the Prophet, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, stayed with us, and it had reached us that the zakat due upon him had been set at two hundred, but he refused except one hundred. I said to him: ""Is what we have heard true — that the zakat due upon you has been set at two hundred, yet you refuse except one hundred?"" He said: ""By Allah, what prevents me — while he is staying with me — from saying: 'May your mother be bereaved of you!' Does one hundred not suffice Ibn Hawalah every year?"" Then he began narrating to us from the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family. He said: ""The Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, sent us on foot around Madinah to obtain spoils. We returned and obtained nothing. When the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, saw what had befallen us of hardship, he said: 'O Allah, do not entrust them to me, for I am too weak for them; and do not entrust them to the people, for they would belittle them or prefer others over them; and do not entrust them to their own souls, for they would be unable to bear it. Rather, provide for them by Yourself alone.' Then he said: 'Sham will be conquered for you, and you will divide the treasures of Persia and Rome, and each one of you will have such and such of wealth, to the point that one of you will be given a hundred dinars and be displeased.'"" Then he placed his hand upon my head and said: ""O Ibn Hawalah, when you see the Caliphate has descended upon the Holy Land, then earthquakes, tribulations, and great events will have come, and the Hour will be closer to people than this hand of mine is to your head."" [Authenticated.]"
18559,"(18553) Abu Muhammad — Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn Abd al-Jabbar al-Sukkari — in Baghdad — informed us that Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar — narrated to us that al-Abbas ibn Abd Allah al-Tarqafi — narrated to us that Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri — narrated to us that Haywah — from Abu Hani' — from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Hubuli — from Abd Allah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with him — that the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, said — (H) And Abd Allah ibn Yusuf — informed us that Abu Saeed ibn al-A'rabi — narrated to us that Muhammad ibn Isma'il — narrated to us that Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri — narrated to us that Haywah and Ibn Lahi'ah — both said: Abu Hani' narrated to us that he heard Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Hubuli saying: I heard Abd Allah ibn Amr saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, saying: ""No army that goes out in the way of Allah and obtains spoils except that they receive two-thirds of their reward in advance in the Hereafter, and one-third remains for them. And if they do not obtain spoils, their reward is completed for them."" The narration of Abd Allah ibn Yusuf does not contain ""in the Hereafter."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abd ibn Humayd from al-Muqri from Haywah. [Authenticated. Muslim 1906.]"
18560,"(18554) Abu Abd Allah al-Hafiz — informed me that Abu Muhammad — Ahmad ibn Abd Allah al-Muzani — informed us that Ali ibn Muhammad ibn Isa — narrated to us that Abu al-Yaman — informed me that Shu'ayb — from al-Zuhri — informed me that Saeed ibn al-Musayyib — informed him that Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, saying: ""By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, were it not that there are men among the believers whose souls do not feel at ease to stay behind me, and I do not find the means to carry them, I would not stay behind from any raiding party that goes out in the way of Allah. By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, I wish I could be killed in the way of Allah, then be brought back to life, then be killed, then be brought back to life, then be killed, then be brought back to life, then be killed."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Abu al-Yaman. [Authenticated. Agreed upon (by al-Bukhari and Muslim).]"
18561,"(18555) Sahl ibn Abī Umāmah ibn Sahl ibn Hunaif narrated to us — from his father — from his grandfather — that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa-ālihi wa-sallam) said:

""Whoever sincerely asks Allah for martyrdom, Allah will grant him the stations of the martyrs, even if he dies upon his bed.""

Muslim narrated it in his *Ṣaḥīḥ* from Abī al-Ṭāhir and Ḥarmalah from Ibn Wahb, and he also recorded it additionally from the ḥadīth of Thābit from Anas. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 1909]"
18562,"(18556) Muʿādh ibn Jabal (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) narrated to them that he heard the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa-ālihi wa-sallam) saying:

""Whoever fights in the cause of Allah — even for the duration of a she-camel's milking — Paradise becomes obligatory for him. And whoever sincerely asks Allah for death at his own hands, then dies or is killed, he shall have the reward of a martyr. And whoever is wounded — or suffers an injury — in the cause of Allah, it shall come on the Day of Resurrection with its colour like saffron and its fragrance like musk. And whoever is wounded in the cause of Allah shall bear the seal of the martyrs.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18563,"(18557) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayy al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayy al-Narsī narrated to us — Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad narrated to us — Ibn Jurayj narrated to us — Sulaymān ibn Mūsā narrated to us — Mālik ibn Yukhāmir narrated to us — from Muʿādh ibn Jabal, and he mentioned it with a similar wording.

Likewise, Abū ʿĀṣim and Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated it from Ibn Jurayj."
18564,"(18558) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayy narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Khazzāz narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Sahm al-Anṭākī narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad al-Fazārī narrated to us — from Ibn Jurayj — from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā — from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Mālik ibn Yukhāmir — from his father, Mālik ibn Yukhāmir — from Muʿādh ibn Jabal (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa-ālihi wa-sallam) said:

""Whoever sincerely asks Allah for martyrdom from his heart, then dies or is killed, he shall have the reward of a martyr. And whoever is wounded in the cause of Allah shall come on the Day of Resurrection — its colour the colour of blood, and its fragrance the fragrance of musk.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18565,"(18559) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayy narrated to us — al-Ḥasan ibn Saʿīd al-Mawṣilī narrated to us — Ghassān ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to us — from his father — from Makhūl — from Mālik ibn Yukhāmir — from Muʿādh ibn Jabal (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) that he said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa-ālihi wa-sallam) said:

""Whoever fights in the cause of Allah for the duration of a she-camel's milking, Paradise becomes obligatory for him."" Then he mentioned what followed, similar to the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Razzāq.

[Ṣaḥīḥ — previously cited]"
18566,"(18560) Anas (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) said:

""The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa-ālihi wa-sallam) was the most handsome of people, the most generous of people, and the bravest of people."" He said: ""The people of Madinah were alarmed one night, so they set out towards the sound. The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa-ālihi wa-sallam) met them upon a horse belonging to Abū Ṭalḥah — bare, with nothing upon it, and a sword hanging from his neck. He said: 'Do not be alarmed.' He had already ascertained the news and outpaced them. He said: 'We have found it to be a sea.' Or he said: 'Indeed, it is a sea.'"" He said: ""And it had been a slow horse.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his *Ṣaḥīḥ* from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and Abū al-Rabīʿ.

And it has been narrated to us from Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ and Anas ibn Mālik from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa-ālihi wa-sallam) that he used to seek refuge in Allah from cowardice.

[Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon]"
18567,"(18561) Abū Hurayrah (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa-ālihi wa-sallam) said:

""The worst of what a man may possess is a miserly greed and a shameless cowardice.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18568,"(18562) ʿUmar (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) said:

""Bravery and cowardice are innate dispositions among people. You will find a man fighting against one he does not know, and you will find a man fleeing from his own father. And honour is wealth, and nobility is taqwā. No one is superior to a Persian or a non-Arab except by taqwā.""

[Ḥasan li-ghayrihi]"
18569,"(18563) Abū Hurayrah (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa-ālihi wa-sallam) saying:

""Whoever spends a pair — that is, two — of anything in the cause of Allah, he will be called from the gates of Paradise: 'O servant of Allah, this is good!' And Paradise has gates. Whoever is among the people of ṣalāh will be called from the Gate of Ṣalāh; whoever is among the people of jihād will be called from the Gate of Jihād; whoever is among the people of ṣadaqah will be called from the Gate of Ṣadaqah; and whoever is among the people of ṣiyām will be called from the Gate of Ṣiyām — the Gate of al-Rayyān.""

Abū Bakr said: ""There is no harm upon one who is called from those gates."" He asked: ""O Messenger of Allah, will anyone be called from all of them?"" He replied: ""Yes, and I hope that you will be among them, O Abū Bakr.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his *Ṣaḥīḥ* from Abī al-Yamān, and Muslim recorded it through various routes from al-Zuhrī.

[Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon]"
18570,"(18564) Ṣaʿṣaʿah ibn Muʿāwiyah said:

""I met Abū Dharr (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) leading his camel — or driving it — with a waterskin around its neck. I said: 'O Abū Dharr, what is your wealth?' He said: 'My deeds are my wealth.' I said: 'O Abū Dharr, what is your wealth?' He said: 'My deeds are my wealth.' I said: 'O Abū Dharr, what is your wealth?' He said: 'My deeds are my wealth' — three times. I said: 'Will you not narrate to me something you heard from the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa-ālihi wa-sallam)?' He said: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa-ālihi wa-sallam) saying: ""No two Muslims — meaning, two parents — die having lost three children who have not yet reached the age of accountability, except that Allah will admit them into Paradise by the grace of His mercy upon them. And no Muslim spends a pair of his wealth in the cause of Allah, except that the gatekeepers of Paradise shall hasten to him.""'""

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18571,"(18565) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā al-Wāsiṭī informed us, ʿAmr ibn ʿAwn informed us, Hushaym narrated from Manṣūr, and Yūnus narrated from al-Ḥasan, and he mentioned it with the same meaning. He added: ""The gatekeepers of Paradise will all compete in calling him to what lies before him."" I asked: ""How is that?"" He said: ""If there are riding animals, he will be given two mounts; if there are camels, he will be given two camels; and if there are sheep, he will be given two sheep."" [Weak]"
18572,"(18566) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim narrated from Bishr ibn Abī Sayf, from al-Walīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Ghuḍayf ibn al-Ḥārith, who said: I heard Abū ʿUbaydah (may Allah be pleased with him) say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: ""Whoever spends a surplus expenditure in the cause of Allah, it shall be multiplied for him seven hundred times. And whoever spends on himself—or he said on his family—or visits a sick person, or removes a harmful thing from the path, a good deed shall be recorded for him ten times over. And fasting is a shield, so long as it is not torn. And whoever is afflicted by Allah with an ailment in his body, it shall be an expiation for him."" [Authentic]"
18573,"(18567) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us, Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim narrated to us, Bishr ibn Abī Sayf narrated from al-Walīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿIyāḍ ibn Ghuṭayf, who said: Yazīd also informed us, and Hishām ibn Ḥassān narrated from Wāṣil, the freedman of Abū ʿUyaynah, from al-Walīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿIyāḍ ibn Ghuṭayf, who said: We visited Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ during the illness in which he died, and his wife Ḥayfāʾ was with him, his face turned toward the wall. We asked: ""How did Abū ʿUbaydah spend the night?"" She said: ""He spent the night in a state of reward."" He then turned toward us and said: ""I did not spend the night in a state of reward."" That distressed us, and we fell silent. He said: ""Will you not ask me about what I said?"" We said: ""That did not please us, so we ask you about it."" He said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: 'Whoever spends a surplus expenditure in the cause of Allah, it shall be multiplied for him seven hundred times. And whoever spends on himself and his family, or removes a harmful thing from the path, or gives charity, a good deed shall be recorded for him ten times over. And fasting is a shield, so long as it is not torn. And whoever is afflicted by Allah with an ailment in his body, it shall be an expiation for him.'"" [Authentic. Previously cited.]"
18574,"(18568) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Asmāʾ informed us, Mahdī ibn Maymūn narrated to us, Wāṣil, the freedman of Abū ʿUyaynah, narrated from Ibn Abī Sayf, from al-Walīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, a man from among the jurists of the people of Shām, from ʿIyāḍ ibn Ghuṭayf.

(Ḥ) And Yūsuf narrated to us, Abū al-Rabīʿ and Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us, and they said: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us, Wāṣil, the freedman of Abū ʿUyaynah, narrated from Bishr ibn Abī Sayf, from al-Walīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿIyāḍ ibn Ghuṭayf, from Abū ʿUbaydah, with this hadith. And Sulaym ibn ʀmir narrated that Ghuḍayf ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to them from Abū ʿUbaydah, who said: ""Illness serves as an expiation for sins.""

Al-Bukhārī al-Ṣaḥīḥ said: Ghuḍayf ibn al-Ḥārith al-Shāmī. [Authentic]"
18575,"(18569) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān al-ʿĀmirī narrated to us, Abū Usāmah narrated from Zāʾidah, from al-Aʿmash, from Abū ʿAmr al-Shaybānī, from Abū Masʿūd ʿUqbah ibn ʿAmr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) with a hobbled she-camel and said: ""This is for me, O Messenger of Allah, in the cause of Allah."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said to him: ""On the Day of Resurrection, you shall have seven hundred she-camels, all of them hobbled.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah, from Abū Usāmah. [Authentic. Muslim 1892]"
18576,"(18570) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us, Ibn Wahb informed me, ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed me, from Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj, from Busr ibn Saʿīd, from Zayd ibn Khālid al-Juhanī (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), who said: ""Whoever equips a warrior in the cause of Allah has indeed fought, and whoever looks after his family in his absence with goodness has indeed fought.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr and others, from Ibn Wahb, and both al-Bukhārī and Muslim transmitted it as previously mentioned. [Authentic. Agreed upon.]"
18577,"(18571) The Shaykh, the Imām Abū al-Ṭayyib Sahl ibn Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān (may Allah have mercy on him) narrated to us by dictation. Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us, my father and Shuʿayb ibn al-Layth informed us, al-Layth narrated from Ibn al-Hād, from al-Walīd ibn Abī al-Walīd, from ʿUthmān ibn Surāqah, from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: ""Whoever provides shade for a warrior, Allah will shade him on the Day of Resurrection. And whoever equips a warrior until he is fully prepared shall have a reward equal to his, until he dies or returns. And whoever builds a mosque in which the name of Allah is remembered, Allah will build for him a house in Paradise."" [Authentic]"
18578,"(18572) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Shādyākhī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, and they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, and they mentioned the hadith with the same wording. They added, and he said: al-Walīd said: I mentioned this hadith to al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad, and he said: ""This hadith has reached me from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family)."" He said: I then mentioned it to Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir and to Zayd ibn Aslam, and both of them said: ""This has reached me from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family)."""
18579,"(18573) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Warrāq narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, ʿAbīdah ibn Ḥumayd narrated to us, al-Aswad ibn Qays narrated from Nubayḥ al-ʿAnazī, from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family): He intended to go on an expedition and said: ""O company of the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār, among your brethren are people who have no wealth nor clan. Let each of you take two or three of them under his care, for none of us shall have a turn on a camel's back except that it shall be a turn equal to that of one of them."" He said: I took two or three under my care, and I had no turn except a turn equal to that of one of them. [Good]"
18580,"(18574) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us, Ibn Wahb informed me, Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb and Saʿīd ibn Abī Ayyūb informed me, from Zabbān ibn Fāʾid, from Sahl ibn Muʿādh ibn Anas al-Juhanī, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Indeed, prayer, fasting, and remembrance of Allah are multiplied over spending in the cause of Allah by seven hundred times."" [Weak]"
18581,"(18575) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb informed me, from Zabbān ibn Fāʾid, from Sahl ibn Muʿādh al-Juhanī, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Whoever recites a thousand verses in the cause of Allah, Allah will record him among the prophets, the truthful, the martyrs, and the righteous."" [Weak]"
18582,"(18576) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed us: Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr and Salamah ibn Shabīb said: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd and Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ, that they both heard al-Nuʿmān ibn Abī ʿAyyāsh al-Zuraqī narrating from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: ""Whoever fasts one day in the cause of Allah, Allah will distance his face from the Fire by seventy years of travel.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Naṣr, from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, and Muslim narrated it from Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr. [Sound — Agreed upon]"
18583,"(18577) The Shaykh al-Imām Abū al-Ṭayyib — Sahl ibn Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān (may Allah have mercy on him) — informed us by dictation: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Abū Zurʿah ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAmr al-Dimashqī informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUthmān al-Tanūkhī informed us: al-Haytham ibn Ḥumayd informed us: al-Muṭʿim ibn al-Miqdām narrated from Mujāhid, who said: I set out for a military expedition, and ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) escorted us. When he wished to depart from us, he said: ""I have nothing with me to give you, but I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: 'When Allah entrusts something, He safeguards it. I entrust to Allah your religion, your trusts, and the seals of your deeds.'"" [Sound]"
18584,"(18578) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb informed me, from Zabbān ibn Fāʾid, from Sahl ibn Muʿādh ibn Anas, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), who said: ""That I escort a mujāhid in the cause of Allah and attend to his mount for a morning or evening journey is more beloved to me than the world and all that is in it."" [Weak]"
18585,"(18579) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn informed us: Ādam informed us: Shuʿbah informed us: Abū al-Fayḍ — a man from the people of Shām — said: I heard Saʿīd ibn Jābir al-Ruʿaynī narrating from his father, that Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him) escorted an army and walked with them. He said: ""All praise is due to Allah, Who has soiled our feet in His cause."" It was said to him: ""How have our feet been soiled when we only escorted them?"" He said: ""We equipped them, escorted them, and supplicated for them."" [Weak]"
18586,"(18580) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr informed us: Sufyān narrated, from Qaʿnab, from ʿAlqamah ibn Marthad, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""The sanctity of the wives of the mujāhids over those who remain behind is like the sanctity of their mothers. There is no man among those who remain behind who betrays a man in his family except that a banner will be raised for him on the Day of Resurrection, and it will be said: 'This one betrayed your family — take from his good deeds as you wish.'"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) then turned to us and said: ""What do you think?"" [Sound — Muslim 1897]"
18587,"(18581) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Bishr ibn Mūsā informed us: al-Ḥumaydī informed us: Sufyān informed us: Qaʿnab al-Tamīmī — who was a trustworthy and excellent man — narrated something similar to the above, except that he said: ""It will be said to him: 'O So-and-so! This is So-and-so ibn So-and-so who betrayed you — take from his good deeds as you wish.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr, and he also reported it from the ḥadīth of al-Thawrī and Misʿar, from ʿAlqamah, from Sulaymān ibn Buraydah, from his father. [Sound — as previously mentioned]"
18588,"(18582) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Thābit al-Marwazī informed me: ʿAlī ibn Ḥusayn informed me, from his father, from Yazīd al-Naḥwī, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said regarding the verses: {""Allah pardon you! Why did you give them permission [to stay behind] until it became clear to you who were truthful and you might know the liars?"" (al-Tawbah, 43) ""Those who believe in Allah and the Last Day do not ask your permission to strive with their wealth and their lives. And Allah is All-Knowing of the muttaqīn. Only those ask your permission who do not believe in Allah and the Last Day, and whose hearts are in doubt, so they waver in their doubt.""} (al-Tawbah, 43–45) — these were abrogated by the verse in Sūrat al-Nūr: {""The believers are only those who believe in Allah and His Messenger, and when they are with him on a matter of collective importance, they do not depart until they ask his permission. Indeed, those who ask your permission are the ones who believe in Allah and His Messenger. So when they ask your permission for some affair of theirs, grant permission to whomever you wish among them, and seek forgiveness for them from Allah. Indeed, Allah is Forgiving, Merciful.""} (al-Nūr, 62) Similarly, ʿAṭiyyah ibn Saʿd narrated it from Ibn ʿAbbās, and with the same meaning, Qatādah said: ""He was granted concession here after He had said to him: {'Allah pardon you! Why did you give them permission?'}"" (al-Tawbah, 43). [Weak]"
18589,"(18583) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: ʿUmar ibn Muḥammad informed me, from Nāfiʿ, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), that when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) returned from a military expedition, he said: ""Do not come upon your women at night."" And he sent someone to announce to the people that he would arrive the following day. [Sound]"
18590,"(18584) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Musaddad narrated, from Yaḥyā, from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid, who informed me: Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim said: Jarīr ibn ʿAbd Allāh (may Allah be pleased with him) said to me: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said to me: ""Will you not relieve me from Dhū al-Khalāṣah?"" — they used to call it the Kaʿbah al-Yamāniyyah. I set out with one hundred and fifty cavalrymen from Aḥmas, but I was not firm on horseback. I mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), so he struck his hand upon my chest until I could see the marks of his fingers on my chest, and he said: ""O Allah, make him firm, and make him a guide and one who is guided."" He said: ""He then went and demolished it and set it ablaze with fire. Then he sent Ḥuṣayn ibn Rabīʿah to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) to give him the glad tidings. He said: 'By the One Who sent you with the truth, I did not come to you until I had left it like a scabby camel.' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) then invoked blessings upon the horses and men of Aḥmas five times.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and Muslim reported it through various chains from Ismāʿīl. [Sound — Agreed upon]"
18591,"(18585) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Saqqāʾ and Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ both reported to us, saying: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn ʿĀṣim narrated from Ḥammād ibn Salamah from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from his father from Usāmah ibn Zayd (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) left ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān and Usāmah ibn Zayd (may Allah be pleased with them both) behind with Ruqayyah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) (may Allah be pleased with her), during the days of Badr. Then Zayd ibn Ḥārithah (may Allah be pleased with him) came upon al-ʿAḍbāʾ, the she-camel of the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), bearing the glad tidings. Usāmah said: I heard a commotion, so I went out and there was Zayd, who had come with the glad tidings. By Allah, I did not believe it until we saw the captives. Then the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) assigned a share of the spoils to ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him). [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18592,"(18586) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: ʿUbayd ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us: al-Layth narrated from ʿUqayl from Ibn Shihāb from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik that ʿAbdullāh ibn Kaʿb, the guide of Kaʿb when he went blind among his sons, said: I heard Kaʿb ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) narrate his ḥadīth regarding when he stayed behind from the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) during the expedition of Tabūk. He mentioned the ḥadīth in its entirety regarding his repentance and the announcement by the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) of Allah's acceptance of his repentance and that of his two companions. He said: I heard the voice of a caller who had ascended the mountain of Salʿ: ""O Kaʿb ibn Mālik, receive glad tidings!"" He said: I fell down in prostration, and I knew that relief had come. When the one whose voice I had heard came to me, giving me glad tidings, I took off my two garments and gave them both to him as a reward for his good news. By Allah, I possessed nothing else that day. I borrowed two garments, wore them, and set out to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). al-Bukhārī narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
18593,"(18587) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: Abū Aḥmad ibn Ziyād narrated to us: Ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from al-Zuhrī from al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: When the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) returned from Tabūk, the people went out to meet him at Thaniyyat al-Wadāʿ. I went out with the people while I was a young boy, and we met him. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī 4420]"
18594,"(18588) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar al-Qaṭīʿī narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdillāh narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from al-Zuhrī from al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I went out with the boys to meet the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) at Thaniyyat al-Wadāʿ upon his return from the expedition of Tabūk. And Sufyān said on one occasion: I remember the arrival of the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) when he returned from Tabūk. al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdillāh. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded above]"
18595,"(18589) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Abū Muslim narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Muḥārib ibn Dithār, who said: I heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh (may Allah be pleased with him) say: I was with the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) on a journey. When we arrived in Madīnah, he said to me: ""Enter the mosque and pray two rakʿahs."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Shuʿbah. The remaining aḥādīth narrated regarding the etiquettes of travel are at the end of the Book of Ḥajj, and the aḥādīth narrated regarding preparation for jihād are in the Book of Racing and Archery. And with Allah lies success. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
18596,"(18590) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us by dictation and recitation: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Ziyād narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad al-Farwī narrated to us: Mālik narrated from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""You will fight against the Jews until one of them hides behind a stone, and the stone will say: 'O ʿAbdullāh, O Muslim! There is a Jew behind me, so kill him!'"" al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad al-Farwī, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Nāfiʿ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
18597,"(18591) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Sallām narrated to us: Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad narrated from Faraj ibn Faḍālah from ʿAbd al-Khabīr ibn Thābit ibn Qays ibn Shammās from his father from his grandfather, who said: A woman came to the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) — she was known as Umm Khallād — veiled, asking about her son who had been killed. Some of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""Have you come to ask about your son while you are veiled?"" She said: ""If I have lost my son, I have not lost my modesty."" The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""Your son shall have the reward of two martyrs."" She said: ""Why is that, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Because he was killed by the People of the Book."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18598,"(18592) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī narrated to us in Makkah: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from al-Zuhrī from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam): ""The Hour will not be established until you fight against people whose shoes are made of hair."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
18599,"(18593) Abū Muḥammad narrated to us: Abū Saʿīd narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Abū al-Zinād from al-Aʿraj from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), who said: ""The Hour will not be established until you fight against people whose shoes are made of hair."" And the Hour will not be established until you fight against people with small eyes and flat noses, as if their faces are hammered shields placed one upon another. Both al-Bukhārī narrated them in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdillāh from Sufyān, and both Muslim narrated them from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah from Sufyān. And Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah narrated it from Abū al-Zinād, and he said: ""Until you fight against the Turks, with small eyes and red faces."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded above]"
18600,"(18594) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to us: al-Manīʿī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād narrated to us: Sufyān narrated, mentioning the first ḥadīth. Abū ʿAbdillāh — meaning Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād — said: ""It has reached me that the companions of Bābak used to wear shoes made of hair."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded above]"
18601,"(18595) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī, may Allah have mercy on him, informed us: Abū al-Qāsim ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Bālawayh al-Muzakkī informed us: Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī informed us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar, from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: This is what Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me. He said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""The Hour shall not be established until you fight the people of Khūz and Kirmān, a nation among the non-Arabs, ruddy of face, flat-nosed, small-eyed, as if their faces were hammered shields.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā, from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon.]"
18602,"(18596) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us: Shaybān ibn Abī Shaybah informed us: Jarīr, who is ibn Ḥāzim, informed us: al-Ḥasan informed us: ʿAmr ibn Taghlib, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""You shall fight, before the Hour, a people whose shoes are made of hair, and you shall fight a people with broad faces, as if their faces were hammered shields.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb and Abī al-Nuʿmān, from Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon.]"
18603,"(18597) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: ʿĪsā ibn Muḥammad al-Ramlī informed us: Ḍamrah, from al-Shaybānī, from Abū Sukaynah, a man from the Muḥarrarīn, from a man from the Companions of the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), that he said: ""Leave the Abyssinians alone so long as they leave you alone, and leave the Turks alone so long as they leave you alone."" [Ḥasan]"
18604,"(18598) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: al-Qāsim ibn Aḥmad al-Baghdādī informed us: Abū ʿĀmir, from Zuhayr ibn Muḥammad, from Mūsā ibn Jubayr, from Abī Umāmah ibn Sahl ibn Ḥunayf, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with both of them, from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), who said: ""Leave the Abyssinians alone so long as they leave you alone, for indeed none shall extract the treasure of the Kaʿbah except the one with the slender shanks from among the Abyssinians."" [Ḥasan]"
18605,"(18599) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Bishr ibn Mūsā informed us: Khalaf, from Hushaym, from Sayyār ibn Abī Sayyār al-ʿAnazī.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī ʿAlī al-Saqqāʾ and Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ both said: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī informed us: Musaddad informed us: Hushaym, from Sayyār Abī al-Ḥakam, from Jabr ibn ʿUbaydah, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) promised us the campaign in India. He said: ""If I live to see it, I shall expend my wealth and my life therein. If I am martyred, I shall be among the most excellent of martyrs, and if I return, then I shall be Abū Hurayrah the freed.""

The Muqriʾ added in his narration: Then Musaddad said: I heard Ibn Dāwūd say: Abū Isḥāq al-Fazārī said: ""I wish I had witnessed Barbīd — every campaign I undertook in the lands of the Romans."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18606,"(18600) Abū Saʿd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Mālinī informed us: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Qutaybah and Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿĀṣim both said: Hishām ibn ʿAmmār informed us: al-Jarrāḥ ibn Malīḥ al-Baḥrānī informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Walīd al-Zubaydī, from Luqmān ibn ʿĀmir, from ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn ʿAdī al-Baḥrānī, from Thawbān, may Allah be pleased with him, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""Two groups from my Ummah shall be protected by Allah from the Fire: a group that campaigns in India, and a group that shall be with ʿĪsā ibn Maryam, peace be upon them both."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18607,"(18601) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us: al-Rabīʿ said: al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, said: Allah, Blessed and Exalted, said: ""It is He who sent His Messenger with guidance and the religion of truth, that He may make it prevail over all religion, even though the polytheists detest it."" [Al-Tawbah, 33] [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18608,"(18602) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Ibn ʿUyaynah, from al-Zuhrī, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""When Kisrā is destroyed, there shall be no Kisrā after him, and when Qayṣar is destroyed, there shall be no Qayṣar after him. By the One in whose Hand is my soul, you shall surely spend their treasures in the cause of Allah.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr al-Nāqid and others, from Sufyān; and al-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it from the ḥadīth of Yūnus and others, from al-Zuhrī; and they both also narrated it from the ḥadīth of Jābir ibn Samurah from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon.]"
18609,"(18603) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Jarīr, from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr, from Jābir ibn Samurah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), and he mentioned the ḥadīth with the same meaning as the ḥadīth of Abū Hurayrah.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah, from Jarīr.

And we have narrated concerning this, in the ḥadīth of ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), regarding Kisrā with the same meaning, and from another perspective regarding both Kisrā and Qayṣar with the same meaning."
18610,"(18604) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: al-Naḍr ibn Shumayl informed us: Isrāʾīl informed us: Saʿd al-Ṭāʾī informed us: Muḥill ibn Khalīfah, from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: While I was with the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), he mentioned a ḥadīth in which the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""If your life is prolonged, you shall surely conquer the treasures of Kisrā."" I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, Kisrā ibn Hurmuz?"" He said: ""Kisrā ibn Hurmuz."" ʿAdī said: ""And I was among those who conquered the treasures of Kisrā ibn Hurmuz.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥakam, from al-Naḍr ibn Shumayl. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18611,"(18605) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us, al-Rabīʿ reported, and al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: When Kisrā was brought the letter of the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family), he tore it apart. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) said: ""His kingdom shall be torn apart."" And we have preserved that Qayṣar honoured the letter of the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) and placed it in musk. The Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) said: ""His kingdom shall endure."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18612,"(18606) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ibn Milḥān informed us, Yaḥyā — and Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī informed us, Ibn Bukayr from al-Layth from ʿUqayl from Ibn Shihāb, who said: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah ibn Masʿūd informed me that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) sent a man with his letter to Kisrā and instructed him to deliver it to the governor of Baḥrayn. The governor of Baḥrayn would then deliver it to Kisrā. When Kisrā read it, he tore it to pieces. I think Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) then supplicated against them that they be torn to pieces, every last one of them. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2939]"
18613,"(18607) Abū Sahl Muḥammad ibn Naṣrūyah ibn Aḥmad al-Marwazī informed us — he came to us in Naysābūr — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Khanb informed us by dictation, Abū Isḥāq Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd from Ṣāliḥ ibn Kaysān from Ibn Shihāb from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) wrote to Qayṣar, calling him to Islam, and sent the letter with Dihyah al-Kalbī. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) instructed him to deliver it to the governor of Buṣrā, who would then deliver it to Qayṣar. The governor of Buṣrā delivered it to Qayṣar. When Allah had driven away the armies of Persia from Qayṣar, he walked from Ḥimṣ to Iliyā' in gratitude for what Allah had granted him. When the letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) reached Qayṣar, he said upon reading it: ""Seek for me someone from his people here, so that I may ask them about the Messenger of Allah."" Ibn ʿAbbās said: Abū Sufyān informed me that he was in Syria among a group of Quraysh. Abū Sufyān said: The messenger of Qayṣar found us, and he took me and my companions until we came to Iliyā'. We were brought before him, and lo, he was seated in his royal court, wearing his crown, surrounded by the nobles of the Romans. He said to his interpreter: ""Ask them: which of them is closest in lineage to this man who claims to be a prophet?"" Abū Sufyān said: I said: ""I am the closest of them to him in lineage."" He said: ""What is the relationship between you and him?"" I said: ""He is my paternal uncle's son."" There was no one from Banū ʿAbd Manāf in the caravan besides me that day. Qayṣar said: ""Bring him near to me."" Then he ordered my companions and they were made to stand behind me at my shoulders. Then he said to his interpreter: ""Tell his companions: I am going to ask this man about the one who claims to be a prophet. If he lies, then contradict him."" Abū Sufyān said: By Allah, were it not for the fear that my companions would attribute lies to me, I would have lied about him when he asked me about him. But I feared that they would attribute lies to me, so I spoke truthfully about him. Then he said to his interpreter: ""Ask him: what is the lineage of this man among you?"" I said: ""He is of noble lineage among us."" He said: ""Has anyone among you said this claim before him?"" I said: ""No."" He said: ""Did you ever accuse him of lying before he said what he said?"" I said: ""No."" He said: ""Was there any king among his forefathers?"" I said: ""No."" He said: ""Do the nobles of the people follow him, or their weak ones?"" I said: ""Rather, their weak ones."" He said: ""Are they increasing or decreasing?"" I said: ""Rather, they are increasing."" He said: ""Does anyone among them apostatize out of displeasure with his religion after entering it?"" I said: ""No."" He said: ""Does he break his pledge?"" I said: ""No. And we are currently in a period of truce with him, and we fear that he may betray us."" Abū Sufyān said: I was unable to insert any word by which to diminish him without fearing that something else would be attributed to me. He said: ""Have you fought him, and has he fought you?"" I said: ""Yes."" He said: ""How has your war with him been?"" I said: ""It has been alternating — sometimes he has the upper hand over us, and sometimes we have the upper hand over him."" He said: ""What does he command you to do?"" I said: ""He commands us to worship Allah alone and not associate anything with him. He forbids us from what our forefathers used to worship. He commands us to pray, to speak truthfully, to be chaste, to fulfil covenants, and to render trusts."" When I said that to him, he said to his interpreter: ""Tell him: I asked you about his lineage among you, and you claimed he is of noble lineage. And so the Messengers are sent from the noble lineage of their people. I asked you: has anyone among you made this claim before him, and you claimed no. I would say: if anyone among you had said this before him, I would say he is a man following a statement that was said before him. I asked you: did you ever accuse him of lying before he said what he said, and you claimed no. I knew that he would not leave lying against people to lie against Allah. I asked you: was there any king among his forefathers, and you claimed no. I would say: if there were a king among his forefathers, I would say he is seeking the kingdom of his forefathers. I asked you: do the nobles of the people follow him, or their weak ones, and you claimed that their weak ones followed him. And those are the followers of the Messengers. I asked you: are they increasing or decreasing, and you claimed they are increasing. And so is faith, until it is complete. I asked you: does anyone among them apostatize out of displeasure with his religion after entering it, and you claimed no. And so is faith, when its joy mingles with the hearts, no one is displeased with it. I asked you: does he betray, and you claimed no. And so the Messengers do not betray. I asked you: have you fought him, and has he fought you, and you claimed yes, and that your war with him is alternating — sometimes you have the upper hand, and sometimes he has the upper hand over you. And so the Messengers are tried, and the final outcome is theirs. I asked you: what does he command you to do, and you claimed he commands you to worship Allah and not associate anything with him, and he forbids you from what your forefathers used to worship, and he commands you to pray, to speak truthfully, to be chaste, to fulfil covenants, and to render trusts. And these are the qualities of a prophet. I already knew that he would appear, but I did not think that he would be from among you. And if what you have said is true, he will soon possess the place of these two feet of mine. And if I could reach him, I would undertake the journey to meet him, and were I in his presence, I would wash his feet."" Abū Sufyān said: Then he called for the letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) and ordered it to be read. In it was: ""In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. From Muḥammad, the servant of Allah and His Messenger, to Hirqal, the sovereign of the Romans. Peace be upon whoever follows guidance. Thereafter, I invite you with the invitation of Islam. Embrace Islam and you will be safe. Allah will grant you your reward twice over. And if you turn away, then upon you will be the sin of the Arīsiyyīn. {O People of the Book, come to a word common between us and you: that we worship none but Allah, and that we associate nothing with Him, and that some of us take not others as lords besides Allah. And if they turn away, then say: Bear witness that we are Muslims.} [Āl ʿImrān: 64]"" Abū Sufyān said: When he finished his speech, the voices of those around him from the nobles of the Romans rose, and their clamour increased. I do not know what they said. We were ordered to leave, and we were brought out. When I went out with my companions and was alone with them, I said to them: ""Indeed, the matter of Ibn Abī Kabshah has reached the point where the king of Banī al-Aṣfar fears him."" Abū Sufyān said: By Allah, I remained in a state of humiliation, certain that his affair would prevail, until Allah caused Islam to enter my heart while I was averse to it. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah, and Muslim narrated it from another chain from Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd. Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) dispatched an army to Syria at the time of its conquest, in accordance with the statement of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family). Parts of it were conquered, and its complete conquest was accomplished during the time of ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him). And ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) conquered Iraq and Persia. The Sheikh said: What al-Shāfiʿī mentioned here is well-established in the historical chronicles, and the narration of those accounts would make the book excessively long. Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Allah, exalted be His praise, manifested the religion with which He sent His Messenger (peace be upon him and his family) over all other religions, making clear to everyone who heard it that it is the truth, and that whatever contradicts it among the religions is false. He manifested it by the fact that the totality of polytheism comprises two religions: the religion of the People of the Book and the religion of the Ummiyyīn. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) subdued the Ummiyyīn until they embraced Islam willingly and unwillingly, and he fought among the People of the Book and took captives until some of them embraced Islam and some paid the jizyah in a state of submission. His judgment (peace be upon him and his family) was enforced upon them. And this is the complete manifestation of the religion. Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: And it may be said: Allah will manifest His religion over all religions until none is worshipped except by His command, and that is whenever Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, wills. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
18614,"(18608) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us, Yūnus ibn Bukayr from Ibn ʿAwn from ʿUmayr ibn Isḥāq, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) wrote to Kisrā and Qayṣar. As for Qayṣar, he placed it with honour. As for Kisrā, he tore it apart. This reached the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family), and he said: ""As for these, they shall be torn apart. As for those, there shall be continuity for them."" Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) promised the people the conquest of Persia and Syria. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18615,"(18609) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Darastawayh informed us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Ḥamzah informed me, Abū ʿAlqamah Naṣr ibn ʿAlqamah, tracing the hadith back to Jubayr ibn Nufayr, who said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ḥawālah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) and we complained to him of nakedness, poverty, and scarcity of provisions. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) said: ""Rejoice! By Allah, abundance of provisions frightens me for you more than their scarcity. By Allah, this affair will continue among you until Allah conquers the land of Persia, the land of the Romans, and the land of Ḥimyar, until you are three armies: an army in Syria, an army in Iraq, and an army in Yemen, until a man is given a hundred and is displeased with it."" Ibn Ḥawālah said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, who can overcome Syria when the Romans, the horned ones, are in it?"" He said: ""By Allah, Allah will certainly conquer it for you and will certainly make you successors in it, until the white-clad group among them, wearing their mail coats, stand with their backs shaven, obedient to the black-haired man among you, doing whatever he commands them. And there are men there today who, in your eyes, are more contemptible than ticks on the hindquarters of camels."" Ibn Ḥawālah said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, choose for me, if that time reaches me."" He said: ""I choose for you Syria, for it is the chosen land of Allah among His lands, and to it He selects His chosen servants. O people of Yemen, hold fast to Syria, for the chosen land of Allah among His lands is Syria. And whoever refuses, let him drink from the pools of Yemen, for Allah has guaranteed me Syria and its people."" Abū ʿAlqamah said: I heard ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jubayr say: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) recognized the description in this hadith in Jazʿ ibn Suhayl al-Sulamī, who was in command of the non-Arabs at that time. When they came to a mosque, they would look at him and at those standing around him, and they marveled at the description of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) regarding him and them. Abū ʿAlqamah said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) swore three oaths in this hadith — we do not know that he swore in any other hadith like it. And there has preceded in this book from Ibn Zughb al-Iyādī from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ḥawālah from the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family): ""Syria will certainly be opened for you, and then you will divide the treasures of Persia and the Romans."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18616,"(18610) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us, Yūnus ibn Bukayr from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Yāsār, in the account of Khālid ibn al-Walīd when he had finished with al-Yamāmah, said: Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to Khālid ibn al-Walīd while he was in al-Yamāmah: ""From ʿAbd Allāh Abī Bakr, the successor of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family), to Khālid ibn al-Walīd and those with him among the Muhājirīn, the Anṣār, and those who follow them with excellence. Peace be upon you. I praise to you Allah, there is no deity but He. Thereafter, all praise is due to Allah who fulfilled His promise, aided His servant, honoured His ally, humbled His enemy, and defeated the confederates single-handedly. For Allah — there is no deity but He — said: {Allah has promised those among you who believe and do righteous deeds that He will surely grant them succession in the earth, as He granted it to those before them, and that He will surely establish for them their religion which He has chosen for them.} [Al-Nūr: 55] He wrote the entire verse and recited it — a promise from Him in which there is no breaking, and a statement in which there is no doubt. And He has made jihād obligatory upon the believers, saying: {Fighting has been prescribed for you, though it is hateful to you.} [Al-Baqarah: 216] Until He finished the verses. So fulfil the promise of Allah to you, and obey Him in what He has prescribed for you, even if the burden is heavy, the calamity severe, the journey distant, and you are afflicted in that with loss of wealth and lives. For that is a small matter in light of the immense reward of Allah. Go forth, may Allah have mercy on you, in the cause of Allah — {light and heavy, and strive with your wealth and your lives.} [Al-Tawbah: 41] He wrote the verse. And know that I have ordered Khālid ibn al-Walīd to march to Iraq. Let him not leave it until my command reaches him. So march with him and do not lag behind him, for it is a path through which Allah magnifies the reward for whoever has a sound intention therein and whose desire for good is great. When you reach Iraq, remain there until my command reaches you. Allah is sufficient for us and for you against the hardships of this world and the Hereafter. Peace be upon you, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings."" The Sheikh said: It is then well-established in the historical chronicles that his letter reached him ordering him to march to Syria, and that he reinforced the commanders of the armies there with support. And there came the victory for the Muslims at the Battle of Ajnādayn during the time of Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him), and the departure of Hirqal heading toward the Romans, and the conquests that followed in Syria, Iraq, and the land of Persia, the destruction of Kisrā, and the transport of his treasures to Madīnah during the time of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him). [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18617,"(18611) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us, Abū Manṣūr al-Naḍrawī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Najdah informed us, Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr informed us, ʿAmr ibn Thābit from his father from Abū Jaʿfar from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, regarding the words of the Most High: {That He may cause it to prevail over all religion.} [Al-Tawbah: 33] He said: ""This refers to the emergence of ʿĪsā ibn Maryam (peace be upon him)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18618,"(18612) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn informed us, Ādam ibn Abī Iyās informed us, Warqā' from Ibn Abī Najīḥ from Mujāhid, regarding the words of the Mighty and Majestic: {Until the war lays down its burdens.} [Muḥammad: 4] He said: ""That is, until ʿĪsā ibn Maryam descends, and every Jew, every Christian, and every adherent of a religion embraces Islam. The sheep will be safe from the wolf, and the mouse will not tear a bag. Enmity will depart from all things. And that is the supremacy of Islam over all religion."" [Ḥasan]"
18619,"(18613) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Isfara'īnī, Ibn al-Saqqā', informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Buṭṭah informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Zakariyyā informed us, Saʿīd ibn Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Umawī informed us, Muslim ibn Khālid from Ibn Abī Najīḥ from Mujāhid, regarding the words of the Most High: {That He may cause it to prevail over all religion, even if the polytheists detest it.} [Al-Tawbah: 33] He said: ""When ʿĪsā ibn Maryam descends, there will be nothing on the earth except Islam, that He may cause it to prevail over all religion."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18620,"(18614) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me, Mūsā — he is Ibn al-ʿAbbās al-Juwaynī — informed us, Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Dhuhlī informed us, Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, my father from Ṣāliḥ from Ibn Shihāb, that Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab heard Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) said: ""By the One in whose hand is my soul, the son of Maryam will soon descend among you as a just ruler. He will break the cross, kill the swine, abolish the jizyah, and wealth will overflow until no one will accept it. And a single prostration will be better than the world and all that is in it."" Then Abū Hurayrah said: ""Read, if you wish: {And there is none of the People of the Book but will believe in him before his death, and on the Day of Resurrection he will be a witness against them.} [Al-Nisā': 159]"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq, and Muslim narrated it from al-Ḥulwānī and others from Yaʿqūb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
18621,"(18615) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Ṣādiq al-Ṣaydalānī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, saying: Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, saying: Ibn Jurayj said: Abū al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh (may Allah be pleased with him) say: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: ""A group from my Ummah will continue fighting for the truth, prevailing, until the Day of Resurrection."" He said: ""Then ʿĪsā ibn Maryam will descend, and their leader will say: 'Come and lead us in prayer.' He will say: 'No, indeed some of you are leaders over others — as an honour from Allah to this Ummah.'"" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Walīd ibn Shuʿjāʿ and others, from Ḥajjāj. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 156]"
18622,"(18616) Al-Sayyid Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Qāsim ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Bālawayh al-Muzakkī narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us, saying: Maʿmar narrated from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: This is what Abū Hurayrah narrated to us, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""The Hour will not come until the sun rises from the west. When it rises and the people see it, they will all believe — but that will be the time when {no soul will benefit from its faith if it had not believed before, or earned some good through its faith} [al-Anʿām 158]."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ — both from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
18623,"(18617) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAlī Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, saying: Muʿādh ibn Hishām narrated to us, saying: My father narrated from Qatādah, from Abū Qilābah, from Abū Asmāʾ, from Thawbān (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, folded the earth for me until I saw its east and west, and He gave me the two treasures — the red and the white. The dominion of my Ummah will reach what was folded for me of it. I asked my Lord, the Mighty and Majestic, not to destroy them by a general famine, not to set upon them an enemy from outside them who would annihilate them, and not to make them into factions inflicting suffering upon one another. He said: 'O Muhammad, when I give a gift, none can take it back. I have granted your Ummah that they will not be destroyed by a general famine, and I will not set upon them an enemy from outside them who would exterminate them — even if all from the far corners of the earth were to gather against them — until some of them destroy one another, some of them take captives from one another, and some of them cause tribulation for one another. Indeed, tribes from my Ummah will return to shirk and the worship of idols. The most fearful thing I fear for them is misguiding imāms. When the sword is placed among them, it will not be lifted until the Day of Resurrection. There will emerge in my Ummah liars and impostors — close to thirty of them. I am the Seal of the Prophets; there is no prophet after me. A group from my Ummah will remain upon the truth, victorious, until the command of Allah comes.'"" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb and others, from Muʿādh ibn Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
18624,"(18618) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqriʾ, Abū Bakr al-Qāḍī, and Abū Ṣādiq ibn Abī al-Fawāris narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Mazyad narrated to us, saying: My father informed me, saying: I heard Ibn Jābir narrate from Sulaym ibn ʿĀmir, who said: al-Miqdād ibn al-Aswad al-Kindī (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: ""There will not remain upon the face of the earth any settlement of brick or hair-tent but that Allah will cause the word of Islam to enter it — either through the honour of the honoured or the humiliation of the humiliated. Either Allah will honour them and make them among its people, so they are honoured by it, or He will humiliate them, so they submit to it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18625,"(18619) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Azhar narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Quddūs Abū al-Mughīrah narrated to us, saying: Ṣafwān ibn ʿAmr narrated to me, saying: Sulaym ibn ʿĀmir narrated to me.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, saying: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Yamān narrated to us, saying: Ṣafwān narrated from Sulaym ibn ʿĀmir al-Kalāʿī, from Tamīm al-Dārī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: ""This matter will certainly reach wherever the night reaches. Allah will not leave any settlement of brick or hair-tent but that He will bring this religion into it — through the honour of the honoured, by which He honours Islam, or through the humiliation of the humiliated, by which He humiliates disbelief."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18626,"(18620) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Sinān al-Qazzāz narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ḥumrān narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd ibn Jaʿfar narrated from al-Aswad ibn al-ʿAlāʾ, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, who said: I heard ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: ""The night and the day will not cease until al-Lāt and al-ʿUzzā are worshipped."" I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I had thought that when Allah revealed {It is He who sent His Messenger with guidance and the religion of truth to make it prevail over all religion} [al-Tawbah 33], that it was complete."" He said: ""That which Allah wills will come to pass. Then Allah will send a gentle wind, and He will cause to die everyone in whose heart there is the weight of a mustard seed of faith. Only those in whom there is no good will remain, and they will return to the religion of their forefathers.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Khālid ibn al-Ḥārith and Abū Bakr al-Ḥanafī, from ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd ibn Jaʿfar.

Imām al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: The Quraysh used to travel frequently to al-Shām, and much of their livelihood came from there, and they would also go to al-ʿIrāq. When they entered Islam, they mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) their fear of the disruption of their livelihood through trade with al-Shām and al-ʿIrāq if they left disbelief and entered Islam, given the opposition of the rulers of al-Shām and al-ʿIrāq to the people of Islam. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""When Kisrā is destroyed, there will be no Kisrā after him."" And so there was no Kisrā in the land of al-ʿIrāq whose authority endured after him. And he said: ""When Qayṣar is destroyed, there will be no Qayṣar after him."" And so there was no Qayṣar in the land of al-Shām after him. He answered them regarding what they had said to him, and it was as he had told them (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). Allah severed the Chosroes from al-ʿIrāq and Persia, and Qayṣar and whoever succeeded him in authority from al-Shām. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said regarding Kisrā: ""His kingdom will be torn apart."" And so the Chosroes had no kingdom remaining. And he said regarding Qayṣar: ""His kingdom will endure."" And so his kingdom endured in the lands of the Romans until this day, while his dominion was removed from al-Shām. All of this is in agreement, some of it confirming the other. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
18627,"(18621) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us, saying: al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, saying: al-Shāfiʿī narrated this discourse and what preceded it in this chapter.

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: Another tafsīr of the verse has been narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās."
18628,"(18622) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated it, saying: Abū al-Ḥasan al-Ṭarāʾifī narrated to us, saying: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ, from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭalḥah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), regarding the words of the Most High: {to make it prevail over all religion} [al-Tawbah: 33]. He said: ""Allah will make His Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) prevail over all matters of religion and grant it to him, concealing nothing from it. The Jews and the polytheists disliked that."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18629,"(18623) Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us, saying: Yūnus ibn Yazīd informed me from Ibn Shihāb, who said: Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab narrated to me that Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""I have been commanded to fight the people until they say: 'There is no deity but Allah.' Whoever says 'There is no deity but Allah' has protected from me his wealth and his person, except by its right. His reckoning is with Allah.""

Muslim narrated it from Abū al-Ṭāhir and others, from Ibn Wahb, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through other chains from al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
18630,"(18624) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us, saying: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Bakhtarī al-Razzāz narrated to us, saying: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, saying: Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, saying: al-Aʿmash narrated from Abū Sufyān, from Jābir, and from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with them both), who both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""I have been commanded to fight the people until they say: 'There is no deity but Allah.' When they say it, they have protected from me their blood and their wealth, except by its right. Their reckoning is with Allah.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth, from al-Aʿmash, with both chains of narration together. [Ṣaḥīḥ — as previously mentioned]"
18631,"(18625) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Nawfal from a man from Muzaynah known as Ibn ʿIṣām, from his father, that the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) would say when he dispatched a military expedition: ""When you hear a mu'adhdhin or see a mosque, do not kill anyone."" [Weak]"
18632,"(18626) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm al-Muzzakkī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Layth narrated from ʿUqayl from al-Zuhrī, who said: ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUtbah ibn Masʿūd informed me from Abū Hurayrah (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said: When the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) passed away and Abū Bakr (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) was appointed as khalīfah after him, and those among the Arabs who disbelieved did so, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb said to Abū Bakr (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā): ""How can you fight the people when the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: 'I have been commanded to fight the people until they say: ""Lā ilāha illallāh."" Whoever says ""Lā ilāha illallāh"" has protected from me his wealth and his life, except by its right, and his reckoning is with Allāh.'"" Abū Bakr (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) said: ""By Allāh, I shall surely fight whoever separates between Ṣalāh and Zakāh, for Zakāh is the right of wealth. By Allāh, if they withhold from me a hobbling cord that they used to give to the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), I shall fight them for withholding it."" ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) said: ""By Allāh, it was nothing but that I saw Allāh had expanded the breast of Abū Bakr (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) for fighting, and I knew that it was the truth.""

Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah. [Sound — Agreed upon]"
18633,"(18627) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ narrated, who said: al-Shāfiʿī (raḥimahullāh) said: ""And this is like the two preceding ḥadīths regarding the polytheists in general terms. What is intended thereby — and Allāh knows best — are the polytheists who worship idols. There was no one from among the People of the Book who were polytheists in the presence of the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) or near him, except the Jews in Madīnah, and they were allies of the Anṣār. The Anṣār had not collectively embraced Islām when the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) first arrived; rather, they made a truce with the Jews and did not manifest any hostility toward him in word or deed until the Battle of Badr. Then some of them spoke of their hostility and incitement against him, so the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) fought them. There was no one in the Ḥijāz, to my knowledge, except a few Jews or Christians in Najrān. The Magians were in Baḥrayn and the lands of the Berbers and Persia, far from the Ḥijāz, and below them were many polytheists who worshipped idols."" [Sound]"
18634,"(18628) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us: Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham narrated to us: Abū al-Yamān narrated to us: Shuʿayb narrated from al-Zuhrī, who informed me: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik — I think from his father, who was one of the three upon whom repentance was accepted — that Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf, the Jew, was a poet who used to satirize the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and incite the disbelievers of Quraysh against him in his poetry. When the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) came to Madīnah, its people were a mixed group: among them were the Muslims who were united by the call of the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), among them were the polytheists who worshipped idols, and among them were the Jews, who were the people of armor and fortresses, and they were allies of the two tribes, al-Aws and al-Khazraj. When the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) arrived in Madīnah, he sought to reconcile all of them. A man would be a Muslim while his father was a polytheist, and a man would be a Muslim while his brother was a polytheist. The polytheists and the Jews among the people of Madīnah, when the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) arrived, used to harm the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and his Companions with the severest harm. Allāh commanded His Messenger and the Muslims to be patient over that and to forgive them. Concerning them, Allāh — exalted be His praise — revealed: ""And you will certainly hear much abuse from those who were given the Scripture before you and from those who associate partners with Allāh"" (Āl ʿImrān, 186) to the end of the verse. And concerning them, Allāh — exalted be His praise — revealed: ""Many of the People of the Book wish they could turn you back to disbelief after you have believed, out of envy from themselves, after the truth has become clear to them. So forgive and overlook"" (al-Baqarah, 109). When Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf refused to desist from harming the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and the Muslims, the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) commanded Saʿd ibn Muʿādh (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) to send a group to kill him. So Saʿd ibn Muʿādh sent Muḥammad ibn Maslamah al-Anṣārī, Abū ʿAbs al-Anṣārī, and al-Ḥārith, the nephew of Saʿd ibn Muʿādh, in a group of five, and he mentioned the ḥadīth regarding his killing. He said: ""When they killed him, the Jews and those polytheists who were with them were terrified, and they came to the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) in the morning and said: 'Our chief was attacked tonight and he was killed.' The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) reminded them of what he used to say in his poetry and warned them against it. The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) then called them to write a treaty between him, them, and the Muslims, to which they would all adhere. So the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) wrote between him, them, and the Muslims a document for one year, which the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) wrote beneath the palm tree in the house of the daughter of al-Ḥārith. That document remained after the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) in the custody of ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu)."" [Weak]"
18635,"(18629) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who narrated to me: Muḥammad ibn Abī Muḥammad, the freedman of Zayd ibn Thābit, narrated from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr or ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā), who said: ""When the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) defeated Quraysh on the day of Badr and returned to Madīnah, he gathered the Jews in the market of Banī Qaynuqāʿ and said: 'O assembly of Jews, embrace Islāh before that befalls you which befell Quraysh.' They said: 'O Muḥammad, let not your soul deceive you, for you have killed a group of Quraysh who were inexperienced in fighting. Had you fought us, you would have known that we are truly the people, and you have never encountered the like of us.' So Allāh, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed concerning that, from their statement: 'Say to those who disbelieve: You will be gathered to Hell, and wretched is the resting place. There has already been a sign for you in the two groups that met' (Āl ʿImrān, 12–13) — the Companions of the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) at Badr — 'and the other group disbelieving, who saw them as twice their number with the naked eye' (Āl ʿImrān, 13) — to His words: 'Indeed in that is a lesson for those of vision' (Āl ʿImrān, 13)."" [Weak]"
18636,"(18630) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: Aḥmad narrated to us: Yūnus narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who narrated to me: ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr ibn Ḥazm and Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Umāmah ibn Sahl ibn Ḥunayf both said: ""When the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) finished with Badr, he sent two heralds to the people of Madīnah: Zayd ibn Ḥārithah and ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah. When this reached Kaʿb ibn al-Ashraf, he said: 'Woe to you! Is it true that these are the kings of the Arabs and the chiefs of the people' — meaning the slain of Quraysh? Then he went out to Makkah and began to weep over the slain of Quraysh and to incite against the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam)."" [Weak]"
18637,"(18631) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān al-Anbārī narrated to us: Wakīʿ narrated from Sufyān from ʿAlqamah ibn Marthad from Sulaymān ibn Buraydah, from his father, who said: ""The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) would, when he appointed a commander over an expedition or an army, counsel him with taqwā of Allāh regarding his own self and with goodness toward those Muslims who were with him. And he would say: 'When you meet your enemy from among the polytheists, call them to one of three qualities — whichever of them they respond to, accept it from them and refrain from them. Call them to Islām; if they respond, accept it from them and refrain from them. Then call them to move from their abode to the abode of the Muhājirūn, and inform them that if they do so, they shall have what the Muhājirūn have and upon them shall be what is upon the Muhājirūn. If they refuse and choose their own abode, inform them that they shall be like the Bedouin Muslims, upon whom the ruling of Allāh that applies to the believers shall apply, and they shall have no share in the fayʾ or the spoils unless they strive alongside the Muslims. If they refuse, call them to pay the jizyah; if they respond, accept it from them and refrain from them. If they refuse, then seek help from Allāh and fight them. And when you besiege the people of a fortress and they ask you to grant them the ruling of Allāh, do not grant them the ruling of Allāh, for you do not know what Allāh will rule concerning them. Rather, grant them your own ruling, then judge among them afterward as you wish.'""

Sufyān said: ʿAlqamah said: ""I mentioned this ḥadīth to Muqātil ibn Ḥayyān, and he said: 'Muslim — who is ibn Hayyṣam — narrated to me from al-Nuʿmān ibn Muqarrin (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), a ḥadīth similar to that of Sulaymān ibn Buraydah.'""

[Sound — Muslim, 1737]"
18638,"(18632) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Mihrān narrated to us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us: Sufyān —

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us: Sufyān al-Thawrī from ʿAlqamah ibn Marthad from Sulaymān ibn Buraydah, from his father, who said: ""The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) would, when he appointed a commander over an army, counsel him,"" and he mentioned the ḥadīth, adding in it: ""And when you besiege the people of a fortress and they ask you to grant them the protection of Allāh and the protection of His Prophet, do not grant them the protection of Allāh and the protection of His Prophet. Rather, grant them your own protection and the protection of your fathers and the protection of your Companions, for if you break your protection and the protection of your fathers, it is lighter upon you than if you break the protection of Allāh and the protection of His Messenger.""

And he did not mention the isnād of Muqātil's ḥadīth.

Muslim recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah from Wakīʿ, without the isnād of Muqātil, and he recorded it from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm from Yaḥyā ibn Ādam, and he mentioned in it the isnād of Muqātil. [Sound — as previously cited]"
18639,"(18633) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wārith ibn ʿAbd al-Ṣamad narrated to me: My father narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to me: ʿAlqamah ibn Marthad narrated that Sulaymān ibn Buraydah al-Aslamī narrated to him from his father, who said: ""The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) would, when he appointed a commander over an army or an expedition, summon him and counsel him regarding his own self and those Muslims who were with him,"" and he mentioned the ḥadīth with its addition in the text.

Muslim recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥajjāj ibn al-Shāʿir from ʿAbd al-Ṣamad. [Sound — as previously cited]"
18640,"(18634) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us in Baghdād: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī narrated to us: Rūḥ ibn al-Faraj narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to me: al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated from Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim from Shuʿbah ibn al-Ḥajjāj, and he mentioned it."
18641,"(18635) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Muḥammad al-Muzanī informed me — and Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us that Abū Muḥammad Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq al-Harawī informed us, both saying that ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā informed us that Abū al-Yamān informed us that Shuʿayb informed us from al-Zuhrī that Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed him that Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, sent me among those who were to make the proclamation on the Day of Sacrifice at Minā that no polytheist should perform Ḥajj after that year, and that no one should circumambulate the House naked, and that the Day of the Greater Ḥajj is the Day of Sacrifice. It was called the Greater Ḥajj only because of the people's saying 'the Lesser Ḥajj' (ʿUmrah)."" Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, made the proclamation to the people that year, and no polytheist performed Ḥajj the following year, the year in which the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) performed the Farewell Pilgrimage. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed in the year in which Abū Bakr proclaimed to the polytheists: *""O you who believe, indeed the polytheists are unclean, so let them not approach al-Masjid al-Ḥarām""* [al-Tawbah: 28] until His words: *""All-Knowing, All-Wise.""* The polytheists used to come with trade from which the Muslims benefited. When Allah forbade the polytheists from approaching al-Masjid al-Ḥarām, the Muslims felt within themselves about the trade that had been cut off from them, which the polytheists used to bring. So Allah, the Most High, said: *""And if you fear poverty, then Allah will enrich you from His bounty, if He wills""* [al-Tawbah: 28]. Then Allah permitted in the following verse the jizyah, which had not been taken before that, and made it a compensation for what He had prevented them of the polytheists' trade. So He said: *""Fight those who do not believe in Allah nor in the Last Day, nor forbid what Allah and His Messenger have forbidden, nor follow the religion of truth, from among those who were given the Scripture, until they pay the jizyah willingly while they are humbled""* [al-Tawbah: 29]. When Allah made this permissible for the Muslims, they knew that He had given them something better than what they had found from the trade that the polytheists used to bring.

al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān up to his words ""no polytheist performed Ḥajj"" without what comes after it, and I think it is from the words of al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ up to his words ""naked.""]"
18642,"(18636) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us that Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn informed us that Ādam ibn Abī Iyās informed us that Warqāʾ informed us from Ibn Abī Najīḥ from Mujāhid regarding His words: *""Fight those who do not believe in Allah nor in the Last Day""* [al-Tawbah: 29] until His words: *""until they pay the jizyah willingly while they are humbled""* [al-Tawbah: 29]. He said: ""This was revealed when the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and his Companions were commanded with the expedition to Tabūk."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18643,"(18637) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us that Yūnus ibn Bukayr informed us from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: ""When the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) reached Tabūk, Yuḥannah ibn Rubāh, the master of Aylah, came to him and made peace with the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and paid the jizyah. And the people of Jarbāʾ and Adhruḥ came and paid the jizyah."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18644,"(18638) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAmr ibn Ḥamdān informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah informed us that Wakīʿ informed us that Fuḍayl ibn ʿIyāḍ informed us from Layth from Mujāhid, who said: ""The worshippers of idols are fought until they accept Islam, and the People of the Book are fought until they pay the jizyah."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18645,"(18639) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq informed us that Wahb ibn Jarīr informed us from Shuʿbah from Abī Bishr from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding the words of Allah, the Most High: *""There is no compulsion in religion""* [al-Baqarah: 256]. He said: ""A woman from the Anṣār would scarcely have a child survive, so she would swear that if a child of hers lived, she would make him a Jew. When Banū al-Naḍīr were expelled, there were among them some sons of the Anṣār. The Anṣār said: 'O Messenger of Allah, our sons!' So Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: *'There is no compulsion in religion'* [al-Baqarah: 256]."" Saʿīd ibn Jubayr said: ""Whoever wished could join them, and whoever wished could enter Islam."" Abū Dāwūd narrated it in the Sunan through various chains from Shuʿbah."
18646,"(18640) Abū ʿAwānah narrated it from Abī Bishr, and he sent it as a mursal report. Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us that Abū Manṣūr al-Naḍrawī informed us that Aḥmad ibn Najdah informed us that Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr informed us that Abū ʿAwānah informed us from Abī Bishr from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr regarding the words of Allah, the Most High: *""There is no compulsion in religion""* [al-Baqarah: 256]. He said: ""It was revealed concerning the Anṣār."" I said: ""Specifically?"" He said: ""Specifically. A woman among them, if she was barren or had few children, would vow that if she bore a child, she would place him among the Jews, seeking thereby the child's long life. Then Islam came, and among them were such people. When Banū al-Naḍīr were expelled, the Anṣār said: 'O Messenger of Allah, our sons and brothers are among them!' The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) was silent concerning them, so *'There is no compulsion in religion'* [al-Baqarah: 256] was revealed. The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) then said: 'Your companions have been given a choice. If they choose you, then they are yours, and if they choose them, then expel them with them.'"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18647,"(18641) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad informed us that Sahl ibn ʿUthmān al-ʿAskarī informed us that Yaḥyā ibn Zakariyyā informed us that Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us from ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar from Anas ibn Mālik and from ʿUthmān ibn Abī Sulaymān that the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) sent Khālid ibn al-Walīd to Ukaydir of Dūmah. They captured him and brought him to him, so he spared his blood and made peace with him upon the payment of the jizyah. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18648,"(18642) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us that Yūnus ibn Bukayr informed us from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Yazīd ibn Rūmān and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr informed me that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) sent Khālid ibn al-Walīd to Ukaydir ibn ʿAbd al-Malik, a man from Kindah who was the king of Dūmah and who was a Christian. The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said to Khālid: ""You will find him hunting wild oxen."" So Khālid set out until, on a clear, moonlit night, he was within sight of his fortress. He was on his rooftop with his wife. The wild oxen came and rubbed their horns against the gate of the fortress. His wife said to him: ""Have you ever seen the like of this?"" He said: ""No, by Allah."" She said: ""Who would leave the like of this?"" He said: ""No one."" So he descended and ordered his horse to be brought and saddled. He rode out accompanied by a group of his household, among whom was his brother Ḥassān. They went out with their lances, and the cavalry of the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) met them and captured them. They killed his brother Ḥassān, who was wearing a brocade cloak embroidered with gold. Khālid ibn al-Walīd stripped it from him and sent it to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) before his arrival. Then Khālid brought Ukaydir to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), who spared his blood and made peace with him upon the jizyah, and set him free. He returned to his village.

Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) took the jizyah from the dhimmīs of Yemen, most of whom were Arabs, and from the people of Najrān, among whom were Arabs."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18649,"(18643) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us that Yaḥyā ibn Ādam informed us that Abū Bakr ibn ʿAyyāsh informed us from ʿĀṣim from Abī Wāʾil from Masrūq from Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) sent me to Yemen and commanded me to take from every person who has reached puberty one dinar, or its equivalent in Maʿāfirī garments.""

Yaḥyā ibn Ādam said: ""This jizyah was levied upon the people of Yemen, who were a people of Arabs, because they were People of the Book. Do you not see that he said: 'Let no Jew be tested through his Judaism' — meaning in his narration from Jarīr from Manṣūr from al-Ḥakam from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) that he wrote to Muʿādh ibn Jabal to that effect."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18650,"(18644) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us that Abū Dāwūd informed us that Muṣarrif ibn ʿAmr al-Yāmī informed us that Yūnus ibn Bukayr informed us that Asbāṭ ibn Naṣr al-Hamdānī informed us from Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Qurashī from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) made peace with the people of Najrān upon two thousand sets of garments."" And he mentioned the hadith. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18651,"(18645) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān said: al-Shāfiʿī said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) took the jizyah from Ukidir al-Ghassānī, and it is narrated that he made peace with some Arab tribes on the condition of jizyah. As for ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) and those caliphs who came after him up to this day, they took the jizyah from Banī Taghlib, Tanūkh, Bahrāʾ, and various Arab groups, and they remain to this hour upon Christianity. The ṣadaqah (charitable levy) is doubled upon them, and that is the jizyah. The jizyah is imposed on the basis of religion, not lineage. Were it not that we would incur sin by wishing for something false, I would have wished that what Abū Yūsuf said had been said, and that no humiliation (ṣaghār) be imposed upon any Arab. But Allah is more exalted in our eyes than that we should love other than what He has decreed."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18652,"(18646) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us that Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Abī Bukayr narrated to us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar al-Qurashī narrated to me that Saʿīd ibn ʿAmr ibn Saʿīd said: ""I heard my father on the day of al-Marj saying: 'I heard my father saying: I heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: Had I not heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) saying: ""Indeed Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, will protect the religion through the Christians of Rabīʿah on the banks of the Euphrates,"" I would not have left any Arab except that I would have killed him or he would have embraced Islam.'"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18653,"(18647) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us that Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq regarding the account of Khālid ibn al-Walīd's arrival at al-Ḥīrah from the direction of Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him), and the discourse between Hānīʾ ibn Qabīṣah and him. Khālid said: ""I call you to Islam, to bear witness that there is no god but Allah alone, and that Muḥammad is His servant and Messenger, to establish the prayer, to give the zakāh, and to submit to the rulings of the Muslims, on the condition that you shall have the same rights as they and upon you shall be the same obligations as upon them."" Hānīʾ said: ""And if I do not wish that?"" He said: ""Then if you refuse that, you shall pay the jizyah with willing submission."" He said: ""And if we refuse that?"" He said: ""Then if you refuse that, I will attack you with a people to whom death is more beloved than life is to you."" Hānīʾ said: ""Grant us this night so that we may consider our affair."" He said: ""I have done so."" When the people arose in the morning, Hānīʾ came and said: ""Indeed, we have agreed upon paying the jizyah. Come, so that I may make terms with you."" Khālid said to him: ""How can that be, when you are an Arab people, and you consider jizyah and humiliation more beloved to you than fighting and honour?"" He said: ""We considered those of us who would be killed, and they would never return, and we considered the wealth that would be taken from us, and Allah would soon replace it for us."" So Khālid made peace with them upon ninety thousand."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18654,"(18648) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us that Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Abī Suwayd narrated to us that Mūsā ibn Masʿūd al-Nahdī narrated to us that Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated—

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us that Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated from Sufyān, from al-Aʿmash, from Yaḥyā ibn ʿUmārah, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) visited Abū Ṭālib while there were people from Quraysh with him, and at his head was a man's seat. When Abū Jahl saw him, he stood and sat in it. He said: 'Your nephew mentions our gods.' Abū Ṭālib said: 'What is the matter with your people that they complain about you?' He said: 'O uncle, I want them to accept a word by which the Arabs will submit to them and the non-Arabs will pay them the jizyah.' He said: 'What is it?' He said: 'The testimony that there is no god but Allah.' They stood and said: 'Has he made the gods into one god?' And there was revealed: '{Ṣād. By the Qurʾan that contains the reminder} until He reached: {Indeed, this is a strange thing}' [Ṣād 1–5]."" The wording is from the ḥadīth of al-Muqriʾ."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18655,"(18649) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr and Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī narrated to us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rajāʾ informed us that ʿImrān informed us from Qatādah, from Abū al-Malīḥ, from Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""The scrolls of Ibrāhīm were revealed on the first night of Ramaḍān, the Tawrāh was revealed on the sixth of Ramaḍān, the Injīl was revealed on the thirteenth of Ramaḍān, the Zabūr was revealed on the eighteenth of Ramaḍān, and the Qurʾān was revealed on the twenty-fourth of Ramaḍān.""

And in what al-Rabīʿ ibn Ṣabīḥ narrated from al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī, who said: ""Allah, the Most High, revealed one hundred and four books from the heavens."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18656,"(18650) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us that al-Shāfiʿī informed us that Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah informed us from Abū Saʿd Saʿīd ibn al-Marzubān, from Naṣr ibn ʿĀṣim, who said: ""Farwah ibn Nawfal al-Ashjaʿī said: 'Why is the jizyah taken from the Magians when they are not People of the Book?' Al-Mustawrid stood up and seized him by his collar and said: 'O enemy of Allah, do you find fault with Abū Bakr, ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), and the Commander of the Faithful—meaning ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him)—when they took the jizyah from them?' He took him to the palace, and ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) came out to them. He said: 'Begin.' So they sat in the shade of the palace. ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) said: 'I am the most knowledgeable of people regarding the Magians. They had a knowledge that they taught and a book that they studied. Their king became drunk and lay with his daughter or his sister. Some of his kingdom's people discovered it. When he recovered, they came to carry out the ḥadd punishment upon him, but he resisted them. He called the people of his kingdom, and when they came to him, he said: 'Do you know any religion better than the religion of Ādam? He used to marry his sons to his daughters, and I am upon the religion of Ādam. I do not desire for you to turn away from his religion.' So they pledged allegiance to him and fought those who opposed them until they killed them. Then they awoke and their book had been taken away from them, raised from their midst, and the knowledge that was in their hearts had vanished. They are People of the Book. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), Abū Bakr, and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) took the jizyah from them.'""

And Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, saying: ""I heard Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-ʿĀṣimī saying: 'I heard Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzaymah saying: ""Ibn ʿUyaynah erred in this chain of narration. He narrated it from Abū Saʿd al-Baqqāl and said: from Naṣr ibn ʿĀṣim, but Naṣr ibn ʿĀṣim is al-Laythī, and it is rather ʿĪsā ibn ʿĀṣim al-Asadī, a Kūfan."" Ibn Khuzaymah said: ""The error is from Ibn ʿUyaynah, not from al-Shāfiʿī, for others besides al-Shāfiʿī narrated it from Ibn ʿUyaynah and said: from Naṣr ibn ʿĀṣim.""'"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18657,"(18651) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us by dictation that Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us that al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us that Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, who heard Bajālah ibn ʿAbdah saying: ""I was a scribe for Juzʾ ibn Muʿāwiyah, the uncle of al-Aḥnaf ibn Qays. A letter from ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) came to him: 'Kill every sorcerer, and separate every mahram relative among the Magians.' ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) had not taken the jizyah from the Magians until ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf (may Allah be pleased with him) testified that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) had taken it from the Magians of Hajar."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh from Sufyān."
18658,"(18652) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us that al-Shāfiʿī informed us that Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated it with his chain of narration, abridged, regarding the jizyah.

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""The ḥadīth of Bajālah is connected and established, for he met ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) and was a man who served as a scribe for his governors in his time. And the ḥadīth of Naṣr ibn ʿĀṣim from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) is connected, and it is upon this that we act. There are also two disconnected narrations from the people of the Ḥijāz regarding the taking of the jizyah from the Magians."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1357]"
18659,"(18653) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us that Ibn Wahb informed us that Mālik ibn Anas informed us. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned.]"
18660,"(Ḥ) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us that al-Shāfiʿī informed us that Mālik narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad from his father, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) mentioned the Magians and said: ""I do not know how to deal with their affair."" ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him: ""I bear witness that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) saying: 'Treat them with the treatment of the People of the Book.'"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18661,"(18655) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʤ al-Ḥakam narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: Mālik informed me.

(Ḥ) And Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ narrated to us: al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us: Mālik reported from Ibn Shihāb that it reached him that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) collected the jizyah from the Magians of Baḥrayn, and that ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) collected it from the Berbers. Ibn Wahb added in his narration: And that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) collected it from the Magians of Fārs.

The Shaykh said: And Ibn Shihāb only took this ḥadīth from Ibn al-Musayyab, and Ibn al-Musayyab is ḥasan in mursal narrations, especially since what has preceded supports it. [Weak]"
18662,"(18656) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʤ al-Ḥakam narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Yazīd informed me from Ibn Shihāb, who said: Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab informed me that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) collected the jizyah from the Magians of Hajar, and that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) collected it from the Magians of al-Sawād, and that ʿUthmān (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) collected it from the Magians of Berber. [Weak]"
18663,"(18657) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Miskīn al-Yamāmī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ḥassān narrated to us: Hushaym narrated to us: Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind reported from Qushayr ibn ʿAmr, from Bajālah ibn ʿAbdah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā), who said: A man from the Asbādhīyīn, who were the Magians of Hajar, came to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and stayed with him. Then he departed, and I asked him: ""What has Allah and His Messenger decreed concerning you?"" He said: ""Evil."" I said: ""What?"" He said: ""Islam or death."" He said: And ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) said: ""He accepted the jizyah from them.""

Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā) said: The people followed the statement of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf and abandoned what I myself had heard from the Asbādhī.

The Shaykh (raḥimahullāh) said: How well they did! They abandoned the narration of the Magian Asbādhī and followed the narration of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), on the understanding that he may have judged between them by what the Asbādhī had said, then revelation came to him (the Prophet) regarding the acceptance of the jizyah from them, so he accepted it, as ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) said. And Allah knows best. [Weak]"
18664,"(18658) And Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAttāb al-ʿAbdī narrated to us: al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mughīrah narrated to us: Ibn Abī Uways narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿUqbah reported from his uncle Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, who said: Ibn Shihāb said: ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that al-Miswar ibn Makhramah informed him that ʿAmr ibn ʿAwf (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) — who was an ally of Banū ʿĀmir ibn Luʾayy and had witnessed Badr with the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) — informed him that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) sent Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) to Baḥrayn to collect its jizyah. The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) had made a treaty with the people of Baḥrayn and appointed over them al-ʿAlāʾ ibn al-Ḥaḍramī. Abū ʿUbaydah arrived with wealth from Baḥrayn, and the Anṣār heard of his arrival. They met for the Fajr prayer with the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). When he finished the prayer, they approached him. The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) smiled when he saw them and said: ""I think you have heard of the arrival of Abū ʿUbaydah, and that he has brought something."" They said: ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""Rejoice and hope for what pleases you. By Allah, it is not poverty that I fear for you; rather, what I fear for you is that the world will be spread out for you as it was spread out for those before you, and you will compete for it and it will distract you as it distracted them.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Uways. [Sound. Agreed upon.]"
18665,"(18659) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to me: My father narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to us: My father narrated from Ṣāliḥ, from Ibn Shihāb, and he mentioned it similarly.

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥasan al-Ḥulwānī, from Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm."
18666,"(18660) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Hilāl ibn al-ʿAlāʾ al-Raqqī narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: al-Muʿtamir ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn ʿUbaydullāh narrated to us: Bakr ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Muzanī and Ziyād ibn Jubayr reported from Jubayr ibn Ḥayyah, who said: ʿUmar (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) sent the people from the outskirts of the garrison towns to fight the polytheists. He mentioned the ḥadīth regarding the Islam of Hurmuzān. He said: He (Hurmuzān) said: ""I seek your counsel regarding these military expeditions of mine. Advise me regarding the military campaigns of the Muslims."" He said: ""Yes, O Commander of the Faithful. The land and the people in it among the enemies of the Muslims are like a bird that has a head, two wings, and two legs. If one of the two wings is broken, the two legs rise up with one wing and the head. If the other wing is broken, the two legs and the head rise up. If the head is crushed, the two legs, the two wings, and the head are gone. The head is Kisrā, one wing is Qaysar, and the other wing is Fārs. Command the Muslims to march against Kisrā."" Bakr and Ziyād both said from Jubayr ibn Ḥayyah: He said: ""So ʿUmar (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) mobilized us and appointed over us a man from Muzaynah called al-Nuʿmān ibn Muqarrin (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), and he gathered the Muslims with him."" He said: ""We set out among the people until we drew near to the enemy, whose equipment and weapons were broken spears, shattered lances, and arrows."" He said: ""We continued marching, and we did not have many horses — or we had no horses — until we were in the land of the enemy, and between us and the people was a river. There came out to us an agent of Kisrā with forty thousand men, and they stood at the river, while we stood on the other side."" He said: ""O people, send us a man to speak to us."" So al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah was brought out to them. He was a man who had engaged in trade and knew languages. He said: ""The interpreter of the people spoke before their king."" He said: ""He said: 'Let one of you speak to me.' Al-Mughīrah said: 'Ask whatever you wish.' He said: 'Who are you?' He said: 'We are a people from the Arabs. We were in severe hardship and prolonged affliction, chewing hides and pits out of hunger, wearing wool and hair, worshipping trees and stones. While we were in that state, the Lord of the heavens and the Lord of the earth sent to us a Prophet from among ourselves, whose father and mother we knew. Our Prophet, the Messenger of our Lord (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), commanded us to fight you until you worship Allah alone or pay the jizyah. Our Prophet informed us of the message of our Lord that whoever among us is killed will go to a Garden and bliss the like of which he has never seen, and whoever among us remains will master your necks.'"" He said: ""The man said: 'Between us and you is the day after tomorrow, until I order the bridge to be constructed.'"" He said: ""They separated, and they constructed the bridge. Then the enemies of Allah crossed to us — one hundred and sixty thousand dragging iron, and forty thousand archers — and they surrounded us ten times."" He said: ""We were twelve thousand."" He said: ""They said: 'Bring us a man to speak to us.' So we brought out al-Mughīrah, and he repeated to them his first speech. The king said: 'Do you know what our likeness and your likeness is?' Al-Mughīrah said: 'What is our likeness and your likeness?' He said: 'The likeness of a man who has a garden of sweet-smelling plants, and he had a fox that harmed him. The lord of the garden said to the fox: ""O fox, were it not that my wall would stink from your carcass, I would have prepared what would kill you. And were it not that your carcasses would stink up our lands, we would have killed you yesterday.""' Al-Mughīrah said to him: 'Do you know what the fox said to the lord of the garden?' He said: 'What did he say to him?' He said: 'He said to him: ""O lord of the garden, that I die in your garden among the sweet-smelling plants is more beloved to me than that I go out to a barren land in which there is nothing. And by Allah, were it not for a religion, and we were in the misery of life that I described to you, we would never return to that misery, even that we share in what you are in, or we die. And how about us when whoever among us is killed goes to the mercy of Allah and His Garden, and whoever among us remains masters your necks?""' Jubayr said: ""We remained against them for a day, neither fighting them nor they fighting us."" He said: ""Al-Mughīrah stood to al-Nuʿmān ibn Muqarrin (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) and said: 'O commander, the day has done what you see. By Allah, were I given charge of the affairs of the people as you have been given charge of them, I would join the people to one another until Allah judges between His servants as He wills.' Al-Nuʿmān said: 'May Allah cause you to witness the like of it, then not cause you regret nor disgrace you. But I witnessed many times with the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam); when he did not fight in the first part of the day, he would wait until the winds blew and the prayers were due. O people, I do not call all of you to attention. Look at this banner of mine. When I move it, prepare yourselves — whoever wishes to thrust with his spear, let him make it ready; whoever wishes to strike with his staff, let him make his staff ready; whoever wishes to stab with his dagger, let him make it ready; whoever wishes to strike with his sword, let him make his sword ready. O people, I am moving it a second time, so prepare yourselves. Then I am moving it a third time, so charge, by the blessing of Allah. If I am killed, then the commander is my brother. If my brother is killed, then the commander is Ḥudhayfah. If Ḥudhayfah is killed, then the commander is al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah.'""

He said: And Ziyād narrated to me that his father said: ""Allah killed them, and we looked at a mule loaded with honey and ghee, upon which the slain had been piled. I can only liken it to a heap of fish, some of them thrown upon others. I knew that killing takes place on the ground, but this is something that Allah decreed. The Muslims prevailed, and al-Nuʿmān and his brother were killed, and the command passed to Ḥudhayfah. This is the ḥadīth of Ziyād and Bakr."" [Sound. Bukhārī 3160]"
18667,"(18661) He said: And Abū Rajāʾ al-Ḥanafī narrated to us, saying: Ḥudhayfah wrote to ʿUmar (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā) that so-and-so and so-and-so from the Muhājirūn had been killed, and among those who were not known, the majority. When he read the letter, he raised his voice, then he wept and wept, and he said: ""Rather, Allah knows them,"" three times.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ in abridged form from al-Faḍl ibn Yaʿqūb, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar al-Raqqī.

And in it is evidence for the collection of the jizyah from the Magians, and Allah knows best, for Kisrā and his companions were Magians. [Sound. Preceded before it.]"
18668,"(18662) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Sinān al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bilāl narrated from ʿImrān al-Qaṭṭān, from Abū Jamrah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā), who said: When the Prophet of the people of Fārs died, Iblīs wrote Magianism for them. [Weak]"
18669,"(18663) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Abū ʿAmr ibn Ḥamdān narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Wakīʿ narrated to us: Sufyān reported from Qays ibn Muslim, from al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) wrote to the Magians of Hajar, offering Islam to them. Whoever accepted Islam, he accepted it from him, and whoever refused, the jizyah was imposed upon him, with the condition that their slaughtered animals would not be eaten and their women would not be married. This is a mursal narration, and the consensus of the majority of Muslims confirms it. And the narration reported from Ḥudhayfah regarding the marriage of a Magian woman is not sound. And the narration regarding the Christians of Banū Taghlib from ʿUmar and ʿAlī (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā) will be mentioned in its place, if Allah the Most High wills. [Weak]"
18670,"(18664) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us: al-Aʿmash reported from Abū Wāʾil, from Masrūq, from Muʿādh ibn Jabal (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) sent him to Yemen and commanded him to take from cattle: from every thirty cows, a tabīʿah (a one-year-old calf), and from every forty cows, a musinnah (a two-year-old cow), and from every adult (who has reached puberty), a dīnār or its equivalent in a garment of Maʿāfir. [Sound]"
18671,"(18665) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad al-Nufaylī narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Abū Wāʾil, from Muʿādh (may Allah be pleased with him) that when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) dispatched him to Yemen, he commanded him to take from cattle: from every thirty, a tabīʿ or a tabīʿah, and from every forty, a musinnah, and from every ḥālim (i.e., one who has reached puberty), a dīnār or its equivalent in Maʿāfīrī garments, which are cloths made in Yemen. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18672,"(18666) He said: And al-Nufaylī narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us: al-Aʿmash narrated from Ibrāhīm, from Masrūq, from Muʿādh (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), a similar hadith.

Abū Dāwūd said in some of the manuscripts: This is a munkar hadith. It has reached me from Aḥmad that he used to reject this hadith with severe rejection.

The Shaykh said: What is munkar is specifically the narration of Abū Muʿāwiyah from al-Aʿmash from Ibrāhīm from Masrūq from Muʿādh. As for the narration of al-Aʿmash from Abū Wāʾil from Masrūq, it is preserved (maḥfūẓah), for a group narrated it from al-Aʿmash, among them Sufyān al-Thawrī, Shuʿbah, Maʿmarr, Jarīr, Abū ʿAwanah, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, and Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth. Some of them said: ""from Muʿādh,"" and some of them said: ""that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), when he sent Muʿādh to Yemen,"" or something to that effect."
18673,"(18667) As for the hadith of al-Aʿmash from Ibrāhīm, the correct version is as Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Muʾammal informed us: he said: Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Baṣrī narrated to us: Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: al-Aʿmash narrated from Shaqīq, from Masrūq, and al-Aʿmash narrated from Ibrāhīm — both said: Muʿādh (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) sent me to Yemen and commanded me to take from every forty cows a thaniyyah, and from every thirty a tabīʿ or a tabīʿah, and from every ḥālim a dīnār or its equivalent in Maʿāfīrī cloth.""

This is the preserved version: the hadith of al-Aʿmash from Abū Wāʾil Shaqīq ibn Salamah from Masrūq. And his hadith from Ibrāhīm is munqaṭiʿ (disconnected), for it does not contain the mention of Masrūq.

And we have narrated it from ʿĀṣim ibn Abī al-Najūd, from Abū Wāʾil, from Masrūq, from Muʿādh ibn Jabal, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family).

[Ṣaḥīḥ. It has been mentioned before.]"
18674,"(18668) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad informed me: Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Ḥakīm narrated from ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) wrote to the people of Yemen: ""Upon every person among you is a dīnār every year, or its value in Maʿāfīrī cloth"" — meaning the People of the Dhimmah among them. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18675,"(18669) Abū Zakariyyā and Abū Bakr both informed us: they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed me: Muṭarrif ibn Māzin and Hishām ibn Yūsuf narrated through a chain of narration that I do not recall precisely, other than that it is ḥasan, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) imposed upon the People of the Dhimmah of the people of Yemen a dīnār every year. I said to Muṭarrif ibn Māzin: ""It is also said that it was upon women as well."" He said: ""That the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) took it from women is not established with us."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18676,"(18670) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us: Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Ḍabbī narrated from Manṣūr, from al-Ḥakam, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) wrote to Muʿādh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him) in Yemen: ""Upon every ḥālim (male) or ḥālimah (female) is a dīnār or its value, and no Jew shall be turned away from his Judaism.""

Yaḥyā said: ""I did not hear that jizyah was imposed upon women except in this hadith.""

The Shaykh said: And this is munqaṭiʿ (disconnected). In the narration of Abū Wāʾil from Masrūq from Muʿādh, there is no mention of ḥālimah, nor in the narration of Ibrāhīm from Muʿādh, except for something that ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated from Maʿmarr, from al-Aʿmash, from Abū Wāʾil, from Masrūq, from Muʿādh.

And Maʿmarr, when he narrates from other than al-Zuhrī, errs frequently — and Allah knows best. Ibn Khuzaymah carried it, if it is preserved, upon the meaning that it is taken from them if they willingly consent.

And Abū Shaybah Ibrāhīm ibn ʿUthmān narrated it from al-Ḥakam, connected (mawṣūl). But Abū Shaybah is weak. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18677,"(18671) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us orally: Ḥāmid ibn Shuʿayb narrated to us: Manṣūr ibn Abī Mazāḥim narrated to us: Abū Shaybah narrated from al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUtbah, from Miqsam, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) wrote to Muʿādh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him): ""Whoever among the Muslims has embraced Islam, he has what the believers have and upon him is what is upon them. And whoever remains upon his Judaism or Christianity, then upon every ḥālim is a dīnār or its equivalent in Maʿāfīrī cloth, whether male or female, free or slave. And in every thirty cows, a tabīʿ or a tabīʿah, and in every forty cows, a musinnah, and in every forty camels, a bint labūn. And in what is irrigated by the sky or by natural flooding, the tenth (ʿushr), and in what is irrigated by the bucket, half of the tenth.""

This is not established through this chain of narration. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18678,"(18672) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī said: ""I asked Muḥammad ibn Khālid, ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn Muslim, and a number of the scholars of the people of Yemen, and all of them related to me from a number of predecessors who came before them, who in turn related from those who came before them — all of them trustworthy — that the treaty of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) with them was with the People of the Dhimmah of Yemen for a dīnār every year, and they do not affirm that women were among those from whom jizyah was taken. And the generality of them said: 'And nothing was taken from their crops, though they had crops, nor from their livestock, as far as we know.'"" And some of them said to me: ""Some of the governors came and took a fifth of their crops or intended to do so, and this was rejected by him."" And everyone whom I have described informed me that the generality of the People of the Dhimmah of the people of Yemen were from Ḥimyar. He said: ""And I asked a great number of the People of the Dhimmah of the people of Yemen, scattered across the lands of Yemen, and all of them affirmed for me — without any difference in their statements — that Muʿādh took from them a dīnār for every adult among them, and they called the adult a ḥālim. They said: 'And in the letter of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) with Muʿādh was: Upon every ḥālim is a dīnār.'"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18679,"(18673) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Muslim ibn ʿAlī narrated from al-Muthannā ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ, from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) imposed the jizyah upon every muḥtalim (one who has reached puberty) of the people of Yemen: a dīnār, a dīnār. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18680,"(18674) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm narrated to me: ""This is the letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) that is with us, which he wrote to ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm when he sent him to Yemen."" He mentioned it, and at the end of it: ""And indeed, whoever among the Jews or Christians embraces Islam sincerely from himself and follows the religion of Islam, then he is of the believers — he has what they have and upon him is what is upon them. And whoever remains upon his Christianity or Judaism, he shall not be turned away from it. And upon every ḥālim, male or female, free or slave, is a full dīnār or its compensation in garments. Whoever pays that, then for him is the protection of Allah and the protection of His Messenger. And whoever withholds that, then he is an enemy of Allah, His Messenger, and the believers.""

This is munqaṭiʿ (disconnected), and it is not in the connected narration. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18681,"(18675) It has also been narrated through another, disconnected chain: Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Jaʿfar al-Baghdādī narrated to us, that Abū ʿUlāthah narrated to us, that my father narrated to us, that Ibn Lahīʿah narrated from Abū al-Aswad from ʿUrwah, who said: ""This is a letter from Muḥammad (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) to the people of Yemen."" Then he mentioned the hadith in a manner similar to the hadith of Ibn Ḥazm. [Weak]"
18682,"(18676) Abū Sahl Muḥammad ibn Naṣrūyah ibn Aḥmad al-Marwazī narrated to us, that Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ al-Maʿāfirī narrated to us, that Abū Yazan al-Ḥimyarī Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUfayr ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUfayr ibn Zurʿah ibn Sayf ibn Dhī Yazan narrated to us, that my uncle Aḥmad ibn Ḥubaysh ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to me, that my father ʿUfayr narrated to me, that my father ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to me, that my father ʿUfayr narrated to me, that my father Zurʿah ibn Sayf ibn Dhī Yazan said: ""The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) wrote a letter to me — this is its copy — and in it was: 'Whoever remains upon his Judaism or upon his Christianity shall not be changed from it, and upon him is the jizyah — upon every adult, male or female, free or slave, one dīnār or its equivalent value in garments of Maʿāfir.'""

This narration has among its narrators one who is unknown, and no similar hadith has been established by the people of knowledge. That which agrees in wording with it and with what precedes it is the narration of Masrūq, which is relied upon. As for that which adds beyond it, caution must be exercised regarding it. And through Allāh comes success. [Weak]"
18683,"(18677) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, that Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us, that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, that Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, that Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Yaḥyā narrated from Abū al-Ḥuwayrith, who said: ""The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) imposed upon the Christians of Makkah one dīnār every year."" [Very Weak]"
18684,"(18678) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us, that Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us, that al-Rabīʿ informed us, that al-Shāfiʿī informed us, that Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad narrated from Abū al-Ḥuwayrith that the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) imposed upon a Christian in Makkah, called Mawhab, one dīnār every year; and that the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) imposed upon the Christians of Aylah three hundred dīnārs every year, that they provide hospitality to any Muslim who passes by them for three days, and that they not deceive any Muslim. [Very Weak]"
18685,"(18679) He said, and Ibrāhīm informed us that Isḥāq ibn ʿAbd Allāh said: ""They were three hundred in number, so the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) imposed upon them on that day three hundred dīnārs every year.""

Al-Shāfiʿī (raḥimahullāh) said: ""Then the people of Najrān made a settlement regarding garments which they would pay to him. His making a settlement with them for something other than dīnārs indicates that whatever they agree upon in settlement is permissible."" [Very Weak]"
18686,"(18680) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us, that Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, that Muṣarrif ibn ʿAmr narrated to us, that Yūnus — meaning Ibn Bukayr — narrated to us, that Asbāṭ ibn Ṣadr al-Hamdānī narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Qurashī from Ibn ʿAbbāy (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā), who said: ""The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) made a settlement with the people of Najrān for two thousand garments — half in Ṣafar and half in Rajab — which they would deliver to the Muslims, and as a loan thirty armours, thirty horses, thirty camels, and thirty of each type of weapon, with which they would fight, and the Muslims would be responsible for them until they returned them to them, should there be any calamity in Yemen."" [Weak. Previously cited under no. 18644]"
18687,"(18681) Al-Shāfiʿī (raḥimahullāh) said: ""I have heard some of the people of knowledge among the Muslims and among the People of the Covenant from the people of Najrān mention that the value of what was taken from each one of them was more than one dīnār."" [Sound]"
18688,"(18682) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us, that Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Khamīrūyah narrated to us, that Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us, that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr narrated to us, that my father narrated to us, that ʿUbayd Allāh narrated to us, that Nāfiʿ narrated from Aslam, the freedman of ʿUmar, that he informed him that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) wrote to the commanders of the people of jizyah: ""Do not levy the jizyah except upon those upon whom the years have passed. Their jizyah is forty dirhams upon those among them who deal in silver, and four dīnārs upon those who deal in gold. Upon them is the provision of the Muslims: one mudd of wheat and three qusṭ of oil for every person each month, for those who are of al-Shām and al-Jazīrah. For those who are of Egypt, one irdabb for every person each month. Of clarified fat and honey there is an amount which we do not preserve. Upon them is of the cloth which the Commander of the Faithful used to clothe the people an amount which we do not preserve. They shall host whoever stays with them from among the people of Islam for three days. And upon the people of Iraq is fifteen ṣāʿ for every person."" ʿUmar (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) used not to levy the jizyah upon women, and he used to place a seal upon the necks of the men of the people of jizyah. [Sound]"
18689,"(18683) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Aṣbahānī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, that Abū ʿAmr ibn Ḥamdān informed us, that al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, that Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, that ʿAbd al-Raḥīm ibn Sulaymān narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar from Nāfiʿ from Aslam, the freedman of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) wrote to his governors: ""Do not levy the jizyah upon women and children, and do not levy it except upon those upon whom the years have passed. Place a seal upon their necks. And their jizyah is levied according to their means: forty dirhams upon those who deal in silver, along with the provision of the Muslims; and four dīnārs upon those who deal in gold. Upon those of al-Shām among them is one mudd of wheat and three qusṭ of oil. Upon those of Egypt is one irdabb of wheat, clothing, and honey — the amount of which Nāfiʿ does not preserve. And upon those of Iraq is fifteen ṣāʿ of wheat.""

ʿUbayd Allāh said: ""And he mentioned clothing which I do not preserve."" [Sound. Previously cited before it]"
18690,"(18684) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us, that Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar informed us, that Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, that ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, that Shuʿbah narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And al-Sharīf Abū al-Fatḥ Nāṣir ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿUmarī informed us, that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Shurayḥ narrated to us, that Abū al-Qāsim al-Baghawī narrated to us, that ʿAlī ibn al-Jaʿd narrated to us, that Shuʿbah narrated to me, that al-Ḥakam informed me, who said: ""I heard ʿAmr ibn Maymūn narrating from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu),"" and he mentioned it, saying: ""Then ʿUthmān ibn Ḥunayf came to him and began speaking to him from behind the tent, saying: 'By Allāh, if you impose upon every jarīb of land one dirham and one qafīz of food, and add upon every head two dirhams, that will not be burdensome upon them nor exhaust them.' He said: 'Yes.' It was eighty-four, and he made it fifty.""

And al-Shāfiʿī (raḥimahullāh) narrated in the older transmission from Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd from Ibn Shihāb that ʿUmar (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) used to increase upon the people of al-Sawād when they became prosperous, and decrease from them when they became impoverished. And this is a disconnected narration. [Sound. Narrated by Ibn al-Jaʿd]"
18691,"(18685) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAmr ibn Ḥamdān informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us that Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us that ʿAlī ibn Muhshir narrated from al-Shaybānī from Abū ʿAwn Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh al-Thaqafī, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) imposed the jizyah upon the heads of men: upon the wealthy, forty-eight dirhams; upon the middle-class, twenty-four dirhams; and upon the poor, twelve dirhams.

Qatādah similarly narrated it from Abī Mijlaz from ʿUmar. Both narrations are mursal. [Weak]"
18692,"(18686) The reliance in this matter is upon what Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us that al-Shāfiʿī informed us that Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Aḥmad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Mihrānjānī informed us that Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us that Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī informed us that Ibn Bukayr informed us that Mālik informed us from Nāfiʿ from Aslam, the freed slave of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him), that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) imposed the jizyah upon the people of gold as four dīnārs and upon the people of silver as forty dirhams, along with provisions for the Muslims and hospitality for three days. [Sound. Reported by Mālik]"
18693,"(18687) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us that al-Shāfiʿī informed us that Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Abū Isḥāq from Ḥārithah ibn Muḍarrib that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) imposed upon the people of al-Sawād the obligation of hosting for one day and one night, and whoever was detained due to illness or rain should spend from his own wealth.

Al-Shāfiʿī said: The ḥadīth of Aslam regarding hospitality of three days is more appropriate, because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) made hospitality three days. It is also possible that he made it three days for some people and one day and one night for others, and did not impose hospitality upon yet others, just as his terms of treaty with them differed. Thus, some narrations do not contradict others. [Sound]"
18694,"(18688) Abū Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Isfarāyīnī informed us through it that Abū Saʿīd ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Rāzī informed us that Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us that Muslim informed us that Hishām informed us that Qatādah narrated from al-Ḥasan from al-Aḥnaf ibn Qays that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) used to stipulate upon the people of the dhimmah hospitality of one day and one night, that they repair the bridges, and that if a person was killed among them, they would be liable for his blood-money. Others narrated from Hishām: And if a Muslim was killed in their land, they would be liable for his blood-money. [Sound. Reported before it]"
18695,"(18689) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs informed me that ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī informed us that Abū al-Walīd al-Ṭayālisī said that Layth ibn Saʿd narrated to us from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Maqburī from Abū Shurayḥ al-ʿAdawī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: My ears heard and my eyes saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) saying: ""Whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him honour his neighbour. And whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him honour his guest by giving him his due hospitality (jā'izah)."" It was said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what is his jā'izah?"" He said: ""One day and one night. Hospitality is three days. Whatever is beyond that is charity. And let him not stay with him so as to cause him hardship. And whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day, let him speak good or remain silent.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Walīd, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah from Layth ibn Saʿd. [Sound. Agreed upon]"
18696,"(18690) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us that Abū Dāwūd said: It was recited to al-Ḥārith ibn Miskīn while I was present: Ashhab informed you that he was asked about the statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family): ""His jā'izah is one day and one night."" He said: He should honour him, present him with gifts, and look after him for one day and one night, and hospitality is for three days. [Sound]"
18697,"(18691) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us that Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār informed us that Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr informed us that ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us that Maʿmar narrated from Saʿīd al-Jurayrī from Abū Naḍrah from Abū Saʿīd that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""The right of hospitality is three days. Whatever exceeds that is charity."" [Sound]"
18698,"(18692) And ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqrī informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us that Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr informed us that Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr from Abū Salamah from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Hospitality is three days. Whatever exceeds that is charity."" [Sound]"
18699,"(18693) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us that al-Ḥārith ibn Muḥammad informed us that Yūnus ibn Muḥammad informed us that Layth informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Muḥammad ibn Shādhān and Aḥmad ibn Salamah said: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd informed us that al-Layth narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb from Abū al-Khayr from ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: We said: ""O Messenger of Allah, you send us and we stay with a people who do not host us. What do you think?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""If you stay with a people and they provide for you what is befitting for a guest, then accept it. But if they do not do so, then take from them the right of the guest that is befitting for them.""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd. [Sound. Agreed upon]"
18700,"(18694) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad al-Aṣbahānī informed us that Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us that Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī informed us that Shuʿbah narrated from Manṣūr, who said: I heard al-Shaʿbī narrating from Abū Karīmah (may Allah be pleased with him) that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) saying: ""The night's hospitality is a right upon every Muslim. When a guest arrives at one's courtyard, it is a right upon him—or he said, a debt. If he wishes, he may claim it, and if he wishes, he may forgo it."" [Sound. Reported by al-Ṭayālisī, 1247]"
18701,"(18695) Abū Bakr ibn Furak informed us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us that Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated to me that Abū al-Jūdī al-Shāmī said: I heard Saʿīd ibn al-Muhājir narrating from al-Miqdām ibn Maʿdīkarib (may Allah be pleased with him), who had been a Companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""There is no man who hosts a people and the guest wakes up deprived, except that it is incumbent upon every Muslim to support him until he takes the due portion of his night's hospitality from the host's crops and wealth."" [Weak]"
18702,"(18696) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī informed us in Baghdād that Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us that ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Tarqafī narrated to me that Yaḥyā ibn Yaʿlā —

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū Saʿīd Muḥammad ibn Mūsā said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Yaʿlā ibn al-Ḥārith al-Muḥāribī narrated to us that my father narrated to us that Ghaylān ibn Jāmiʿ narrated from Qays ibn Muslim from Ṭāriq ibn Shihāb who said: A group of the Anṣār set out from al-Kūfah to al-Madīnah. They came upon a clan of Banī Asad who had run out of provisions. They asked them to sell them something, and they had fine livestock with them. They said: ""We have nothing to sell."" They then asked them for hospitality (qirā). They said: ""We cannot afford to host you."" Matters continued between them and the Bedouins until they fought, and the Bedouins abandoned their houses and everything in them. They took for every ten of them one sheep. He said: They came to ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) and mentioned that to him. He stood and praised Allah and extolled Him, and said: ""Had I gone ahead in this matter, I would have done such and such."" Then he wrote to the people of the garrison cities and the people of the covenant (ahl al-dhimmah) regarding the provision of one night's hospitality for the guest.

Qays said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā informed me that his father informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) distributed sheep among his Companions, giving every ten of them one sheep, and that this was a Sunnah. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) had ordered on that day that the cooking pots be overturned, and that was at Khaybar. Qays said: Ibn Abī Laylā informed me that ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote regarding one night's hospitality for the Muslims and the allied fighters (muʿāhidīn). Ibn Abī Laylā said: I recall that the people of the land used to receive us with one night's hospitality, saying in Persian ""bāshām,"" meaning supper. Al-Tarqafī said in his narration: They say ""shām,"" meaning supper."
18703,"(18697) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us that Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us that Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Abī Bukayr narrated to us that Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated to me that al-Aḥwaṣ ibn Ḥakīm and Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Maryam narrated from Ḥakīm ibn ʿUmayr who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to the commanders of the military corps, mentioning it, and said: ""Any company of the Muhājirūn whom the night overtakes in a village of the allied people (muʿāhidīn) while they are travelling, and they do not give them hospitality, then their covenant is void."" [Hasan due to supporting narrations]"
18704,"(18698) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us that Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us that Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Abī ʿImrān al-Jawnī from Jundub ibn ʿAbdullāh who said: ""We used to obtain the fruits and fodder of the People of the Covenant, and we did not interfere with their women or their property. And we would employ a guide to show us the way."" [Hasan]"
18705,"(18699) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad narrated to us that ʿUbaydullāh ibn Muʿādh narrated to us that my father narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated from Abī Isḥāq from Zayd ibn Ṣaʿṣaʿah who said: I said to Ibn ʿAbbās: ""We come to a village in the Sawād and knock at the door. If it is not opened for us, we break the door, take a sheep, and slaughter it."" He said: ""Why do you do that?"" I said: ""We consider it lawful for us."" He then recited this verse: 'That is because they say: ""There is no blame upon us regarding the unlettered ones,"" and they utter falsehood against Allah while they know' (Āl ʿImrān, 75). He said: ""If this concerns the allied people (muʿāhidīn), it is because they did not make a treaty with them regarding hospitality, so it is not lawful for them to consume any of it. And Allah knows best."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18706,"(18700) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us that Zuhayr ibn Muʿāwiyah narrated from al-Ḥasan ibn al-Ḥurr from Nāfiʿ from Aslam from ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) that he wrote to the collectors of the jizyah among the people of the covenant: ""Do not levy the jizyah except upon those upon whom the razor has passed (i.e., those who have reached puberty)."" He said: ""And he did not levy the jizyah upon women or children.""

Yaḥyā said: ""And this is the well-known practice among our scholars."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18707,"(18701) And Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Aṣbahānī informed us that Abū ʿAmr ibn Ḥamdān informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us that Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us that ʿAbdah ibn Sulaymān narrated from ʿUbaydullāh from Nāfiʿ from Aslam, the freedman of ʿUmar, who said: ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to the collectors of the jizyah: ""Do not levy the jizyah except upon those upon whom the razor has passed, and do not levy the jizyah upon women or children."" And ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) used to place seals upon the necks of the people of the jizyah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18708,"(18702) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Maḥbūr al-Dahhān informed us that Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz narrated to us that Abū al-Azhar narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣalt narrated to us that Abū Kudaynah narrated from Qābūs ibn Abī Zabyān from his father from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: ""There is no jizyah upon a believer, and two qiblahs cannot coexist in the Arabian Peninsula."" And Jarīr likewise narrated it from Qābūs. [Weak]"
18709,"(18703) Abū al-Fatḥ Hilāl ibn Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Ḥaffār informed us in Baghdād that al-Ḥusayn ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAyyāsh al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn al-Sarī narrated to us that Jarīr narrated from ʿAṭāʾ ibn al-Sāʾib from Ḥarb ibn Hilāl —

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Musaddad narrated to us that Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated to us that ʿAṭāʾ ibn al-Sāʾib narrated from Ḥarb ibn ʿUbaydullāh from his grandfather, his mother's father, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The ʿushr (tithe) is levied only upon the Jews and the Christians, and there is no ʿushr upon the Muslims.""

The wording is that of Abū al-Aḥwaṣ.

And in the narration of Jarīr, he said: from Ḥarb ibn Hilāl from his mother's father, a man from Banī Taghlib, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: ""There is no ʿushr upon the Muslims; the ʿushr is levied only upon the Jews and the Christians."" [Very weak]"
18710,"(18704) ʿAbd al-Salām ibn Ḥarb narrated it from ʿAṭāʾ from Ḥarb ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmayr al-Thaqafī from his grandfather, a man from Banī Taghlib, who said: ""I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and accepted Islam. He taught me Islam and taught me how to collect the ṣadaqah from my people among those who had accepted Islam. Then I returned to him and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, everything you taught me I have preserved, except the ṣadaqah — should I take the ʿushr (tenth) from them?' He said: 'No, the ʿushr is only upon the Christians and the Jews.'""

Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Bazzāz narrated to us that Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Salām mentioned it. [Very weak]"
18711,"(18705) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd al-Muḥāribī informed us, Wakīʿ narrated from Sufyān, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn al-Sāʾib, from Ḥarb ibn ʿUbayd Allāh, from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), with the meaning of the ḥadīth of Abī al-Aḥwaṣ, except that he said: ""Kharāj"" in place of ""al-ʿUshūr.""

And Abū Nuʿaym narrated it from Sufyān, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ḥarb, from his maternal uncle, from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). [Very Weak]"
18712,"(18706) Abū ʿAlī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bashshār informed us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed us, Sufyān narrated from ʿAṭāʾ, from a man from Bakr ibn Wāʾil, from his maternal uncle, who said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, shall I collect the ʿushr (tithe) from my people?"" He said: ""The ʿushr is only upon the Jews and the Christians.""

And Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated it from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ḥarb ibn ʿUbayd Allāh, from a man among his maternal uncles. [Very Weak]"
18713,"(18707) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, informed us, al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Yūnus informed us, Abū Bakr ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated from Nuṣayr, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn al-Sāʾib, from Ḥarb ibn ʿUbayd Allāh, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""There is no ʿushr upon the Muslims; the ʿushr is only upon the Jews and the Christians.""

Al-ʿAbbās said: Thus did Aḥmad ibn Yūnus say, ""from his grandfather.""

Imām Aḥmad (raḥimahullāh) said: And al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Tārīkh from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus, from Abū Bakr, from Nuṣayr, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ḥarb ibn ʿUbayd Allāh, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). And he said: Abū Ḥamzah said from ʿAṭāʾ: al-Ḥārith al-Thaqafī informed us that his father informed him, and he was among those who came as a delegation to the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam).

(Q) And if this is authentic, then he intended—and Allah knows best—the tithe on their wealth when they engaged in trade. But when Islam spread among them, that was lifted from them. [Very Weak]"
18714,"(18708) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Kārizī informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us, from Abī ʿUbayd, who said: Ibn Mahdī narrated to us, from Ḥammād ibn Salamah, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Rawāḥah, Masrūq narrated to me: A man from the non-Arabs (al-Shuʿūb) embraced Islam, and the jizyah was being taken from him. He came to ʿUmar (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) and informed him, so ʿUmar wrote that the jizyah should not be taken from him. Abū ʿUbayd said: ""Al-Shuʿūb"" here means the non-Arabs (al-ʿAjam). [Weak]"
18715,"(18709) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah and ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Jarrāḥ informed us, from Jarīr, from Mughīrah, from al-Shaʿbī, from ʿAlī (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu): A Jewish woman used to revile the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and speak ill of him. A man strangled her until she died, so the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) declared her blood wasted (i.e., no blood-money was due). [Authentic]"
18716,"(18710) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fārisī informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Aṣfahānī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān ibn Fāris informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl informed us, who said: Nuʿaym ibn Ḥammād said, Ibn al-Mubārāk informed us, Ḥarmalah ibn ʿImrān informed us, Kaʿb ibn ʿAlqamah informed me: Ghurfah ibn al-Ḥārith al-Kindī passed by a Christian and called him to Islam. The Christian spoke ill of the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and mentioned him, so Ghurfah raised his hand and struck his nose. The matter was raised to ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, and ʿAmr said: ""We gave them a covenant."" Ghurfah said: ""Allah forbid that we gave them a covenant allowing them to openly revile the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam)! We only gave them a covenant that we would leave them and their churches, and they may say in them whatever they wish; that we would not burden them beyond their capacity; and that if an enemy sought to fight them, we would fight the enemy from behind them. And we would leave them and their judgments, unless they came to us willingly accepting our judgments, in which case we would judge between them by the judgment of Allah and the judgment of His Messenger. But if they were absent from us, we would not interfere in their affairs."" ʿAmr said: ""You have spoken the truth."" And Ghurfah was a Companion. [Good]"
18717,"(18711) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Shaʿrānī informed us, my grandfather informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn al-Mundhir al-Ḥizāmī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Fulayḥ narrated from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, who said: Ibn Shihāb said: This is the ḥadīth of the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) when he went out to Banī al-Naḍīr to seek their assistance in the blood-money of the two men of Kalb. The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) went out with a group of his Companions to Banī al-Naḍīr to seek their assistance in the blood-money of the two men of Kalb. They (Banī al-Naḍīr) claimed that they had conspired with Quraysh when they descended at Uḥud to fight the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), inciting them to fight and revealing the vulnerabilities (of the Muslims). When the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) spoke to them about the blood-money of the two men of Kalb, they said: ""Sit, Abū al-Qāsim, so that you may eat and return with your need fulfilled, and we will rise to consult and settle the matter for which you have come to us."" So the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) sat, along with those of his Companions who were with him, in the shade of a wall, waiting for them to settle their affair. When they were alone—and Satan was with them, never leaving them—they conspired to kill the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). They said: ""You will never find him closer than he is now. Relieve yourselves of him, so you may live safely in your homes and the affliction be lifted from you."" A man said: ""If you wish, I will climb onto the roof of the house and drop a stone upon him and kill him."" But Allah revealed to His Prophet and informed him of what they had conspired concerning him, so Allah protected him. The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) stood up as though he wished to relieve himself, leaving his Companions in their place. The enemies of Allah waited for him, but he delayed in returning. A man from the people of Madinah came, and they asked him about the Prophet. He said: ""I met him; he has entered the alleys of Madinah."" So they said to his Companions: ""Abū al-Qāsim has hastened; let us settle the matter regarding the need for which he came."" Then the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) stood up and returned. The Qurʾān was revealed—and Allah knows best what the enemies of Allah had brought—and He said: ""O you who believe, remember the favour of Allah upon you when a people intended to stretch out their hands against you, but He restrained their hands from you. And fear Allah, and upon Allah let the believers rely."" (Al-Māʾidah, 5:11) When Allah made His Messenger triumph over what they had intended and over their treachery to Allah and His Messenger, He commanded their expulsion and their removal from their homes. He commanded them to go wherever they wished, and so on to the end of the ḥadīth. [Weak]"
18718,"(18712) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Baḥr ibn Naṣr informed us, Ibn Wahb informed me, Jarīr ibn Ḥādim al-Azdī narrated from Mujālid, from ʿĀmir al-Shaʿbī, from Suwayd ibn Ghaflah, who said: We were with ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), the Commander of the Faithful, in al-Shām. A beaten and wounded Nabatean came to him seeking justice. ʿUmar became extremely angry and said to Ṣuhayb: ""Find out who the assailant of this man is."" Ṣuhayb went and found that it was ʿAwf ibn Mālik al-Ashjaʿī. He said to him: ""The Commander of the Faithful has become extremely angry. If you come with Muʿādh ibn Jabal and he walks with you to the Commander of the Faithful, I fear his hastiness toward you."" So Muʿādh (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) came with him. When ʿUmar finished the prayer, he said: ""Where is Ṣuhayb?"" He said: ""Here I am, O Commander of the Faithful."" He said: ""Have you brought the man who struck him?"" He said: ""Yes."" Muʿādh ibn Jabal stood up and said to him: ""O Commander of the Faithful, he is ʿAwf ibn Mālik. Listen to him and do not be hasty with him."" ʿUmar said to him: ""What do you have to do with this?"" He said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, I saw him driving a Muslim woman with a donkey. He goaded the donkey to make her fall, but she did not fall. Then he pushed her, and she fell off the donkey, and he assaulted her and did what you see."" He said: ""Bring me the woman so that she may confirm your account."" ʿAwf brought the woman, and he mentioned to her what ʿUmar (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) had said to him. Her father and her husband said: ""You intended nothing but to disgrace our companion."" The woman said: ""By Allah, I will go with him to the Commander of the Faithful."" When she resolved to do so, her father and her husband said: ""We will convey your message to the Commander of the Faithful."" They came and confirmed ʿAwf ibn Mālik's account of what he had said. ʿUmar (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) said to the Jewish man: ""By Allah, this is not what we covenanted with you."" He ordered that he be crucified. Then he said: ""O people, fulfil the covenant of Muḥammad (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). Whoever among them does this has no covenant."" Suwayd ibn Ghaflah said: He was the first crucified person I ever saw.

(T) Ibn Ashwaʿ followed it up, from al-Shaʿbī, from ʿAwf ibn Mālik. [Weak]"
18719,"(18713) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī, Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿUmar ibn Burhān, and Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān said: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿArafah informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Maʿmar, from Zayd ibn Rufayʿ, from Ḥarām ibn Muʿāwiyah, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) wrote to us: ""Train your horses, let the cross not be raised among you, and let the swine not live near you."" [Good]"
18720,"(18714) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh, Abū Muslim, informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Manṣūr ʿAbd al-Qāhir ibn Ṭāhir al-Baghdādī al-Imām, Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAlī ibn Ḥamdān al-Fārisī, and Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Qatādah said: Abū ʿAmr Ismāʿīl ibn Najayd al-Sulamī informed us, Abū Muslim Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Anṣārī informed us, Sulaymān al-Taymī narrated from Ḥanash, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā), who said: In every city that the Muslims have established, no church or place of worship shall be built, no bell shall be struck, and no pork shall be sold. [Weak]"
18721,"(18715) Abu 'Ali al-Rawdhbari informed us — Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us — Abu Dawud informed us — Musarrif ibn 'Amr al-Yami informed us — Yunus ibn Bukayr informed us — Asbat ibn Nast al-Hamdani informed us — from Isma'il ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Qurashi — from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, concluded a peace treaty with the people of Najran for two thousand suits of clothing. He mentioned the hadith as previously stated, in it: that their churches would not be demolished, nor would a priest be expelled from them, nor would they be compelled to abandon their religion, so long as they did not commit any new offence or consume usury. [Da'if]"
18722,"(18716) Abu al-Husayn ibn Burhan al-'Adl in Baghdad informed us — Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us — Abu Qilabah informed us — my father informed us — Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman informed us — he said: I heard my father narrate from Hanash, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Whatever city the Arabs have established, the non-Arabs have no right to build a church therein — or he said: a cathedral — nor to strike a bell therein, nor to introduce wine or swine into it. And whatever city the non-Arabs have established, it is incumbent upon the Arabs to fulfil their covenant with them therein and not to burden them beyond their capacity. [Da'if]"
18723,"(18717) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us — Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn Sakhawayh informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Ya'qub ibn Yusuf al-Mutawwi'i informed us — al-Rabi' ibn Tha'lab informed us — Yahya ibn 'Uqbah ibn Abi al-'Ayzar informed us — from Sufyan al-Thawri, al-Walid ibn Nuh, and al-Sariyy ibn Musarrif, who all mention from Talhah ibn Musarrif, from Masruq, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ghanm, who said: I wrote to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, when he concluded peace with the people of Syria: ""In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. This is a letter from 'Abd Allah, 'Umar, Commander of the Faithful, to the Christians of such-and-such city. When you came to us, you asked for safe conduct for yourselves, your children, your properties, and the people of your community. We stipulated upon ourselves for you that we shall not build in our city, nor in its environs, any monastery, church, hermitage, or monk's cell; nor shall we restore any ruin thereof, nor revive any part thereof that lay in the settlements of the Muslims. We shall not prevent our churches from being entered by any Muslim, by night or by day, and we shall open wide their gates for the passerby and the wayfarer. We shall host any Muslim who comes to us for three days and feed them. We shall not harbour a spy in our churches or in our homes, nor shall we conceal any deceit from the Muslims. We shall not teach our children the Qur'an. We shall not manifest shirk, nor call anyone to it. We shall not prevent any of our relatives from entering Islam if they so desire. We shall respect the Muslims, and we shall rise from our gatherings if they wish to sit. We shall not imitate them in any of their attire — in the qalansuwah, the turban, the sandals, the parting of the hair. We shall not adopt their manner of speech, nor take their kunyah. We shall not ride saddled mounts, nor hang swords upon ourselves, nor take up arms, nor carry them with us. We shall not engrave our signet rings in Arabic, nor sell wine. We shall shave the forelocks of our heads, and we shall adhere to our dress wherever we may be. We shall fasten zananir around our waists. We shall not display our crosses or our books upon any road of the Muslims or in their markets. We shall not place crosses upon our churches. We shall not strike bells in their churches in the presence of the Muslims. We shall not bring out processions or celebrate festivals, nor raise our voices with our dead, nor display fire with them upon any road of the Muslims. Our dead shall not be buried alongside theirs. We shall not acquire any slave in whom the Muslims have a share. We shall guide the Muslims, and we shall not look into their homes."" When I brought the letter to 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he added: ""And that we shall not strike any Muslim."" We have stipulated all of that upon ourselves and upon the people of our community, and we have accepted safe conduct from them. If we violate any of what we have stipulated for you, then our guarantee is void upon ourselves, and what is permissible for you regarding the people of obstinacy and dissension becomes permissible for you. [Da'if]"
18724,"(18718) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad informed us — Qabisah ibn 'Uqbah informed us — from Sufyan — from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar — from Nafi' — from Aslam, who said: 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to the commanders of the military detachments: ""Place seals upon the necks of the people of the jizyah upon their necks."" [Sahih]"
18725,"(18719) Our companions also cited as evidence what was narrated to us by Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Dawud al-'Alawi, may Allah have mercy on him — dictating — Abu Hamid ibn al-Sharqi informed us — Ahmad ibn Hafs informed me — my father informed me — Ibrahim ibn Tahman informed me — from Musa ibn 'Uqbah — from Safwan ibn Sulaym — from Ata' ibn Yasar — from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""The young shall greet the old, the passerby shall greet the seated, and the few shall greet the many."" Al-Bukhari recorded it in the Sahih, and he said: ""And Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated it."" [Sahih — agreed upon]"
18726,"(18720) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq al-Faqih informed us — al-Harith ibn Abi Usamah informed us — Ruh ibn 'Ubadah informed us — Ibn Jurayj informed me — Ziyad informed me — that Thabit, the mawla of 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd, informed him — from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him — from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, who said: ""The rider shall greet the pedestrian, the pedestrian shall greet the seated, and the few shall greet the many."" Ibn Jurayj said: And Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir saying: ""When two pedestrians meet, whichever of them initiates the greeting is more meritorious."" Al-Bukhari recorded it in the Sahih from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim from Ruh, without the statement of Jabir. And Muslim recorded it from Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn Marzuq from Ruh with it. [Sahih — agreed upon]"
18727,"(18721) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us — Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Qattan informed us — Ahmad ibn Yusuf al-Sulami informed us — Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi informed us — he said: Sufyan mentioned from 'Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""You shall meet the Jews tomorrow, so do not initiate the greeting of peace with them. If they greet you, then say: 'And upon you.'"" Al-Bukhari and Muslim both recorded it in their Sahihs from the hadith of Sufyan. [Sahih — agreed upon]"
18728,"(18722) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq both informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — Bahr ibn Nasser informed us — Ibn Wahb informed me — Malik informed me — from 'Abd Allah ibn Dinar — from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both — that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""When one of the Jews greets you, he merely says: 'Al-sam upon you' [death upon you], so say: 'Upon you.'"" Al-Bukhari recorded it in the Sahih from 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf from Malik. [Sahih — agreed upon]"
18729,"(18723) Abu al-Husayn ibn Burhan informed us — Isma'il al-Saffar informed us — Ahmad ibn Mansur informed us — 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us — Ma'mar informed us — from al-Zuhri — from 'Urwah — from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: A group of Jews entered upon the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and said: ""Al-sam upon you"" [death upon you]. 'A'ishah said: I understood it, so I said: ""Al-sam upon you, and the curse [of Allah]."" She said: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Gently, O 'A'ishah! Indeed, Allah loves gentleness in all matters."" She said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, did you not hear what they said?"" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Did I not say: 'Upon you'?"" Muslim recorded it in the Sahih from 'Abd ibn Humayd from 'Abd al-Razzaq. And al-Bukhari recorded it from another route from Ma'mar. Our colleagues said: These sunnahs cannot be applied except after having knowledge of them, and not everyone possesses such knowledge. Therefore, there must be a distinguishing mark by which they are distinguished from the Muslims. [Sahih — agreed upon]"
18730,"(18724) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi both informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — Bahr ibn Nasser informed us — Ibn Wahb informed me — 'Asim ibn Hakim informed me — from Yahya ibn Abi 'Amr al-Saybani — from his father — from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir al-Juhani, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: He passed by a man whose appearance was that of a Muslim, and he greeted him with salam. 'Uqbah returned the greeting and added: ""And upon you, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings."" The young man said to him: ""Do you know to whom you have returned the greeting?"" He said: ""Is he not a Muslim man?"" They said: ""No, but he is a Christian."" So 'Uqbah stood and followed him until he caught up with him and said: ""The mercy of Allah and His blessings are upon the believers. However, may Allah prolong your life and increase your wealth."" And we have narrated from Ibn 'Umar the meaning of this regarding the initiation of the greeting of peace."
18731,"(18725) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf said: Sufyān mentioned from Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""When you meet the polytheists on the road, do not initiate the greeting of peace with them, and force them onto the narrowest part of it.""

Muslim narrated it from another route from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1267]"
18732,"(18726) Abū Ṭāhir al-Ziyādī informed us; Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad al-Ṭūsī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥīm ibn Munīb narrated to us; Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd informed us; Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""When you meet them, do not initiate the greeting of peace with them, and force them onto the narrowest of paths.""

He said: ""This was the description of the Christians, and we understand it to refer to the polytheists.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb, from Jarīr. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18733,"(18727) Abū al-Qāsim Zayd ibn Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-ʿAlawī and Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Najjār al-Muqriʾ in Kūfah informed us; they both said: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn Ḥammād, from Asbāṭ, from Simāk, from ʿIyāḍ al-Ashʿarī, from Abū Mūsá (may Allah be pleased with him), that ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) instructed him to bring to him what he had recorded and what he had given in a single piece of leather. Abū Mūsá had a Christian secretary who would bring that to him. ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) was astonished and said: ""Indeed, this one is a ḥāfiẓ (one who has memorized well)."" He said: ""We have a letter in the mosque that came from Shām; call him so that he may read it."" Abū Mūsá said: ""He is not able to enter the mosque."" ʿUmar said: ""Is he in a state of janābah?"" He said: ""No, rather he is a Christian."" Abū Mūsá said: ""He rebuked me and struck my thigh and said: 'Remove him,' and he recited: {O you who believe, do not take the Jews and the Christians as allies; they are allies of one another. And whoever among you takes them as allies, then indeed he is of them. Indeed, Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people} [Al-Māʾidah 51]."" And he mentioned the ḥadīth. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18734,"(18728) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsá narrated to us; Ashʿath ibn Shuʿbah informed us; Arṭāh ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us; he said: I heard Ḥakīm ibn ʿUmayr, Abū al-Aḥwaṣ, narrating from al-ʿIrbāḍ ibn Sāriyah al-Sulamī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: We arrived with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) at Khaybar, and with him were those of his Companions who were with him. The master of Khaybar was a defiant and wicked man. He came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and said: ""O Muhammad, is it your right that you slaughter our donkeys, eat our fruits, and strike our women?"" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) became angry and said: ""O Ibn ʿAwf, mount your horse and announce: 'Indeed, Paradise is not permissible except for a believer,' and that they should gather for the prayer."" He said: They gathered, and the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) led them in prayer. Then he stood and said: ""Does any one of you, reclining upon his couch, think that Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has not forbidden anything except what is in this Qurʾān? Nay, by Allah, I have commanded, admonished, and prohibited from things whose prohibition is equal to that of the Qurʾān or even greater. Indeed, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has not made it permissible for you to enter the homes of the People of the Book without their permission, nor to strike their women, nor to eat their fruits when they have given you what is due from them."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18735,"(18729) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Shādhān al-Jawharī narrated to us; Muʿāwiyah ibn ʿAmr narrated to us; Zāʾidah narrated to us; Manṣūr, from Hilāl ibn Yasāf, from a man from Thaqīf, from a man from Juhaynah, from among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""You will perhaps fight a people and overcome them, and they will ransom themselves with their wealth, keeping themselves and their children apart, and you will make a peace treaty with them upon terms. Do not take from them beyond that, for it is not permissible for you.""

The Thaqafī said: ""I accompanied the Juhaynī on a campaign or a journey, and he was among the most protective of people regarding the rights of enemies.""

Abū Dāwūd narrated it from the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAwanah, from Manṣūr. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18736,"(18730) Abū ʬAlī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Musaddad and Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr said: Abū ʿAwanah narrated to us, from Manṣūr, from Hilāl, from a man from Thaqīf, from a man from Juhaynah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Perhaps you will fight a people and overcome them, and they will protect themselves from you with their wealth, keeping themselves and their children apart.""

Saʿīd said in his ḥadīth: ""And they will make a peace treaty with you upon terms."" Then they both agreed: ""So do not take from them beyond that, for it is not permissible for you."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18737,"(18731) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Mazzakkī said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Abū Ṣakhr al-Madanī informed me that Ṣafwān ibn Sulaym informed him, from thirty of the sons of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), from their fathers, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), who said: ""Beware: whoever wrongs a muʿāhad (one under a covenant of protection), diminishes his rights, burdens him beyond his capacity, or takes something from him without his goodwill, I will be his adversary on the Day of Resurrection."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) pointed with his finger toward his chest. ""And whoever kills a muʿāhad who has the protection of Allah and the protection of His Messenger, Allah has forbidden for him the fragrance of Paradise, even though its fragrance can be detected from a distance of seventy autumns."" [Ḥasan]"
18738,"(18732) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed him; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; al-Manīʿī and al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān said: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us, from al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAmr, from Mujāhid, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Whoever kills a muʿāhad unjustly shall not smell the fragrance of Paradise, even though its fragrance can be detected from a distance of forty years.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qays ibn Ḥafṣ, from ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād, from al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAmr.

Likewise, ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd al-Ghaffār narrated it from al-Ḥasan. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18739,"(18733) Marwān ibn Muʿāwiyah al-Fazārī contradicted him and narrated it from al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAmr, from Mujāhid, from Junādah ibn Abī Umayyah, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Whoever kills a person from the People of the Covenant shall not smell the fragrance of Paradise, even though its fragrance can be detected from such and such a distance.""

Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn Ziyād narrated to us; Ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us; Marwān ibn Muʿāwiyah narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAmr narrated to us, and he mentioned it. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18740,"(18734) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Firyābī narrated to us; Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated to us, from Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd; al-Ḥakam ibn al-Aʿraj narrated to me, from al-Ashʿath ibn Tharmalah al-ʿIjlī, from Abū Bakrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Whoever kills a muʿāhad soul without it being lawful to do so, Allah has forbidden Paradise for him to smell its fragrance."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18741,"(18735) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Yazīd informed me, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUrwah, that Hishām ibn Ḥakīm (may Allah be pleased with him) found a man in Ḥimṣ who was exposing people of the Copts to the sun in the collection of the jizyah. He said: ""What is this? I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: 'Indeed, Allah will punish those who punish people in this world.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī al-Ṭāhir, from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2613]"
18742,"(18736) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us: Jaʿfar al-Aḥmar narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr narrated to us: A man from Thaqīf informed me, saying: ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) appointed me over Buzurj Sābūr and said: ""Do not strike a man with a whip for the collection of a single dirham, and do not sell them their provisions, nor winter or summer clothing, nor a beast upon which they work, and do not station a man standing for the collection of a dirham."" I said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, then I shall return to you just as I left you."" He said: ""And if you return just as you left, woe to you! We have been commanded only to take from them the surplus (al-ʿafw), meaning the excess (al-faḍl)."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18743,"(18737) Abū Saʿīd narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: al-Ḥasan narrated to us: Yaḥyā narrated to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated, from Maʿmar, from Ibn Ṭāwūs, from his father, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both): Ibrāhīm asked him about the wealth of the People of the Dhimmah. Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: ""The surplus (al-ʿafw), meaning the excess (al-faḍl)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18744,"(18738) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Bashshār narrated to us: Sufyān narrated, from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr, from one who heard Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) say: I entered upon ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) while he was turning his hand over like this. I said to him: ""What is the matter with you, O Commander of the Faithful?"" He said: ""An agent of ours in Iraq has mixed the prices of wine and swine into the fayʾ (war booty) of the Muslims. Do you not know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: 'Allah has cursed the Jews; fats were forbidden upon them, so they melted them down, sold them, and consumed their prices.'"" Sufyān said: ""Do not take wine and swine in their jizyah, but leave them to sell them. When they have sold them, then take their prices in their jizyah."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18745,"(18739) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us: Ḥarmalah ibn ʿImrān al-Tujībī informed me, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Shumāsah al-Mahrī, who said: I heard Abū Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""You will conquer a land in which the qīrāṭ is mentioned. Treat its people well, for they have a covenant (dhimmah) and kinship ties (raḥim). When you see two men fighting over the placement of a single brick, then leave it."" He said: He passed by Rabīʿah and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Shuraḥbīl ibn Ḥasanah disputing over the placement of a brick, so he left it.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Hārūn al-Aylī, from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 2543]"
18746,"(18740) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥmawayh al-ʿAskarī narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī narrated to us: Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us: Abū Jamrah narrated to us: I heard Juwayriyah ibn Qudāmah al-Tamīmī say: I performed Ḥajj and came to Madīnah. I heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) delivering a sermon and saying: ""I saw a rooster peck me once or twice."" He said: It was only a Friday or thereabouts until he was struck (with the wound). He then gave permission to the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), then to the people of Madīnah, then to the people of Shām, then to the people of Iraq. We were among the last to enter. There was a black turban or a black garment bound over his wound, and blood was flowing. We said: ""Give us your last testament, O Commander of the Faithful."" He said: ""I enjoin you to hold fast to the Book of Allah, for you will never go astray so long as you follow it. I enjoin you regarding the Muhājirūn, for people will increase and decrease (in number). I enjoin you regarding the Anṣār, for they are the branch of Islam to which refuge is sought. I enjoin you regarding the Bedouins (al-Aʿrāb), for they are your origin and your source."" On another occasion he said: ""For they are your brothers and the enemies of your enemies."" And he said: ""I enjoin you regarding the covenant of Allah (dhimmat Allāh), for they are under the covenant of your Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and the sustenance of your dependents."" Then he said: ""Rise and depart from me.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon]"
18747,"(18741) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated, from Ḥuṣayn ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿAmr ibn Maymūn, from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him), that he said: ""I enjoin the caliph after me to treat the People of the Dhimmah well: that their covenant be fulfilled, that those behind them be fought (if they break it), and that they not be burdened beyond their capacity.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus, from Abī Bakr ibn ʿAyyāsh. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in numerous places]"
18748,"(18742) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Muḥammad al-Muzanī informed me: ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā informed us: Abū al-Yamān informed me: Shuʿayb informed me, from al-Zuhrī: Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed me that Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) sent me with those who made the proclamation on the Day of Sacrifice (Yawm al-Naḥr) in Minā: that no polytheist should perform Ḥajj after that year, and that no one should circumambulate the House naked. The Day of the Greater Ḥajj (Yawm al-Ḥajj al-Akbar) is the Day of Sacrifice. The ""Greater Ḥajj"" was only called so because people referred to ʿUmrah as the ""Lesser Ḥajj."" Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) announced to the people that year. No polytheist performed Ḥajj the following year, the year in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) performed the Farewell Pilgrimage. Allah revealed in the year in which Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) made the proclamation to the polytheists: {O you who believe, indeed the polytheists are impure, so let them not approach al-Masjid al-Ḥarām after this year of theirs} [al-Tawbah: 28]. And he mentioned the rest of the hadith.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī al-Yamān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon]"
18749,"(18743) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Usāmah informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Mūsā informed us: Abū Khaythamah Zuhayr narrated to us: Abū Isḥāq narrated, from Zayd ibn Yuthayʿ, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""I was sent to the people of Makkah with four (proclamations): no one should circumambulate the Kaʿbah naked; no polytheist should approach al-Masjid al-Ḥarām after that year; no soul shall enter Paradise except a believing soul; and whoever has a covenant with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), his covenant shall last for its full duration."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18750,"(18744) Abū Naṣr informed us: Abū Manṣūr al-Naḍrūwī informed us: Aḥmad ibn Najdah informed us: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr informed us: Sufyān narrated, from Abī Isḥāq al-Hamdānī, from Zayd ibn Yuthayʿ, who said: ""We asked ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him): 'With what were you sent?' He said: 'With four (proclamations).' He mentioned them, except that he said: 'And no Muslim and polytheist shall gather together in Ḥajj after this year of theirs.'"" And he added: ""And whoever has no covenant, then it is for four months."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded above]"
18751,"(18745) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Isfarāʾīnī informed us, Mūsā ibn Hārūn narrated to us, al-Murrār ibn Ḥamawayh al-Hamadhānī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Kinānī narrated to us — Mūsā said: and he was Abū Ghassān al-Kinānī — from Mālik, from Nāʿif, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: When I was summoned to Khaybar, ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) stood among the people and delivered a sermon, saying: ""Indeed, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) employed the Jews of Khaybar over their properties and said: 'We shall keep you settled as long as Allah keeps you settled.' And ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar went out to his property there, and he was attacked at night, and his hands were broken. We had no enemy there other than them, and they were our suspects. I resolved to expel them. When he was determined upon that, one of the Banī Abī al-Ḥuqayq came to him and said: 'O Commander of the Faithful, will you expel us when Muḥammad settled us and employed us over the properties and stipulated that for us?' ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: 'Do you think I have forgotten the words of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family): ""What will be your state when you are expelled from Khaybar and your young she-camel carries you night after night?""' So he expelled them and gave them the value of their fruit in wealth, camels, and goods — saddles, ropes, and other things.""

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Aḥmad, who is al-Murrār ibn Ḥamawayh. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18752,"(18746) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Bisṭāmī informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, al-Qāsim ibn Zakariyyā informed us, Ibn Bazīʿ and Abū al-Ashʿath both said: al-Fuḍayl ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah informed us, Nāʿif informed me, from Ibn ʿUmar: that ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) expelled the Jews and the Christians from the land of the Ḥijāz. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), when he conquered Khaybar, had intended to expel the Jews from it. The land, when conquered, belonged to Allah, His Messenger, and the Muslims. The Jews asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) to keep them settled on it on the condition that they would tend the crops and they would have half of the fruit. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""We shall keep you settled on that for as long as we wish."" So they were kept settled, and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) expelled them during his caliphate to Taymāʾ and Arīḥā.

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī al-Ashʿath Aḥmad ibn al-Miqdām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
18753,"(18747) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us by dictation, Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī narrated to us in Makkah, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us, from Sulaymān ibn Abī Muslim, who said: I heard Saʿīd ibn Jubayr say: I heard Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with both of them) say: ""Thursday — and what a Thursday!"" Then he wept and said: ""The illness of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) intensified, and he said: 'Bring me something so that I may write for you a document after which you will never go astray.' They disputed, and it is not fitting for there to be disputing in the presence of a Prophet. He said: 'Leave me, for that which I am in is better than that to which you call me.' And he commanded them with three things: 'Expel the polytheists from the Arabian Peninsula, and treat the delegations as I used to treat them.' The third thing, I have forgotten.""

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah and others, from Sufyān; and Muslim narrated it from Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr and Qutaybah and others, from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
18754,"(18748) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf narrated to us, Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yaḥyā al-Zuhrī al-Qāḍī narrated to us in Makkah, Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṣāʾigh narrated to us. Both said: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us, Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated to us, from Abī al-Zubayr, from Jābir (may Allah be pleased with him): that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""If I live, I shall surely expel the Jews and the Christians from the Arabian Peninsula, until I leave none in it but Muslims.""

It was narrated by Muslim in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb, from Rūḥ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1767]"
18755,"(18749) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd informed us, from Ibrāhīm ibn Maymūn, Saʿīd ibn Samurah ibn Jundub narrated to us, from his father, from Abī ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The last thing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) spoke was: ""Expel the Jews of the Ḥijāz and the people of Najrān from the Arabian Peninsula, and know that the worst of people are those who have taken their graves as places of prostration."" [Ḥasan]"
18756,"(18750) Abū Aḥmad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Mihrajānī al-ʿAdl informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr informed us, Mālik informed us, from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Ḥakīm, that he heard ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz say: ""It has reached me that among the last things the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) spoke was that he said: 'May Allah curse the Jews and the Christians who have taken the graves of their prophets as places of prostration. Let there not remain two religions in the land of Arabia.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18757,"(18751) He said: And Mālik narrated to us, from Ibn Shihāb, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Two religions shall not coexist in the Arabian Peninsula.""

Mālik said: Ibn Shihāb said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) investigated that matter until certainty came to him from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) that he said: ""Two religions shall not coexist in the Arabian Peninsula."" So he expelled the Jews of Khaybar.

Mālik said: And ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) had expelled the Jews of Najrān and Fadak. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18758,"(18752) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAtakī narrated to us, Jarīr narrated to us, from Qābūs ibn Abī Ẓabyān, from his father, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""There shall not be two qiblahs in one land.""

And it was narrated to us from Abī Kudaynah, from Qābūs ibn Abī Ẓabyān, with his chain: ""Two qiblahs shall not coexist in the Arabian Peninsula.""

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) had expelled the Jews of Banī al-Naḍīr, and then the Jews of Madīnah.

And it is narrated in the ḥadīth of Ibn Jurayj, from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, from Nāʿif, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him). [Ḍaʿīf]"
18759,"(18753) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū al-Sarī Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥāmid narrated to us in al-Ṭabarān, Aḥmad ibn Dāwūd al-Ḥanẓalī narrated to us, Suwayd ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, Ḥafṣ ibn Mayṣarah narrated to us, from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, from Nāʿif, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with both of them): that the Jews of Banī al-Naḍīr and Qurayẓah waged war against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) expelled Banī al-Naḍīr and kept Qurayẓah settled. He mentioned the ḥadīth and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) expelled all the Jews of Madīnah — Banī Qaynuqāʿ, who were the people of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Salām, and Banī Ḥārithah — and every Jew who was in Madīnah. The Jews, the Christians, and other than them among the disbelievers were not permitted to remain in it for more than three days during the time of ʿUmar, and I do not know whether he used to do that with them or not.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1766]"
18760,"(18754) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Baḥr ibn Naṣr al-Khawlānī narrated to us. He said: It was read to Shuʿayb ibn al-Layth: ""Your father informed you,"" he said: Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd narrated to me, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: While we were sitting in the mosque, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) came out to us and said: ""Go to the Jews."" So we went with him until we reached the Bayt al-Midras. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) stood and called out to them, saying: ""O company of Jews, embrace Islam and you will be safe."" They said: ""You have delivered the message, O Abā al-Qāsim."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said to them: ""That is what I desire — embrace Islam and you will be safe."" They said: ""You have delivered the message, O Abā al-Qāsim."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said to them: ""That is what I desire."" Then he said it a third time and said: ""Know that the land belongs to Allah and to His Messenger, and I intend to expel you from this land. So whoever among you finds any of his property, let him sell it; otherwise, know that the land belongs to Allah and to His Messenger.""

Al-Bukhārī extracted it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf, and Muslim extracted it from Qutaybah, both from al-Layth ibn Saʿd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
18761,"(18755) Abū ʿAliyy al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Maḥmūd ibn Khālid narrated to us; ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid narrated to us, who said: Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz said: The Island of Arabia (Jazīrat al-ʿArab) extends from al-Wādī to the farthest reaches of Yemen, to the borders of Iraq, to the sea. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Sijistānī.]"
18762,"(18756) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan al-Kārizī informed us; ʿAlīyy ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us, from Abī ʿUbayd, from Abī ʿUbaydah, who said: The Island of Arabia is from the well of Abū Mūsā to the farthest reaches of Yemen in length, and as for its breadth, it is from the sands of Yabrīn to the end of al-Samāwah.

And al-Aṣmaʿī said: The Island of Arabia extends from the farthest reaches of ʿAdan Abyan to the coastal region of Iraq in length, and as for its breadth, it is from Jeddah and what adjoins it along the seacoast to the borders of Shām. [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
18763,"(18757) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us; Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us, who said: Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, meaning al-Muqriʾ, said: The Island of Arabia extends from al-Qādisiyyah to the depths of ʿAdan, to al-Baḥrayn. [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
18764,"(18758) Abū ʿAliyy al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, who said: It was recited to al-Ḥārith ibn Miskīn while I was present: Ashhab ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed you, who said: Mālik said: ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) expelled the people of Najrān, but he did not expel the people of Taymāʾ, because it is not from the lands of Arabia. As for al-Wādī, I believe that the Jews therein were not expelled because they did not consider it to be from the land of the Arabs. [Ḍaʿīf.]"
18765,"(18759) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us; al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, who said: al-Shāfiʿī said: If those from whom the jizyah is taken were asked to pay it and the ruling were applied to them that they must reside in the Ḥijāz, that would not be permissible for them. And the Ḥijāz comprises Makkah, al-Madīnah, al-Yamāmah, and all their surrounding districts.

Al-Shāfiʿī said: I do not know of anyone who expelled any of the People of the Dhimmah from Yemen, although there were dhimmīs there. Yemen is not part of the Ḥijāz, so no one expelled them from Yemen, and there is no harm in making a treaty with them for their continued residence in Yemen.

The Sheikh said: They considered Yemen to be from the land of the Arabs, and the expulsion befell the people of Najrān, while the covenant of the people of the Ḥijāz was distinct from the covenant of the people of Yemen, because Yemen is not part of the Ḥijāz, and nor did they consider it to be from the land of the Arabs. In the hadith there is a specification, and in the hadith of Samurah from Abī ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ (may Allah be pleased with him) there is evidence or quasi-evidence for the point of specification. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
18766,"(18760) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn al-Jaham narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faraj narrated to us; al-Wāqidī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us, from al-Zuhrī, from Abī Salamah, from Abī Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) from Khaybar to Wādī al-Qurā, and he mentioned the hadith concerning the conquest of Wādī al-Qurā. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) stayed in Wādī al-Qurā for four days and divided what was obtained among his Companions in Wādī al-Qurā, and he left the land and the date palms in the hands of the Jews and employed them on it. When ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) came to power, he expelled the Jews of Khaybar and Fadak, but he did not expel the people of Taymāʾ and Wādī al-Qurā, because both of them fall within the land of Shām. And we hold that what lies on the side of Wādī al-Qurā toward al-Madīnah is part of the Ḥijāz, and that what lies beyond that is part of Shām.

The Sheikh said: This last statement, I believe it is from the words of al-Wāqidī. [Very weak (Ḍaʿīf jiddan).]"
18767,"(18761) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ said: I heard Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAnbarī say: I heard Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ, meaning al-Naysābūrī, say: I heard ʿAlīyy ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Rāzī say: I heard ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Yaḥyā al-Madanī say: I heard Mālik ibn Anas say: The Island of Arabia is al-Madīnah, Makkah, and Yemen. As for Egypt, it is from the lands of the Maghrib; Shām is from the lands of the Romans; and Iraq is from the lands of Persia. [Mawḍūʿ (fabricated).]"
18768,"(18762) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū ʿAmr Ismāʿīl ibn Najayd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik narrated from Nāfiʿ, from Aslam, the freedman of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb: that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) set for the Jews, the Christians, and the Magians a stay of three nights in al-Madīnah, during which they could trade and attend to their needs, and none of them was permitted to stay longer than three nights. [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
18769,"(18763) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Bilāl narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn al-Rabīʿ al-Makkī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Hishām, from Anas ibn Sīrīn, who said: Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) sent me to collect the ʿushr (tenth), so I said: ""You are sending me to collect the ʿushr from among your duties?"" He said: ""Are you not pleased that I appoint you to the same position to which ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) appointed me? He commanded me to take from the Muslims a quarter of the tenth (2.5%), from the People of the Dhimmah half of the tenth (5%), and from those who have no dhimmah the full tenth (10%)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
18770,"(18764) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Muʿādh ibn Muʿādh narrated from Ibn ʿAwn, from Anas ibn Sīrīn, who said: Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) sent for me, so I delayed in responding to him. Then he sent for me again, so I came to him, and he said: ""I believe that if I were to command you to bite upon such-and-such a stone seeking my pleasure, you would do it. I have chosen for you the best of duties, yet you disliked it. I am writing for you the practice (sunnah) of ʿUmar."" I said: ""Then write for me the practice of ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him)."" So he wrote: ""From the Muslims, one dirham from every forty dirhams; from the People of the Dhimmah, one dirham from every twenty dirhams; and from those who have no dhimmah, one dirham from every ten dirhams."" He said: I asked: ""Who are those who have no dhimmah?"" He said: ""The Romans (al-Rūm), who used to come to Shām."" [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
18771,"(18765) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; he said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Aḥmad ibn Hārūn al-Faqīh narrated to us; he said: Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us; he said: al-Muqriʾ narrated to us; he said: Abū Ḥanīfah narrated to us, from al-Haytham — who was a money-changer (ṣayrafī) in Kūfah — from Anas ibn Sīrīn, the brother of Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu) appointed Anas ibn Mālik over the ṣadaqah of al-Baṣrah. Anas ibn Mālik then said to me: ""Shall I send you to the task to which ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu) has appointed me?"" I said: ""I will not work for you until you write down for me the covenant (ʿahd) of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh that he entrusted to you."" So he wrote for me: ""Take from the wealth of the Muslims one-fortieth (quarter of the ʿushr), and from the wealth of the People of the Dhimmī when they trade with it across different cities, half of the ʿushr (one-twentieth), and from the wealth of the People of War, the ʿushr (one-tenth)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18772,"(18766) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; he said: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; he said: al-Shāfiʿī informed us; he said: Mālik informed us, from Ibn Shihāb, from Sālim, from his father, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu) used to take from the Nabat (Nabataean agriculturalists) from wheat and oil half of the ʿushr (one-twentieth), intending thereby that they would bring more goods to al-Madīnah, and he used to take from the qaṭānīyah (grain produce) the ʿushr (one-tenth). [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18773,"(18767) He said: al-Shāfiʿī informed us; he said: Mālik informed us, from Ibn Shihāb, from al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd, who said: I used to work alongside ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah in the market of al-Madīnah during the time of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu), and he used to take from the Nabat the ʿushr (one-tenth). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Mālik]"
18774,"(18768) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrānī informed us; he said: Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us; he said: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; he said: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; he said: Mālik narrated to us, that he asked Ibn Shihāb: ""On what basis did ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu) take the ʿushr (one-tenth) from the Nabat?"" He said: ""That was taken from them during the Jāhiliyyah, so ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu) made it binding upon them."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18775,"(18769) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; he said: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us; he said: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us; he said: Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us; he said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us, from Maʿmar, from al-Zuhrī, from al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd, who said: I used to collect the ʿushr alongside ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah during the time of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu), and he used to take from the People of the Dhimmī half of the ʿushr (one-twentieth) on their wealth in whatever they traded. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18776,"(18770) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; he said: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us; he said: al-Ḥasan narrated to us; he said: Yaḥyā narrated to us; he said: Qays narrated to us, from ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal, from al-Ḥasan, who said: Abū Mūsā wrote to ʿUmar (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhumā): ""When the Muslim merchants enter the Dār al-Ḥarb, the ʿushr (one-tenth) is taken from them."" So ʿUmar wrote back to him: ""Take from them, when they come to us, the same — the ʿushr (one-tenth). And take from the merchants of the People of the Dhimmī half of the ʿushr (one-twentieth), and from the Muslims, from every two hundred, five; and whatever exceeds that, then from every forty dirhams, one dirham."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18777,"(18771) Abū Saʿīd informed us; he said: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us; he said: al-Ḥasan narrated to us; he said: Yaḥyā narrated to us; he said: Qays ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, from Mughallis, from Muqātil ibn Ḥayyān, from Abī Mijlaz, from Ziyād ibn Ḥudayr, who said: I wrote to ʿUmar regarding some people from the People of War who enter our land — the land of Islam — and settle there. So ʿUmar (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu) wrote to me: ""If they settle for six months, then take from them the ʿushr (one-tenth), and if they settle for a year, then take from them half of the ʿushr (one-twentieth)."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18778,"(18772) Abū Saʿīd informed us; he said: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us; he said: al-Ḥasan narrated to us; he said: Yaḥyā narrated to us; he said: Sufyān ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, from Khālid ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-ʿAbsī, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maʿqil, from Ziyād ibn Ḥudayr, who said: ""We used not to take the ʿushr from a Muslim or a muʿāhid (one under treaty)."" He (the narrator) said: ""So I asked: 'From whom did you take the ʿushr?'"" He said: ""From the merchants of the People of War, just as they would take the ʿushr from us when we went to them."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18779,"(18773) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; he said: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us; he said: Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us; he said: Abū Bakr ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated to us, from Nuṣayr, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn al-Sāʾib, from Ḥarb ibn ʿUbayd Allāh, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallā Allāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""There is no ʿushr (one-tenth) upon the Muslims; the ʿushr is only upon the Jews and the Christians.""

Al-ʿAbbās said: ""Thus said Aḥmad ibn Yūnus — 'from his grandfather' — and in this narration it is 'from his father, from his grandfather.' Al-Bukhārī mentioned it in al-Tārīkh without mentioning his father. The rest of its chains have been discussed, and we have mentioned the ḥadīth of ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz on this matter in the Book of Zakāh."" [Ḍaʿīf. They counted it under number 18703]"
18780,"(18774) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; he said: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us; he said: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us; he said: Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us; he said: Abū Bakr ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated to us, from Abī Ḥuṣayn, from Ziyād ibn Ḥudayr, who said: ""I used to take the ʿushr from Banī Taghlib every time they came and went. Then an elder from among them went to ʿUmar and said: 'Indeed, Ziyād takes the ʿushr from us every time we come and go.' He (ʿUmar) said: 'That will suffice.' Then the elder came to ʿUmar (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu) again while ʿUmar was among a group of people, and said: 'O Amīr al-Muʾminīn, I am the Christian elder.' ʿUmar (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu) said: 'And I am the elder Ḥanīf (upright monotheist). You have been sufficed.'"" He (the narrator) said: ""Then he wrote to me: 'Do not take the ʿushr from them in a year except once.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Ibn Ādam]"
18781,"(18775) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us, Abū ʿAmr ibn Nujayd informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Mālik narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from Ruzayq ibn Ḥayyān, that ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz wrote to him: ""Whoever of the People of the Dhimmah passes by you, take from the wealth they circulate in trade one dinar from every twenty dinars. Whatever falls short shall be calculated accordingly, until it reaches ten dinars. If it falls short by one-third of a dinar, then leave it and take nothing from it. And write for them a document for what you take from them until the same time next year."" [Ḥasan]"
18782,"(18776) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-ʿUṭāridī narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Saʿd ibn Ṭāriq narrated to me from Salamah ibn Nuʿaym ibn Masʿūd, from his father, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) when the envoy of Musaylimah the Liar came to him with his book, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said to them both: ""You two say the same as he says."" They said: ""Yes."" He said: ""By Allah, were it not that envoys are not killed, I would strike your necks."" [Ḥasan]"
18783,"(18777) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from Abī Isḥāq, from Ḥārithah ibn Muḍarrib, that he came to ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: ""There is no bond of kinship between me and any of the Arabs. I passed by the mosque of Banī Ḥanīfah and found them believing in Musaylimah."" So ʿAbd Allāh sent for them and they were brought to him. He called them to repentance, except Ibn al-Nawwāḥah. He said to him: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: 'Were it not that you are an envoy, I would strike your neck.' But today you are not an envoy."" So he ordered Qaraẓah ibn Kaʿb to strike his neck in the marketplace. Then he said: ""Whoever wishes to see Ibn al-Nawwāḥah slain in the marketplace, let him look."" [Ḍaʿīf; previously referenced as 16886]"
18784,"(18778) Abū Ṭāhir the Faqīh informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, Abū ʢĀṣim narrated from Sufyān, from ʢĀṣim, from Abī Wāʾil, from ʿAbd Allāh (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said to Ibn al-Nawwāḥah: ""Were it not that you are an envoy, I would kill you."" [Ḥasan]"
18785,"(18779) Abū Ṭāhir informed us, Abū Bakr narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us, al-Masʿūdī narrated from ʢĀṣim, from Abī Wāʾil, from ʿAbd Allāh (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""The established Sunnah is that envoys are not killed."""
18786,"(18780) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq the Faqīh informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us. He said: I said to Mālik ibn Anas: ""Did Ibn Shihāb narrate to you from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) entered Makkah on the day of the Conquest with a helmet on his head? When he removed it, a man came and said: 'Ibn Khaṭal is clinging to the curtains of the Kaʿbah.' He said: 'Kill him.'"" He said: ""Yes.""

Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf, from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ; agreed upon]"
18787,"(18781) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us, Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq the just merchant al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Naṣr narrated to us, ʿAmr ibn Ṭalḥah al-Qannād narrated to us, Asbāṭ ibn Naṣr narrated from al-Suddī, from Muṣʿab ibn Saʿd, from his father, who said: ""On the day of the Conquest of Makkah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) granted safety to all the people except four men and two women, and he said: 'Kill them even if you find them clinging to the curtains of the Kaʿbah.' They were: ʿIkrimah ibn Abī Jahl, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Khaṭal, Miqyas ibn Ṣubābah, and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Sarḥ."" [Ḥasan]"
18788,"(18782) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith the Faqīh informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar the Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥammād narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn Ḥarb ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, Zayd ibn al-Ḥubāb narrated to us, ʿUmar ibn ʿUthmān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Saʿīd al-Makhzūmī narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said on the day of the Conquest of Makkah: ""Four persons shall not be granted safety, whether in the Ḥill or the Ḥaram: al-Ḥuwayrith ibn Nuqayd, Miqyas, Hilāl ibn Khaṭal, and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Sarḥ.""

""As for al-Ḥuwayrith, ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) killed him. As for Miqyas, a cousin of his killed him. As for Hilāl ibn Khaṭal, al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) killed him. As for ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Sarḥ, ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with him) granted him safety, for he was his foster-brother. There were two singing-girls belonging to Miqyas who used to sing satirical verses against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). One of them was killed, and the other escaped and later accepted Islam."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18789,"(18783) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh the litterateur informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us, Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Saʿād narrated from Saʿīd al-Maqburī, from Abī Shuraīḥ al-ʿAdawī, that he said to ʿAmr ibn Saʿd, who was sending delegations to Makkah: ""Permit me, O Commander, to relate to you a statement which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) delivered the day after the Conquest. My ears heard it, my heart retained it, and my eyes beheld him when he spoke it. He praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: 'Indeed, Makkah was sanctified by Allah, not by people. It is not permissible for anyone who believes in Allah and the Last Day to shed blood therein or to cut down a tree therein. If anyone makes an exception on account of the fighting of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), say: ""Allah permitted His Messenger but did not permit you. He permitted me only for an hour of the day, and its sanctity has returned today as it was yesterday. Let the one who is present convey to the one who is absent.'""

It was said to Abī Shuraīḥ: ""What did ʿAmr say to you?"" He said: ""ʿAmr said: 'I know that better than you, O Abā Shuraīḥ. Indeed, the Ḥaram does not grant refuge to a sinner, nor to one who flees after committing murder, nor to one who flees after causing corruption.'""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ; agreed upon]"
18790,"(18784) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us, al-Rabīʿ narrated, and al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""The meaning of that, and Allah knows best, is that it was not made lawful to wage war against it until it became like any other place. Indeed, the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) ordered the killing of Abī Sufyān in a house in Makkah by stealth, if it could be done, at the time when ʿĀṣim ibn Thābit and Khubayb were killed. This indicates that it does not prevent anyone from a duty incumbent upon him; it only prevents the waging of war against it, just as war is waged against other places."""
18791,"(18785) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Battah al-Isbahani informed us; al-Hasan ibn al-Jaham narrated to us; al-Husayn ibn al-Faraj narrated to us; al-Waqidi narrated to me; Ibrahim ibn Ja'far narrated from his father. Al-Waqidi said: and 'Abdullah ibn Abi 'Ubaydah narrated to us from Ja'far ibn 'Amr ibn Umayyah al-Damri.

(Chain variant) He said: and 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated from 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Abi 'Awn. And some of them added upon others in wording. He mentioned a story regarding Abu Sufyan's dispatch of someone to kill Muhammad (peace be upon him and his family) by treachery, and that Allah, the Most High, informed His Prophet thereof, and the man accepted Islam. He said: then the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) said to 'Amr ibn Umayyah al-Damri and Salamah ibn Aslam ibn Harish: ""Go forth until you reach Abu Sufyan ibn Harb; if you find him off guard, then kill him.""

Then he mentioned a story regarding Mu'awiyah's sighting of 'Amr and his informing his father of that, and that 'Amr ibn Umayyah and Salamah ibn Aslam took refuge on the mountain and concealed themselves in a cave. Then 'Amr ibn Umayyah went out and killed 'Ubaydullah ibn Malik, the nephew of Talhah ibn 'Ubaydullah, and came to Khubayb ibn 'Adi while he was crucified, took him down, and heaped earth over him. Then he mentioned their return, each separately, to Madinah. [Da'if]"
18792,"(18786) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran al-'Adl informed us in Baghdad; Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Amr informed us; Muhammad ibn 'Ubaydullah ibn Yazid narrated to us; Ishaq al-Azraq narrated to us; Zakariyya narrated from al-Sha'bi; from al-Harith ibn Malik ibn Bursa'ah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) said on the day of the conquest of Makkah: ""It shall not be invaded after this until the Day of Resurrection."" [Sahih]"
18793,"(18787) 'Abdullah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar informed us in Baghdad; Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us; Ahmad ibn Mansur narrated to us; 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us; Ma'mar informed us; from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: Whoever commits murder or theft in the non-sacred territory (al-hill) and then enters the Sacred Sanctuary (al-Haram), he shall not be sat with, nor spoken to, nor given refuge. He shall be called upon to leave, and when he leaves, that which he committed shall be executed upon him. If he commits murder or theft in the non-sacred territory and is then brought into the Sacred Sanctuary, and they wish to execute upon him that which he committed, they shall expel him from the Sacred Sanctuary to the non-sacred territory. And if he commits murder or theft within the Sacred Sanctuary, the punishment shall be executed upon him within the Sacred Sanctuary.

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: This is a view of Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), and we have left it due to the explicit evidences that have come regarding the execution of the hudud without specifying the Sacred Sanctuary as a place of exemption from them by the possessor of the Sacred Law. And Allah knows best. [Sahih]"
18794,"(18788) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Sahl Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Qattan informed us; Yahya ibn Ja'far narrated to us; 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us; Sa'id narrated from Qatadah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him): that Ukidir Dumah sent as a gift to the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) a jubbah (long outer garment), and he wore it. And he mentioned the hadith. Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih, and he said: ""And Sa'id narrated from Qatadah."" [Sahih. Agreed upon]"
18795,"(18789) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us; 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us; 'Arim narrated to us; Mu'tamir narrated to us.

(Chain variant) And Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed me; Abu al-Nadr al-Faqih informed us; Muhammad ibn Nasr al-Marwazi narrated to us; 'Ubaydullah ibn Mu'adh narrated to us; al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us; my father narrated to us; from Abu 'Uthman, who said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Bakr (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrated to me, saying: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family), one hundred and thirty in number. The Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) said: ""Does any of you have food?"" There was a man who had a sa' of food or something close to it, and it was kneaded. Then a polytheist came, tall and disheveled, driving sheep. He said: ""Is it a sale, a gift, or a grant?"" He said: ""Rather, a sale."" He said: ""So he bought a sheep from them, and it was prepared. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) ordered that the liver be roasted. By Allah, there was not one among the one hundred and thirty except that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) cut for him a portion from its liver—if he was present, he gave it to him, and if he was absent, he set it aside for him."" He said: ""And he made from it two platters."" He said: ""So we all ate and were satisfied, and there remained in the two platters, so we loaded it upon the camel, or as he said.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from 'Arim, and Muslim narrated it from 'Ubaydullah ibn Mu'adh. [Sahih. Agreed upon]"
18796,"(18790) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu al-Hasan Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-'Anazi narrated to us by dictation; 'Uthman ibn Sa'id al-Darimi narrated to us; Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us; Wuhaib narrated from 'Amr ibn Yahya al-Ansari; from al-'Abbas al-Sa'idi; from Abu Humayd al-Sa'idi (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I traveled with the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) to Tabuk. And he mentioned the hadith, in which he said: The king of Aylah sent as a gift to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) a white mule. The Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) clothed him with a burdah (cloak) and wrote for him regarding their sea. And he mentioned the hadith.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Sahl ibn Bakkar, and Muslim narrated it from another route from Wuhaib. [Sahih. Agreed upon]"
18797,"(18791) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Abu Tawbah Rabi' ibn Nafi' narrated to us; Mu'awiyah ibn Salam narrated from Zayd, that he heard Abu Salam say: 'Abdullah al-Hawzani narrated to me, saying: I met Bilal, the mu'adhdhin of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family), and I said: ""O Bilal, tell me about the expenditure of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family)."" And he mentioned the hadith, in which he said: ""Then a person came running, calling out: 'O Bilal, answer the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family)!' So I set out until I came to him, and there were four mounts, halted and laden with their burdens. I sought permission, and the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) said to me: 'Rejoice, for Allah has come to you with the settlement of your debt.' Then he said: 'Have you not seen the four halted mounts?' I said: 'Yes.' He said: 'Their necks belong to you, and what is upon them, for upon them is clothing and food that the chief of Fadk sent to me as a gift. So take them and settle your debt.' So I did."" [Sahih. Narrated by al-Sijistani, 3055]"
18798,"(18792) Abu Muhammad 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf al-Isbahani informed us; Abu Sa'id Ibn al-A'rabi informed us; al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'farani narrated to us; Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us; Isra'il narrated from Thawr ibn Abi Fakhitah, from his father, from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: Chosroes sent a gift to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family), and he accepted it from him. Caesar sent a gift to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family), and he accepted it from him. And the kings sent gifts to him, and he accepted from them.

Al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy upon him) said in the older view: Abu Sufyan ibn Harb sent as a gift to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) a skin of musk, and he accepted it from him. The ruler of Alexandria sent Mariyah, the mother of Ibrahim, as a gift, and she accepted her, and others besides them sent gifts to him, and he did not distribute that among the Muslims. [Da'if]"
18799,"(18793) Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak informed us; 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far ibn Ahmad narrated to us; Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; 'Imran narrated from Qatadah, from Yazid ibn 'Abdullah, from 'Iyyad ibn Himar (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I sent as a gift to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) a she-camel or a present, and he said: ""Have you accepted Islam?"" I said: ""No."" He said: ""I have been forbidden from accepting the gifts of the polytheists.""

And Abu Bakr informed us; 'Abdullah narrated to us; Yunus narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us; Abu al-Tayyah narrated to us; al-Hasan narrated from 'Iyyad ibn Himar (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I sent as a gift to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) a present, or he said, a she-camel, and he said to me: ""Have you accepted Islam?"" I said: ""No."" So he refused to accept it and said: ""We do not accept the gifts of the polytheists."" I said to al-Hasan: ""What are the gifts of the polytheists?"" He said: ""Their offerings (rifd).""

The Shaykh said: His rejection of his gift may be due to prohibition (tahrim) or due to disapproval (tanzih). And it may be that his anger at the rejection of his gift compelled him to accept Islam. And the reports regarding his acceptance of their gifts are more sound and more numerous. And with Allah lies success. [Sahih]"
18800,"(18794) Abu Sa'id Ibn Abi 'Amr al-Sayrafi informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affan narrated to us; Yahya ibn Adam narrated to us; Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash narrated from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani; from al-Saffah; from Dawud ibn Kurzus, who said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) made a treaty with Banu Taghlib on the condition that the charity (sadaqah) be doubled upon them, that they prevent none of them from accepting Islam, and that they not submerge their children (in baptism). [Da'if]"
18801,"(18795) Abū Saʿīd informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from Abū Isḥāq al-Shaybānī, from al-Saffāḥ, from Dāwūd ibn Kurdūs, from ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, that he made a treaty with Banū Taghlib on the condition that they would not baptize any child in their religion, that the ṣadaqah (charity) would be doubled upon them, and that they would not be compelled to any religion other than their own. Dāwūd used to say: ""Banū Taghlib have no covenant, for they have indeed baptized [their children]."" [Weak]"
18802,"(18796) Abū Saʿīd informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, al-Ḥasan narrated to us, Yaḥyā narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Salām ibn Ḥarb narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from al-Saffāḥ, from Dāwūd ibn Kurdūs, from ʿUbadah ibn al-Nuʿmān al-Taghlībī, that he said to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him: ""O Commander of the Faithful, Banū Taghlib are a people whose strength you know well, and they are positioned against the enemy. If they support the enemy against you, their burden will be severe. If you see fit to give them something, then do so."" He said: ""I shall do so."" He said: ""So he made a treaty with them on the condition that they would not immerse any of their children in Christianity, and that the ṣadaqah would be doubled upon them."" He said: ""And ʿUbadah used to say: 'They have done [what they were forbidden], so they have no covenant.'"" [Weak]"
18803,"(18797) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us, al-Rabīʿ said: al-Shāfiʿī said, following this hadith: ""And thus the people of the maghāzī (military expeditions) have preserved it, and they narrated it in a better context than this context. They said: 'He imposed the jizyah upon them, and they said: ""We are Arabs; we will not pay what the non-Arabs pay. But take from us as some of you take from others,"" meaning the ṣadaqah. So ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""No, this is an obligation upon the Muslims."" They said: ""Then increase whatever you wish under this name, but not under the name of jizyah."" So he did that, and he and they were agreed that the ṣadaqah would be doubled upon them.'"" [Sound]"
18804,"(18798) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad informed us, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dīnār, from Saʿd al-Jārī, or ʿAbd Allāh ibn Saʿd, the freedman of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""The Christians of the Arabs are not People of the Book, and their slaughtered animals are not lawful for us. I will not leave them until they embrace Islam or I strike their necks.""

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""We only refrained from compelling them to Islam or striking their necks because the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, accepted the jizyah from the Christians of the Arabs, and because ʿUmar, ʿUthmān, and ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with them, confirmed them [in their status], even though ʿUmar had said this. Likewise, their women are not lawful for us in marriage, because Allah, exalted be His praise, only made lawful for us from among the People of the Book those upon whom the Scripture was revealed."" [Weak]"
18805,"(18799) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us, ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar al-Ḍabbī informed us, Hishām informed us, from Muḥammad — who is Ibn Sīrīn — from ʿUbaydah, who said: ""I asked ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, about the slaughtered animals of the Christians of Banū Taghlib. He said: 'Do not eat it, for they have not adhered to anything of their religion except the drinking of wine.'"" [Sound]"
18806,"(18800) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Mihrān al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us, Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us, Sharīk narrated from Ibrāhīm ibn al-Muhājir al-Bajalī, from Ziyād ibn Ḥudayr al-Asadī, who said: ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""If I remain [in authority] over the Christians of Banū Taghlib, I shall surely kill their fighting men and take their offspring captive, for I wrote the covenant between the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, and them, that they would not Christianize their children."" [Weak]"
18807,"(18801) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us, Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Mūsā al-Ḥāsib narrated to us, Jubārah narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd ibn Bahrām narrated to us, Shahr ibn Ḥawshab narrated to us, Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, forbade [eating] the slaughtered animals of the Christians of the Arabs.""

This chain of narration is weak, and it has been narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, a narration contradicting it. [Weak]"
18808,"(18802) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Mālik informed us, from Thawr ibn Zayd al-Dīlī, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he was asked about the slaughtered animals of the Christians of the Arabs. He said: ""There is no harm in them,"" and he recited this verse: ""And whoever among you takes them as allies, then indeed he is of them"" (al-Māʾidah, 51). [Sound]"
18809,"(18803) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Bālawayh narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Khazzāz narrated to us, Khālid ibn Khidāsh narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb informed us, Mālik informed me, from Thawr ibn Zayd, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, and he mentioned it with the same meaning."
18810,"(18804) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan informed us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī said: ""What is narrated from the hadith of Ibn ʿAbbās regarding the permissibility of their slaughtered animals is only from the hadith of ʿIkrimah. Ibn al-Dārawardī and Ibn Abī Yaḥyā informed me of it, from Thawr al-Dīlī, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he was asked about the slaughtered animals of the Christians of the Arabs, and he stated a view which he reported — that they are lawful — and he recited: 'And whoever among you takes them as allies, then indeed he is of them.' However, our companion omitted the name of ʿIkrimah, and Thawr did not meet Ibn ʿAbbās.""

The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""By 'our companion' he means Mālik ibn Anas. He did not mention ʿIkrimah in most of the narrations from him, as though he did not consider it a valid proof. And Thawr al-Dīlī only narrated it from him from Ibn ʿAbbās, so it should not be used as a proof. And Allah knows best."" Thus said Ibn ʿAbbās in what ʿIkrimah narrated from him, and we only turned away from it because of the statement of ʿUmar and ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with them both. [Sound; it was mentioned before it.]"
18811,"(18805) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us — al-ʿAbbās, he is al-Aṣamm, narrated to us — al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us — Sharīk narrated from Ibrāhīm ibn Muhājir — from Ziyād ibn Ḥudayr, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, sent me to the Christians of Banū Taghlib and commanded me to take half of the ʿushr (one-twentieth) of their wealth, and he forbade me from levying the ʿushr upon any Muslim or upon a dhimmī who pays the kharāj.

He (the narrator) says: I think by his statement ""Muslim"" he means those among them who had recently embraced Islam, for he was sent only to the Christians of Banū Taghlib. And by his statement ""or a dhimmī who pays the kharāj"" he means: the People of the Dhimmah are not to be interfered with regarding their livestock, nor the ʿushr of their crops and fruits — except the Banū Taghlib, for they had been reconciled upon that condition.

The Shaykh said: It is also possible that there was no stipulation in the treaty of those dhimmīs who were under his governance regarding the levying of the ʿushr upon the wealth they trade with. [Weak. Previously referenced as no. 18800]"
18812,"(18806) And Abū Saʿīd narrated to us — al-ʿAbbās narrated to us — al-Ḥasan narrated to us — Yaḥyā narrated to us — Abū Bakr narrated from Abī Isḥāq al-Shaybānī — from Jāmiʿ ibn Shaddād — from Ziyād ibn Ḥudayr, who said: ʿUmar wrote to me: ""Do not levy the ʿushr upon Banū Taghlib in the year except once."" [Good (Ḥasan)]"
18813,"(18807) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar al-Ṭaʿī narrated to us — Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to me — my father narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated from Maʿmar — al-Zuhrī said: ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr informed me — from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam, each one corroborating the narration of his companion, who said: The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, set out at the time of al-Ḥudaybiyyah with some one hundred of his Companions. When they reached Dhul-Ḥulayfah, the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, garlanded the sacrificial animals, marked them, and entered iḥrām for ʿUmrah. He sent ahead a spy from Khuzāʿah to inform him about Quraysh. The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, continued until, when he was in Wādī al-Ashṭāt, near ʿUsfān, his Khuzāʿī spy came to him and said: ""I left Kaʿb ibn Luʾayy and ʿĀmir ibn Luʾayy — they have gathered for you the Aḥābīsh.""

Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal said: And Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated from Ibn al-Mubārak, who said: ""They have gathered for you the Aḥābīsh and have assembled for you great multitudes. They are the ones who will fight you and bar you from the House."" The Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: ""Advise me — do you think we should attack the offspring of those who aided them, and thus strike them? If they remain seated, they will remain aggrieved and sorrowful; and if they escape, it will be a neck that Allah has severed. Or do you think we should head to the House, and whoever bars us from it, we shall fight him?"" Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""Allah and His Messenger know best, O Prophet of Allah. We have come only as ʿumrah-performers, and we have not come to fight anyone. But whoever stands between us and the House, we shall fight him."" The Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: ""Then go forth.""

Al-Zuhrī said: And Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, used to say: ""I never saw anyone who consulted his Companions more than the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam.""

Al-Zuhrī said in the ḥadīth of al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam: So they set out until, when they were partway along the road, the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: ""Indeed, Khālid ibn al-Walīd is in al-Ghaym with a cavalry detachment of Quraysh serving as an advance guard. So take the right-hand path."" By Allah, Khālid did not become aware of them until he was at the rear of the army, and he galloped as a warner to Quraysh. The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, continued until, when he was at the pass through which the enemy would descend upon him, his she-camel knelt down. The people called out: ""Ḥal! Ḥal!"" (Get up! Get up!) But she remained firm. They said: ""Al-Ṣuwayqāʾ has bolted! Al-Ṣuwayqāʾ has bolted!"" The Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: ""Al-Ṣuwayqāʾ has not bolted, nor is that her nature. Rather, she was held back by the One who restrains the elephant."" Then he said: ""By the One in whose Hand is my soul, they will not ask me for any matter in which they honour the sanctities of Allah except that I grant it to them."" Then he urged her, and she leapt up with him. He then turned away from the road until he alighted at the farthest part of al-Ḥudaybiyyah, at a small pool of water from which people merely drank sparingly. The people did not tarry before they exhausted it. Thirst was complained of to the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, so he drew an arrow from his quiver and ordered them to place it in the well. By Allah, it continued to gush forth with water for them until they departed, fully satiated.

While they were in that state, Budayl ibn Warqāʾ al-Khuzāʿī arrived with a group of his people, who were trusted confidants of the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, from the people of Tihāmah. He said: ""I have left Kaʿb ibn Luʾayy and ʿĀmir ibn Luʾayy.""

Aḥmad said: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us from Ibn al-Mubārak, who said: ""I left Kaʿb ibn Luʾayy and ʿĀmir ibn Luʾayy encamped at the waters of al-Ḥudaybiyyah, with them the young camels and the women and children, and they are the ones who will fight you and bar you from the House."" The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: ""We have not come to fight anyone; rather, we have come to perform ʿUmrah. Quraysh have been exhausted and afflicted by war. If they wish, I will grant them a respite, and they shall leave a way between me and the people. If they prevail — if they wish to enter into what the people have entered, let them do so. Otherwise, they have gathered. And if they refuse, then by the One in whose Hand is my soul, I shall fight them upon this command of mine until my forelock is severed, or Allah fulfils His command."" Budayl said: ""I shall convey to them what you say."" He went to Quraysh and said: ""We have come from this man, and we heard him say something. If you wish, we shall present it to you."" Their foolish ones said: ""We have no need for you to tell us anything about him."" But their men of judgment said: ""Tell us what you heard him say."" He said: ""I heard him say such and such,"" and he related to them what the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, had said. ʿUrwah ibn Masʿūd al-Thaqafī stood up and said: ""O my people, are you not a father?"" They said: ""Indeed."" He said: ""Am I not a son?"" They said: ""Indeed."" He said: ""Do you accuse me?"" They said: ""No."" He said: ""Do you not know that I called the people of ʿUkāẓ, and when they rebelled against me, I came to you with my family, my children, and whoever obeyed me?"" They said: ""Indeed."" He said: ""Then this man has offered you a sound course of action. Accept it, and let me go to him."" They said: ""Go to him."" So he went to him and spoke to the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, with words similar to what he had said to Budayl. ʿUrwah said at that point: ""O Muḥammad, tell me — if you exterminate your people, have you heard of any Arab who exterminated his people before you? And if it is the other outcome, then by Allah, I see faces, and I see motley crowds of people, who will flee and leave you."" Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: ""Suck the clitoris of al-Lāt! Would we flee from him and leave him?"" He said: ""Who is this?"" He said: ""Abū Bakr."" He said: ""By the One in whose Hand is my soul, were it not for a favour you owe me, I would not compensate you for it — I would answer you."" Then he resumed speaking with the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam. Whenever he reached for the Prophet's beard, al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah, who was standing at the head of the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, with his sword upon him and wearing a helmet, struck his hand with the scabbard of the sword and said: ""Withdraw your hand from the beard of the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam."" ʿUrwah raised his hand and said: ""Who is this?"" They said: ""Al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah."" He said: ""O traitor! Am I not striving to rectify your treachery?"" Al-Mughīrah had accompanied a people in the Jāhiliyyah and killed them and took their wealth, then came and embraced Islam. The Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: ""As for Islam, I accept it; and as for the wealth, I have no part in it.""

Then ʿUrwah began to gaze upon the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, with his eye. He said: ""By Allah, whenever the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, expectorated phlegm, it fell into the palm of one of them, and he would rub it upon his skin and his face. When he commanded them, they hastened to obey his command. When he performed wuḍūʾ, they nearly fought over his ablution water. When they spoke, they lowered their voices in his presence, and they would not look at him directly, out of reverence for him."" He returned to his companions and said: ""O my people, by Allah, I have been sent to kings — I have been sent to Caesar, Chosroes, and the Negus. By Allah, I have never seen a king whose companions revere him as the companions of Muḥammad revere Muḥammad. By Allah, whenever he expectorated phlegm, it fell into the palm of one of them, and he would rub it upon his face and his skin. When he commanded them, they hastened to obey his command. When he performed wuḍūʾ, they nearly fought over his ablution water. When they spoke, they lowered their voices in his presence, and they would not look at him directly, out of reverence for him. He has offered you a sound course of action — accept it."" A man from Banū Kinānah said: ""Let me go to him."" They said: ""Go to him."" When he came in sight of the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, and his Companions, the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: ""This is so-and-so, from a people who revere the sacrificial animals. Send them to him."" So they were sent to him, and the people went out to meet them, calling out the talbiyah. When he saw that, he said: ""Glory to Allah! It is not fitting for these people to be barred from the House."" When he returned to his companions, he said: ""I saw the sacrificial animals garlanded and marked, and I do not see that they should be barred from the House."" A man among them named Mikzar ibn Ḥafṣ stood up and said: ""Let me go to him."" They said: ""Go to him."" When he came in sight of them, the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: ""This is Mikzar, and he is a wicked man."" He began to speak with the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam. While he was speaking with him, Suhayl ibn ʿAmr arrived. Maʿmar said: Ayyūb informed me from ʿIkrimah that when Suhayl arrived, the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: ""Your matter has been made easy for you.""

Al-Zuhrī said in his ḥadīth: Suhayl ibn ʿAmr came and said: ""Come, let us write a document between us and you."" He called for the scribe. The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: ""Write: Bismillāh al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm."" Suhayl said: ""As for al-Raḥmān, by Allah, I do not know what it is. But write: Bismika Allāhumma, as you used to write."" The Muslims said: ""We shall not write it except as Bismillāh al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm."" The Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: ""Write: Bismika Allāhumma."" Then he said: ""This is what Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah, has agreed upon."" Suhayl said: ""By Allah, if we knew that you were His Messenger, we would not have barred you from the House, nor fought you. But write: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh."" The Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: ""By Allah, I am the Messenger of Allah, even if you deny me. Write: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh.""

Al-Zuhrī said: And that was in accordance with his statement: ""They will not ask me for any matter in which they honour the sanctities of Allah except that I grant it to them."" The Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: ""On the condition that you leave a way between us and the House, so that we may perform ṭawāf around it."" Suhayl said: ""By Allah, the Arabs will not say that we were taken by force. But you may return next year."" So he wrote. Suhayl said: ""On the condition that no man comes to you from us, even if he be upon your religion, except that you return him to us."" The Muslims said: ""Glory to Allah! How can he be returned to the polytheists when he has come as a Muslim?"" While they were thus, Abū Jandal ibn Suhayl ibn ʿAmr came, shackled in his chains. Yaḥyā said from Ibn al-Mubārak: He came dragging in his chains, having emerged from the lower part of Makkah, and cast himself among the Muslims. Suhayl said: ""O Muḥammad, this is the first matter I shall negotiate with you — that you return him to me."" The Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: ""We have not yet completed the document."" He said: ""Then by Allah, I shall never make peace with you upon anything."" The Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: ""Then grant him asylum for me."" He said: ""I shall not grant him asylum."" He said: ""Yes, so do it."" He said: ""I shall not do it."" Mikzar said: ""Yes, we have granted him asylum for you."" Abū Jandal said: ""O company of Muslims, shall I be returned to the polytheists when I have come as a Muslim? Do you not see what has befallen me?"" He had been severely tormented for the sake of Allah, the Almighty. ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""I came to the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, and said: 'Are you not the Prophet of Allah?' He said: 'Indeed.' I said: 'Are we not upon the truth, and our enemy upon falsehood?' He said: 'Indeed.' I said: 'Then why do we show weakness in our religion, and return before Allah judges between us and them?' He said: 'O Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, I am the Messenger of Allah, and I shall not disobey Him, and He is my Helper.' I said: 'Did you not used to tell us that we would come to the House and perform ṭawāf around it?' He said: 'Indeed. And did I tell you that you would come to it this year?' I said: 'No.' He said: 'Then you shall come to it and perform ṭawāf around it.'""

ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""I came to Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: 'O Abū Bakr, is this truly the Prophet of Allah?' He said: 'Indeed.' I said: 'Are we not upon the truth, and our enemy upon falsehood?' He said: 'Indeed.' I said: 'Then why do we show weakness in our religion, and return before Allah judges between us and them?' He said: 'O man, he is the Messenger of Allah, and he will never disobey his Lord, and He is his Helper. Hold fast to his stirrup until you die. By Allah, he is upon the truth.' I said: 'Did he not used to tell us that he would come to the House and perform ṭawāf around it?' He said: 'Indeed. Did he inform you that you would come to it this year?' I said: 'No.' He said: 'Then you shall come to it and perform ṭawāf around it.'""

Al-Zuhrī said: ʿUmar said: ""I performed certain deeds for that purpose."" When the document was completed, the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said to his Companions: ""Rise, then offer the sacrifice, and then shave your heads."" By Allah, no man among them stood up. He said that three times, and still no one stood up. He rose and went in to Umm Salamah and mentioned to her what he had experienced with the people. Umm Salamah said: ""O Messenger of Allah, do you desire this? Go out, then do not speak a word to any of them until you have offered your sacrifice and called for your barber to shave your head."" So he went out and did not speak to any of them until he had done that — he offered his sacrifice and called for his barber, who shaved his head. When they saw that, they rose and offered their sacrifices, and some of them began shaving the heads of others, until some of them nearly killed one another out of grief. Then believing women came, and Allah, the Almighty, revealed: {O you who have believed, when the believing women come to you as emigrants...} [al-Mumtaḥanah: 10] until He reached: {...while they are not lawful for the disbelievers...} [al-Mumtaḥanah: 10]. ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, divorced two wives he had from the period of shirk that day. One of them was married by Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Sufyān, and the other by Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah.

Then he returned to al-Madīnah. Abū Baṣīr, a man from Quraysh who was a Muslim, arrived. Yaḥyā said from Ibn al-Mubārak: Abū Baṣīr ibn Asīd al-Thaqafī arrived as a Muslim emigrant. Al-Akhnas ibn Sharīq, a disbeliever from Banū ʿĀmir ibn Luʾayy, had hired a man from among the disbelievers and a mawlā of his, and written with them to the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, asking for the fulfilment of the pact. They sent two men to retrieve him. They said: ""The pact that you have made with us."" So he handed him over to the two men. They set out with him until they reached Dhul-Ḥulayfah, where they stopped to eat from their dates. Abū Baṣīr said to one of the two men: ""By Allah, I see this sword of yours, O so-and-so, to be of fine quality."" The other man unsheathed it and said: ""Yes, by Allah, it is indeed fine. I have tested it and tested it."" Abū Baṣīr said: ""Let me look at it."" He handed it to him, and he struck him with it until he was dead. The other man fled until he reached al-Madīnah, entering the mosque at a run. The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: ""This one has seen something terrifying."" When he reached the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, he said: ""By Allah, my companion has been killed, and I too shall be killed."" Abū Baṣīr came and said: ""O Prophet of Allah, Allah has fulfilled your covenant. You returned me to them, and then Allah rescued me from them."" The Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, said: ""Woe to his mother — a war-igniter! Even if he had but one companion."" When he heard that, he knew that he would be returned to them. He went out until he reached the coast of the sea. Abū Jandal ibn Suhayl broke his chains and caught up with Abū Baṣīr. No man from Quraysh who had embraced Islam would go out except that he would join Abū Baṣīr, until a band of them had gathered. By Allah, whenever they heard of a caravan of Quraysh heading to al-Shām, they would intercept it, kill them, and seize their property. Quraysh sent to the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, adjuring Allah and the bonds of kinship, asking him to send to them, and that whoever came to him would be safe. The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, sent to them, and Allah, the Almighty, revealed: {And it is He who restrained their hands from you and your hands from them...} [al-Fatḥ: 26] until He reached: {...in a state of pre-Islamic fanaticism (ḥamiyyat al-jāhiliyyah).} [al-Fatḥ: 26] Their fanaticism was that they did not acknowledge him as the Prophet of Allah, nor acknowledged Bismillāh al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm, and they barred between him and the House.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 2734]"
18814,"(18808) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAttāb narrated to us — al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn al-Mughīrah narrated to us — Ibn Abī Uways narrated to us — Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿUqbah narrated from his paternal uncle Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, who mentioned the meaning of this story, adding: Then the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, called ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, to send him to Quraysh while they were at Baldaḥ. ʿUmar said: ""O Messenger of Allah, do not send me to them, for I fear for myself against them. But send ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān."" So he sent him to them. He met Abān ibn Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ, who granted him asylum and carried him before him on the horse until he came to Quraysh. He spoke to them with what the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, had commanded him. They sent with him Suhayl ibn ʿAmr to negotiate peace with him. At that time, there were many Muslims in Makkah from among its people. They invited ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with him, to perform ṭawāf around the House, but he refused, saying: ""I shall not perform ṭawāf around it until the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, performs ṭawāf around it."" He returned to the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, along with Suhayl ibn ʿAmr, who had granted him asylum to negotiate peace for the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam. He mentioned the story of the peace treaty and its writing. Then the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, sent the document with ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with him, to Quraysh. Then he mentioned the story of what occurred between the two parties of pelting with stones and arrows, and the holding as hostage of ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with him, by the polytheists, and the holding as hostage of Suhayl ibn ʿAmr by the Muslims. The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, called the Muslims to the pledge of allegiance. When Quraysh saw that, Allah struck terror into their hearts, and they sent those whom they had held as hostages, and called for a truce and peace. The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, made peace with them and wrote a treaty. [Weak]"
18815,"(18809) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us — Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Al-Zuhrī narrated to me from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, regarding the story of al-Ḥudaybiyyah. He said: Quraysh called for Suhayl ibn ʿAmr and said: ""Go to this man and make peace with him, and let there be in his peace only that he returns from us this year, so that the Arabs do not say that he entered it by force."" Suhayl ibn ʿAmr went out from them. When the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, saw him approaching, he said: ""The people desire peace, since they have sent this man."" When he reached the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, conversation ensued between them until peace was agreed upon on the condition that war between them would be suspended for ten years, that people would be safe from one another, and that they would return from them that year. When the next year came, he would come and they would leave a way between him and Makkah, and he would stay there for three days. He would not enter it except with the weapon of a rider — swords in their scabbards. And that any man who came to us from among your companions without the permission of his walī, we would not return him to you. And any man who came to you from among us without the permission of his walī, you would return him to us. And between us and you there would be a sealed pact, with no betrayal or treachery. And he mentioned the ḥadīth. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain]"
18816,"(18810) And ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ al-ʿUmarī — and he is very weak — narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār, from Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with both of them, who said: The truce (hudnah) between the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, and the people of Makkah at the time of al-Ḥudaybiyyah was for four years. Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us — Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī informed us — al-Qāsim ibn Mahdī narrated to us — Yaʿqūb ibn Kāsib narrated to us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Nāfiʿ narrated from ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar, who mentioned it. The preserved narration is the first one.

And this ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar narrates what is not corroborated. Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn and al-Bukhārī and other imāms have declared him weak. [Weak]"
18817,"(18811) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me — Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to us — Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad al-Shāmātī narrated to us — Naṣr ibn ʿAlī and Abū al-Ashʿath both said: Khālid ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to us — Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah narrated from Qatādah — that Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to them, saying: When this verse was revealed upon the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam: {Indeed, We have granted you a clear victory. That Allah may forgive for you what preceded of your sin and what will come later...} [al-Fatḥ: 1–2], upon their return from al-Ḥudaybiyyah, while grief and sorrow still mingled with them, and he had offered the sacrifice, he said: ""Verses have been revealed to me that are dearer to me than the entire world."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, we already know what Allah will do with you. But what will He do with us?"" So there was revealed: {That He may admit the believing men and believing women into gardens beneath which rivers flow...} [al-Fatḥ: 5] until the end of the verse.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Naṣr ibn ʿAlī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1786]"
18818,"(18812) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me — Abū Aḥmad, that is Ibn Isḥāq al-Ḥāfiẓ, informed us — Abū ʿArūbah narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Yazīd al-Asfāṭī narrated to us — ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated to us — Shuʿbah narrated from Qatādah — from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, regarding the verse: {Indeed, We have granted you a clear victory} [al-Fatḥ: 1]. He said: ""The victory of al-Ḥudaybiyyah."" A man said: ""Congratulations, O Messenger of Allah! This is for you — but what is for us?"" So Allah, the Almighty, revealed: {That He may admit the believing men and believing women into gardens beneath which rivers flow...} [al-Fatḥ: 5]. Shuʿbah said: ""I went to al-Kūfah and narrated to them from Qatādah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him. Then I went to al-Baṣrah and mentioned that to Qatādah. He said: 'As for the first part, it is from Anas. As for the second — {That He may admit the believing men and believing women into gardens beneath which rivers flow} [al-Fatḥ: 5] — it is from ʿIkrimah.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq from ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī 4172]"
18819,"(18813) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Abī ʿĪsá narrated to us — Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us — ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Siyāh narrated to us. And Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar narrated to me — Abū Yaʿlá narrated to us — Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr narrated to us — ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Siyāh narrated to us — Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit narrated from Abū Wāʾil, who said: Sahl ibn Ḥunayf, may Allah be pleased with him, stood up on the day of Ṣiffīn and said: ""O people, blame yourselves! We were with the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyyah. Had we seen fighting, we would have fought — and that was in the peace treaty that existed between the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, and the polytheists."" He said: ""Then ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, came and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, are we not upon the truth, and they upon falsehood?' He said: 'Indeed.' He said: 'Are our slain in Paradise, and their slain in the Fire?' He said: 'Indeed.' He said: 'Then why do we show weakness in ourselves, and return before Allah judges between us and them?' He said: 'O Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, I am the Messenger of Allah, and Allah will not forsake me.' So Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb departed, not restraining himself, seething with anger. He came to Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: 'O Abū Bakr, are we not upon the truth, and they upon falsehood?' He said: 'Indeed.' He said: 'Are our slain in Paradise, and their slain in the Fire?' He said: 'Indeed.' He said: 'Then why do we show weakness in our religion, and return before Allah judges between us and them?' He said: 'O Ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, he is the Messenger of Allah, and Allah will never forsake him.'""

He said: ""Then the Qurʾān was revealed upon Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, and he sent for ʿUmar and recited it to him. ʿUmar said: 'O Messenger of Allah, is it a victory?' He said: 'Yes.' He said: 'Then his soul was at peace, and he returned.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq al-Sulamī from Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah.

Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy upon him, said: Ibn Shihāb said: ""There was no victory in Islam greater than it. War had restrained the people, but when they felt secure, no one was addressed with Islam except that he accepted it. So in two years of that truce, more people embraced Islam than had embraced it before that."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18820,"(18814) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us — Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Al-Zuhrī narrated to me from ʿUrwah, from Marwān and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, regarding the story of al-Ḥudaybiyyah — and in it is something interpolated: Then the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, turned back. When he was between Makkah and al-Madīnah, Sūrat al-Fatḥ was revealed to him, from its beginning to its end: {Indeed, We have granted you a clear victory.} The judgment is in Sūrat al-Fatḥ, and what Allah mentioned of the pledge of His Messenger beneath the tree. When the people felt secure and interacted, no one was addressed with Islam except that he entered into it. So in those two years, more people entered into Islam than had been in it before that. And the peace of al-Ḥudaybiyyah was a great victory. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18821,"(18815) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Rajāʾ informed us: Isrāʾīl narrated from Abū Isḥāq from al-Barāʾ (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""You count the Conquest as the conquest of Makkah, and the conquest of Makkah was indeed a conquest for us. But we count the Conquest as the Pledge of Riḍwān. We descended at al-Ḥudaybiyyah, which is a well, and we found that the people had drawn out all its water, leaving not a single drop in it. This was mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family). So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) sat down, called for a bucket, drew water from it, then took some in his mouth and spat it into the well, and supplicated to Allah. Its water then increased abundantly until we and our mounts had drunk our fill, and we were fourteen hundred in number.""

Narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Mālik ibn Ismāʿīl and others from Isrāʾīl. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī 4150]"
18822,"(18816) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī al-Zinād narrated from Abī al-Zinād from al-Aʿraj from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: ""The Imām is a shield; one fights behind him."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
18823,"(18817) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Nājiyah informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā narrated: al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-ʿAlāʾ ibn Zabr, who said: I heard Busr ibn ʿUbaydullāh al-Ḥaḍramī narrating that he heard Abū Idrīs al-Khawlānī say: I heard ʿAwf ibn Mālik al-Ashjaʿī (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) during the expedition of Tabūk, while he was in a tent of leather. He said to me: 'O ʿAwf, count six things that will come before the Hour: my death; then the conquest of Bayt al-Maqdis; then a plague that will strike you like the plague that strikes sheep; then an abundance of wealth among you, to the extent that a man will be given a hundred dinārs yet remain discontent; then a trial that will enter every household of the Arabs; then a truce between you and the Banī al-Aṣfar, but they will betray it, and they will come against you under eighty banners, under each banner twelve thousand.'""

Al-Walīd said: ""We discussed this hadith with a shaykh from among the scholars of Madīnah regarding his statement 'then the conquest of Bayt al-Maqdis.' The shaykh said: Saʿīd informed me from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that he used to narrate this hadith from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) and say in place of 'the conquest of Bayt al-Maqdis': 'the flourishing of Bayt al-Maqdis.'""

Narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥumaydī from al-Walīd ibn Muslim, without the chain of Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him). [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
18824,"(18818) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Sūsī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Mazyad informed us: My father informed me: al-Awzāʿī informed me: Ḥassān ibn ʿAṭiyyah informed me: ""Makkḥūl and Ibn Abī Zakariyyāʾ inclined toward Khālid ibn Maʿdān, so I went with them. Khālid narrated to us from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, who said: 'Take us to Dhi Mukhbar, a man from among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family).' So we went to him, and he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) say: 'The Romans will make a secure peace treaty with you. Then you and they will fight an enemy together, and you will be victorious, be safe, and gain spoils. Then you will return and encamp in a meadow with hillocks. A man from among the Christians will raise the cross and say: ""The cross has triumphed!"" A man from among the Muslims will become angry, go to him, and smash it. At that point the Romans will become angry and gather for the great battle.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18825,"(18819) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal informed me: My father informed me: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Ibn Jurayj narrated from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar: ""That ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) expelled the Jews and the Christians from the land of the Ḥijāz. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) had conquered Khaybar, he had intended to expel the Jews from it. The land, when he conquered it, belonged to Allah, His Messenger, and the Muslims. He intended to expel the Jews from it, so the Jews asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) to allow them to remain on the condition that they would work the land and have half of its produce. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said to them: 'We will allow you to remain on that for as long as we wish.' So they remained until ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) expelled them to Taymāʾ and Arīḥāʾ.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ and Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. Al-Bukhārī also narrated it, saying: ""And ʿAbd al-Razzāq said…"" Similarly, al-Fuḍayl ibn Sulaymān narrated from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah: ""We will allow you on that for as long as we wish."" And Usāmah ibn Zayd narrated from Nāfiʿ: ""I will allow you on that for as long as we wish.""

In the narration of ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar from Nāfiʿ: ""for as long as it pleases the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family).""

In the narration of Mālik from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar from ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family): ""We will allow you for as long as Allah allows you.""

Similarly, in the narration of Ibn Shihāb from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), as a mursal narration: ""I will allow you for as long as Allah allows you.""

And Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī al-Akhḍar narrated it from Ibn Shihāb from Saʿīd from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) as a connected narration, and these narrations have been transmitted through their respective chains.

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""If it is said: 'Why does he not say: ""I will allow you for as long as Allah allows you,"" meaning every Imām after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family)?' It is said: The difference between him and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) is that the command of Allah used to come to His Messenger through revelation, and it did not come to anyone else through revelation."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18826,"(18820) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdad: Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk informed us: Ḥanbal ibn Isḥāq informed us: Saʿdawayh informed us: ʿAbbād ibn al-ʿAwwām informed us: Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn narrated from al-Ḥakam from Miqsam from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both): ""That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) sent Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) during the season [of Hajj] and commanded him to proclaim these words. While Abū Bakr was encamped at a certain place along the road, he heard the bellowing of the she-camel of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), al-Qaṣwāʾ, and came out alarmed, thinking it was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family). But it was ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him). He handed him the letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family). ʿAlī came to the season [of Hajj], and he commanded ʿAlī to proclaim these words. So they set out and performed Hajj. ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) stood and proclaimed in the middle of the days of Tashrīq: 'Allah and His Messenger are free of all polytheists. So travel in the land for four months, and know that you cannot escape Allah.' [al-Tawbah 2] 'No polytheist shall perform Hajj after this year, and no one shall circumambulate the House naked, and no one shall enter Paradise except a believer.' He used to proclaim this, and when his voice grew hoarse, Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) would stand and proclaim it."" [Ḥasan]"
18827,"(18821) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, Abū Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqrīʾ, and Abū Ṣādiq Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-ʿAṭṭār all said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq informed us: ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar informed us: Shuʿbah narrated from al-Mughīrah from al-Shaʿbī from al-Muḥarrir ibn Abī Hurayrah from his father, who said: ""I was with ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) sent him with Barāʾah to the people of Makkah. I used to call out until my voice became hoarse. He was asked: 'What were you calling out?' He said: 'We were commanded to call out that no one shall enter Paradise except a believer; and that whoever has a covenant with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), his term is four months; and when the months have passed, Allah is free of the polytheists and so is His Messenger; and no one shall circumambulate the House naked; and no polytheist shall perform Hajj after this year.'"" This has also been mentioned in the hadith of Zayd ibn Yuthayʿ from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) in this hadith: ""And whoever has a covenant, his covenant is for its duration, and whoever has no covenant, then four months.""

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) gave Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah, after the conquest of Makkah, a respite of four months.""

The Shaykh said: This has been mentioned in the hadith of Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī in the Book of Marriage. [Ṣaḥīḥ — previously cited under number 17948]"
18828,"(18822) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs informed us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd informed us: Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl informed us: Hammām narrated from Isḥāq ibn ʿAbdullāh, who informed me: Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) informed me: ""That the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) sent his maternal uncle, whose name was Ḥarām, the brother of Umm Sulaym, with seventy men. They were killed at Biʾr Maʿūnah. The leader of the polytheists was ʿĀmir ibn al-Ṭufayl. He had come to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) and said: 'I give you the choice between three things: that you have the people of the lowland and I have the people of the highland, and I be your successor after you; or that I fight against you with Ghaṭafān with a thousand ashqar and a thousand shaqrāʾ.' He was struck by a lance in the house of a woman from Banī Fulān and said: 'A morning like the morning of a young camel in the house of a woman from Banī Fulān. Bring me my horse.' He mounted it and died on the back of his horse. Ḥarām, the brother of Umm Sulaym, set out with two men with him—one was lame and one was from Banī Fulān. He said: 'Stay close to me so that I may approach them. If they grant me security, you will be thus; and if they kill me, go to your companions.' Ḥarām went to them and said: 'Will you grant me security so that I may deliver to you the message of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family)?' They said: 'Yes.' So he began to narrate to them, and they gestured to a man, who came at him from behind and stabbed him."" Hammām said: ""I think he said: 'He pierced him through with the spear.' He said: 'Allāhu Akbar, I have won, by the Lord of the Kaʿbah.' The man was pursued, and they were all killed except the lame one, who was at the top of the mountain."" Isḥāq said: Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) informed me, saying: ""It was revealed to us, and then it was abrogated: 'We have met our Lord, and He has been pleased with us and we with Him.'"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) supplicated against Riʿl, Dhakwān, Banī Liḥyān, and ʿUṣayyah—who had disobeyed Allah and His Messenger—for seventy mornings. Narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī 4091]"
18829,"(18823) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us: Ḥibbān informed us: ʿAbdullāh informed us: Maʿmar informed me: Thumāmah ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Anas heard Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""When Ḥarām ibn Milḥān was struck by a lance—and he was his maternal uncle—on the day of Biʾr Maʿūnah, he took his blood and sprinkled it over his face and head, then said: 'I have won, by the Lord of the Kaʿbah.'"" Narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥibbān. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī 4092]"
18830,"(18824) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ṣūfī informed us: Khalaf, who is Ibn Sālim al-Mukharramī, informed us: Abū Usāmah informed us: Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated from his father from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her): ""Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) sought permission from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) to leave Makkah. He mentioned the hadith regarding the Hijrah, and that ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah was with them. ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah was killed on the day of Biʾr Maʿūnah, and ʿAmr ibn Umayyah al-Ḍamrī was captured. ʿĀmir ibn al-Ṭufayl said to him: 'Who is this?'—pointing to a slain man. ʿAmr ibn Umayyah said: 'This is ʿĀmir ibn Fuhayrah.' He said: 'I saw him, after he was killed, raised to the sky, to the point that I could see the sky between him and the earth.' News of them reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), and he announced their deaths and said: 'Your companions were struck, and they asked their Lord, saying: ""Our Lord, inform our brothers about us, that we are pleased with You and You are pleased with us.""' So He informed them about them. Among those struck that day were ʿUrwah ibn Asmāʾ ibn al-Ṣalt, after whom ʿUrwah was named, and Mundhir ibn ʿUmar, after whom Mundhir was named."" Narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUbayd ibn Ismāʿīl from Abū Usāmah, and he made the end of the hadith from the words of ʿUrwah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī 4093]"
18831,"(18825) Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Faqīh informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Ḥarashī narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb, from Abū Qilābah, from Abū Asmāʾ, from Thawbān (may Allāh be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""There will always remain a group of my Ummah manifest upon the truth; those who forsake them will not harm them, until the command of Allāh comes while they are in that state.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon.]"
18832,"(18826) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqrīʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb, from Abū Qilābah, from Abī al-Muhallab, from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn (may Allāh be pleased with him), that the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) ransomed one man for two men.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ as mentioned above.

And there is the ḥadīth of Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ regarding the woman whom the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) had been given as a gift, and he sent her to Makkah while there were captives in their hands, and he ransomed them with that woman. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18833,"(18827) Abū Saʿd Muḥammad ibn Manṣūr al-Raʾīs al-Jurjānī informed us: Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-ʿAbdī informed us: Abū Khalīfah al-Faḍl ibn al-Ḥubāb al-Jumaḥī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Kathīr informed us: Sufyān narrated from Manṣūr.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Jarīr narrated from Manṣūr, from Abū Wāʾil, from Abū Mūsá (may Allāh be pleased with him), that the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""Feed the hungry, free the captive, and visit the sick."" Sufyān said: ""And al-ʿānī means the captive.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Kathīr and from Qutaybah from Jarīr. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī 5373]"
18834,"(18828) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Saqqāʾ and Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqrīʾ both said: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn Marzūq narrated to us: Zuhayr narrated from Muṭarrif, from ʿĀmir, from Abū Ḥuṣayn, who said: I said to ʿAlī (may Allāh be pleased with him): ""O Commander of the Faithful, do you have anything from revelation?"" He said: ""No, by the One who split the seed and created the soul, I know of nothing except an understanding that Allāh, the Mighty and Majestic, grants to a man, and what is in the scroll."" I said: ""And what is in the scroll?"" He said: ""Blood-money (al-ʿaql), the freeing of captives, and that a believer shall not be killed in retaliation for a polytheist."" Zuhayr said: I said to Muṭarrif: ""And what is the freeing of captives?"" He said: ""That they be freed from the enemy."" And the Sunnah has been established upon that. Muṭarrif said: ""Al-ʿaql means the blood-money (al-maqʿulah).""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus from Zuhayr ibn Muʿāwiyah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon.]"
18835,"(18829) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Sulmān ibn al-Ḥasan informed us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsá narrated to us: Abū Ḥudhayfah narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from al-Barāʾ (may Allāh be pleased with him), who said: The Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) made peace with the polytheists on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyyah on three conditions: that whoever came to him from the polytheists, he would return him to them; and whoever came to them from the Muslims, they would not return him; and that he would enter [Makkah] the following year and stay there for three days, and that he would not enter it except with the sheaths of weapons — the sword and the bow and the like. Then Abū Jandal came hopping in his shackles, and he was returned to them. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Ḥudhayfah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon.]"
18836,"(18830) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqrīʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Hudbah ibn Khālid narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Thābit, from Anas (may Allāh be pleased with him), that when the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) made peace with Quraysh on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyyah, he said to ʿAlī (may Allāh be pleased with him): ""Write: Bismillāh al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm (In the name of Allāh, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful)."" Suhayl ibn ʿAmr said: ""We do not recognize al-Raḥmān al-Raḥīm. Write: Bismika Allāhumma (In Your name, O Allāh)."" The Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said to ʿAlī (may Allāh be pleased with him): ""Write: Bismika Allāhumma."" Then the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said to ʿAlī (may Allāh be pleased with him): ""Write: This is what Muḥammad, the Messenger of Allāh, has agreed upon."" Suhayl ibn ʿAmr said: ""If we knew that you were the Messenger of Allāh, we would have believed you and not denied you. Write your name and your father's name."" The Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said to ʿAlī (may Allāh be pleased with him): ""Write: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh."" And it was written: ""Whoever comes to us from among you, we shall return him to you; and whoever comes to you from among us, we shall leave him with you."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allāh, shall we give them this?"" He said: ""Whoever goes to them from among us, Allāh has distanced him from us; and whoever comes to us from among them and we return him to them, Allāh, the Mighty and Majestic, shall grant him relief and a way out.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of ʿAffān from Ḥammād ibn Salamah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon.]"
18837,"(18831) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr al-Qāḍī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who narrated to me: al-Zuhrī narrated from ʿUrwah, from Marwān and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, in the story of al-Ḥudaybiyyah and the coming out of Suhayl ibn ʿAmr to the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), and that when he reached the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), conversation took place between them until peace was concluded on the condition that war between them would be suspended for ten years, that people would be secure from one another, and that he [the Prophet] would depart from them that year, and when the following year came, they would leave him and Makkah, and he would stay there for three days, and that he would not enter it except with the weapons of the rider — swords in their sheaths — and that whoever came to us from among your companions without the permission of his guardian, we would not return him to you; and that whoever came to you from among us without the permission of his guardian, you would return him to us. He mentioned the ḥadīth about the writing of the document. He said: ""The document was being written when Abū Jandal ibn Suhayl ibn ʿAmr appeared, walking in chains. His father had imprisoned him, but he had escaped. When Suhayl saw him, he stood up to him, struck his face, and grabbed him by his collar, pulling him, and said: 'O Muḥammad, the matter has been settled between me and you before this one came to you.' He said: 'You have spoken the truth.' Abū Jandal cried out at the top of his voice: 'O company of Muslims, am I to be returned to the polytheists while they test me concerning my religion?' The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said to Abū Jandal: 'O Abū Jandal, be patient and seek reward, for Allāh shall grant you and those who are with you among the oppressed relief and a way out. We have made peace with these people, and a covenant has been established between us and them, and we do not betray.'""

Then ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allāh be pleased with him) stood up, walking beside Abū Jandal while his father was pulling him, and he said: ""O Abū Jandal, be patient and seek reward, for they are only polytheists, and the blood of any one of them is the blood of a dog."" And ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) kept bringing the hilt of the sword close to him. ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) said: ""I hoped that he would take it and strike his father with it, but he refrained out of regard for his father."" Then he mentioned the ḥadīh about exiting the state of iḥram from the ʿUmrah and the return. They both said: ""When the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) arrived in Madīnah and settled there, Abū Baṣīr ʿUtbah ibn Asīd ibn Jāriyah al-Thaqafī, an ally of Banū Zuhrah, escaped to him. Then al-Akhnas ibn Sharīq and al-Azhar ibn ʿAbd ʿAwf wrote to the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) concerning him, and they sent their letter with a mawlā of theirs and a man from Banū ʿAmir ibn Luʾayy whom they had hired to return their companion Abū Baṣīr to them. They came to the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and handed him their letter. The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) called Abū Baṣīr and said to him: 'O Abū Baṣīr, these people have made peace with us on what you know, and we do not betray. Go back to your people.' He said: 'O Messenger of Allāh, will you return me to the polytheists while they test me concerning my religion and torment me?' The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: 'Be patient, O Abū Baṣīr, and seek reward, for Allāh shall grant you and those who are with you among the oppressed believers relief and a way out.'""

He said: ""So Abū Baṣīr went out, and they both went out until they were at Dhū al-Ḥulayfah. They sat in the shade of a wall. Abū Baṣīr said to the ʿĀmirī: 'Is your sword sharp, O brother of Banū ʿAmir?' He said: 'Yes.' He said: 'Let me look at it.' He said: 'If you wish, draw it.' So he struck his neck with it. The mawlā went off running and came upon the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) while he was sitting in the mosque. When the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) saw him, he said: 'This man has seen something frightening.' When he came to him, he said: 'What is the matter with you?' He said: 'Your companion has killed my companion.' It was not long before Abū Baṣīr appeared, girded with his sword, and he stood before the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and said: 'O Messenger of Allāh, your covenant has been fulfilled, and Allāh has discharged your obligation. I have protected myself from the polytheists lest they test me concerning my religion or torment me.' The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: 'Woe to his mother — he is a kindler of war, if only he had men with him.'""

Abū Baṣīr went out until he settled at al-ʿĪṣ, which was the route of the people of Makkah to Shām. Those Muslims who were in Makkah heard about him and about what the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) had said about him, and they joined him until he was in a band of Muslims close to sixty or seventy. They would not capture a man from Quraysh without killing him, and no caravan would pass them without them seizing it. Until Quraysh wrote to the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), asking him by their ties of kinship, to give them refuge, for they had no need of them. So the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) did so, and they came to him in Madīnah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon.]"
18838,"(18832) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us: Abū Bakr ibn ʿAttāb al-ʿAbdī narrated to us: al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mughīrah narrated to us: Ibn Abī Uways informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿUqbah narrated from his paternal uncle Mūsá ibn ʿUqbah, who mentioned this story. He said in it: The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""Woe to his mother — he is a kindler of war, if only he had someone with him."" And Abū Baṣīr came with the spoils of his victim to the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and said: ""Take the fifth, O Messenger of Allāh."" He said: ""If I were to take the fifth, I would not fulfill for them that which I covenanted with them. But take the spoils of your companion and go wherever you wish."" So Abū Baṣīr went out with five men who had come with him from the Muslims of Makkah, until they were between al-ʿĪṣ and Dhū al-Marwah, in the land of Juhaynah, on the route of the caravans of Quraysh along the coast. No caravan of Quraysh would pass them without them seizing it and killing its companions. And Abū Jandal ibn Suhayl ibn ʿAmr escaped with seventy riders who had accepted Islam and emigrated, and they joined Abū Baṣīr. They disliked coming to the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) during the truce with the polytheists. Then he mentioned what came after that with the same meaning as what preceded and completed it. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18839,"(18833) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us: Yaḥyá narrated to us: al-Layth narrated from ʿUqayl, from Ibn Shihāb, who said: ""It has reached us that the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) judged between himself and the polytheists of Quraysh for the period that he established between them on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyyah. Allāh revealed concerning what He decreed between them. ʿUrbah ibn al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah narrating from the Companions of the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) that when the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) wrote the treaty with Suhayl ibn ʿAmr on that day, among the conditions that Suhayl ibn ʿAmr imposed upon the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) was that no one would come to you from us, even if he were upon your religion, except that you would return him to us, and you would leave him between us and him. The believers disliked that and were agitated by it, or he said another word.""

Imām Aḥmad (may Allāh have mercy on him) said: ""Our shaykh did not get this word right, and I saw it in a copy: 'and they were indignant.'"" And Suhayl refused except that, so the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) wrote the treaty, and on that day he returned Abū Jandal to his father Suhayl ibn ʿAmr. No man came to him during that period except that he returned him, even if he was a Muslim. And believing women came as emigrants. Umm Kulthūm bint ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ was among those who came out to the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) on that day, and she was a young woman. Her family came asking the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) to return her to them, but he did not return her to them, because of what Allāh had revealed concerning them: {When the believing women come to you as emigrants, test them. Allāh is most knowing of their faith. And if you know them to be believers, then do not return them to the disbelievers. They are not lawful for them, nor are they lawful for them.} [Al-Mumtaḥanah: 10] ʿUrwah said: ʿĀʾishah (may Allāh be pleased with her) informed me that the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) used to test them with this verse: {O Prophet, when the believing women come to you pledging to you that they will not associate anything with Allāh, nor steal, nor commit unlawful sexual intercourse, nor kill their children…} [Al-Mumtaḥanah: 12] ʿUrwah said: ʿĀʾishah (may Allāh be pleased with her) said: ""Whoever among them accepted this condition, the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) would say to her: 'I have accepted your pledge.' He would speak words to her, and by Allāh, his hand never touched a woman's hand during the pledge of allegiance. He only accepted their pledge by his words.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyá ibn Bukayr. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18840,"(18834) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd informed us that Muḥammad ibn Thawr narrated to them from Maʿmar, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, who said: ""The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) went out at the time of al-Ḥudaybiyyah."" He mentioned the ḥadīth with the same meaning as what preceded, and he added: ""Then believing women came as emigrants,"" [mentioning] the verse. So Allāh forbade them from returning them and ordered them to return the dower. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18841,"(18835) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Ibn Isḥāq: al-Zuhrī narrated to me, saying: I entered upon ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, and Ibn Abī Hunaydah had written to him asking him about the statement of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: {When believing women come to you as emigrants, test them} [al-Mumtaḥanah 10]. ʿUrwah wrote to him: Indeed, the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) made peace with the people of al-Ḥudaybiyyah and stipulated for them that whoever came to him without the permission of his guardian, he would return him to them. When the Muslim women emigrated to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), Allah commanded that they be tested. If they had come out of desire for Islam, he would not return them to them. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, said: {If you know them to be believers, do not return them to the disbelievers} [al-Mumtaḥanah 10]. So the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) detained the women and returned the men. [Ḥasan]"
18842,"(18836) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, who said: al-Zuhrī and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr narrated to me, saying: Umm Kulthūm bint ʿUqbah ibn Abī Muʿayṭ emigrated to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) in the year of al-Ḥudaybiyyah. Her two brothers, al-Walīd and so-and-so, the sons of ʿUqbah, came to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) requesting her, but he refused to return her to them.

And it has been mentioned in the narration of Maʿmar from al-Zuhrī regarding the peace of Ḥudaybiyyah that Suhayl said: ""On the condition that no man comes to you from us, even if he be upon your religion, except that you return him to us."" And in that is an indication that the women were not included in this condition. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18843,"(18837) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Nasawī narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Shākir narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to me: Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā informed us: Hishām narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: ʿAṭāʾ said, from Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā): ""If a male or female slave of the polytheists—the people of the covenant—emigrates, they are not to be returned, but their prices are to be paid."" Muḥammad [al-Bukhārī] recorded it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5287]"
18844,"(18838) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū ʿAbd Allāh ibn Qāniʿ, the judge of the Two Sacred Mosques, in Baghdād, informed us: Abū Shuʿayb ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Yaḥyā al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Salamah al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, from Abān ibn Ṣāliḥ, from Manṣūr ibn al-Muʿtamir, from Ribʿī ibn Ḥirāsh, from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said: Two slaves came out to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyyah, before the peace treaty. Their masters wrote to him, saying: ""O Muḥammad, by Allah, they did not come to you out of desire for your religion; rather, they came fleeing from bondage."" Some people said: ""They have spoken the truth, O Messenger of Allah; return them to them."" The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) became angry and said: ""I do not think you will cease, O people of Quraysh, until Allah sends over you one who will strike your necks for this."" And he refused to return them, saying: ""They are the freedmen of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18845,"(18839) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Ibn Isḥāq, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mukaddam al-Thaqafī, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) besieged the people of al-Ṭāʾif, slaves from among their slaves came out to him—Abū Bakrah, who was a slave of al-Ḥārith ibn Kaladah, and al-Munbaʿith, and Yuḥannis, and Wardān, among a group of their slaves—and they accepted Islam. When the delegation of the people of al-Ṭāʾif came to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and accepted Islam, they said: ""O Messenger of Allah, return to us our slaves who came to you."" He said: ""They are the freedmen of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic."" And he returned to each man the walāʾ of his slave, assigning it to him. This is munqaṭiʿ. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18846,"(18840) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaḥyā al-Sukkārī informed us in Baghdād: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us, from al-Ḥajjāj, from al-Ḥakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā): Indeed, the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) freed those slaves of the polytheists who came out to him on the day of al-Ṭāʾif. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18847,"(18841) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Ismāʿīl al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Ḥajjāj ibn Minhāl and Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us, saying: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us, from al-Ḥajjāj, from al-Ḥakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā): Indeed, four slaves rushed to the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) at the time of al-Ṭāʾif, and he freed them. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18848,"(18842) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Abū Kurayb narrated to us: Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated to us: al-Ḥajjāj narrated to us, from al-Ḥakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā): Two slaves came out from al-Ṭāʾif and accepted Islam, and the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) freed them—one of them was Abū Bakrah. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18849,"(18843) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Nasawī narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Shākir narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to me: Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā informed us: Hishām narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: ʿAṭāʾ said, from Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā), who said: ""If a male or female slave from among them—meaning the people of war—emigrates, then both are free, and they have what the emigrants have."" Al-Bukhārī recorded it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5287]"
18850,"(18844) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: al-Layth narrated to us, from Abī al-Zubayr, from Jābir (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said: A slave came and pledged allegiance to the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) upon emigration, and he did not realize that he was a slave. His master came seeking him, so the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said to him: ""Sell him to me."" So he purchased him with two black slaves. Thereafter, he did not accept allegiance from anyone until he asked him: ""Are you a slave?""

Muslim recorded it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah and others.

Al-Shāfiʿī (raḥimahullāh) said: ""Had Islam freed him, he would not have purchased a free person from him. Rather, he accepted Islam without coming out from a land upon which war was waged."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 1602]"
18851,"(18845) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān al-ʿĀmirī narrated to us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr narrated from al-Aʿmash from ʿAbdullāh ibn Murrah from Masrūq from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""There are four qualities; whoever possesses them is a pure hypocrite, and whoever possesses one of them possesses a quality of hypocrisy until he abandons it: when he speaks he lies, when he makes a covenant he betrays it, when he promises he breaks his promise, and when he disputes he resorts to falsehood.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr from his father, and both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of al-Thawrī from al-Aʿmash. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon]"
18852,"(18846) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that al-Qaʿnabī narrated from Mālik from ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said:

(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr Kashmard narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us that Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār that he heard ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Indeed, the one who acts treacherously shall have a banner erected for him on the Day of Resurrection, and it shall be said: 'This is the treachery of so-and-so.'""

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Ismāʿīl. And in the narration of Mālik: ""Indeed, the one who acts treacherously shall have a banner erected for him on the Day of Resurrection, and it shall be said: 'This is the treachery of so-and-so ibn so-and-so.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon]"
18853,"(18847) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar al-Namarī narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated from Abū al-Fayḍ from Sulaym ibn ʿĀmir, a man from Ḥimyar, who said: There was a treaty between Muʿāwiyah and the Romans, and he was proceeding towards their lands so that when the treaty expired he would invade them. Then a man came on a horse or a mule, saying: ""Allāhu Akbar, Allāhu Akbar — fulfil the covenant and do not act treacherously."" They looked and found it was ʿAmr ibn ʿAbasah (may Allah be pleased with him). So Muʿāwiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) sent for him and asked him, and he said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: ""Whoever has a covenant between himself and a people, let him not tighten its knot nor undo it until its appointed term expires, or he announces its termination to them on equal terms."" So Muʿāwiyah turned back. [Ḥasan]"
18854,"(18848) And Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us that Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated from Abū al-Fayḍ from Sulaym ibn ʿĀmir, who said: There was a treaty between Muʿāwiyah and the Romans, and he mentioned it.

And likewise Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Qaṭṭān, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Bukayr, Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb, and a group narrated it from Shuʿbah. [Ḥasan]"
18855,"(18849) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak narrated to us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us that Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that ʿUyaynah — meaning Ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jūshan — narrated from his father from Abū Bakrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: ""Whoever kills a person under treaty without right, Allah has forbidden Paradise for him.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ — narrated by al-Sijistānī, 2760]"
18856,"(18850) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us that Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Isḥāq ibn al-Khurāsānī informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Salām narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Shaybānī informed us that Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim al-Ghifārī narrated to us. They both said: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā informed us that Bashīr ibn Muhājir informed us from ʿAbdullāh ibn Buraydah from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""No people ever break a treaty but that killing will be rampant among them, and no indecency ever becomes widespread among a people but that Allah will send death upon them, and no people ever withhold zakāh but that Allah will withhold rain from them.""

Al-Ḥusayn ibn Wāqid contradicted him and narrated it from ʿAbdullāh ibn Buraydah from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) as his own statement, which is more complete. And something to that effect has been narrated from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). [Ḍaʿīf]"
18857,"(18851) Abū al-Khayr Jāmiʿ ibn Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Mahdī al-Wakīl informed us that Abū Ṭāhir al-Muḥammadbādhī informed us that ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī informed us that Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm informed us that Abū Hilāl narrated from Qatādah from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) addressed us and said: ""There is no faith for one who has no trustworthiness, and there is no religion for one who does not keep his pledge.""

[Ḍaʿīf — previously cited under number 126902]"
18858,"(18852) By way of evidence, Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī, and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us that Ibn Wahb informed me that Mālik ibn Anas informed me from Ṭalḥah ibn ʿAbd al-Malik al-Aylī from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad from ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) that he said: ""Whoever vows to obey Allah, let him obey Him, and whoever vows to disobey Allah, let him not disobey Him.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Nuʿaym and others from Mālik.

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: And the polytheists captured a woman from the Anṣār and took a she-camel belonging to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). The Anṣārī woman escaped on the Prophet's she-camel, and she vowed that if Allah saved her upon it, she would sacrifice it. This was mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), and he said: ""There is no vow in disobedience, nor in that over which the son of Ādam has no authority."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī, 6696, 6700]"
18859,"(18853) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us that Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us that al-Rabīʿ informed us that al-Shāfiʿī informed us that ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAbd al-Majīd narrated to us that Ayyūb narrated from Abū Qilābah from Abū al-Muhallab from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, and he mentioned its meaning.

Muslim narrated it as previously mentioned. Al-Shāfiʿī said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Whoever swears an oath and then sees something better than it, let him do that which is better and expiate his oath."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim, 6141]"
18860,"(18854) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us that al-Asfāṭī — meaning al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl — narrated to us that Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways narrated to us that ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn al-Muṭṭalib narrated from Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ from his father from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Whoever swears an oath and then sees something better than it, let him do that which is better and expiate his oath.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways.

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""Know that obedience to Allah lies in not fulfilling the oath when something else is better, and in making the expiation that Allah has ordained. All of this indicates that every vow, pledge, or covenant made with a Muslim or a polytheist is fulfilled only when it is permissible and does not involve disobedience to Allah."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim, 1650]"
18861,"(18855) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Dāwūd ibn Sufyān narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar reported, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik, from a man from the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), regarding the story of Banū al-Naḍīr and what they conspired of deception against the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). He said: ""When the following morning came, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) went out to them with the battalions and besieged them. He said to them: 'By Allah, you shall not be secure with me except by a covenant by which you bind yourselves to me.' They refused to give him a covenant, so he fought them that same day. Then he went out to Banū Qurayẓah with the battalions, leaving Banū al-Naḍīr, and called them to enter into a covenant with him, so they made a covenant with him. He then turned away from them and went out to Banū al-Naḍīr with the battalions, fighting them until they agreed to exile. Thus was the covenant of Banū Qurayẓah."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18862,"(18856) As for their breaking of the covenant, Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated, from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Yazīd ibn Rūmān narrated to me, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, who said: Yazīd ibn Ziyād narrated to me, from Muḥammad ibn Kaʿb al-Quraẓī, and ʿUthmān ibn Yahūdhā, one of Banū ʿAmr ibn Qurayẓah, from men of his people, who said: ""Those who assembled the confederate parties (al-Aḥzāb) were a group from Banū al-Naḍīr and a group from Banū Wāʾil. From Banū al-Naḍīr were Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab, Kinānah ibn al-Rabīʾ ibn Abī al-Ḥuqayq, and Abū ʿAmmār. From Banū Wāʾil were a clan from the Anṣār of Aws Allāh, Ḥawḥ ibn ʿAmr, and men from among them. They went out until they came to Quraysh and called them to wage war against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and they became eager for that."" Then he mentioned the story of the expedition of Abū Sufyān ibn Ḥarb and the confederates. He said: ""Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab went out until he came to Kaʿb ibn Asad, the holder of the covenant and pact of Banū Qurayẓah. When Kaʿb heard of his coming, he shut his fortress against him. Ḥuyayy said: 'Woe to you, O Kaʿb! Open for me so that I may enter upon you.' Kaʿb said: 'Woe to you, O Ḥuyayy! You are an ill-omened man, and I have no need of you or of what you have brought me. I have seen nothing from Muḥammad but truthfulness and fidelity to his covenant. He has made a truce with me and I have made a truce with him. Leave me and go away.' Ḥuyayy said: 'By Allah, you have only shut the gate against me out of fear that I should eat with you from your provisions.' This alarmed him, so he opened the gate for him. When Ḥuyayy entered upon him, he said: 'Woe to you, O Kaʿb! I have come to you with the glory of all time — Quraysh with their leaders, whom I have encamped at Rūmah; and I have come to you with Ghifṭān with their chiefs and nobles, whom I have encamped at the side of Uḥud. I have come to you with a surging sea that nothing can repel.' Kaʿb said: 'By Allah, you have come to me with humiliation! Woe to you! Leave me and what I am upon. I have no need of you or of what you are calling me to.' Ḥuyayy ibn Akhṭab continued to coax him persistently until he obeyed him. Ḥuyayy gave him a covenant and solemn pledge that if Quraysh and Ghifṭān returned before they could strike Muḥammad, he would enter with him in his fortress and share whatever befell him. So Kaʿb broke the covenant and declared his dissociation from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and from all that existed between them.""

Ibn Isḥāq said: ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah narrated to me, saying: ""When the news of Kaʿb and the treachery of Banū Qurayẓah reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), he sent Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah, Saʿd ibn Muʿādh, Khawwāt ibn Jubayr, and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rawāḥah to ascertain their condition. When they reached them, they found them in the worst state that had been reported to them.""

Ibn Isḥāq said: ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah narrated to me, from an elder of Banū Qurayẓah, who mentioned the story of the conversion of Thaʿlabah and Asīd, the two sons of Saʿyah, and Asad ibn ʿUbayd, and their descent from the fortress of Banū Qurayẓah, and their acceptance of Islam. On that night, ʿAmr ibn Saʿdī al-Quraẓī went out — and Ibn Isḥāq claimed this — and passed by the guard of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), over whom Muḥammad ibn Maslamah was stationed that night. When Muḥammad saw him, he said: 'Who is this?' He said: 'I am ʿAmr ibn Saʿdī.' ʿAmr had refused to join Banū Qurayẓah in their treachery and had said: 'I shall never betray Muḥammad.' When Muḥammad ibn Maslamah recognized him, he said: 'O Allah, do not deprive me of the stumbling of the noble ones.' Then he let him pass. ʿAmr went and spent that night in the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), then departed, and no one knew where on earth he had gone. His affair was mentioned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and he said: 'That is a man whom Allah saved by his faithfulness.'""

Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah mentioned in this story that Ḥuyayy persisted with them until he brought ill fortune upon them, and their leaders gathered to commit treachery upon the command of a single man, apart from Asad, Asīd, and Thaʿlabah, who went out to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). [Ḍaʿīf]"
18863,"(18857) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Fāris narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Ibn Jurayj reported, from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), that the Jews of al-Naḍīr and Qurayẓah waged war against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) exiled Banū al-Naḍīr and left Qurayẓah in place, showing them favor, until Qurayẓah subsequently waged war. He killed their men and divided their women, wealth, and children among the Muslims, except for some of them who joined the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), so he granted them security and they accepted Islam.

Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it as above. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
18864,"(18858) Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: ""Likewise, if one among them breaks the covenant and fights, it is for the Imam to fight their entire group. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) was aided against Khuzāʿah by three men from Quraysh who witnessed their fighting, so the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) raided Quraysh in the Year of the Conquest on account of the treachery of those three men and their abandonment of Khuzāʿah, and their sheltering of those who fought against Khuzāʿah."""
18865,"(18859) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated, from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: al-Zuhrī narrated to me, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, who both narrated to him, saying: ""In the treaty of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyah between him and Quraysh, it was stipulated that whoever wished to enter into the covenant and pact of Muḥammad could do so, and whoever wished to enter into the covenant and pact of Quraysh could do so. Khuzāʿah leapt forward and said: 'We enter into the covenant and pact of Muḥammad.' Banū Bakr leapt forward and said: 'We enter into the covenant and pact of Quraysh.' They remained in that truce for approximately seven or eighteen months. Then Banū Bakr, who had entered into the covenant and pact of Quraysh, attacked Khuzāʿah, who had entered into the covenant and pact of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), at night at a watering place belonging to them called al-Watīr, near Makkah. Quraysh said: 'Muḥammad does not know of us, and this is the night when no one can see us.' So they aided them against Khuzāʿah with weapons and fought alongside them, out of rancor against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). ʿAmr ibn Sālim rode to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) when the affair of Khuzāʿah and Banū Bakr at al-Watīr occurred, until he reached al-Madīnah and informed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) of the news, having composed verses of poetry. When he came before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), he recited them to him:

'O Allah, I adjure Muḥammad:
We were his father and he was his son.
So aid the Messenger of Allah with a prepared aid,
Among them is the Messenger of Allah, who has stripped himself for battle,
In a host like the sea, surging and abundant.
They have broken your firm covenant.
They are more despised and fewer in number.
They attacked us at al-Watīr while we were in deep sleep,
The alliance of our father and his father, al-Atladā.
Then we accepted Islam and did not withdraw our hand.
Call upon the servants of Allah to come with reinforcements!
If his face is treated with disgrace, it will darken.
Quraysh have broken their promise to you,
And they claim that I do not call upon anyone.
They have set for me an ambush at Kadāʾ.
They killed us while we were bowing and prostrating.'

The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'You have been aided, O ʿAmr ibn Sālim.' He did not cease his place until a cloud formation passed in the sky. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'This cloud is about to pour forth with the aid of Banū Kaʿb.' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded the people to make preparations and concealed from them his point of departure, and he asked Allah to keep the news hidden from Quraysh so that he might take them unawares in their own land."" [Ḥasan]"
18866,"(18860) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād; Abū Bakr ibn ʿAttāb al-ʿAbdī narrated to us; al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mughīrah narrated to us; Ibn Abī Uways informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿUqbah informed us, from his paternal uncle Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, who said: ""Then Banū Nufāthah, from Banū al-Dīl, raided Banū Kaʿb during the period of truce between the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and Quraysh. Banū Kaʿb were in the treaty of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and Banū Nufāthah were in the treaty of Quraysh. Banū Bakr aided Banū Nufāthah, and Quraysh aided them with weapons and slaves. Banū Mudlaj, however, kept apart and fulfilled the covenant. It is mentioned that among those who aided them were Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah, Shaybah ibn ʿUthmān, and Suhayl ibn ʿAmr. Banū al-Dīl raided Banū ʿAmr — and their common folk, it is claimed, were women, children, and weak men — and inflicted heavy casualties upon them, killing some of them until they drove them into the house of Budayl ibn Warqāʾ in Makkah."" He said: ""A rider from Banū Kaʿb went out until he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and informed him of what had befallen them, what Quraysh had done against them, and how they had aided them against them."" Then he mentioned the preparations of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and the coming of Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) to him. He said: Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""O Messenger of Allah, do you intend to set out?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Perhaps you intend the Romans?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Do you intend the people of Najd?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Then perhaps you intend Quraysh?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Is there not between you and them a period of truce?"" He said: ""Has it not reached you what they did to Banū Kaʿb?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) then announced the expedition to the people.

As for adjudication between those bound by covenant, its mention has already passed in the book on Ḥudūd, Ghaṣb, and other subjects. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18867,"(18861) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us; Sufyān narrated, from Thawr ibn Yazīd, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Dīnār, who said: ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Do not learn the jargon of the non-Arabs, and do not enter upon the polytheists in their churches on their festival day, for divine wrath descends upon them."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18868,"(18862) Abū Bakr al-Fārisī informed us; Abū Isḥāq al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us; Abū Aḥmad ibn Fāris narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us. He said: Ibn Abī Maryam said to me: Nāfiʿ ibn Yazīd narrated to us; he heard Sulaymān ibn Abī Zaynab and ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith narrate; they heard Saʿid ibn Salamah narrate; he heard his father narrate; he heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""Avoid the enemies of Allah on their festival days."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18869,"(18863) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us. He said: Sufyān mentioned, from ʿAwf, from al-Walīd — or Abī al-Walīd — from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, who said: ""Whoever builds in the lands of the non-Arabs, celebrates their Nayrūz and Mihrajān, and imitates them until he dies in that state, he will be resurrected with them on the Day of Judgment.""

The Shaykh al-Imām (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: The Shaykh Abū Sulaymān (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: ""That is the correct view."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18870,"(18864) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us; Abū Usāmah narrated to us; ʿAwf narrated, from Abī al-Mughīrah, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, who said: ""Whoever builds in the lands of the non-Arabs, celebrates their Nayrūz and Mihrajān, and imitates them until he dies in that state, he will be resurrected with them on the Day of Judgment.""

And thus it was narrated by Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, Ibn Abī ʿAdī, Ghundar, and ʿAbd al-Wahhāb, from ʿAwf, from Abī al-Mughīrah, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, as his own statement. [Ḍaʿīf]"
18871,"(18865) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Abū Usāmah narrated to us, from Ḥammād ibn Zayd, from Hishām, from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, who said: ʿAlī (may Allāh be pleased with him) was presented with the gift of Nawrūz, so he said: ""What is this?"" They said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, this is the day of Nawrūz."" He said: ""Then make every day a day of triumph (Fīrūz)."" Abū Usāmah said: He disliked that one should say ""Nawrūz."" The Shaykh said: In this [narration] there is [an indication of] the dislike of singling out a specific day for such [celebration], since the Sharīʿah has not designated it as such. [Weak]"
18872,"(18866) Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawayh narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Shādhān al-Jawharī narrated to us: Muʿallā ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Ibn Abī Zāʾidah narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, from Abān ibn Ṣāliḥ, from al-Qaʿqāʿ ibn Ḥakīm, from Salmā Umm Rāfiʿ, from Abī Rāfiʿ, who said: The Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) commanded us to kill the dogs. The people said: ""O Messenger of Allāh, what has been made lawful for us of this species that you have commanded us to kill?"" So Allāh, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: *""They ask you what has been made lawful for them. Say: 'Lawful for you are [all] good things, and [the prey of] those hunting animals which you have trained'""* [al-Māʾidah 4]. [Weak]"
18873,"(18867) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Khālid narrated to us: My father narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Aʿyan narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Abū Shuʿayb al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us: My grandfather narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Aʿyan narrated to us, from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid, from al-Mujālid, from al-Shaʿbī, from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim, who said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allāh, I have dogs with which I hunt."" He said: ""Look at these hunting animals; teach them what Allāh has taught you, and eat of what they retain for you."" [Weak]"
18874,"(18868) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Muzakkī informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Ṭarāʾifī narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us, from Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ, from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭalḥah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allāh be pleased with them both), regarding His, the Most High's, words: *""And [the prey of] those hunting animals which you have trained""* [al-Māʾidah 4]. He said: ""Trained dogs, falcons, and every bird that is trained for hunting."" And regarding His words: *""Mukallibīn""* [al-Māʾidah 4], he said: It means ""predatory beasts (ḍawārī).""

And it has been narrated to us from Mujāhid that he said: ""Al-Jawāriḥ (hunting animals) are birds and dogs.""

And from Qatādah regarding His words: *""Mukallibīn""* [al-Māʾidah 4], he said: ""Those with whom you hunt using predatory animals.""

And it has been narrated to us from Mujāhid regarding His words: *""Which your hands can reach""* [al-Māʾidah 94], he said: It means arrows. And it is also said that ""your hands"" refers to the young of game—chicks and eggs—and ""your spears"" refers to large game. [Weak]"
18875,"(18869) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Jarīr narrated to us, from Manṣūr, from Ibrāhīm, from Hammām ibn al-Ḥārith, from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim (may Allāh be pleased with him), who said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allāh, we send out trained dogs and they retain [the prey] for us, and I mention the name of Allāh."" He said: ""When you send out your trained dog and you have mentioned the name of Allāh, then eat."" I said: ""Even if they kill?"" He said: ""Even if they kill, provided no other dog is with them."" I said to him: ""I also hunt with a miʿrāḍ (broad-headed arrow) and strike game."" He said: ""When you strike with the miʿrāḍ and it pierces [the game], then eat it. But if it strikes it with its broad side, then do not eat it.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm, and al-Bukhārī reported it from two other routes from Manṣūr.

[Sound. Agreed upon]"
18876,"(18870) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh informed us: It was recited to Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā al-Birtī: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us: Zakariyyā narrated to us, from al-Shaʿbī, from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim (may Allāh be pleased with him), who said: I asked the Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) about the prey of a dog. He said: ""Whatever it retains for you, eat, for its seizing it is its slaughtering [of it]. But if you find with your dog or dogs another dog besides it, then do not eat, for you only mentioned the name of Allāh upon your dog and you did not mention it upon the dogs of others.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Nuʿaym, and Muslim reported it from another route from Zakariyyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah.

[Sound. Agreed upon]"
18877,"(18871) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us: ʿĀrim (Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl) narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Zayd narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥakam al-Bunānī informed us that Nāfiʾ ibn al-Asraq asked Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allāh be pleased with them both), saying: ""O Ibn ʿAbbās, what is your opinion: if I send out my dog and pronounce the name [of Allāh] and it kills the game, may I eat it?"" He said: ""Yes."" Nāfiʾ said: ""Allāh says: *'Except what you slaughter'* [al-Māʾidah 3]—you say: 'Even if it is killed.'"" He said: ""Woe to you, O Ibn al-Asraq! What is your opinion: if a cat seizes something for me and I find its slaughtering [opportunity], would there be any harm upon me? By Allāh, I know in reference to which dogs this verse was revealed—it was revealed concerning the dogs of Banī Nabhān of Ṭayyiʾ. Woe to you, O Ibn al-Asraq! There will surely come to you some news."" [Good]"
18878,"(18872) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Abū Muslim Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī al-Safar, from al-Shaʿbī, who said: I heard ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim (may Allāh be pleased with him) say: I asked the Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) about the miʿrāḍ (broad-headed arrow). He said: ""If it strikes with its sharp edge, then eat. But if it strikes with its broad side and kills, then it is waqīdh (killed by a blow), so do not eat."" He said: I said: ""I send out my dog."" He said: ""When you send out your dog and mention the name of Allāh, then eat."" He said: I said: ""If it eats [from the prey]?"" He said: ""Then do not eat, for it has only restrained it for itself and has not restrained it for you."" He said: I said: ""I send out my dog and find with it another dog."" He said: ""Do not eat, for you only pronounced the name upon your dog and you did not pronounce it upon the other.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb, and Muslim reported it from other routes from Shuʿbah.

[Sound. Agreed upon]"
18879,"(18873) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī narrated to us: Mālik ibn Yaḥyā Abū Ghassān narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us: Zakariyyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah and ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal narrated to us, from al-Shaʿbī, from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim (may Allāh be pleased with him), who said: I asked the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) about the prey of the miʿrāḍ. He said: ""What it strikes with its sharp edge, eat. And what it strikes with its broad side is waqīdh (killed by a blow)."" And I asked him about the prey of the dog. He said: ""When you send out your dog and mention the name of Allāh and it retains [the prey] for you, then eat. But if it eats from it, then do not eat. And if you find with it a dog other than your dog, and you fear that it may have seized it together with it and killed it, then do not eat, for you only mentioned the name of Allāh upon your dog and you did not mention it upon other than it."" [Sound]"
18880,"(18874) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Yazīd narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Yūsuf al-Azraq narrated to us: Zakariyyā narrated to us, from al-Shaʿbī, from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim (may Allāh be pleased with him), who said: I asked the Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) about the prey of the dog. He said: ""Whatever it retains for you and does not eat from, then eat it, for its seizing it is its slaughtering [of it]. But if you find with it another dog and you fear that it may have seized it together with it and killed it, then do not eat it, for you only mentioned the name of Allāh upon your dog and you did not mention it upon other than it."" And I asked him about the prey of the miʿrāḍ. He said: ""What you strike with its sharp edge, eat it. And what you strike with its broad side is waqīdh (killed by a blow)."" [Sound. Previously mentioned]"
18881,"(18875) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar informed me; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿĪsā informed us; Ibn al-Mubārak informed us; ʿĀṣim narrated from al-Shaʿbī, from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim (may Allah be pleased with him), that he asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) about hunting. He said: ""When you send your dog, mention the name of Allah. If you overtake it before it has killed [the game], then slaughter it and mention the name of Allah. If you find that it has killed [the game] but has not eaten from it, then eat, for it has caught it for you. But if you find that it has eaten from it, then do not eat anything of it, for it has only caught it for itself."" And he mentioned the hadith.

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Zakariyyā and ʿĀṣim, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb from Ibn al-Mubārak.

[Ṣaḥīḥ — Agreed upon]"
18882,"(18876) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; al-Manīʿī and al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us; Abū Bakr — that is, Ibn Abī Shaybah — narrated to us; Ibn Fuḍayl narrated from Bayān, from al-Shaʿbī, from ʿAdī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). I said: 'We are a people who hunt with these dogs.' He said: 'When you send your trained dogs and mention the name of Allah over them, then eat of what they catch for you, even they kill, unless the dog eats. If the dog eats, then do not eat, for I fear that it has only caught it for itself. And if other dogs are mixed with them, then do not eat.'""

Al-Bukhārī recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah and others from Muḥammad ibn Fuḍayl, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah.

[Ṣaḥīḥ — Agreed upon]"
18883,"(18877) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us; Ibn al-Junayd narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us; my father narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Ibrāhīm, from Hammām, from ʿAdī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, what if it eats from it?' He said: 'If it eats from it, then do not eat, for it is not trained.'""

[Ḍaʿīf]"
18884,"(18878) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us; al-Rabīʿ narrated; al-Shāfiʿī said: ""Analogical reasoning may suggest that one may eat even if the dog has eaten from it. This is the view of Ibn ʿUmar, Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, and some of our companions. However, we abandoned this view due to the athar narrated by al-Shaʿbī from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim (may Allah be pleased with him) that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: 'If it eats, then do not eat.' When a report is established from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), it is not permissible to abandon it for anything.""

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""As for the narration from Ibn ʿUmar regarding this…""

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18885,"(18879) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us; Ibn Numayr narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: ""When one of you sends his trained dog and mentions the name of Allah, let him eat of what it catches for him, whether it has eaten from it or not.""

As for the narration from Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ (may Allah be pleased with him), Mālik mentioned it in al-Muwaṭṭaʾ as munqaṭiʿ (disconnected).

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18886,"(18880) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan al-Sarrāj informed us; Abū Khalīfah narrated to us; Abū ʿAmr al-Ḥawḍī narrated from Shuʿbah, from ʿAbd Rabbih ibn Saʿīd, from Bukayr ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from Saʿd, who said: ""Eat, even if it [the dog] has eaten half of it."" This is also mursal (disconnected).

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18887,"(18881) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ardistānī informed us; Abū Naṣr al-ʿIrāqī narrated to us; Sufyān ibn Muḥammad al-Jawharī narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Hilālī narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Walīd narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb, from Bukayr ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ashajj, from a man called Ḥumayd ibn Mālik, who said: ""I asked Saʿd. I said: 'We have hunting dogs that catch for us and eat from [the game] and leave [some].' He said: 'Eat, even if they leave nothing but half of it.'""

This is mawṣūl (connected). It has also been narrated regarding this from ʿAlī, Salmān al-Fārisī, and Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with them). And it has been narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him) a view contrary to their statements.

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18888,"(18882) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Bishr narrated from Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah, from Qatādah, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, that Salmān al-Fārisī (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say: ""When you send your trained dog and it eats two-thirds of [the game] and one-third remains, then eat what remains.""

And from Saʿīd, from Qatādah, that Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) used to disapprove of that and say: ""If it were truly trained, it would not have eaten.""

And it has been narrated regarding the permissibility of eating it from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), if the ḥadīth is authentic.

[Ḍaʿīf]"
18889,"(18883) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us; Hushaym informed us; Dāwūd ibn ʿAmr narrated from Busr ibn ʿUbayd Allāh, from Abī Idrīs al-Khawlānī, from Abī Thaʿlabah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said regarding game hunted by a dog: ""When you send your dog and mention the name of Allah, then eat, even if it has eaten from it. And eat what your hand retrieves"" — or he said: ""Eat what your hand retrieves for you.""

[Ḥasan]"
18890,"(18884) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn al-Minhāl al-Ḍarīr narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Zurayʿ narrated to us; Ḥabīb al-Muʿallim narrated from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that a Bedouin called Abū Thaʿlabah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have trained dogs, so give me a ruling regarding their game."" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""If you have trained dogs, then eat of what they catch for you."" He asked: ""[Whether the game is] slaughtered or not slaughtered?"" He said: ""Even if it has eaten from it.""

This is in agreement with the ḥadīth of Dāwūd ibn ʿAmr, except that the ḥadīth of Abū Thaʿlabah (may Allah be pleased with him) is recorded in the two Ṣaḥīḥs from the ḥadīth of Rabīʿah ibn Yazīd al-Dimashqī from Abī Idrīs al-Khawlānī from Abū Thaʿlabah, and it does not contain mention of eating. The ḥadīth of al-Shaʿbī from ʿAdī is more authentic than the ḥadīth of Dāwūd ibn ʿAmr al-Dimashqī and the ḥadīth of ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb. And Allah knows best.

(T) Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAbd Rabbih ibn Saʿīd, from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from a man from Hudhayl, that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) about a dog that hunts. He said: ""Eat, whether it has eaten or not."" Thus the ḥadīth of ʿAmr becomes maʿlūl (defective) with this chain.

[Ḥasan]"
18891,"(18885) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr informed us, from Mujāld, from al-Shaʿbī, from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: ""Whatever dog or falcon you have trained, then you send it and you mention the name of Allah, eat from what it has held back for you."" He (ʿAdī) said: ""Even if it kills?"" He said: ""If it kills and does not eat from anything of it, then it has held it back for you.""

He combined the two (the dog and the falcon) in the prohibition, except that the mention of the falcon in this narration was not reported by the ḥuffāẓ whom we have previously mentioned as narrating from al-Shaʿbī; rather, it was Mujāld who reported it. And Allah knows best.

It is narrated from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from Salmān al-Fārisī (may Allah be pleased with him), that he said: ""When you send your dog, your falcon, or your hawk upon the game, and it eats from it, then eat; and if it eats half of it, then this is a combination of the two in permissibility.""

It is narrated from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), that he said: ""If the dog eats, do not eat; and if the hawk eats, then eat, because the dog is able to strike it (the game), whereas the hawk is not able to (strike it in the same manner)."" This is a distinction between the two. And Allah knows best.

In the ḥadīth of al-Thawrī, from Sālim al-Afṭas, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, he said: ""If the falcon eats, do not eat."" And this is contrary to the first (opinion).

It is narrated from Rabīʿ ibn Ṣabīḥ regarding the falcon or the hawk: if it eats, he (ʿAṭāʾ) considered it disliked. And from ʿIkrimah, he said: ""If the falcon or the hawk eats, do not eat."" [Weak]"
18892,"(18886) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Raffāʾ informed us, ʿUthmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Bishr informed us, Ismāʿīl al-Qāḍī informed us, Ibn Abī Uways informed us, Ibn Abī al-Zinād informed us, from his father, from the fuqahāʾ whose opinion is relied upon among the people of Madīnah, that they used to say: ""Whatever the trained dog, hawk, or falcon kills is permissible, even if it eats from it."" [Weak]"
18893,"(18887) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me, al-Ḥasan, who is Ibn Sufyān, informed us, Ḥibbān ibn Mūsā informed us, ʿAbdullāh informed us, ʿĀṣim informed us, from al-Shaʿbī, from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim (may Allah be pleased with him), that he asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) about game. He said: ""When you send your dog, mention the name of Allah. If you overtake it before it has killed, then slaughter it and mention the name of Allah. If you overtake it having killed but not eaten, then it has held it back for you. If you find that it has eaten from it, then do not eat anything from it, for it has held it back for itself. If other dogs have mingled with your dog and they killed but did not eat, then do not eat from it, for you do not know which of them killed it. And when you shoot your arrow, mention the name of Allah.""

And he mentioned the rest of the ḥadīth.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb, from ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak, and al-Bukhārī reported it from another route from ʿĢṣim. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon]"
18894,"(18888) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Yūsuf ibn Mūsā informed us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥayyān and Muḥāḍir, with the same meaning, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me, Ibrāhīm al-Jawzī informed us, Yūsuf informed us, Abū Khālid al-Aḥmar and Muḥāḍir informed us. Abū Khālid said: I heard Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that they said: ""O Messenger of Allah, there are people here who are recent converts to Islam who bring us meats, and we do not know whether they mention the name of Allah upon them or not."" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: ""Mention the name of Allah and eat.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yūsuf ibn Mūsā, from Abū Khālid Sulaymān ibn Ḥayyān al-Aḥmar, and he also reported it from the ḥadīth of Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ṭufāwī and Usāmah ibn Ḥafṣ from Hishām, connected (mawṣūl).

He said: And al-Dārawardī followed them from Hishām.

The Shaykh said: And also Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl, ʿAbd al-Raḥīm ibn Sulaymān, Maslamah ibn Qaʿnab, Yūnus ibn Bukayr, ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ḥārith al-Jumaḥī, and ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿĀṣim — all of them — from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2052]"
18895,"(18889) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us, Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us, Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us, Hishām informed us, from his father, that some people from the desert used to bring meats that they had slaughtered, and they asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) what they should do. He said: ""Pronounce the name of Allah over them and eat them.""

And likewise Mālik ibn Anas and Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated it from Hishām, as a mursal narration, without mentioning ʿĀʾishah, with the meaning of the narration of those who narrated it as connected (mawṣūl). [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18896,"(18890) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Ayyūb al-Ṭūsī informed us, Abū Ḥātim al-Rāzī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Yazīd informed us, Maʿqil ibn ʿUbaydullāh informed us, from ʿAmr, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), that he said: ""The Muslim is sufficient by his name; if he forgets to pronounce the name (of Allah) at the time of slaughtering, then let him mention the name of Allah and eat it.""

Thus he narrated it as marfūʿ (raised to the Prophet). [Munkar]"
18897,"(18891) And others narrated it from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Jābir ibn Zayd, from ʿAyn, who is ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, as mawqūf (stopped at the Companion). Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah informed us, Abū Manṣūr al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl ibn Zakariyyā al-Naḍrūwī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Najdah informed us, Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr informed us, Sufyān informed us, from ʿAmr, from Jābir ibn Zayd, from ʿAyn, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), regarding one who slaughtered and forgot the tasmiyah (pronouncement of Allah's name): ""The Muslim bears the name of Allah, even if he did not pronounce the tasmiyah."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18898,"(18892) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ḥumaydī informed us, Sufyān informed us, ʿAmr informed us, from Abī al-Shaʿthāʾ, who is Jābir ibn Zayd, who said: ʿAyn informed me, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""When a Muslim slaughters and forgets to mention the name of Allah, then let him eat, for in the Muslim is one of the names of Allah.""

He means by ʿAyn: ʿIkrimah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18899,"(18893) And Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us, Abū Manṣūr al-Naḍrūwī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Najdah informed us, Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr informed us, al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl informed us, Khālid ibn ʿAbdullāh informed us, from Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: ""Whoever slaughters and forgets to pronounce the name, let him mention the name of Allah upon it and eat it, and let him not leave it for the devil, when he has slaughtered upon the fiṭrah (primordial nature of Islam)."" [Weak]"
18900,"(18894) Abū Saʿd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Mālinī informed us, Abū Aḥmad ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, ʿAbdān informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Yazīd and al-Ḥasan ibn al-Ḥārith informed us, Abū Hammām informed us, from Marwān ibn Sālim, from al-Awzāʿī, from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from Abī Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what do you say about a man among us who slaughters and forgets to pronounce the name?"" The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: ""The name of Allah is upon every Muslim.""

Abū Aḥmad said: ""The majority of the ḥadīth of Marwān ibn Sālim are such that the trustworthy narrators do not corroborate him in them.""

The Shaykh said: Marwān ibn Sālim al-Jazarī is weak (ḍaʿīf); Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal, al-Bukhārī, and others declared him weak. And this ḥadīth is munkar (objectionable) with this isnād. [Weak]"
18901,"(18895) And in what Abu Dawud narrated in *al-Marāsīl*, from Musaddad, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Dāwūd, from Thawr ibn Yazīd, from al-Ṣalt, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""The slaughter of a Muslim is lawful, whether he mentions the name of Allah or does not mention it, for if he mentions it, he mentions only the name of Allah.""

Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad informed us; Abū al-Ḥusayn al-Fasawī informed us; Abū ʿAlī al-Luʾluʾī narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated it. [Weak]"
18902,"(18896) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Ismāʿīl al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; ʿImrān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn al-Sāʾib, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā), who said: ""The Jews disputed with the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and said: 'We eat what we slaughter, but we do not eat what Allah has killed.' So Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: {And do not eat of that upon which the name of Allah has not been mentioned} [al-Anʿām: 121]."" [Authentic]"
18903,"(18897) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated to us; Isrāʾīl narrated to us; Simāk narrated to us, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā), regarding the words of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: {And indeed, the devils inspire their allies to dispute with you} [al-Anʿām: 121]. He said: ""They say: 'What Allah has slaughtered, do not eat; and what you have slaughtered, eat.' So Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, revealed: {And do not eat of that upon which the name of Allah has not been mentioned} [al-Anʿām: 121]."" [Authentic; narrated previously]"
18904,"(18898) In what Abū Dāwūd al-Sijistānī narrated in *al-Marāsīl*, from al-Nufaylī, from Zuhayr, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn al-Sāʾib, from ʿĀmir: ""That a Bedouin presented a gazelle to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). He said: 'Where did you obtain this?' He said: 'I shot it yesterday, and I searched for it but it eluded me until evening overtook me, so I returned. When I arose in the morning, I followed its tracks and found it in a cave or among rocks, and here is my arrow in it—I recognize it by my arrow.' He (the Prophet) said: 'It has been away from you a night, and I fear that some vermin may have assisted you against it. I have no need of it.'"" [Weak]"
18905,"(18899) And from Naṣr ibn ʿAlī, from Jarīr, from Mūsā ibn Abī ʿĀʾishah, from Abū Razīn, who said: ""A man came to the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) with some game and said: 'I shot it at night and it exhausted me, and I found my arrow in it the next morning, and I recognized my arrow.' He said: 'Night is a creation of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, a great creation. Perhaps something assisted you against it—cast it away from you.'""

Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad informed us with both; Abū al-Ḥusayn al-Fasawī informed us; Abū ʿAlī al-Luʾluʾī narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated them both. [Weak]"
18906,"(18900) And Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; al-Bāghandī narrated to us; Qabīṣah narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us, from Mūsā ibn Abī ʿĀʾishah, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Razīn, from Abū Razīn, from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam): ""When the game disappears from you and then you encounter it…"" And he mentioned the vermin of the earth.

And this Abū Razīn—his name is Masʿūd, the freedman of Shaqīq ibn Salamah—and he is not Abū Razīn, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). And the hadith is mursal, as al-Bukhārī stated. [Weak]"
18907,"(18901) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us; ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us, from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn al-Ḥārith ibn al-Ruḥayl, who reported to him that ʿAmr ibn Maymūn narrated to him from his father: ""That a Bedouin came to ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā) while Maymūn was with him, and said: 'May Allah set you right—I shoot game and sometimes kill it outright and sometimes wound it. What do you think?' Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā) said: 'Eat what you kill outright, and leave what you wound.'"" [Authentic]"
18908,"(18902) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; ʿUbaydullāh ibn Muʿādh narrated to us; my father narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated to us, from al-Ḥakam, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī al-Hudhayl, who said: ""Some of my family instructed me to ask ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā) about certain things, so I wrote them down in a sheet and went to him to ask him. There were people with him asking him, and they asked him about everything that was in my sheet, and I did not ask him about anything. Then a Bedouin man asked him and said: 'I am a slave who tends the camels of my family. A man comes to me asking for water—should I give him water?' He said: 'No.' He said: 'What if I fear he may perish?' He said: 'Then give him enough to sustain him, and then inform his family.' He said: 'I am a man who shoots game and sometimes kills it outright and sometimes wounds it.' He said: 'What you kill outright, eat; and what you wound, do not eat.' I said to al-Ḥakam: 'What is al-iṣmāʾ?' He said: 'Al-iqʿāṣ (killing outright).' I said: 'And what is al-inmāʾ?' He said: 'That which disappears from you.'""

And this has been narrated through another chain from Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā) as a marfūʿ narration, but it is weak. [Authentic]"
18909,"(18903) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī said: ""What you kill outright (al-iṣmāʾ) is that which the dogs kill while you are watching. And what you wound (al-inmāʾ) is that whose place of death disappears from you.""

Al-Shāfiʿī (raḥimahullāh) said: ""And in my view, nothing is valid in this regard except this, unless there comes from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) something—for I would assume it, and everything that contradicts the command of the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) would fall away, and no opinion or analogy would stand alongside it, for Allah has cut off all excuse by the statement of the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam)."" The Sheikh said: ""And this is what al-Shāfiʿī (raḥimahullāh) assumed."" [Authentic]"
18910,"(18904) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Adīb informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; Ḥamdān ibn ʿAmr al-Mawṣilī informed me; Ghassān—that is, ibn al-Rabīʿ al-Mawṣilī—narrated to us; Thābit—that is, ibn Yazīd—narrated to us; ʿĀṣim narrated to us, from al-Shaʿbī, from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), that the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""When you send your dog and mention the name of Allah, and it holds the game for you and kills it, then eat. But if it has eaten from it, then do not eat, for it has held it only for itself. And if your dog mixes with other dogs over which the name of Allah was not mentioned, and they catch and kill, then do not eat, for you do not know which of them killed it. And if you shoot the game and find it after a day or two, and there is nothing in it except the mark of your arrow, then if you wish, eat. But if it falls into water, then do not eat.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the *Ṣaḥīḥ* from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl, from Thābit ibn Yazīd. [Authentic; agreed upon]"
18911,"(18905) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Amr ibn Abi Ja'far informed me; 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn 'Isa informed us; Ibn al-Mubarak informed us; 'Asim informed us, from al-Sha'bi, from 'Adiyy ibn Hatim (may Allah be pleased with him), that he asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) about hunting. He mentioned the hadith, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""When you shoot your arrow, invoke the name of Allah. If you find the game, then eat it — unless you find it has fallen into water, for you do not know whether the water killed it or your arrow. If you find it after one or two nights and you find no mark upon it other than the mark of your arrow, then if you wish to eat from it, eat.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Ayyub, from 'Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak. And Khalid al-Hadha' narrated something similar from al-Sha'bi.

Al-Bukhari said: 'Abd al-A'la narrated from Dawud, from 'Amir, from 'Adiyy. [Sahih — agreed upon.]"
18912,"(18906) Abu 'Amr — Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Adib — informed us; Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed me; Abu Ya'la narrated to us; 'Ubaydullah al-Qawariri narrated to us; 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us; Dawud — who is ibn Abi Hind — narrated to us, from 'Amir — who is al-Sha'bi — from 'Adiyy ibn Hatim (may Allah be pleased with him), that he said: ""O Messenger of Allah, one of us shoots game and follows its trail for a day or two and finds it dead with his arrow in it — may he eat?"" He said: ""Yes, if he wishes."" Or he said: ""He may eat if he wishes."" [Sahih]"
18913,"(18907) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran al-'Adl in Baghdad informed us; Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Misri narrated to us; Sulayman ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us; 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us; Shu'bah informed us; 'Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah informed me.

(And) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us; Muhammad ibn al-Faraj al-Azraq narrated to us; Abu al-Nadr narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah. He said: I heard Sa'id ibn Jubayr narrating from 'Adiyy ibn Hatim al-Ta'i (may Allah be pleased with him), that he said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I shoot the game and search for the trail after a night."" He said: ""If you see the mark of your arrow in it and no wild beast has eaten from it, then eat.""

Shu'bah said: I mentioned that to Abu Bishr, and he said: Ibn Jubayr said, from 'Adiyy ibn Hatim (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""If you see your arrow in it and you do not see in it any mark other than it, and you know that it killed it, then eat it."" [Sahih]"
18914,"(18908) Ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us; Ahmad ibn 'Ali al-Khazzaz narrated to us; 'Ali ibn al-Ja'd al-Jawhari narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated to us, mentioning something similar to it, except that he said: Shu'bah said: ""I narrated it to Abu Bishr, and he said: Sa'id ibn Jubayr said, from 'Adiyy ibn Hatim (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family): 'If you recognize your arrow in it and do not see in it any mark other than it, and you know that it killed it, then eat.'"" [Sahih]"
18915,"(18909) Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak — informed us; 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us; Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Shu'bah and Hushaym narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from 'Adiyy ibn Hatim (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I shoot the game and find it the next day with my arrow in it."" He said: ""If you find your arrow in it and you know that it killed it, and you do not see in it the mark of a wild beast, then eat."" [Sahih]"
18916,"(18910) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Ahmad ibn Ja'far al-Qati'i narrated to us; 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us; my father narrated to us; Hammad ibn Khalid narrated to us; Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from his father, from Abu Tha'labah al-Khushani (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""When you shoot your arrow and it is gone for three nights, and then you find it, eat it as long as it has not putrefied.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn Mihran al-Razi, from Hammad ibn Khalid al-Khayyat. [Sahih — agreed upon.]"
18917,"(18911) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abdillah ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Sahl ibn Bahr narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn Sa'id al-Jawhari narrated to us.

(And) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Isbahani informed us; Abu Muhammad ibn Hayyan narrated to us; Ibn Abi Dawud narrated to us; 'Abdullah ibn Nasr al-Antaki narrated to us. Both said: Ma'n ibn 'Isa narrated to us; Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to us, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from his father, from Abu Tha'labah al-Khushani (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), regarding the one who finds his game after three days: ""He may eat it unless it has putrefied.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Abi Khalaf, from Ma'n. [Sahih]"
18918,"(18912) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Muhammad ibn al-Minhal al-Darir narrated to us; Yazid ibn Zurai' narrated to us; Habib al-Mu'allim narrated to us, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that a Bedouin called Abu Tha'labah said: ""O Messenger of Allah, give me a ruling regarding my bow."" He said: ""Eat what your bow returns to you."" He said: ""Whether slaughtered or not slaughtered?"" He said: ""Even if it disappears from me?"" He said: ""Even if it disappears from you, as long as it has not gone off or you find in it a mark other than your arrow."" He said: ""Give me a ruling regarding the vessels of the Magians if I am compelled to use them."" He said: ""Wash them and eat from them."" [Hasan]"
18919,"(18913) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us; Abu Muhammad ibn Hayyan al-Isbahani informed us; Ibn Abi 'Asim informed us; Abu Musa narrated to us; al-Ansari narrated to us; 'Ubaydullah ibn al-Akhnas narrated to me; 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, from Abu Tha'labah (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, give me a ruling regarding my bow."" He said: ""Eat what your bow returns to you.""

I said: ""If it disappears from me?"" He said: ""Even if it disappears from you, so long as you see in it nothing but the mark of your arrow, or it has not gone off (changed).""

Abu Musa said, meaning: its smell has not changed. The Sheikh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: And it has reached me from Abu Sulayman al-Khattabi (may Allah have mercy on him) that he said: His statement ""as long as it has not gone off (lam yasilla)"" means: as long as it has not putrefied and its smell has not changed. It is said: ""salla al-lahm"" and ""asalla"" — two linguistic forms. This carries the meaning of recommendation (istihbab) rather than prohibition (tahrim), because the change of its smell does not render eating it impermissible. He said: And it has been narrated that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) ate melted fat (ihalah) that had gone off — that is, whose smell had changed. [Hasan]"
18920,"(18914) Abu Muhammad 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf al-Isbahani informed him; Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi narrated to us; 'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Dawri narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn al-Ashyab narrated to us; Shayban ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was invited to barley bread and melted fat (ihalah) that had gone off.""

Al-Bukhari extracted it from the hadith of Hisham, from Qatadah, just as the two Shaykhs (al-Bukhari and Muslim) extracted it in the Book of Pledges (al-Rahn), and the hadith of al-Bahzi regarding the wild donkey that was found shot, and the gazelle in which an arrow was found that had already been discussed in the Book of Hajj and elsewhere. [Sahih — al-Bukhari 2069]"
18921,"(18915) Abū al-Ḥasan — ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ — informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq — informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī — informed us; Abū al-Rabīʿ and Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr — both said: Ḥammād ibn Zayd — informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd — from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm — from ʿĪsā ibn Ṭalḥah — from ʿUmayr ibn Salamah al-Ḍamrī, who said:

The Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) went out until he arrived at al-Rawḥāʾ, where he saw a wounded donkey (that had been hunted). Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr added in his narration: ""in one of its thickets."" They both said together: It was said, ""O Messenger of Allah, this is a wounded donkey."" The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""Leave it, for the one who wounded it will come."" Then a man from the tribe of Bahz came and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I am the one who wounded it; do with it as you wish."" The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallahu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) commanded Abū Bakr (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), so he divided it among the companions. Then the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) continued travelling until he reached al-Uthāyah, between al-ʿArj and al-Ruwaythah, where a gazelle was crouching in the shade, with an arrow in it. The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) ordered a man to stand by it so that the rest of the people could pass by without disturbing it.

[Ṣaḥīḥ]

---"
18922,"(18916) And Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ — informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad — informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr — informed us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī — said: I heard Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd say: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm — informed me, from ʿĪsā ibn Ṭalḥah, that ʿUmayr ibn Salamah al-Ḍamrī informed him, from the man of Bahz, that:

The Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) went out while he was in the state of iḥrām, until he was in one of the thickets of al-Rawḥāʾ, where there was a wounded wild donkey. The people mentioned it to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). And he mentioned the hadith.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. It was cited before.]

---"
18923,"(18917) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us; Abū ʿAbdillāh ibn Yaʿqūb — informed me; Muḥammad ibn al-Naḍr, meaning al-Jārūdī — informed us; Abū Hammām, al-Walīd ibn Shujaʿ — informed us; ʿAlī ibn Muhāhir — from ʢāṣim — from al-Shaʿbī — from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said:

The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said to me: ""When you send your dog, mention the name of Allah. If your dog holds the quarry for you and you find it alive, then slaughter it. If you find that it has killed the quarry but has not eaten from it, then eat it. If you find, along with your dog, another dog that has killed it, then do not eat, for you do not know which of the two killed it. And when you shoot your arrow, mention the name of Allah. If it goes missing from you for a day and you find nothing in it but the trace of your arrow, then eat if you wish. But if you find it drowned in water, then do not eat.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Walīd ibn Shujaʿ.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon (muttafaq ʿalayh).]

---"
18924,"(18918) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī, and Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī — all said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam — informed us; Ibn Wahb — informed me; Ḥaywah ibn Sharīḥ — informed me that he heard Rabīʿah ibn Yazīd al-Dimashqī say: I heard Abū Idrīs al-Khawlānī narrate that he heard Abū Thaʿlabah al-Khushanī (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) say:

I came to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, our land is a land of hunting. I hunt with a trained dog and with a dog that is not trained. Inform me what is lawful for us and what is unlawful for us in that regard."" He said: ""As for what your trained dog catches, eat from what it holds for you, and mention the name of Allah. As for what your untrained dog catches, if you reach it while it still has life enough to be slaughtered, then eat from it. But if you do not reach it in time to slaughter it, then do not eat from it.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdillāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ, from Ḥaywah. And Muslim narrated it from Abū al-Ṭāhir, from Ibn Wahb.

And ʿAbdillāh ibn al-Mubārak said, from Ḥaywah, in this hadith: ""I hunt with my trained dog and with my dog that is not trained.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon (muttafaq ʿalayh).]

---"
18925,"(18919) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed me; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī — informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn — informed us; Ādam ibn Abī Iyās — informed us; Shuʿbah — informed us; ʿAbdillāh ibn Abī al-Safar — from al-Shaʿbī — from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said:

I asked the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) about hunting. I said, ""I send my dog and I find with my dog another dog, and I do not know which of the two caught it."" He said: ""Do not eat it, for you only mentioned the name of Allah upon your own dog and did not mention it upon the other.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam. And Muslim narrated it through other chains from Shuʿbah.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon (muttafaq ʿalayh).]

---"
18926,"(18920) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak — informed us; ʿAbdillāh ibn Jaʿfar — informed us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb — informed us; Abū Dāwūd — informed us; Shuʿbah — from al-Ḥakam — from al-Shaʿbī — from ʿAdī (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said:

I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I send my dog upon the game."" And he mentioned the hadith.

Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Ghundar, from Shuʿbah.

[Ṣaḥīḥ]

---"
18927,"(18921) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed me; Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar — informed us; ʿAbdillāh ibn Muḥammad — informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿĪsā — informed us; Ibn al-Mubārak — informed us; ʿĀṣim — from al-Shaʿbī — from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), that:

He asked the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) about hunting, and he mentioned the hadith. In it, from the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam): ""If your dog mingles with other dogs and they kill it without eating from it, then do not eat anything from it, for you do not know which of them killed it.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb, from Ibn al-Mubārak.

[Ṣaḥīḥ]

---"
18928,"(18922) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr — both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us; Abū ʿUtbah Aḥmad ibn al-Faraj — informed us; Baqiyyah ibn al-Walīd — informed me; al-Zubaydī — informed me; Yūnus ibn Sayf — informed me; Abū Idrīs ʿĀʾidhullāh — from Abī Thaʿlabah al-Khushanī (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said:

I came to the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, we are in a land of hunting. I shoot with my bow, and sometimes I reach the quarry in time to slaughter it, and sometimes I do not reach it in time to slaughter it. And I send my trained dog, and sometimes I reach the quarry in time to slaughter it, and sometimes I do not reach it in time to slaughter it."" The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""That which your bow, your dog, and your hand return to you — eat, whether properly slaughtered or not.""

[Ḥasan. It was cited before under no. 18883.]

---"
18929,"(18923) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī — informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam — informed us; Ibn Wahb — informed me; ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith — informed me that ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb narrated to him that a client of Sharḥabīl ibn Ḥasanah narrated to him that he heard ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir al-Juhanī and Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān, two Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), both say:

The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""That which your bow returns to you is lawful.""

[Ḍaʿīf]

---"
18930,"(18924) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī — both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq — informed us; Hāshim ibn al-Qāsim — informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Dīnār — from Zayd ibn Aslam — from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār — from Abī Wāqid al-Laythī (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said:

When the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) arrived in Madīnah, the people were cutting off the humps of camels and the tails of sheep. The Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""Whatever is cut from a living animal is carrion (maytah).""

[Ḍaʿīf]"
18931,"(18925) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us — Abu al-Hasan al-Tarā'ifī narrated to us — ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from Muʿāwiya ibn Ṣāliḥ — from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭalḥah — from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: ""Eat of the game hunted by the People of the Book, but do not eat of the game hunted by the Magians."""
18932,"(18926) Abu al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Maʿrūf al-Isfarā'inī narrated to us in Herat — Bishr ibn Aḥmad narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Ṣūfī narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abu Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us — Abu Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to me — al-Ṣūfī, meaning Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār, narrated to us — Abū Muslim, Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Mu'addib, narrated to us — Sharīk narrated from Ḥajjāj — from al-Qāsim ibn Abī Bazza — from Sulaymān al-Yashkurī — from Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""We were forbidden from the game of the Magian's dog and his bird.""

And Wakīʿ also narrated it from Sharīk. However, Ḥajjāj ibn Arṭāh is not used as a proof (in hadith criticism). And Allah knows best.

And Yaḥyā ibn Abī Bukayr narrated from Sharīk — from Ḥajjāj ibn Arṭāh — from al-Qāsim ibn Abī Bazza and Abū al-Zubayr — from Sulaymān al-Yashkurī — from Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""He forbade the slaughter of the Magian and the game of his dog and his bird.""

In this chain of narration there is one who is not used as a proof. And Allah knows best. [Weak (Ḍaʿīf)]"
18933,"(18927) Abu ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abu Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said — Abu al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us — Saʿ ibn ʿĀmir narrated from Shuʿbah — from Saʿīd ibn Masrūq — from ʿAbāyah ibn Rifāʿah ibn Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj — from his grandfather Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""We said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we shall meet the enemy tomorrow and we have no knives.' He said: 'Whatever causes the blood to flow and upon which the name of Allah has been mentioned — then eat, but not with the tooth and the claw. As for the tooth, it is bone; and as for the claw, it is the knife of the Ethiopians.'""

He said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ acquired some war spoils, and a camel bolted from among them. They pursued it but could not overtake it. A man shot it with an arrow and restrained it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'These camels — or he said, these beasts of burden — have their wild ones just as the wild animals have theirs. So whatever of them overcomes you, deal with it in this manner.'""

He said: ""A camel fell into a well and they were unable to slaughter it except from the side of its shank. Ibn ʿUmar bought a tenth of it for two dirhams."" Abu ʿAbd Allāh and Abu Saʿīd said in al-Fawā'id: ""A tenth.""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah and others. [Authentic (Ṣaḥīḥ). Agreed upon (Muttafaq ʿalayh)]"
18934,"(18928) Abu Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak narrated to us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us — Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Zā'idah ibn Qudāmah al-Thaqafī narrated to us — Saʿīd ibn Masrūq al-Thawrī narrated from ʿAbāyah ibn Rifāʿah ibn Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj — from his grandfather Rāfiʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Dhū al-Ḥulayfah in Tihāmah. The people were hungry and acquired camels and sheep. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came upon them while the cooking pots had been set up. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered that the pots be overturned. Then he divided among them, making ten sheep equal to one camel.""

He said: ""A camel bolted from among the people's camels, and the people had only a few horses. A man shot it with an arrow and restrained it. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'These camels have their wild ones just as the wild animals have theirs. So whatever of them overcomes you, deal with it in this manner.'""

And from ʿAbāyah, from Rāfiʿ, who said: ""We said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we shall meet the enemy tomorrow and we have no knives — may we then slaughter with reeds?' The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Whatever causes the blood to flow and upon which you have mentioned the name of Allah — then eat, except the tooth and the claw. And I shall inform you about that: as for the tooth, it is bone; and as for the claw, it is the knife of the Ethiopians.'""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Zā'idah. [Authentic (Ṣaḥīḥ). Agreed upon (Muttafaq ʿalayh)]"
18935,"(18929) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — al-Bāghandī narrated to us — Qabīṣah narrated to us — Sufyān narrated from Abīyah — from ʿAbāyah ibn Rifāʿah ibn Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj — from Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ at Dhū al-Ḥulayfah. The people acquired camels and sheep."" He mentioned the ḥadīth in similar terms. ʿAbāyah said: ""Then a watering camel fell into a well in al-Madīnah and was slaughtered from the side of its shank. Ibn ʿUmar took a tenth of it for two dirhams.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Qabīṣah the ḥadīth of the tooth, and he narrated it in full from another chain from Sufyān. [Authentic (Ṣaḥīḥ). Agreed upon (Muttafaq ʿalayh)]"
18936,"(18930) Abu Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh narrated to us — Abu Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us — Abū Marwān narrated to us — ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Dārawardī narrated from Ḥarām — from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān and Muḥammad, the two sons of Jābir — from their father, who said: ""A refractory, bolting cow passed by us — it would not pass anyone without goring and overpowering him. We went out to drive it until we reached al-Ṣamā'. With us was a Coptic slave boy of the sons of Ḥarām, and he had a wrapped garment. The cow charged at him to gore him, so he struck it below the slaughtering place and above the shoulder joint. It carried its wound and fled, and no sharpness could be found for it (i.e., it could not be properly slaughtered). Jābir said: 'I informed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ of its condition. He said: ""When a domestic camel becomes wild and unruly, that which renders the wild animal lawful renders it lawful as well. Return to your cow and eat it."" So we returned to it and consumed it.'"""
18937,"(18931) The Imām Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Isfarā'inī narrated to us — Abu Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm al-Shāfiʿī in Baghdad narrated to us — Abū Qilābah ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad al-Raqāshī narrated to us — Yaʿqūb ibn Isḥāq al-Ḥaḍramī narrated to us — Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Abū al-ʿAsharā' al-Dārimī — from his father, who said: ""I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, is the slaughter (dhakāh) only in the throat and the breast?' He said: 'By your father, if you were to thrust a spear into its thigh, it would suffice you.'""

The Shaykh said: ""And this pertains to the animal that falls into a well and its like."" [Weak (Ḍaʿīf)]"
18938,"(18932) Abu Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ardistānī narrated to us — Abu Naṣr al-ʿIrāqī narrated to us — Sufyān ibn Muḥammad narrated to us — ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Walīd narrated to us — Sufyān narrated from Khālid al-Ḥadhā' — from ʿIkrimah — from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: ""Whatever domestic animal you are unable to restrain, it is in the position of game — so shoot it with a spear."" [Authentic (Ṣaḥīḥ)]"
18939,"(18933) He said: And Sufyān narrated to us — Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit said: ""A man came to ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: 'A camel of mine bolted and I struck it with a spear.' He said: 'Give me its rump as a gift.'"" [Weak (Ḍaʿīf)]"
18940,"(18934) Abu ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — Abu ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us — Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to us — Abū ʿUmays narrated from Ghaḍbān, who is Yazīd ibn Ghaḍbān al-Bajalī — from his father, who said: ""The people arrived in al-Kūfah. A man from the tribe encamped and purchased a she-camel. It bolted and fled. Then he purchased another, fearing it might bolt as well. He hamstrung it and mentioned the name of Allah, and it died. They came to ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with him, and asked him. He commanded them to eat it. But by Allah, the souls of the tribe were not at ease to eat anything from it until they set aside a portion for him. Then they brought it to him, and he ate it. The tribe returned to their food and ate."" [Weak (Ḍaʿīf)]"
18941,"(18935) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Ibn Saʿīd ibn Masrūq — and in the narration of Abū Saʿīd, from ʿUmar ibn Saʿid ibn Masrūq, from his father, from ʿAbāyah ibn Rifāʿah, from Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, we shall meet the enemy tomorrow and we do not have knives with us. May we slaughter with a piece of bamboo or the like?"" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""That which causes the blood to flow and upon which the name of Allah has been mentioned, eat from it, except that which is done with a tooth or a nail. For the tooth is a bone of man, and the nail is the knife of the Ethiopians."" [Sahih. Previously cited.]"
18942,"(18936) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Sahl ibn Baḥr narrated to us: Ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Muslim, from Saʿīd ibn Masrūq, from ʿAbāyah ibn Rifāʿah, from his grandfather Rāfiʿ, who said — Sufyān said: then ʿUmar ibn Saʿīd ibn Masrūq narrated it to me afterwards from his father, from ʿAbāyah ibn Rifāʿah, from his grandfather — who said: We said: ""O Messenger of Allah, we shall meet the enemy tomorrow and we do not have knives with us. May we slaughter with a piece of bamboo?"" He said: ""That which causes the blood to flow and upon which the name of Allah has been mentioned, eat from it, except that which is done with a nail or a tooth. For the tooth is a bone of man, and the nail is the knife of the Ethiopians.""

He said: ""We acquired camels and sheep, and we would consider one camel equal to ten sheep. Then one of the camels bolted from us, and we shot it with arrows until we subdued it. We asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), and he said: 'Indeed, these camels have wild tendencies like the wild beasts. So if any of them bolts, do the same with it, and eat.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar. [Sahih. Previously cited.]"
18943,"(18937) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us. Both said: Musaddad narrated to us: Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Masrūq narrated from ʿAbāyah ibn Rifāʿah, from his father, from his grandfather Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we shall meet the enemy tomorrow and we do not have knives with us.' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: 'Be quick or hasten — that which causes the blood to flow and upon which the name of Allah has been mentioned, eat from it, so long as it is not a tooth or a nail. And I shall tell you about that: as for the tooth, it is a bone, and as for the nail, it is the knife of the Ethiopians.'""

""The people rushed ahead and hastened, acquiring spoils of war, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was among the last of the people. They set up cooking pots. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) passed by the cooking pots and ordered that they be overturned, and he distributed among them, considering one camel equal to ten sheep. Then one of the people's camels bolted, and they had no horses with them. A man shot it with an arrow, and Allah restrained it. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: 'Indeed, these camels have wild tendencies like the wild beasts. So whatever does this among them, do the same with it.'""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad. Thus said Abū al-Aḥwaṣ: ""from his father, from his grandfather."" The rest of the narrators from Saʿīd said: ""from ʿAbāyah, from his grandfather."" Ḥassān ibn Ibrāhīm al-Kirmānī agreed with Abū al-Aḥwaṣ upon his narration. [Sahih. Agreed upon.]"
18944,"(18938) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed him: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us: ʿAlī ibn al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAqīl al-Khuzāʿī informed us: Abū Shuʿayb al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdillāh narrated to us. Both said: Ḥassān ibn Ibrāhīm al-Kirmānī narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Masrūq narrated from ʿAbāyah ibn Rifāʿah, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), a similar narration."
18945,"(18939) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq and ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad said: Hannād ibn al-Sarī narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak informed us: Ḥaywah ibn Sharīḥ informed us, saying: I heard Rabīʿah ibn Yazīd al-Dimashqī say: Abū Idrīs — ʿĀʾidhullāh — informed me, saying: I heard Abū Thaʿlabah al-Khushanī (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family)…"" and he mentioned the hadith, in which he said: ""As for what you mentioned about your being in a land of hunting — whatever you strike with your bow, mention the name of Allah upon it, then eat.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Hannād ibn al-Sarī, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from Ibn al-Mubārak.

Abū Bakr al-Ardistānī informed us: Abū Naṣr al-ʿIrāqī informed us: Sufyān narrated to us: al-Aʿmash narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Murrah, from Masrūq, who said: ʿAbdullāh said: ""When one of you shoots a game animal and it falls from a mountain and dies, do not eat it, for I fear that the fall killed it. Or if it falls into water and dies, do not eat it, for I fear that the water killed it."" [Sahih.]"
18946,"(18940) Abū Bakr al-Ardistānī informed us: Abū Naṣr al-ʿIrāqī informed us: Sufyān informed us: al-Aʿmash narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Murrah, from Masrūq, who said: ʿAbdullāh said: ""When one of you shoots a game animal and it falls from a mountain and dies, do not eat it, for I fear that the fall killed it. Or if it falls into water and dies, do not eat it, for I fear that the water killed it."" [Sahih.]"
18947,"(18941) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us: Ḥāmid ibn Shuʿayb narrated to us: Sharīḥ narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated from ʿĀṣim, from al-Shaʿbī, from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) about hunting. He said: 'When you shoot your arrow, mention the name of Allah. If you find that it has killed it, then eat. But if you find that it has fallen into water and died, then you do not know whether the water killed it or your arrow killed it, so do not eat.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb from Ibn al-Mubārak. [Sahih. Agreed upon.]"
18948,"(18942) Abū Bakr al-Ardistānī informed us: Abū Naṣr al-ʿIrāqī informed us: Sufyān ibn Muḥammad informed us: ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Walīd informed us: Sufyān informed us: al-Aʿmash narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Murrah, from Masrūq, who said: ʿAbdullāh said: ""When one of you shoots a game animal and it falls from a mountain and dies, do not eat it, for I fear that the fall killed it. Or if it falls into water and dies, do not eat it, for I fear that the water killed it."" [Sahih. Previously cited.]"
18949,"(18943) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAmr ʿUthmān ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Daqqāq in Baghdād narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated to us: Kahmas narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh informed me: Abū al-Walīd narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Muʿādh narrated to us: my father narrated to us: Kahmas narrated from Ibn Buraydah, who said: ""ʿAbdullāh ibn Mughaffal (may Allah be pleased with him) saw one of his companions throwing stones (khadhf). He said: 'Do not throw stones, for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) used to dislike — or he said, used to forbid — stone-throwing, for no game is hunted by it and no enemy is injured by it. Rather, it breaks the tooth and pierces the eye.' Then he saw him throwing stones again after that, and he said to him: 'Shall I inform you that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) used to dislike — or used to forbid — stone-throwing, and yet you still throw stones? I shall not speak a word to you for such-and-such a period.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUbaydullāh ibn Muʿādh, and from Abū Dāwūd Sulaymān ibn Maʿbad from ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar. al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from Kahmas. [Sahih. Agreed upon.]"
18950,"(18944) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Qatādah, who heard ʿUqbah ibn Ṣuhbān narrate from ʿAbdullāh ibn Mughaffal al-Muzanī (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade stone-throwing (al-khadhf) and said: ""No game is hunted by it and no enemy is injured by it. Rather, stone-throwing breaks the tooth and pierces the eye."" Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥs from the hadith of Shuʿbah with its meaning, and this wording is more explicit in what we intended. [Sahih. Agreed upon.]"
18951,"(18945) Abu 'Abd al-Rahman — Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Sulami — informed us; Abu al-Hasan — Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Karazi — informed us; 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz — narrated to us; from Abu 'Ubayd — narrated to us; Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash — narrated to us; from 'Asim ibn Abi al-Najud — from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, who said:

""I came to Madinah and went out on the day of 'Id, and suddenly there was a man, ambidextrous, walking among the people as though he was riding, and he was saying: 'Make hijrah, but do not merely imitate hijrah. And guard against the rabbit being struck by one of you with a stick; rather, let the bamboo spears and arrows slaughter it for you.'""

Abu 'Ubayd said: His statement ""Make hijrah, but do not merely imitate hijrah"" means: Sincerise your intention in hijrah, and do not resemble the Muhajirun without intention from you — for that is mere imitation (tajahhur). He said: And the speech of the Arabs is ""a'sar yasr,"" meaning one who works with both his hands equally.

[Hasan]"
18952,"(18946) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr — said: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn Marzuq — narrated to us; Abu 'Amir — narrated to us; from Zuhayr — from Zayd ibn Aslam — from Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with both of them), that he used regard an animal killed by a bullet as: ""That is al-mawquthah (the one struck dead).""

[Hasan]"
18953,"(18947) Abu Ahmad al-Mihranjani — informed us; Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Muzakka — narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ibrahim — narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr — narrated to us; Malik — from Nafi', who said:

""I hunted two birds with a stone and caught them. As for one of them, it died, so 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) threw it away. As for the other, 'Abd Allah went to slaughter it with an adze, but it died before he could slaughter it, so he also threw it away.""

[Sahih]"
18954,"(18948) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakka — said: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam — informed us; Ibn Wahb — informed us; Sufyan al-Thawri and another man — informed me; from Mansur — from al-Nakha'i — from Hammam ibn al-Harith — from 'Adiyy ibn Hatim (may Allah be pleased with him), who said:

""I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) about the mi'radh (a blunt hunting implement), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: 'When you shoot it and you have pronounced the name of Allah, and it pierces through, then eat it. But if you strike it with its broad side and it kills it, then do not eat it.'""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Qabisah, from Sufyan, and Muslim also transmitted it as previously mentioned.

[Sahih — Agreed upon]"
18955,"(18949) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran al-'Adl in Baghdad — informed us; Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar — narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan — narrated to us; Yazid ibn Harun — informed us; 'Asim and Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah — narrated to us; from al-Sha'bi — from 'Adiyy ibn Hatim (may Allah be pleased with him), who said:

""I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) about the game of the mi'radh, and he said: 'What you strike with its sharp edge, eat it; and what you strike with its broad side, it is waqith (killed by blunt force).'""

Al-Bukhari and Muslim both transmitted it in al-Sahih from the hadith of 'Asim al-Ahwal and Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah and others.

[Sahih — Agreed upon]"
18956,"(18950) Abu Zakariyya — Yahya ibn Ibrahim al-Muzakka — narrated to us; Abu al-Hasan — Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Tara'ifi — narrated to us; 'Uthman ibn Sa'id — narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Salih — narrated to us; from Mu'awiyah ibn Salih — from 'Ali ibn Abi Talhah — from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with both of them), regarding this verse:

""{And what is slaughtered for other than Allah}"" (al-Ma'idah 3) — he said: It means what is dedicated to all the taghut (false deities).

""{And al-munkhaniqah}"" (al-Ma'idah 3) — it is the animal that is strangled and dies.

""{And al-mawquthah}"" (al-Ma'idah 3) — it is the animal that is struck with wood until it is felled and dies.

""{And al-mutaddiyah}"" (al-Ma'idah 3) — it is the animal that falls from a mountain and dies.

""{And al-natiqah}"" (al-Ma'idah 3) — it is the sheep that is gored by another sheep.

""{And what a predator has eaten}"" (al-Ma'idah 3) — he says: What a predator has seized — so whatever you catch of all this and its tail is still moving or its eye is still blinking, then slaughter it and mention the name of Allah over it, and it is halal.""

And he said in another place of this tafsir: ""{Except what you slaughter}"" (al-Ma'idah 3) — he says: What you slaughter of these while it still has life in it, then eat it — it is a proper slaughter. And what is slaughtered upon the nusub (sacrificial stones) — and the nusub are stone altars upon which they used to slaughter and dedicate.

And in another place of this tafsir he said: They are the idols.

And in His statement: ""{And that you seek division by the azlam (arrows)}"" (al-Ma'idah 3) — he means the divining arrows, by which they used to seek decisions in their affairs.

""{That is fisq (transgression)}"" (al-Ma'idah 3) — he means: whoever eats of all of that, it is fisq.

[Da'if]"
18957,"(18951) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz — narrated to us; Abu al-Hasan — 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn Sakhtawayh — informed us; 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz — informed us that Mu'alla ibn al-Asad al-'Ammi — narrated to them, saying: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar — narrated to us; Musa ibn 'Uqbah — narrated to us; Salim — informed me that he heard 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with both of them) narrate from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) that he met Zayd ibn 'Amr ibn Nufayl at the lower part of Baldah, and that was before revelation had come down upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). He presented to him a platter containing meat, but he refused to eat from it. Then he said: ""I do not eat what you slaughter upon your ansab (sacrificial stones), and I do not eat except that over which the name of Allah has been mentioned.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Mu'alla ibn al-Asad.

[Sahih — Bukhari 3026]"
18958,"(18952) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan — informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar — narrated to us; Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi — narrated to us; 'Amr — he is ibn Marzuq — informed us; Shu'bah — narrated to us; from al-Qasim ibn Abi Bazzah — from Abu al-Tufayl, who said:

""'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) was asked: 'Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) single out anything for you?' He said: 'He did not single out anything for us that was not for all the people generally, except what is in the sheath of this sword of mine.' He said: Then he produced a scroll, and in it was: 'May Allah curse the one who slaughters for other than Allah, and may Allah curse the one who steals the landmarks of the earth, and may Allah curse the one who curses his parents, and may Allah curse the one who gives refuge to a muhdith (one who commits innovation).'""

Muslim transmitted it in al-Sahih from the hadith of Ghundar, from Shu'bah.

[Sahih — Muslim 1978]"
18959,"(18953) Abu Muhammad — 'Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar in Baghdad — informed us; Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar — narrated to us; Sa'dan ibn Nasr — narrated to us; Abu Mu'awiyah — narrated to us; from Yahya ibn Sa'id — from Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Habban — from Muhammad ibn Zayd, that:

""A man slaughtered a sheep while he thought it had died, but it moved. So he asked Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), and he said to him: 'Eat it.' Then he asked Zayd ibn Thabit, and he said to him: 'Do not eat it, for the dead animal (maytah) can move.'""

[Sahih]"
18960,"(18954) Abu Ahmad al-Mihranjani — informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Ja'far al-Muzakka — narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ibrahim — narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr — narrated to us; Malik — from Yahya ibn Sa'id — from Abu Murrah, the freedman of 'Aqil, that:

""He asked Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) about a sheep that had been slaughtered and part of it moved, and he commanded him to eat it. Then he asked Zayd ibn Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) about that, and Zayd said: 'The dead animal (maytah) — I think he said — does move,' and he forbade him from it.""

And similarly it was narrated by Sulayman ibn Bilal from Yahya ibn Sa'id.

[Sahih]"
18961,"(18955) And a marfūʿ hadith has been narrated regarding it from Zayd, as Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Bālawayh informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us: my father narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us, he said: I heard Ḥāḍir ibn Muhājir, Abū ʿĪsā al-Bāhilī, say: I heard Sulaymān ibn Yasār narrating from Zayd ibn Thābit (may Allah be pleased with him) that a wolf attacked a sheep, so they slaughtered it with a stone, and the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) permitted its consumption. [Weak]"
18962,"(18956) And as Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us: Sahl ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUmar al-Wāqidī narrated to us: Rabīʿah ibn ʿUthmān narrated from Zayd ibn Abī ʿAttāb, from Sulaymān ibn Yasār, from Zayd ibn Thābit, who said: A question was posed to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) regarding a sheep in which a wolf had bitten, and it was caught while still alive, so it was slaughtered. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) commanded its consumption. [Very weak]"
18963,"(18957) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Yaʿqūb narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, from a man of Banī Ḥārithah, who said: He was grazing a she-camel in a valley of the valleys of Uḥud when death overtook it. He found nothing with which to slaughter it, so he took a peg and struck it into its chest until its blood flowed. Then he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and informed him of that, and he commanded him to consume it. [Weak]"
18964,"(18958) Abū al-Ṭayyib, Sahl ibn Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān (may Allah have mercy on him), informed us by dictation: Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Maṭar al-ʿAdl narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Abī Suwayd al-Baṣrī narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Rajāʾ informed us: Isrāʾīl narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from ʿAmr ibn Sharḥabīl, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: We had a sheep that was about to die, so we slaughtered it and divided it. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) came and said: ""O ʿĀʾishah, what happened to your sheep?"" She said: ""It was about to die, so we slaughtered it and divided it, and nothing remains of us from it except a shoulder."" He said: ""The entire sheep is yours except the shoulder.""

And it is narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Ibn al-Musayyab, that he used to say: Slaughter (al-dhakāh) is valid when the eye blinks, the tail moves, and the leg kicks. ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr, Ṭāwūs, and Qatādah said something to that effect. [Weak]"
18965,"(18959) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad al-Kaʿbī informed me: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed me: ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us — what we preserved from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār — he said: I heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent us out as three hundred riders, our commander being Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ, to intercept a caravan of Quraysh. We encamped by the coast. Sufyān said on another occasion: We reached the coast and encamped there for half a month, and severe hunger afflicted us until we ate al-khaṭb (leaves). That army was called the Army of al-Khaṭb. Then the sea cast up for us a creature called al-ʿAnbar (a whale). We ate from it for half a month and used its fat as oil until our bodies regained strength. Abū ʿUbaydah took one of its ribs and erected it, and he went to the tallest man among them. Sufyān said on another occasion: Abū ʿUbaydah took one of its ribs and erected it, and he took a man and a camel, and they passed beneath it. Jābir said: A man from the people slaughtered three camels, then three camels, then three camels, but Abū ʿUbaydah forbade him.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī, and Muslim narrated it from ʿAbd al-Jabbār ibn al-ʿAlāʾ from Sufyān. [Sound — agreed upon]"
18966,"(18960) Al-Ḥumaydī narrated it from Sufyān, but he did not mention the coast. He said: Abū ʿUbaydah took one of its ribs and erected it, then looked for the tallest man and the largest camel in the army and commanded him to ride the camel and pass beneath it, so he did so and passed beneath it. We came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and informed him. He said: ""Do you have any of it with you?"" We said: ""No.""

Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us: al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us, and he mentioned it. [Sound — as previously mentioned]"
18967,"(18961) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Yaḥyā narrated from Ibn Jurayj, who informed me: ʿAmr ibn Dīnār informed me that he heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh (may Allah be pleased with him) say: We went out with the Army of al-Khaṭb, and our commander was Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ. We were afflicted with severe hunger, and the sea cast up a dead whale the like of which had never been seen, called al-ʿAnbar. We ate from it for half a month. Abū ʿUbaydah (may Allah be pleased with him) took one of its bones, and a rider passed beneath it.

Ibn Jurayj said: And Abū al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jābir (may Allah be pleased with him) say: Abū ʿUbaydah said: ""Eat."" When we arrived, we mentioned that to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). He said: ""Eat, for it is a provision that Allah has brought forth. Feed us if you have any of it."" So some of them brought some of it to him, and he ate from it.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, with the addition of Abū al-Zubayr as such. [Sound]"
18968,"(18962) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Ismāʿīl al-Qāḍī informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūnus narrated to us: Zuhayr narrated to us: Abū al-Zubayr narrated from Jābir.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Abū Khaythamah narrated from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent us out and appointed over us Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ to intercept a caravan of Quraysh. He provisioned us with a bag of dates, and he could find nothing else for us. Abū ʿUbaydah used to give us one date at a time. We said: ""How did you used to manage with it?"" He said: ""We would suck it as a child sucks, then drink water over it, and it would suffice us from our day until nightfall. We would beat al-khaṭb (leaves) with our sticks, then moisten them with water and eat them."" Then we came upon the coast a creature like a large mound, called al-ʿAnbar. Abū ʿUbaydah said: ""It is carrion."" Then he said: ""No, rather we are messengers of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), and we are in the cause of Allah, and you have been compelled, so eat."" We ate from it for a month, we being three hundred, until we grew fat. We used to scoop oil from the socket of its eye in large vessels, and cut from it pieces the size of an ox. Abū ʿUbaydah took thirteen men and seated them in the socket of its eye. He took one of its ribs and erected it, then saddled the largest camel, and it passed beneath it. We provisioned ourselves from its meat and strips of dried flesh. When we arrived in Madīnah, we came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and mentioned that to him. He said: ""It is a provision that Allah has brought forth for you. Do you have any of its meat with you, that you may feed us?"" So we sent some of it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), and he ate from it.

The wording is that of the hadith of Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. In the narration of Aḥmad ibn Yūnus, he said: ""We set out along the coast, and there was raised up for us on the coast something like a large mound. We came to it, and it was the creature al-ʿAnbar."" He said: ""I saw us scooping oil from its eye in large vessels, and cutting from it pieces the size of an ox or the measure of an ox."" He said: ""He seated them in its eye."" And at the end of it he said: ""We sent some of it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), and he ate.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and Aḥmad ibn Yūnus.

Abū Saʿīd informed us in the book Taḥrīm al-Jamʿ: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: al-Thaqafī — and he is ʿAbd al-Wahhāb — narrated from Ayyūb, from Ibn Sīrīn, who said: I asked ʿUbaydah about the slaughtered animals of the Christians of Banī Taghlib. He said: ""I do not eat their slaughtered animals, for they have held on to nothing of their Christianity except the drinking of wine."" Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""This is how I preserve it, and I do not think it — or anyone else — reached me through this chain except that it has reached ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him)."" The Sheikh said: And from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib there is no doubt about it, for al-Shāfiʿī narrated it in the book al-Ḍaḥāyā from al-Thaqafī without doubt. [Sound]"
18969,"(18963) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ghālib al-Khwārazmī al-Ḥāfiẓ in Baghdād informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥamdān al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī — he is Ibn Ziyād al-Surrī — narrated to us: Ibn Abī Uways narrated to me: Mālik narrated from Wahb ibn Kaysān, from Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent an expedition toward the coast and appointed Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ over them, and they were three hundred. Jābir said: ""I was among them."" We went out until, when we were partway along the road, the provisions ran out. Abū ʿUbaydah commanded that the provisions of that army be gathered, and they were collected, amounting to two bags of dates. He said: ""He used to provide for us each day — meaning a little at a time — until it was exhausted, and each person would receive nothing each day except a date."" I said: ""What would a date avail?"" He said: ""We felt its loss when it was exhausted."" Then we reached the sea, and there was a whale like a large hill. The army ate from it for eighteen nights. Then Abū ʿUbaydah commanded that two of its ribs be erected, and they were set up. Then he commanded a camel to be saddled, and it was saddled, and it passed beneath them without reaching them.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Mālik. [Sound — agreed upon]"
18970,"(18964) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū Jaʿfar Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Ḥārithī narrated to us: Abū Usāmah narrated from al-Walīd — meaning Ibn Kathīr — who said: I heard Wahb ibn Kaysān say: I heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent a detachment — I was among them — toward the coast of the sea. Our provisions ran out, so we gathered what each person had and made it into one portion, so that each person would receive what fell to his share, until each person received nothing each day except a date. A man said to Jābir: ""O Abā ʿAbdillāh, what would avail a man — a date?"" He said: ""O son of my brother, we felt its loss when it was exhausted."" Jābir said: ""While we were in that state, we saw a dark mass. When it came upon us, it was a creature from the sea that had emerged from the sea. The army encamped around it for eighteen nights, eating from it as they wished, until they grew fat.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Kurayb from Abū Usāmah. [Sound — agreed upon]"
18971,"(18965) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm narrated — they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us — Ibn Wahb informed me — Mālik ibn Anas informed me from Ṣafwān ibn Sulaym from Saʿīd ibn Salamah, the mawlā of al-Azraq, that al-Mughīrah ibn Abī Burdah — who was from Banū ʿAbd al-Dār — informed him that he heard Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: A man asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, we travel by sea and carry a small amount of water with us. If we perform ablution with it, we become thirsty. Shall we perform ablution with the water of the sea?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Its water is pure (ṭahūr), and its dead are lawful (ḥill)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18972,"(18966) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī narrated to us by dictation. Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us. Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us. Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us. Abū al-Qāsim ibn Abī al-Zinād narrated to us. Isḥāq ibn Ḥāzim narrated to me from Ibn Miqsam, meaning ʿUbaydullāh, from Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was asked about the sea, and he said: ""Its water is pure (ṭahūr), and its dead are lawful (ḥill)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18973,"(18967) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us — they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn Munqidh narrated to me — Idrīs ibn Yaḥyā narrated to me — al-Faḍl ibn al-Mukhtār narrated to me from ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mawhab from ʿIṣmah ibn Mālik al-Khaṭmī from Ḥudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Indeed, Allah has made the game of the sea lawful (dhakkā) for you.""

This chain of narration is not strong, and it has also been narrated from Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him). [Ḍaʿīf]"
18974,"(18968) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us — ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad al-ʿUmarī narrated to us — ʿAbbād ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Sharīk narrated from Ibn Abī Bashīr from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: I heard Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: ""Indeed, Allah has slaughtered (dhabaḥa) for you whatever is in the sea, so eat all of it, for it is slaughtered (dhakiyy).""

And Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār who said: I heard a man known as Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān say: I heard Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""There is nothing in the sea except that Allah has made it slaughtered (dhakkāhu) for you."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18975,"(18969) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us — Ibn Numayr narrated from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār from Abī al-Ṭufayl that Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was asked about the dead of the sea, and he said: ""Its water is pure (ṭahūr), and its dead are lawful (ḥalāl).""

And it has been narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār and Abī al-Zubayr that they heard Sharayḥ, a man who met the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), say: ""Everything in the sea is slaughtered (madhbūḥ).""

And that has been narrated from Abī al-Zubayr from Sharayḥ in a marfūʿ narration, and from Jābir and ʿAbdillāh ibn Sarjis in a marfūʿ narration. And in some of what we have mentioned, the chain of narration is sufficient. And success is through Allah alone. [Ḥasan]"
18976,"(18970) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us — they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us — Rūḥ ibn Aslam narrated to us — Zāʾidah narrated from Simāk ibn Ḥarb from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: ""Whatever the sea casts out and whatever is caught from it — whether the one who catches it is a Jew, a Christian, or a Magian — and its food is what the sea casts out."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18977,"(18971) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us — Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq ibn Shaybān al-Baghdādī al-Harawī informed us — Muʿādh ibn Najdah narrated to us — Bishr ibn Ādam narrated to us — Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated from Simāk ibn Ḥarb from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: ""Eat all fish, and it will not harm you whoever among the people catches it."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
18978,"(18972) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us — they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us — Ibn Wahb informed me — ʿUmar ibn Muḥammad informed me that Nāfiʿ narrated to him that ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: ""We went out on a military expedition and became so hungry that the army was dividing a single date and two dates among themselves. While we were on the shore of the sea, the sea cast out a dead fish, and the people cut from it as much meat or fat as they wished. It was like a mountain (ẓarib). It reached me that when the people came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and informed him, he asked them: 'Do you have any of it with you?'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18979,"(18973) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Shaybān al-Ramlī narrated to us — Sufyān narrated — ʿAmr heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: ""The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sent us as three hundred riders, and our commander was Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ, seeking the caravan of Quraysh. We encamped on the shore until our provisions were exhausted, and we ate the leaves of trees (khabaṭ). Then the sea cast out for us a creature called al-ʿAnbar (a whale), and we ate from it for half a month until our bodies were restored. Abū ʿUbaydah took one of its ribs, set it up, and looked at the tallest camel in the army and the tallest man. He had the man mount the camel, and it passed beneath the rib. A man had slaughtered three camels, then three more, and then Abū ʿUbaydah forbade him. Qays ibn Saʿd used to consider it to be him. Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it as we have mentioned."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
18980,"(18974) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī al-Faqīh informed us — ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me — Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to me — ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to me — Yūsuf ibn Saʿīd narrated to us — Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us — they both said: Sufyān narrated from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Bashīr from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) who said: ""I bear witness that Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: 'The fish that floats on the surface of the water (al-samakah al-ṭāfiyah) is lawful (ḥalāl) for whoever wishes to eat it.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18981,"(18975) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith informed us; Ali ibn Umar informed us; Muhammad ibn Nuh narrated to us; Harun ibn Ishaq narrated to us; Waki' narrated from Sufyan, with this wording: ""The fish that floats on the surface of the water is lawful (halal)."" [Sahih]"
18982,"(18976) Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn Abi al-Ma'ruf al-Faqih informed us; Abu Sa'id 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab al-Razi in Nishapur narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ayyub informed us; Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; Hisham narrated to us; Qatadah narrated from Jabir ibn Zayd that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""Locusts and fish are all slaughtered (i.e., their dead bodies are lawful without slaughter)."" [Da'if]"
18983,"(18977) Abu Bakr al-Ardastani informed us; Abu Nasr al-'Iraqi narrated to us; Sufyan ibn Muhammad al-Jawhari narrated to us; 'Ali ibn al-Hasan narrated to us; 'Abdullah ibn al-Walid narrated to us; Sufyan narrated from Ja'far, from his father, from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""Fish and locusts are all slaughtered (i.e., their dead bodies are lawful without slaughter)."" [Da'if]"
18984,"(18978) Abu Hamid Ahmad ibn 'Ali al-Hafiz al-Isfarayini informed us; Abu 'Ali Zahir ibn Ahmad narrated to us; Abu Bakr ibn Ziyad al-Naysaburi narrated to us; Yazid ibn Sinan narrated to us; 'Abd al-Samad narrated to us; 'Abdullah ibn al-Muthanna narrated from Thumamah, from Anas, from Abu Ayyub, may Allah be pleased with him, that he embarked on a sea voyage with a group of his companions and they found a dead fish floating on the water. They asked him about it, so he said: ""Is it wholesome and has it not changed (in smell)?"" They said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Then eat it and set aside my portion from it,"" and he was fasting.

Thus did Zahir narrate it. However, al-Daraqutni narrated it from Abu Bakr, saying: from Thumamah ibn Anas, from Abu Ayyub. But the correct is Thumamah ibn 'Abdullah ibn Anas. It is possible that the narration of Zahir is more accurate, and Allah knows best.

It is also narrated by Jabalah ibn 'Atiyyah from Abu Ayyub, and it is mentioned from Murayh and Bishr, the two sons of al-Khawlani — one of them or both — that Abu Ayyub and Abu Sirmah al-Ansari ate the floating fish. [Da'if]"
18985,"(18979) Abu Sa'd al-Malini informed us; Abu Ahmad ibn 'Adi al-Hafiz informed us; Zakariyya al-Saji narrated to us; Bundar narrated to us; Muhammad, meaning Ibn Ja'far, narrated to us; Shu'bah narrated from Ajlah, from 'Abdullah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: ""There is no harm in eating the floating fish."" [Da'if]"
18986,"(18980) Abu Ahmad 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Mihrajanī informed us; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Muzakkī narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Bushanjī narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Malik narrated from Abu al-Zinad, from Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah and Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with them both, that they both did not see any harm in eating what the sea casts out. [Sahih]"
18987,"(18981) Abu Bakr al-Ardastani informed us; Abu Nasr al-'Iraqi narrated to us; Sufyan ibn Muhammad al-Jawhari narrated to us; 'Ali ibn al-Hasan narrated to us; 'Abdullah ibn al-Walid narrated to us; Sufyan narrated from Abu al-Zinad, from Abu Salamah, from Thuwayb, who said: ""The sea cast out a large number of dead fish, so we came to Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and sought his legal opinion. He commanded us to eat it. Then we felt disinclined towards the verdict of Abu Hurayrah, so we came to Marwan, who sent to Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, and asked him. He said: 'It is lawful (halal), so eat it.'"" [Da'if]"
18988,"(18982) Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""I arrived in al-Bahrayn, and the people of al-Bahrayn asked me about what the sea casts out of fish. I commanded them to eat it. When I arrived, I asked 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, about that. He said: 'What did you command them?' I said: 'I commanded them to eat it.' He said: 'Had you said anything other than that, I would have struck you with the whip.' Then 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, recited: {Lawful to you is the game of the sea and its food as provision for you} [al-Ma'idah 96]. He said: 'Its game is what is hunted, and its food is what it casts out.'"" [Da'if]"
18989,"(18983) Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""I came from al-Bahrayn, and when I was in al-Rabadhah, some people from Iraq who were in the state of ihram asked me about game found floating on the water. They asked me about purchasing and eating it. I permitted them to purchase and eat it while they were in the state of ihram. Then I came to Madinah, and it was as though doubt entered my heart regarding what I had commanded them. So I mentioned that to 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: 'What did you command them?' I said: 'I commanded them to purchase and eat it.' He said: 'Had you commanded them otherwise, I would have done (such and such to you)' — as though he was threatening him."" [Sahih]"
18990,"(18984) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran al-'Adl in Baghdad informed us; Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us; Yahya ibn Abi Talib narrated to us; 'Ali ibn 'Asim narrated to us; Sulayman al-Taymi narrated from Abu Mijlaz, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding His statement: {Lawful to you is the game of the sea and its food as provision for you} [al-Ma'idah 96]. He said: ""Its game is what is hunted, and its food is what it casts out."" [Sahih]"
18991,"(18985) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū Manṣūr al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl al-Naḍrūwī informed us; Aḥmad ibn Najdah informed us; Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr informed us; Khalaf ibn Khalīfah informed us; Ḥuṣayn narrated from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: ""Its game is what is caught from it, and its food is what the sea casts out."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18992,"(18986) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Ibn Bukayr informed us; Mālik narrated from Nāʿif that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Hurayrah asked ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) about what the sea casts out, and he forbade him from eating it. Nāʿif said: Then ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar turned back, brought the Muṣḥaf, and recited: ""Lawful to you is the game of the sea and its food"" [Al-Māʾidah 96]. Nāʿif said: Then ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar sent me to ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Hurayrah to tell him that there is no harm in it, so eat it. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18993,"(18987) Abū Aḥmad informed us; Abū Bakr informed us; Muḥammad informed us; Ibn Bukayr informed us; Mālik narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Saʿd al-Jārī, the freedman of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, that he said: ""I asked ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) about fish that kill one another or die of cold, and he said: 'There is no harm in that.'"" Saʿd said: ""Then I asked ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, and he said the same."""
18994,"(18988) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Nayrūz informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ḥasanī informed us; Ibn Numayr narrated; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir (may Allah be pleased with him), that he used to say: ""Whatever the sea strikes, or casts out, or is hunted within it—eat it. And whatever dies in it and then floats—do not eat it."" (T) And with the same meaning, Ayyūb al-Sakhtiyānī, Ibn Jurayj, Zuhayr ibn Muʿāwiyah, Ḥammād ibn Salamah, and others narrated it from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir, as a mawqūf (stopped) narration.

And likewise, the group—Wakīʿ, ʿAbd al-Razzāq, ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Walīd al-ʿAdanī, Abū ʾĀṣim, Muʾammal ibn Ismāʿīl, and others—narrated it from Sufyān al-Thawrī, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir, as a mawqūf narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18995,"(18989) Abū Aḥmad al-Zubayrī contradicted them and narrated it from al-Thawrī as a marfūʿ (raised) narration, and he was mistaken in that. Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Lakhmī informed us; ʿAlī ibn Isḥāq al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Naṣr ibn ʿAlī informed us; Abū Aḥmad al-Zubayrī informed us; Sufyān narrated, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), who said: ""When the fish floats on the water, do not eat it. And when the sea casts it out, eat it. And whatever is on its shores, eat it.""

Sulaymān said: ""No one narrated this ḥadīth as marfūʿ from Sufyān except Abū Aḥmad."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
18996,"(18990) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdah informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Sulaym al-Ṭāʾifī informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Umayyah narrated, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: ""Whatever the sea casts out or the tide recedes from—eat it. And whatever dies in it and then floats—do not eat it.""

Abū Dāwūd said: Sufyān al-Thawrī, Ayyūb, and Ḥammād narrated this ḥadīth from Abū al-Zubayr and stopped it at Jābir. He said: This ḥadīth has also been narrated with a weak chain from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family).

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: Yaḥyā ibn Sulaym al-Ṭāʾifī is prone to many errors and has poor memory.

Others narrated it from Ismāʿīl ibn Umayyah as a mawqūf narration. Abū ʿĪsā al-Tirmidhī narrated the ḥadīth of Ibn Abī Dhiʾb from al-Ḥusayn ibn Yazīd al-Kūfī, from Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth, from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), who said: ""Whatever you catch while it is alive—eat it. And whatever you find dead and floating—do not eat it."" [Munkar]

Abū ʿĪsā said: ""I asked Muḥammad, meaning al-Bukhārī, about this ḥadīth, and he said: 'This is not preserved. The opposite of this is narrated from Jābir, and I do not know of anything from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb from Abū al-Zubayr.'""

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: It was also narrated by Yaḥyā ibn Abī Unaysah, from Abū al-Zubayr, as a marfūʿ narration. And Yaḥyā ibn Abī Unaysah is abandoned (matrūk) and cannot be used as evidence. ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUbayd Allāh narrated it from Wahb ibn Kaysān, from Jābir, as a marfūʿ narration.

(c) And ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz is weak and cannot be used as evidence.

Baqiyyah ibn al-Walīd narrated it from al-Awzāʿī, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir, as a marfūʿ narration. And the unique narrations of Baqiyyah cannot be used as evidence—so how much less when he is contradicted in it. The statement of the group of the Companions contradicts the statement of Jābir, along with what we have narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) that he said about the sea: ""It is pure, its water is purifying, and its dead are lawful."" And with Allah is success."
18997,"(18991) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Abū Muslim informed us; Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Bisṭāmī informed us; Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; al-Faḍl ibn al-Ḥubāb informed us; Abū al-Walīd and al-Ḥawḍī informed us; they said: Shuʿbah narrated, from Abū Yaʿfūr, who heard Ibn Abī Awfā (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""I went on seven military expeditions with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), and we used to eat locusts with him.""

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of al-Bisṭāmī.

In the narration of Ibn ʿAbdān, he said: ""I heard Ibn Abī Awfā (may Allah be pleased with him) when he was asked about locusts, and he said: 'I went on six or seven military expeditions with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), and we used to eat them.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Walīd, and he said: ""Seven or six military expeditions."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon]"
18998,"(18992) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī informed us; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-Naḍr ibn Salamah al-Jārūdī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bashshār ibn ʿUthmān informed us; Muḥammad, who is Ibn Jaʿfar, informed us; Shuʿbah narrated, from Abū Yaʿfūr, who said: ""I asked my companion ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Awfā (may Allah be pleased with him), while I was with him, about locusts. He said: 'There is no harm in it. I went on seven military expeditions with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), and we used to eat them.'""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Bashshār. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon]"
18999,"(18993) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan informed us; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated, from Abū Yaʿfūr, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Awfā, who said: ""We went on seven or six military expeditions with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), and we used to eat locusts.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar and others, from Sufyān."
19000,"(18994) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh informed us orally; Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn Abī Mūsā informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-Faraj, the freedman of Banī Hāshim, informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-Faraj al-Baghdādī informed us; Ibn al-Zibriqān narrated; Sulaymān al-Taymī narrated, from Abū ʿUthmān al-Nahdī, from Salmān (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) was asked about locusts, and he said: 'They are the most numerous of the soldiers of Allah. I do not eat them, nor do I declare them unlawful.'"" Abū Dāwūd said: al-Muʿtamir narrated it from his father, from Abū ʿUthmān, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), without mentioning Salmān.

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: And likewise, al-Anṣārī narrated it from Sulaymān. [Munkar]"
19001,"(18995) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bashran informed us; Isma'il al-Saffar informed us; Abu Muslim Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah narrated to us; al-Ansari Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah narrated to me; Sulayman al-Taymi narrated from Abu 'Uthman al-Nahdi, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: ""The most numerous of Allah's soldiers upon the earth are the locusts. I neither eat them nor declare them forbidden."" [Da'if]"
19002,"(18996) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abu Dawud informed us; Nasr ibn 'Ali and 'Ali ibn 'Abd Allah both said: Zakariyya ibn Yahya ibn 'Umara narrated from Abu al-'Awwam al-Jazzar from Abu 'Uthman al-Nahdi from Salman (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) was asked, and he said the same as the above, and he said: ""The most numerous of Allah's soldiers."" 'Ali said: His name is Fa'id, meaning Abu al-'Awwam. Abu Dawud said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated it from Abu al-'Awwam from Abu 'Uthman from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), without mentioning Salman.

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: If this is authentic, then it also indicates permissibility, for if he did not declare it forbidden, he thereby made it lawful. He simply did not eat it out of personal aversion. And Allah knows best. [Munkar]"
19003,"(18997) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Zayd ibn Aslam narrated from his father from Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: ""Two dead creatures and two types of blood have been made lawful for us. The two dead creatures are the fish and the locust, and the two types of blood—"" I think he said: ""the liver and the spleen."" Isma'il ibn Abi Uways narrated it from 'Abd al-Rahman, 'Abd Allah, and Usamah, the sons of Zayd ibn Aslam, from their father, thus as a marfu' narration. And Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated it from Zayd ibn Aslam from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, who said: ""Two dead creatures have been made lawful for us."" And this is the correct version. [Munkar]"
19004,"(18998) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq, Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan, and Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami narrated, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us. He said: I heard Haywa ibn Shurayh say: I heard Sinan ibn 'Abd Allah al-Ansari say: I asked Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) about the locust, and he said: ""We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) to Khaybar, and with us was 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), who had a basket containing locusts that he had strapped behind him. He would reach his hand behind him, take from it, and give it to us, and we would eat, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) looked on."" Anas said: ""Then we returned to Madinah, and we would be given locusts, so we would purchase them, acquire them in abundance, and dry them on the rooftops, and we would eat from them for a long time."" [Sahih]"
19005,"(18999) Abu Zakariyya and Abu Bakr narrated, saying: Abu al-'Abbas narrated to us; Muhammad informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Malik informed me from 'Abd Allah ibn Dinar from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, who said: ""'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) was asked about the locust, and he said: 'I wish we had a basket from which we could eat.'"" [Sahih]"
19006,"(19000) Abu Zakariyya and Abu Bakr narrated, saying: Abu al-'Abbas narrated to us; Muhammad informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me that al-Lajlaj narrated to him that Wahib ibn 'Abd Allah al-Ma'afiri narrated to him: He and 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar entered upon the foster-mother of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), and she brought near to them locusts fried in ghee. She said: ""O Egyptian, eat from this, perhaps the small fish are more beloved to you than this."" He said: I said: ""We do love the small fish."" She said: ""O Egyptian, eat! A Prophet among the Prophets asked Allah for the flesh of a bird that was not slaughtered, so Allah granted him the fish and the locusts.""

Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi narrated, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Abu 'Utba Ahmad ibn al-Faraj narrated to us; Baqiya narrated to us; Numayr ibn Yazid al-Qayni narrated from his father, who said: I heard Suddi ibn 'Ajlan, Abu Umamah al-Bahili (may Allah be pleased with him), say: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: ""Indeed, Maryam bint 'Imran asked her Lord to feed her flesh that had no blood, so He fed her the locusts. She said: 'O Allah, sustain her without nursing, and cause her to follow without a voice.'"" I said: ""O Abu al-Fadl, what is al-shi'a'?"" He said: ""A voice."" [Da'if]"
19007,"(19001) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz, Abu Sadiq al-'Attar, Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami from his original copy, and Abu Hamid Ahmad ibn Muhammad Amirak al-Naysaburi, and Abu Ishaq Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Ibrahim ibn Mu'awiya al-'Attar narrated, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; al-Hasan ibn Mukram narrated to us; Yazid ibn Harun informed us; Abu Sa'd al-Baqqal narrated from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""The wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) used to eat locusts and present them to one another as gifts."" Yazid said: I said to Sa'id: ""Did you hear it from Anas?"" He said: ""Yes."" [Da'if]"
19008,"(19002) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bashran al-'Adl in Baghdad informed us; Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Amr al-Razzaz narrated to us; 'Ali ibn Ibrahim al-Wasiti informed us; Yazid ibn Harun informed us; Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib that 'Umar, Ibn 'Umar, al-Miqdad ibn Suwayd, and Suhayb (may Allah be pleased with them all) ate locusts. 'Umar said: ""If only we had a basket or two of them.""

Abu 'Ubayd said: ""Al-qafa' is something resembling a basket, not large, made from palm leaves, and it has no handles.""

And we have narrated from Ja'far ibn Muhammad from his father from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: ""The fish and the locust are all properly slaughtered (i.e., their consumption is lawful without the requirement of ritual slaughter)."" [Sahih]"
19009,"(19003) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id al-Sayrafi narrated, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us; Ibn Wahb narrated from Sulayman ibn Bilal from 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Abi 'Awn from Ya'qub ibn 'Utba ibn al-Akhnas from Sa'd ibn Ishaq from Zaynab bint Ka'b ibn 'Ujra that Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him) used to see his sons and his family eating locusts, and he would neither forbid them nor eat them himself. Zaynab said: ""I think he found them distasteful."" [Da'if]"
19010,"(19004) Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak informed us; 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far informed us; Yunus ibn Habib informed us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated from Sa'id ibn Khalid ibn Qariz from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Uthman, who said: ""A physician asked the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) about using a frog in a remedy, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) forbade him from killing it."" [Da'if]"
19011,"(19005) Abu Zakariyya — Yahya ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya — informed us: Abu al-Hasan — Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Tara'ifi — informed us: 'Uthman ibn Sa'id al-Darimi — narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Salih — from Mu'awiyah ibn Salih — from 'Ali ibn Abi Talhah — from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both — regarding His statement {"
19012,"(19006) Abu 'Ali — al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Rudhbari — and Abu al-Hasan — 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan — both informed us: Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Mahmuyah al-'Askari — narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Qalanisi — narrated to us: Adam ibn Abi Iyas — narrated to us: Shu'bah — narrated to us: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Suhayb — he said: I heard Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, would sacrifice two rams. Anas said: And I would sacrifice two rams.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Adam. [Sahih (Authentic)]"
19013,"(19007) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz — informed me: Abu al-Nadr — Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Faqih — narrated to us: 'Uthman ibn Sa'id al-Darimi — narrated to us: Abu 'Umar al-Hawdi —

(Transition) He said: And Muhammad ibn Ayyub — narrated to us: Abu 'Umar al-Hawdi — narrated to us: Hisham — from Qatadah — from Anas: that the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, sacrificed two horned, greyish-white rams. He would invoke the name of Allah (yusammi) and pronounce the takbir, place his foot on their sides, and slaughter them with his own hand.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Abu 'Umar al-Hawdi in an abridged form. [Sahih. Muttafaqun 'alayh (Agreed upon)]"
19014,"(19008) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari and 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan — both informed us: Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Mahmuyah — narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Muhammad — narrated to us: Adam — narrated to us: Shu'bah — narrated to us: Qatadah — from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him — he said: The Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, sacrificed two greyish-white, horned rams, placing his foot on their sides, invoking the name of Allah and pronouncing the takbir, and then he slaughtered them — meaning with his own hand. [Sahih. Muttafaqun 'alayh (Agreed upon)]

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Adam, and Muslim extracted it from two other routes from Shu'bah."
19015,"(19009) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz — narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Yunus al-Dabbi — narrated to us: Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi — narrated to us: Zuhayr ibn Muhammad al-'Anbari — from 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn 'Aqil ibn Abi Talib — from 'Ali ibn al-Husayn — regarding the verse {لِكُلِّ أُمَّةٍ جَعَلْنَا مَنْسَكًا هُمْ نَاسِكُوهُ} [Al-Hajj 67]: He said: ""They are the ones who perform the slaughter."" Abu Rafi' informed me: that the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, when he would sacrifice, he would purchase two fat, greyish-white, horned rams. When he had delivered the sermon and prayed, he would slaughter one of the two rams himself with a knife, and then say: ""O Allah, this is on behalf of my entire Ummah — those who bear witness to Your Oneness and bear witness to my conveyance of the Message."" Then he would bring the other and slaughter it, and say: ""O Allah, this is on behalf of Muhammad and the family of Muhammad."" Then he would feed both to the poor, and he and his family would eat from them. We remained for some years — may Allah suffice us against the burden and the hardship — no one from the Banu Hashim would offer a sacrifice.

And with the same meaning, it was narrated by 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Amr al-Raqi and Qays ibn al-Rabi' from 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn 'Aqil, from 'Ali ibn al-Husayn, from Abu Rafi', from the Prophet, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family. [Da'if (Weak)]"
19016,"(19010) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz — narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us: al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Dawri — narrated to us: 'Uthman ibn 'Umar ibn Faris — informed us: Ibn 'Awn — informed us: Abu Ramlah — informed us: Mikhnaf ibn Sulaym — he said: While we were standing with the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, at 'Arafah, he said: ""Upon every household, every year, there is an udhiyah and an 'atirah."" Do you know what the 'atirah is? He said: ""I do not know what they replied."" He said: ""It is that which the people call the Rajabiyyah."" [Da'if (Weak)]"
19017,"(19011) Abu al-Hasan — 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' — informed us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq — narrated to us: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi — narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Harb — narrated to us: Hammad ibn Zayd — from Hisham — from Hafsah — from a woman of the family of al-Ash'ath — from an elderly woman of theirs — she said: Our delegation, the delegation of Ghamid, informed us when they came from the Prophet, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, that he said: ""Upon every household of the Muslims there is an udhiyah and an 'atirah."" [Da'if (Weak)]"
19018,"(19012) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz — informed us: al-Hasan ibn Ya'qub al-'Adl — narrated to us: Yahya ibn Abi Talib — narrated to us: Zayd ibn al-Hubab — from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Ayyash al-Misri — from 'Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj — from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him — he said: The Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, said: ""Whoever finds the means to offer a sacrifice but does not do so, let him not approach our place of prayer (musalla).""

(T) And similarly, it was narrated by Haywah ibn Shurayh and Yahya ibn Sa'id al-'Attar from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Ayyash al-Qitbani. It has reached me that Abu 'Isa al-Tirmidhi said: The authentic narration from Abu Hurayrah is mawquf (stopping at the Companion). He said: And Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah and others narrated it from 'Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj from Abu Hurayrah as mawquf. And the hadith of Zayd ibn Hubab is not preserved (ghayr mahfuz).

The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: And similarly, 'Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Ja'far narrated it from al-A'raj from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, as mawquf; and Ibn Wahb from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Ayyash from al-A'raj from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, as mawquf. [Munkar (Denounced)]"
19019,"(19013) And Ibn Wahb also narrated from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Ayyash — from 'Isa ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Farwah al-Ansari — from Ibn Shihab — from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib — from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him — that he said: ""Whoever finds the means but does not sacrifice, let him not approach us in our mosque"" — mawquf. Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith — informed us: 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz — narrated to us: Abu Bakr al-Naysaburi — narrated to us: Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Wahb — narrated to me: My uncle — narrated to me: 'Abd Allah ibn 'Ayyash — and he mentioned it. [Da'if (Weak)]"
19020,"(19014) Abu Muhammad — 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf al-Isbahani — informed us: Abu 'Abd Allah — Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Jurjani — informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad — I think he was al-Baghawi — narrated to us: Dawud ibn Rashid — narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Rabi'ah — narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Yazid — from 'Amr ibn Dinar — from Tawus — from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both — he said: The Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, said: ""Silver is not spent on anything superior to a sacrifice (nahirah) on the Day of 'Eid.""

Muhammad ibn Rabi'ah is the sole narrator of this from Ibrahim al-Khuzi, and they are not both strong (laysa bi-al-qawiyyayn). [Da'if (Weak)]"
19021,"(19015) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: al-Musayyibī, i.e. Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Madanī, narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Nāfiʿ narrated to me from Abī al-Muthannā Sulaymān ibn Yazīd, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""No deed performed by a human being on the Day of Sacrifice is more beloved to Allah than the shedding of blood (i.e., offering a sacrifice). Indeed, it shall come on the Day of Resurrection with its dung, its horns, its hair, and its hooves. And indeed, the blood (of the sacrifice) reaches Allah before it falls to the ground. So be pleased with it.""

Al-Bukhārī stated, as Abū ʿĪsā reported from him: It is a mursal hadith; Abī al-Muthannā did not hear from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah.

Al-Shaykh Aḥmad said: Ibn Khuzaymah narrated it from Yūnus ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā, from Ibn Wahb, from Abī al-Muthannā, from Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿUqbah, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) — or from his paternal uncle Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah, with this doubt — that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""No deed performed by a human being on the Day of Sacrifice is more beloved to Allah than the shedding of blood."" Then he mentioned it. [Weak]"
19022,"(19016) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us: Hishām ibn ʿAlī al-Sīrāfī narrated to us: Hudbah ibn Khālid narrated to us: Salām ibn Miskīn narrated to us, from ʿĀʾidh Allāh, from Abī Dāwūd, from Zayd ibn Arqam (may Allah be pleased with him), that they said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family): ""What are these sacrifices?"" He said: ""It is the Sunnah of your father Ibrāhīm (peace be upon him)."" They said: ""What reward do we have in them?"" He said: ""For every drop (of blood), a good deed."" [Very weak]"
19023,"(19017) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Bazzāz in Baghdād informed us: Muḥammad ibn Maslamah al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us: Salām ibn Miskīn narrated to us, from ʿĀʾidh Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Mujāshiʿī, from Abī Dāwūd al-Sabīʿī, from Zayd ibn Arqam (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: We said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what are these sacrifices?"" He said: ""It is the Sunnah of your father Ibrāhīm (peace be upon him)."" He said: We said: ""Then what do we have in them?"" He said: ""For every hair, a good deed."" He said: We said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what about the wool?"" He said: ""For every hair of the wool, a good deed.""

(c) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī informed us: I heard Ibn Ḥammād say: Al-Bukhārī said: ʿĀʾidh Allāh al-Mujāshiʿī narrated from Abī Dāwūd; Salām ibn Miskīn narrated from him. His hadith is not authentic. Abū Aḥmad said: This hadith is known through ʿľʾidh Allāh, and no one other than Salām ibn Miskīn narrates it from him. Abī Dāwūd was not named; he is Nuʿaym ibn al-Ḥārith. [Very weak]"
19024,"(19018) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Maḥmūd al-Aṣbahānī informed us — he came to us — Abū Ḥafṣ ʿUmar ibn Aḥmad ibn Shāhīn in Baghdād informed us: Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān al-Bāghandī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Saʿīd, i.e. Ibn Masrūq al-Kindī, narrated to us: al-Musayyib ibn Sharīk narrated to us, from ʿUbayd al-Mukātib —"
19025,"(19019) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us, saying: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf ibn Sulaymān al-Khallāl narrated to us: al-Haytham ibn Sahl narrated to us: al-Musayyib ibn Sharīk narrated to us: ʿUbayd al-Mukātib narrated to us, from ʿĀmir, from Masrūq, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""The sacrifice (al-Uḍḥī) has abrogated every other slaughter, the fast of Ramaḍān has abrogated every other fast, the bath of janābah has abrogated every other bath, and the zakāh has abrogated every other charity.""

ʿAlī said: al-Musayyib ibn Wāḍiḥ contradicted him regarding al-Musayyib ibn Sharīk, and both of them are weak. Al-Musayyib ibn Sharīk is abandoned (matrūk). [Very weak]"
19026,"(19020) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us: al-Musayyib ibn Wāḍiḥ narrated to us: al-Musayyib ibn Sharīk narrated to us, from ʿUtbah ibn al-Yaqẓān, from al-Shaʿbī, from Masrūq, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""The zakāh has abrogated every charity mentioned in the Qurʾān, the bath of janābah has abrogated every other bath, the fast of Ramaḍān has abrogated every other fast, and the sacrifice (al-Uḍḥī) has abrogated every other slaughter."" [Very weak]"
19027,"(19021) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us, saying: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Ibn Mubashshir narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Sinān narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Muḥammad al-Zuhrī narrated to us: Rifāʿah ibn Hurayr narrated to us: My father narrated to me from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her). She said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, should I take on debt and offer a sacrifice?"" He said: ""Yes, for it is a debt that will be repaid."" ʿAlī said: This chain of narration is weak. And Hurayr is ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj, and he did not hear from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), nor did he meet her. [Weak]"
19028,"(19022) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥmawayh al-ʿAskarī narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī narrated to us: Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us: al-Aswad ibn Qays narrated to us. He said: I heard Jundub ibn Sufyān al-Bajalī (may Allah be pleased with him) say: I was present with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) on the Day of Sacrifice, and he said: ""Whoever slaughtered before the prayer, let him repeat it in its place, and whoever has not yet slaughtered, let him slaughter.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam, and Muslim narrated it from two other routes from Shuʿbah. [Authentic. Agreed upon]"
19029,"(19023) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Naḍr al-Ḥarashī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Hushaym informed us, from Dāwūd, from al-Shaʿbī, from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib (may Allah be pleased with him): His maternal uncle, Abū Burdah ibn Niyār, slaughtered before the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) slaughtered. He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, this is a day in which meat is disliked (to be withheld), and I hastened my sacrifice so that I may feed my family, my neighbours, and the people of my household."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Repeat your sacrifice."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have a young she-goat that is better than two sheep (in terms of meat)."" He said: ""It is the best of your two sacrifices. And no jadhʿah (a young animal) will suffice for anyone after you.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī used it as a supporting narration. [Authentic. Agreed upon]"
19030,"(19024) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Sakhtawayh informed us: Abī al-Muthannā informed me that Musaddad narrated to them, saying: Ismāʿīl informed us: Ayyūb informed us, from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said on the Day of Sacrifice: ""Whoever slaughtered before the prayer, let him repeat it."" A man stood up and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, this is a day in which meat is desired,"" and he mentioned something about his neighbours, as though the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) believed him. He said: ""And I have a jadhʿah that is more beloved to me than two sheep."" He said: ""He granted him permission."" He (the narrator) said: ""I do not know whether the permission reached anyone other than him or not."" [Authentic. Agreed upon]"
19031,"(19025) Abū ʿAbdullāh informed me; Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar informed us; Abū Yaʿlā informed us; Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb informed us; Ibn ʿUlayyah narrated it with its chain of narration, and added: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) turned to two rams and slaughtered them. Then the people went to a small flock of sheep and distributed them among themselves — or he said, they divided them up.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ṣadaqah ibn al-Faḍl from Ismāʿīl ibn ʢUlayyah in its full length, and from Musaddad in an abridged form. And Muslim narrated it from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon]"
19032,"(19026) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrānī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Ibn Bukayr informed us; Mālik narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd from ʿAbbād ibn Tamīm: that ʿUwaymir ibn Ashqar slaughtered his sacrificial animal before going out (to the prayer ground) on the Day of Sacrifice, and he mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), so he commanded him to offer another sacrifice.

And with his chain he said: Mālik narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd from Bushayr ibn Yasār: that Abū Burdah ibn Niyār (may Allah be pleased with him) slaughtered his sacrificial animal before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) slaughtered on the Day of Sacrifice, and he claimed that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) commanded him to offer another sacrifice. Abū Burdah said: ""I can find only a jadʿah (a young sheep)."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Even if you find only a jadʿah, then slaughter it.""

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) mentioned these two hadiths from Mālik (may Allah have mercy on him). [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19033,"(19027) Then he said: Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; he said: al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: It is possible that he only commanded him to offer another sacrifice because the sacrifice is obligatory, and it is also possible that he commanded him to do so only if he wished to offer a sacrifice, because a sacrifice offered before its proper time does not count as a valid sacrifice, and thus he would be among those who offered sacrifice. We have found evidence from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) indicating that the sacrifice is not obligatory and that abandoning it is not permissible; rather, it is a Sunnah, and we love its observance and dislike its abandonment — but not on the basis of its being obligatory. If it is asked: ""Where is the Sunnah that indicates it is not obligatory?"" It is said: Sufyān informed us from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥumayd from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib from Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her); she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""When the ten days begin and one of you wishes to offer a sacrifice, let him not touch any of his hair or his skin.""

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: In this hadith there is evidence that the sacrifice is not obligatory, due to the words of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family): ""and one of you wishes to offer a sacrifice."" Had the sacrifice been obligatory, it would have been more appropriate for him to say: ""Let him not touch any of his hair until he offers his sacrifice.""

The Sheikh said: And in the established hadith from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) addressed us on the Day of Sacrifice and said: ""The first thing we begin with on this day of ours is that we pray, then we return and offer sacrifice. Whoever does so has followed our Sunnah.""

And this is mentioned in the chapter on the minimum requirement for the sacrificial animal. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19034,"(19028) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī informed us; Yūnus ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us; ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith, ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAyyāsh, and Saʿīd ibn Abī Ayyūb informed us that ʿAyyāsh ibn ʿAbbās narrated to them from ʿĪsā ibn Hilāl al-Ṣadafī, who narrated to them from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ (may Allah be pleased with both of them): that a man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said to him: ""I have been commanded that the Day of Sacrifice is a festival which Allah has appointed for this Ummah."" The man said: ""If I can find nothing but the milch-sheep of my son — or the sheep of my son and my family and their milch-sheep — should I slaughter it?"" He said: ""No. Rather, trim your nails, cut your moustache, and shave your pubic hair. That is the completeness of your udḥiyyah (sacrifice) before Allah, the Mighty and Majestic."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
19035,"(19029) And Abū ʿAbdullāh informed me by way of ijāzah (permission); Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us, and he narrated it with its chain of narration, the same."
19036,"(19030) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī and Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us; they said: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; Saʿdān ibn Naṣr informed us; Abū Badr informed us; Abū Janāb al-Kalbī narrated from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with both of them): that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Three things are obligatory upon me and are voluntary (supererogatory) for you: the sacrifice (naḥr), the Witr prayer, and the two rakʿahs of the morning (ḍuḥā) prayer."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
19037,"(19031) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Tamtām informed us; Ibn bint al-Suddī informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān informed us; Abū Yaʿlā informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Mūsā — and he is Ibn bint al-Suddī — informed us; Sharīk narrated from Simāk from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with both of them), raising it (to the Prophet), he said: ""The sacrifice (naḥr) has been made obligatory upon me but has not been made obligatory upon you."" Al-Aṣbahānī added in his narration: ""And I have been commanded with the ḍuḥā prayer, but you have not been commanded with it."" Both of them narrated this from Simāk. [Ḍaʿīf]"
19038,"(19032) And Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Ibn Nājiyah informed us; Ismāʿīl al-Suddī informed us; Sharīk narrated from Jābir from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with both of them), raising it (to the Prophet), he said: ""The sacrifice (naḥr) has been made obligatory upon me but has not been made obligatory upon you, and I have been commanded with the ḍuḥā prayer, but you have not been commanded.""

And al-Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ and Qays ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated it from Jābir — that is, Ibn Yazīd al-Juʿfī — from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with both of them) from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf]"
19039,"(19033) And some of our companions cited as evidence what Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us; Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Sālim and Yaʿqūb ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed me from ʿAmr, the freed slave of al-Muṭṭalib, from al-Muṭṭalib ibn ʿAbdullāh, and from a man from Banī Salamah, that both of them narrated to him that Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh (may Allah be pleased with him) informed them: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) led the people in prayer on the Day of Sacrifice, and when he finished his sermon and his prayer, he called for a ram and slaughtered it himself, and said: ""In the name of Allah, and Allah is the Greatest. O Allah, (accept this) on my behalf and on behalf of those who did not offer a sacrifice from my Ummah.""

And something to that effect has also been narrated from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, Abū Hurayrah, and Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with them) from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family).

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: And it has reached us that Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with both of them) used not to offer sacrifices, out of dislike that people might follow their example and those who saw them might think that it was obligatory. [Ḍaʿīf]"
19040,"(19034) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdad informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī informed us; Ibn Abī Maryam informed us; al-Firyābī informed us; Sufyān narrated from his father, Muṭarrif, and Ismāʿīl, from al-Shaʿbī, from Abū Sarīḥah al-Ghifārī, who said: ""I met Abū Bakr — or I saw Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with both of them) — and they did not offer sacrifices."" In some of his narration: ""Out of dislike that people might follow their example.""

Abū Sarīḥah al-Ghifārī is Ḥudhayfah ibn Asīd, a Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). [Ḍaʿīf]"
19041,"(19035) And Abū al-Fatḥ — Hilāl ibn Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Ḥaffār in Baghdād — informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥusayn ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAyyāsh al-Qaṭṭān informed us: Abū al-Ashʿath informed us: al-Muʿtamir ibn Sulaymān said: I heard Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid from Muṭarrif from ʿĀmir from Ḥudhayfah ibn Asīd, who said: ""I saw Abū Bakr and ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with them both — and they would not offer a sacrifice on behalf of their households, lest it be taken as a Sunnah (established practice) to be followed. When I came to this town of yours, my family urged me toward negligence, after I had come to know the Sunnah.""

Thus stated Muʿtamir ibn Sulaymān regarding ʿĀmir, and he erred therein. [Munkar]"
19042,"(19036) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us — what I read aloud to him — Abū Isḥāq al-Buzārī informed us: Abū al-Ḥusayn al-Ghāzī informed us: ʿAmr ibn ʿAlī said: I said to Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, ""Indeed, al-Muʿtamir narrated to us, saying: Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Muṭarrif narrated from al-Shaʿbī from Abī Sarīḥah."" He said, ""This is like his ḥadīth from al-Shaʿbī from ʿAmr al-Jamalī — meaning ʿAmr ibn Murrah — Ismāʿīl narrated: ʿĀmir informed us,"" and he mentioned it, intending that Yaḥyā erred in this just as he erred in that other narration. And the narration of Sufyān al-Thawrī confirms the statement of Yaḥyā. Al-Shāfiʿī said: And from Ibn ʿAbbās."
19043,"(19037) And the meaning was mentioned by what Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī informed us: my grandfather, Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr, informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr informed us: al-Qaʿnabī informed us: Salamah ibn Bukht narrated from ʿIkrimah, the mawlā of Ibn ʿAbbās, that Ibn ʿAbbās — may Allah be pleased with them both — used to, when the Day of Sacrifice arrived, give his mawlā two dirhams and say, ""Buy meat with them and inform the people that it is the sacrifice of Ibn ʿAbbās."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
19044,"(19038) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Burhān informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī informed us: Ibn Abī Maryam informed us: Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Firyābī informed us: Sufyān from al-Aʿmash from Abī Wāʾil from Abī Masʿūd al-Anṣārī — may Allah be pleased with him — who said: ""I would indeed leave the sacrifice, though I am well-off, out of fear that my neighbours might consider it an obligation upon me."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19045,"(19039) And Ibn Burhān informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan al-Miṣrī informed us: Ibn Abī Maryam informed us: al-Firyābī from Sufyān from Manṣūr and Wāṣil from Abī Wāʾil from Abī Masʿūd — ʿUqbah ibn ʿAmr al-Anṣārī — who said: ""I was resolved to leave the sacrifice, even though I was among the most well-off of you, out of fear that the soul might consider it a binding obligation upon it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19046,"(19040) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq informed us: Wahb ibn Jarīr informed us: Shuʿbah from ʿUqayl ibn Ṭalḥah from Abī al-Khaṣīb, a man from Banū Qays ibn Thaʿlabah, who said: ""I witnessed Ibn ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with them both — and a man asked him something regarding the matter of the sacrifice. He said: 'I dislike it, or avoid it' — Wahb was uncertain — 'the one whose lameness is manifest, and the one whose limp is evident, and the sick one whose illness is apparent, and the emaciated one whose thinness is clear.' Then Ibn ʿUmar said to him: 'Perhaps you consider it obligatory?' He said: 'No, but it is a reward, a good deed, and a Sunnah.' He said: 'Indeed, it is so.'""

Al-Shāfiʿī — may Allah have mercy upon him — said: ""And the discourse on sacrifices does not go beyond this, or it is said that they are obligatory, and thus they are upon every person, young and old, and no other animal suffices for every individual."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
19047,"(19041) Abū Zakariyyā — Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Muzakkī — informed us: the Qāḍī Abū Muḥammad Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us: Ibn Abī ʿUmar informed us: Sufyān from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed us that he heard Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib narrating from Umm Salamah — may Allah be pleased with her — that the Messenger of Allah — ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam — said: ""When the first ten days of Dhul-Ḥijjah have entered and one of you wishes to offer a sacrifice, let him not touch any of his hair or his skin.""

It was said to Sufyān, ""But some of them do not raise it (as a marfūʿ ḥadīth)?"" He said, ""But I do raise it.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar. [Munkar]"
19048,"(19042) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Kāmil ibn Khalaf ibn Shajarah, the Qāḍī in Baghdād, and Abū Aḥmad Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥamdān al-Ṣayrafī in Marw, both said: Abū Qilābah al-Raqāshī informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us: Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Isḥāq al-Khurāsānī informed us: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad, meaning al-Raqāshī, informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Kathīr informed us: Shuʿbah from Mālik ibn Anas from ʿAmr ibn Muslim from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib from Umm Salamah — may Allah be pleased with her — who said: The Messenger of Allah — ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam — said: ""When the first ten days have entered and one of you wishes to offer a sacrifice, let him refrain from his hair and his nails.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥajjāj ibn Yūsuf from Yaḥyā ibn Kathīr al-ʿAnbarī, and he also recorded it from the ḥadīth of Ghundar from Shuʿbah, except that he said ""ʿUmar"" or ""ʿAmr ibn Muslim.""

And Ibn Wahb and ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar and others narrated it from Mālik from ʿUmar ibn Muslim, mawqūf, from Umm Salamah; and Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAlqamah al-Laythī and Saʿīd ibn Abī Hilāl narrated it from ʿUmar ibn Muslim al-Jundaʿī, marfūʿ. [Munkar]"
19049,"(19043) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: al-Naḍr ibn Shumayl informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr informed us: ʿUmar ibn Muslim ibn ʿUmārah ibn Ukaymah said: ""We were in the bathhouse before the Day of Sacrifice, and some people applied henna therein. Some of the bathhouse attendants said, 'Indeed, Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib dislikes this and forbids it.' So I met Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib and mentioned that to him. He said, 'O son of my brother, this is a ḥadīth that has been forgotten and abandoned. Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet — ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam — narrated to me from the Messenger of Allah — ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam — who said: ""Whoever has an animal to be slaughtered and intends to slaughter it, when the new moon of Dhul-Ḥijjah appears, let him not touch any of his hair or his nail until he offers his sacrifice.""'""

Muslim extracted it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Muʿādh ibn Muʿādh and Abī Usāmah from Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr. Muʿādh said ""ʿUmar"" and Abī Usāmah said ""ʿAmr,"" and Abī Usāmah narrated the story in full. [Munkar]"
19050,"(19044) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān said: al-Shāfiʿī said: ""If someone asks, 'What indicates that it is a matter of choice, not obligation — meaning the prohibition of cutting hair and nails?' it is said to him: Mālik narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr from ʿAmrah from ʿĀʾishah — may Allah be pleased with her — who said: 'I used to fasten the necklaces of the sacrificial animals of the Messenger of Allah — ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam — with my own hands, and then the Messenger of Allah — ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam — would place them on the animals with his own hand, and then send them with my father, and nothing was prohibited for the Messenger of Allah — ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam — of what Allah had made permissible for him, until the sacrificial animals were offered.'""

Al-Shāfiʿī — may Allah have mercy upon him — said: ""And in this there is an indication of what I have described, and that a person does not become muḥrim merely by sending forth his sacrificial animal. Sending forth the sacrifice is more common than merely intending the sacrifice."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Shāfiʿī]"
19051,"(19045) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq — informed us, they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Malik ibn Anas informed me, from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr, from 'Amrah bint 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: I used to weave the garlands of the sacrificial animals of the Messenger of Allah, may the peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, with my own hands, then the Messenger of Allah, may the peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, would place them around their necks with his own hands, then he would send them with my father Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and nothing that Allah had made lawful for him was prohibited for the Messenger of Allah, may the peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, until the sacrificial animals were slaughtered.

Al-Bukhari and Muslim recorded it in their Sahih collections from the hadith of Malik. [Sahih. Agreed upon (mutafaqqun 'alayh).]"
19052,"(19046) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Wahb informed me: Haywah narrated to me: Abu Sakhrah narrated to me, from Ibn Qusayt, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her: that the Messenger of Allah, may the peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, ordered a ram with two black horns, black eyes, and a black belly, and it was brought so that he might sacrifice it. He said: ""O 'A'ishah, bring the knife."" Then he said: ""Sharpen it on a stone."" She did so. He took it and took the ram, laid it down, and slaughtered it, saying: ""Bismillah, Allahumma taqabbal min Muhammad wa ali Muhammad wa min ummat Muhammad."" Then he sacrificed it.

Muslim recorded it in his Sahih from Harun ibn Ma'ruf, from Ibn Wahb. [Sahih. Muslim 1967.]"
19053,"(19047) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us: Sulayman ibn Ahmad al-Tabrani narrated to us: Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us: al-Firyabi narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Ibn Aqil.

(And) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us: Hisham ibn 'Ali narrated to us: Abu Hudhayfah narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad, from Abu Salamah, from 'A'ishah, or from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with both of them, who said: When the Messenger of Allah, may the peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, offered a sacrifice, he would have brought to him two rams with black horns, black patches, and black bellies. He would slaughter one of them on behalf of his Ummah — those who bore witness to the Oneness of Allah and bore witness to his [the Prophet's] conveyance [of the Message] — and he would slaughter the other on behalf of Muhammad and the family of Muhammad.

In the narration of al-Firyabi: When he intended to offer a sacrifice, he would purchase two fat rams with black horns, black patches, and black bellies, and he would mention it.

And Hammad ibn Salamah related it from 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir, from his father. And Zuhayr ibn Muhammad related it from 'Abd Allah, from 'Ali ibn al-Husayn, from Abu Rafi', as though he had heard it from both of them. [Da'if (Weak).]"
19054,"(19048) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab al-Farra' narrated to us: 'Arim ibn al-Fadl narrated to us: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, from his father: that the Prophet, may the peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, was brought two rams with black horns, black patches, large, and black bellies. He laid one of them down and said: ""Bismillah, wallahu akbar, Allahumma hadha 'an Muhammad.""

Then he laid down the other and said: ""Bismillah, wallahu akbar, Allahumma hadha 'an Muhammad wa ummatihi mimman shahidaka bi al-tawhid wa shahida li al-balagh."" [Da'if (Weak).]"
19055,"(19049) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Ahmad Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn Hamdan al-Sayrafi in Marw narrated to us: Abu Qilabah narrated to us: Abu 'Amir 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Amr al-'Aqadi narrated to us: Zuhayr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ibn Aqil, from 'Ali ibn al-Husayn, from Abu Rafi', the freedman of the Messenger of Allah, may the peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, who said: When the Messenger of Allah, may the peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, offered a sacrifice, he would purchase two fat rams with black horns and black patches. When he had delivered the sermon and prayed, he would stand in his place of prayer and slaughter one of the two rams himself with a spear, and he would say: ""This is on behalf of all my Ummah — those who bear witness to Your Oneness and bear witness to my conveyance [of the Message]."" Then the other would be brought and he would slaughter it and say: ""Allahumma hadha 'an Muhammad wa ali Muhammad."" Then he would feed both of them entirely to the poor, and he and his family would eat from them. He said: ""We remained for some years, and Allah sufficed us from burden and debt through the Messenger of Allah, may the peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, so that no one among the Banu Hashim offered a sacrifice."" [Da'if (Weak).]"
19056,"(19050) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi — both informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Abu 'Utbah narrated to us: Baqiyyah narrated to us: 'Uthman ibn Zufar al-Juhani narrated to me: Abu al-Asad al-Sulami narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: I was the seventh of seven men with the Messenger of Allah, may the peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family. The Messenger of Allah, may the peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, commanded us, so each man among us collected a dirham, and we purchased a sacrificial animal for seven dirhams. We said: ""O Messenger of Allah, we have found it to be very expensive."" The Prophet, may the peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, said: ""Indeed, the most virtuous sacrificial animals are the most expensive and the most excellent of them."" Then the Messenger of Allah, may the peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, commanded that one man take it by a hand, another by a hand, another by a leg, another by a leg, another by a horn, another by a horn, and the seventh slaughtered it, and we all said the takbir over it. [Da'if jiddan (Very weak).]"
19057,"(19051) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Abu Usamah narrated to us: Musa ibn Ayyub al-Nasibi, whose kunya is Abu 'Imran, narrated to us: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walid said: Hammad ibn Zayd and Yazid ibn Harun asked me in Makkah twenty years ago. Baqiyyah said: ""And I had heard it from him forty years before I narrated it to them."" I said: ""'Uthman ibn Zufar narrated to me."" He said: ""Abu al-Asad al-Sulami narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: I was the seventh of seven men with the Messenger of Allah, may the peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family. He commanded us, so each man among us collected a dirham, and we purchased a sacrificial animal for seven dirhams, and he commanded that one take it…"" and he mentioned the hadith. Baqiyyah said: I said to Hammad ibn Zayd: ""Who was the seventh?"" He said: ""I do not know."" I said: ""The Messenger of Allah, may the peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family."" [Da'if jiddan (Very weak).]"
19058,"(19052) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Sukkari in Baghdad informed us: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us: 'Abbas ibn 'Abd Allah al-Tarqufi narrated to us: Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri' narrated to us: Sa'id, that is Ibn Abi Ayyub, narrated to me: Abu 'Aqil Zuhrah ibn Ma'bad narrated to me, from his grandfather 'Abd Allah ibn Hisham, who had met the Prophet, may the peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family. His mother, Zaynab bint Hamid, took him to the Messenger of Allah, may the peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, accept his pledge of allegiance."" The Messenger of Allah, may the peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family, said: ""He is young."" He stroked his head and prayed for him. He [the narrator] said: And he used to sacrifice with a single sheep on behalf of his entire household.

Al-Bukhari recorded it in his Sahih from al-Muqri'. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari 7210.]"
19059,"(19053) Abu Ahmad al-Mihrani al-'Adl informed us: Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Muzakki narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Bushanji narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Malik narrated to us, from 'Umarah ibn Sayyad, from 'Ata' ibn Yasar, that Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, said: We used to sacrifice with a single sheep, and a man would slaughter it on behalf of himself and the members of his household. Then people began to vie with each other [in ostentation], and it became a matter of mutual boasting. [Sahih.]"
19060,"(19054) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to me: 'Isa ibn Muhammad informed me: 'Amr ibn al-Rabi' ibn Tariq narrated to us, from Rashid ibn Sa'd, from 'Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Qatadah, from his father: that he used to sacrifice with a sheep on behalf of the members of his household. [Da'if (Weak).]"
19061,"(19055) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād that Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī informed us that Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us that al-Firyābī narrated to us that Sufyān narrated from Bayān from al-Shaʿbī from Abū Sarīḥah al-Ghifārī — Ḥudhayfah ibn Usayd, may Allah be pleased with him — who said: ""My family urged me to perform the sacrifice after I had come to know of the Sunnah. The people of the household used to sacrifice a sheep, but now our neighbours stingily say to us: 'There is no obligation of sacrifice upon you.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19062,"(19056) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us that Abū ʿUthmān al-Baṣrī narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us that Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us that Sufyān narrated from Khālik from ʿIkrimah, who said: ""Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, would bring a sheep and his family would say, 'This is on our behalf.' He would say, 'And on your behalf as well.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19063,"(19057) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Aḥmad ibn Salmān narrated to us that Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us that Zuhayr narrated to us that Abū al-Zubayr narrated from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Do not slaughter except a musinnah (a mature animal with permanent teeth), unless it is difficult for you, in which case you may slaughter a jadhaʿah (a young animal) from the sheep.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim 1963]"
19064,"(19058) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī informed us in Ṭūs that Abū Bakr ibn Maḥmūyah al-ʿAskarī narrated to us that Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī narrated to us that Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated to us that Zubayd al-Iyāmī narrated to us, saying: I heard al-Shaʿbī narrating from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""The first thing we begin with on this day of ours is that we pray, then we return and then sacrifice. Whoever does so has attained the Sunnah. And whoever slaughters before the prayer, it is merely meat he has presented to his family, and it is not in any way an act of worship (nusuk).""

A man from the Anṣār called Abū Burdah ibn Niyār said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have already sacrificed, and I have a jadhaʿah that is better than a musinnah."" He said to him: ""Sacrifice it in its place, but it will not suffice for anyone after you.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19065,"(19059) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us that Khālid ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated from Muṭarrif from ʿĀmir from al-Barāʾ, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""My uncle Abū Burdah sacrificed before the prayer, so the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: 'That is a sheep of meat.' He said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I have a jadhaʿah of goats.' He said: 'Sacrifice with it, but it will not be valid for anyone else.'""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
19066,"(19060) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Musaddad narrated to us that Khālid narrated it with its chain, similar to it, except that he said: ""He said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I have a domestic jadhaʿah of goats.' He said: 'Slaughter it, but it is not valid for anyone else.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19067,"(19061) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us that ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us that Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Aḥmad Bakr ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣayrafī in Marw narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn al-Faḍl al-Balkhī narrated to us that Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us. Both said: Hishām narrated to us from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr from Baʿjah al-Juhanī from ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, distributed sacrificial animals among his Companions, and a jadhaʿah fell to ʿUqbah's share. I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, a jadhaʿah has fallen to my share.' He said: 'Sacrifice it.'""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Makkī. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muʿādh ibn Faḍālah from Hishām, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
19068,"(19062) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb informed us that Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us that Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us that al-Layth narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb from Abū al-Khayr from ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀzib, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, gave me sheep to distribute as sacrificial offerings among his Companions. An ʿatūd (a young goat that has grown strong) remained from them. I mentioned it to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and he said: 'Sacrifice it yourself.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah and others.

Abū ʿUbayd said: ""The ʿatūd is from the offspring of goats — it is one that has grown and become strong.""

The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""This, if it is from the goats, then the jadhaʿah of goats does not suffice for anyone else, as though it were a concession specifically for him. This has also been narrated in the ḥadīth of al-Layth."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19069,"(19063) And that is in what Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, that Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh and Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzzakkī both narrated to us that Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Būshanjī narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bukayr narrated to us that al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb from Abū al-Khayr — Mirthad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Yazanī — from ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, gave me sheep to distribute as sacrificial offerings among my Companions. An ʿatūd remained from them. He said: 'Sacrifice it yourself, and there is no concession for anyone after you.'""

If this addition is preserved, then it was a concession specifically for him, just as a concession was granted to Abū Burdah ibn Niyār. [Ḍaʿīf]"
19070,"(19064) And something similar to this is carried in what Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī informed us, that Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us that Abū al-Azhar al-Salīṭī narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Khālid al-Wahbī narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Ṣudrān narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to me that ʿUmarah ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṭuʿmah narrated from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib from Zayd ibn Khālid al-Juhanī, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, distributed sheep among his Companions, and he gave me a jadhaʿah ʿatūd. He said: 'Sacrifice it.' I said: 'Shall I sacrifice with a jadhaʿah of goats?' He said: 'Yes, sacrifice it.' So I sacrificed with it.""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of al-Wahbī, and the narration of Abū Dāwūd does not contain the phrase 'of the goats.' [Ḍaʿīf]"
19071,"(19065) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdad; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī informed us; Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us; al-Firyābī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Usāmah ibn Zayd, from a man, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from a man from Juhaynah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""The jadhaʿ (yearling) of sheep suffices for sacrifice (al-aḍāḥī)."" [Weak]"
19072,"(19066) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan al-Miṣrī informed us; Yaḥyā ibn ʿUthmān ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us; Bakr ibn Muḍar narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith narrated from Bukayr ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from Muʿādh ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who reported from ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""We offered sacrifice with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) using jadhaʿ (yearlings) of sheep.""

Wakīʿ and Ibn Wahb narrated it from Usāmah ibn Zayd al-Laythī, from Muʿādh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Khubayb al-Juhanī, who said: ""I asked Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab about the jadhaʿ of sheep, and he said: 'The Sunnah among you is that ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir al-Juhanī asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) about the jadhaʿ of sheep, and he said: Sacrifice with it.'"" [Weak]"
19073,"(19067) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us; al-Bāghandī narrated to us; Qabīṣah narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from ʿĀṣim ibn Kulayb, from his father, who said: ""We were on an expedition, and with us (or in charge of us) was Mujāshiʿ ibn Masʿūd, a Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). Sheep became scarce, and he said: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: The jadhaʿ suffices of that which the thaniyyah (two-toothed) suffices.'"" [Ḥasan]"
19074,"(19068) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan al-Miṣrī informed us; Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us; al-Firyābī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from ʿĀṣim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from a man, who said: ""Among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), they were with Mujāshiʿ al-Sulamī when the sacrificial animals ran short. A man among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) stood and said: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: The jadhaʿ of sheep suffices that which the thaniyyah suffices.'"" He (the narrator) said: ""I think he said: 'Of goats (al-maʿiz).'"" Sufyān was uncertain in this narration. [Ḥasan]"
19075,"(19069) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥamdān al-Jallāb narrated to us; Hilāl ibn al-ʿAlāʾ al-Raqqī narrated to us; Abū Ḥudhayfah narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from ʿĀṣim ibn Kulayb, from his father, who said: ""We were on an expedition with a man from Banī Sulaym called Mujāshiʿ. Sheep became scarce, and he ordered a crier, who called out: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: The jadhaʿ of sheep suffices of that which the thaniyyah suffices.'""

Abū Dāwūd narrated it in Kitāb al-Sunan from the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Razzāq from al-Thawrī. [Ḥasan]"
19076,"(19070) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us; Tamtām narrated to us; Abū Ḥudhayfah narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from ʿĀṣim ibn Kulayb, from his father, who said: ""There was a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) called Mujāshiʿ ibn Masʿūd al-Sulamī. Sheep became scarce, and he ordered a crier, who called out: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: The jadhaʿ of sheep suffices that which the thaniyyah suffices.'"" [Ḥasan]"
19077,"(19071) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us; Wahb ibn Jarīr narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from ʿĀṣim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) from Juhaynah or Muzaynah, that they were with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) one or two days before al-Aḍḥā, and they used to be given two thaniyyahs (two-toothed animals). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""The jadhaʿah suffices of that which the thaniyyah suffices."" [Ḥasan]"
19078,"(19072) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqrīʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated from Muḥammad ibn Abī Yaḥyā; my mother narrated to me from Umm Bilāl, a woman from Aslam whose father was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) on the day of al-Ḥudaybiyyah. She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Sacrifice the jadhaʿ of sheep, for it is permissible."" [Weak]"
19079,"(19073) Abū Ḍamrah narrated from Muḥammad ibn Abī Yaḥyā, from his mother, who said: Umm Bilāl bint Hilāl informed me that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""The jadhaʿ of sheep is permissible as an uḍḥiyyah (sacrifice).""

Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith narrated it to us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Sawwār narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn al-Mundhir al-Ḥizāmī narrated to us; Abū Ḍamrah narrated it. [Weak]"
19080,"(19074) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdad; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Wakīʿ informed us; ʿUthmān ibn Wāqid al-ʿUmarī informed us; Kidām ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Kidām informed us from Abī Kibāsh, who said: ""I brought young sheep (judhʿān) to Madīnah, but they did not sell. I met Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) and told him. He said: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: What an excellent sacrifice — or what an excellent uḍḥiyyah — is the jadhaʿ of sheep.'"" He said: ""The people then rushed to buy them."" It has reached me from Abū ʿĪsā al-Tirmidhī that he said: Al-Bukhārī said: Others besides ʿUthmān ibn Wāqid narrated it from Abū Hurayrah as a mawqūf (stopped) narration. [Weak]"
19081,"(19075) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿUqbah al-Shaybānī in Kūfah informed us; Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Burd al-Anṭākī narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ḥunaynī narrated to us. He said: Hishām ibn Saʿd mentioned it from Zayd ibn Aslam from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: Jibrīl (peace be upon him) came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) on the Day of Sacrifice and said: ""What is your opinion of our sacrifice today?"" He said: ""The inhabitants of the heavens take pride in it. And know, O Muḥammad, that a jadhaʿ from sheep is better than a noble camel or cow. And had Allah known any sacrifice better than it, He would have ransomed Ibrāhīm (peace be upon him) with it."" It was also narrated by Abū Jaʿfar al-Simnānī from Isḥāq, who added therein: ""And a jadhaʿ from sheep is better than a noble goat."" Isḥāq is alone in narrating it, and his hadith contains weakness. [Munkar]"
19082,"(19076) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Ḥārithī narrated to us; Abū Usāmah narrated to us, from al-Walīd ibn Kathīr; Yazīd ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Qusayṭ narrated to me that Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab narrated to him that some of the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) used to say: ""That I sacrifice a jadhaʿ from sheep is more beloved to me than that I sacrifice a musinnah from goats."" It was also narrated by Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Yasār from Yazīd ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Qusayṭ from Ibn al-Musayyab from Umm Salamah, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family). [Ḥasan]"
19083,"(19077) Abū al-Fatḥ al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Shurayḥī informed us; Abū al-Qāsim al-Baghawī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn Ḥammād narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us from Qatādah from Muṭarrif from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""Were a thousand sheep to come to us, I would not sacrifice except with a jadhaʿ from sheep."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19084,"(19078) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Khalīl informed us; ʿAlī ibn Mushir informed us; Muḥammad, meaning Ibn Abī Laylā, informed us from al-Ḥakam from ʿAbbād ibn Abī al-Dardāʾ from his father, who said: ""Two jadhaʿ rams, both aḥmar (of mixed colour), were gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), and he sacrificed with them."" [Munkar]"
19085,"(19079) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Kabīr al-Ḥanafī narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Nāfiʿ narrated to us from his father from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) used to sacrifice in Madīnah with a camel (jazūr) at times, and with a ram when he could not find a camel. [Ḍaʿīf]"
19086,"(19080) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī narrated to us by dictation; my grandfather Abū ʿAmr, meaning Ismāʿīl ibn Nujayd al-Sulamī, informed us; Abū Muslim al-Kajjī narrated to us; Abū ʿĀṣim al-Nabīl narrated to us from Sufyān from Ibn Abī Laylā from al-Ḥakam from Miqsam from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) was gifted one hundred badanah (camels for sacrifice), among which was a camel belonging to Abū Jahl ibn Hishām that had a silver ring in its nose. The rest of its chains of narration have been mentioned at the end of the Book of Ḥajj. In it, if it is established, there is evidence for the permissibility of male animals in gifts and sacrifices. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ for other than it; previously referenced under no. 5/10159]"
19087,"(19081) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Abī ʿĪsā narrated to us; ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us; Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated to us from his father from Abū Murāwigh al-Ghifārī from Abū Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family): 'Which deed is best?' He said: 'Faith in Allah and striving in His cause.' I said: 'Which slaves (for emancipation) are best?' He said: 'The most expensive of them and the most precious to their owners.' He said: I said: 'And if I cannot do that?' He said: 'Then help a craftsman or make something for one who is inept.' I said: 'And if I cannot do that?' He said: 'Then keep people from harm, for it is a charity that you give on yourself.'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā, and Muslim reported it from another route from Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ; agreed upon]"
19088,"(19082) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Khalaf ibn Hishām narrated to us; Khalaf narrated to us; Baqiyyah ibn al-Walīd narrated to us from ʿUthmān ibn Zufar; Abū al-Aswad al-Anṣārī narrated to me from his father from his grandfather. Khalaf said: Baqiyyah named him. He said: ""I was the seventh of seven with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family)."" He mentioned the hadith regarding the udḥiyyah (sacrifice), and said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: ""Indeed, the most beloved of sacrifices to Allah are the most expensive of them and the fattest of them."" [Ḍaʿīf; previously referenced under no. 19050]"
19089,"(19083) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaydalānī narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated to us from Abū Isḥāq from ʿAṭāʾ from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) regarding the verse: ""{Eight pairs: two of sheep and two of goats}"" (al-Anʿām: 143). He said: ""The eight pairs of camels, cattle, sheep, and goats are according to one's means. Whatever is larger is more excellent."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
19090,"(19084) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Aḥmad informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Qutaybah narrated to us; Ḥarmalah informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us; Ḥaywah informed me; Abū Ṣakhr narrated to me from Ibn Qusayṭ from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr from ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) ordered a horned ram that treads in darkness, sees in darkness, and kneels in darkness to be brought, so that he might sacrifice with it. He mentioned the hadith. Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Hārūn ibn Maʿrūf from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ; Muslim 1967]"
19091,"(19085) Abu ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Adīb informed us — Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us — Abū al-Qāsim al-Baghawī informed us — Khalaf ibn Hishām narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated from Ayyūb — from Abū Qilābah — from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) was brought two horned, piebald rams, and he slaughtered them with his own hand.

al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah, from ʿAbd al-Wahhāb. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon.]"
19092,"(19086) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan informed us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Ḥunaynī narrated to us — ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated to us — my father narrated to us —

AND Abū Bakr al-Qāḍī informed us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us — al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn narrated to us — Ḥafṣ, i.e., ibn Ghiyāth, narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad — from his father — from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) sacrificed a horned, black-mouthed ram — one that ate in blackness, drank in blackness, looked in blackness, and walked in blackness. This is the wording of the ḥadīth of al-Faḍl. [Ḍaʿīf.]"
19093,"(19087) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā al-Rāzī narrated to us — ʿĪsā narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb — from Abū ʿAyyāsh — from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) slaughtered on the Day of Sacrifice two horned, piebald, castrated rams. [Ḍaʿīf.]"
19094,"(19088) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us — Muḥammad ibn Ghālib narrated to us — Abū Ḥudhayfah narrated to us — Sufyān narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAqīl — from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān — from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), or from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) — the doubt being from Sufyān — that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), when he wished to offer a sacrifice, would call for two large, fat, piebald, castrated, horned rams. He would slaughter one of them on behalf of his Ummah — for the one who had conveyed to him the message of the Message and bore witness to the Oneness of Allah — and he would slaughter the other on behalf of Muḥammad and the family of Muḥammad. [Ḍaʿīf.]"
19095,"(19089) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us — Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us — Bayān ibn Aḥmad al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us — Dāwūd ibn Rushayd narrated to us — al-Walīd narrated from ʿUfayr ibn Maʿdān — from Sulaym ibn ʿĀmir — from Abū Umāmah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: ""The best of sacrificial animals is the horned ram.""

And it has also been narrated from ʿUbādah ibn Nusayy, from his father, from ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: ""The best of sacrificial animals is the horned ram, and the best of shrouds is the white garment.""

And this has already passed in the Book of Funerals. [Ḍaʿīf.]"
19096,"(19090) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us — Abū al-Jumāhir narrated to us — ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated from Abū Thifāl al-Murrī — from Rabāḥ ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān — from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: ""The blood of a reddish-brown (ʿafrāʾ) animal is more beloved to Allah than the blood of two black ones.""

And al-Thawrī narrated it from Tawbah al-ʿAnbarī, from Sulaym, i.e., ibn ʿAttāb, who said: I heard Abā Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""The blood of a white (bayḍāʾ) animal is more beloved to me than the blood of two black ones.""

al-Bukhārī said: Some of them raise it, but it is not authentic."
19097,"(19091) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us — Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us — ʿAmr ibn Marzūq informed us — Shuʿbah narrated from Abū Isḥāq — who heard Hubayrah and ʿUmarah ibn ʿAbd say: We heard ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: ""Offer as sacrifice an animal of two years of age or older, and seek a fat one, for if you eat, you eat what is goodly, and if you feed others, you feed them what is goodly."" [Ṣaḥīh.]"
19098,"(19092) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh informed us — Yazīd ibn al-Haytham informed us that Ibrāhīm ibn Abī al-Layth narrated to them — al-Ashjaʿī narrated from Sufyān — from ʿĀṣim ibn Sulaymān — from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn — from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""A two-year-old animal is more beloved to me than an aged one. Allah is more deserving of the command (fatāʾ) and generosity. I love it more than the two-year-old — that I offer it as a sacrifice."" This is mawqūf. [Ṣaḥīh.]"
19099,"(19093) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us — Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn Matwayh narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Manīʿ narrated to us — ʿAbbād ibn al-ʿAwwām narrated to us — ʿUmar ibn ʿĀmir narrated to us — al-Ḥajjāj ibn al-Ḥajjāj narrated from Salamah ibn Junādah — from Sinān ibn Salamah, who said that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: ""Allah, the Almighty, is more deserving of the command and of fidelity. Purchase a fat jadhʿah (young animal) and offer it as a sacrifice on your own behalf"" — meaning, offer it as a sacrifice. [Ḍaʿīf.]"
19100,"(19094) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdad informed us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿf ibn Durustawayh narrated to us — Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Maslamah narrated from Mālik ibn Anas — from ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith — from ʿUbayd ibn Fayrūz — from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) was asked: ""What defects in sacrificial animals should be avoided?"" He gestured with his hand and said: ""Four."" And al-Barāʾ would gesture with his hand and say: ""My hand is shorter than the hand of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family): the lame animal whose lameness is manifest, the one-eyed animal whose blindness is manifest, the sick animal whose illness is manifest, and the emaciated animal that has no marrow in its bones."" [Ṣaḥīh.]"
19101,"(19095) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Qatādah informed us — ʿAlī ibn al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAqīl al-Khuzāʿī informed us — Abū Shuʿayb al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us — ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī said: ʿUbayd ibn Fayrūz — this man was from the people of Egypt, and we did not know whether ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith had heard from him or not. So we investigated, and lo, ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith had not heard it from ʿUbayd ibn Fayrūz."
19102,"(19096) Abū Naṣr informed us — ʿAlī informed us — Abū Shuʿayb narrated to us — ʿAlī said: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us — Usāmah ibn Zayd narrated from ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith — from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb — from ʿUbayd ibn Fayrūz.

ʿAlī said: Then we investigated, and lo, Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb had not heard it from ʿUbayd ibn Fayrūz.

ʿAbd al-Aʿlā narrated to us from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq that he narrated to them from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb — from Sulaymān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān — from ʿUbayd ibn Fayrūz.

ʿAlī said: Thus the hadith revolves around the hadith of Shuʿbah."
19103,"(19097) He means what Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad narrated to us — Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Shuʿbah narrated from Sulaymān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, who said: I heard ʿUbayd ibn Fayrūz say: I asked al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib (may Allah be pleased with him) what the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) disliked or forbade regarding sacrificial animals. He said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) stood up like this — and my hand is shorter than his hand — and said: Four are not permissible as sacrifices: the one-eyed whose blindness is manifest, the sick whose illness is manifest, the lame whose limping is manifest, and the emaciated that has no marrow. I said: I dislike deficiency in the teeth or in the ear. He said: What you dislike, leave it, but do not forbid it upon anyone. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19104,"(19098) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us — ʿAlī ibn al-Faḍl al-Khuzāʿī informed us — Abū Shuʿayb narrated to us — ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us — Shuʿbah narrated to me — Sulaymān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated from ʿUbayd ibn Fayrūz, and he mentioned the hadith similarly, but he did not mention Sulaymān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān's hearing from ʿUbayd.

ʿAlī said: Then we investigated, and lo, Sulaymān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān had not heard it from ʿUbayd ibn Fayrūz."
19105,"(19099) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us — ʿAlī informed us — Abū Shuʿayb narrated to us — ʿAlī. And Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Isfarāʾīnī informed us with it — Bishr ibn Aḥmad narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Naṣr al-Ḥaddāʾ narrated to us — ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us — ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated to us — Layth ibn Saʿd narrated to us — Sulaymān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated from al-Qāsim, the freedman of Khālid ibn Yazīd ibn Muʿāwiyah — from ʿUbayd ibn Fayrūz, who said: I asked al-Barāʾ (may Allah be pleased with him) — I said: Tell me, what did the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) dislike regarding sacrificial animals? He said: Four — and my hand is shorter than the hand of the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). He said: Four that are not permissible: the one-eyed whose blindness is manifest, the sick whose illness is manifest, the lame whose limping is manifest, and the emaciated that has no marrow. ʿAlī said: Thus the hadith is the hadith of Layth.

ʿAlī said: ʿUthmān said: I said to Layth ibn Saʿd: O Abā al-Ḥārith, Shuʿbah narrates this hadith from Sulaymān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, claiming he heard it from ʿUbayd ibn Fayrūz? He said: No, rather Sulaymān narrated it to us from al-Qāsim, the freedman of Khālid, from ʿUbayd ibn Fayrūz. ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar said: I met Shuʿbah and said: Layth narrated this hadith to us from Sulaymān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from al-Qāsim from ʿUbayd ibn Fayrūz, and he replaced ""al-kasīrah that has no marrow"" with ""al-ʿajfāʾ that has no marrow."" He said: Shuʿbah said: Thus I preserved it, just as I narrated it. This is how ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated it from Layth ibn Saʿd. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19106,"(19100) And ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah informed us — Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdah narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bukayr narrated to me — al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated from Sulaymān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān — from ʿUbayd ibn Fayrūz, the freedman of Banī Shaybān — from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) was asked: What is to be avoided regarding sacrificial animals? He said: Four. And he gestured with his hand and said: My hand is shorter than the hand of the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam): the one-eyed whose blindness is manifest, the lame whose limping is manifest, the sick whose illness is manifest, and the emaciated that has no marrow. He said: I then said to al-Barāʾ: I dislike deficiency in the horn, the ear, and the teeth. He said: Dislike for yourself what you wish, but do not make it forbidden upon anyone.

And similarly Abū al-Walīd al-Ṭayālisī narrated it from al-Layth ibn Saʿd, without mentioning al-Qāsim in his chain. And similarly Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb and Shuʿbah ibn al-Ḥajjāj narrated it from Sulaymān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, and Shuʿbah mentioned Sulaymān's hearing from ʿUbayd ibn Fayrūz. And according to what reached me from Abū ʿĪsā al-Tirmidhī from Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī: he was inclined toward authenticating the narration of Shuʿbah and was not pleased with the narration of ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19107,"(19101) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsá — and ʿAlī ibn Baḥr narrated to us — ʿĪsá, that is Ibn Yūnus al-Maʿná — from Thawr — Abū Ḥumayd al-Ruʿaynī narrated to me — Yazīd Dhū Miṣr narrated, who said: I came to ʿUtbah ibn ʿAbd al-Sulamī and said: O Abā al-Walīd, I went out seeking sacrificial animals and found nothing that pleased me except one with a cut ear, and I disliked it. What do you say? He said: Why did you not bring it to me? I said: Glorified is Allah — it would be permissible for you but not permissible for me? He said: Yes, you doubt while I do not doubt. The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) forbade the muṣfarrah, the mustaʾṣalah, the bakhqāʾ, the mushayyiʿah, and the kasrāʾ. The muṣfarrah is the one whose ear is cut away until its canal becomes visible; the mustaʾṣalah is the one whose horn is uprooted from its base; the bakhqāʾ is the one whose eye is gouged out; the mushayyiʿah is the one that cannot keep up with the sheep due to emaciation and weakness; and the kasrāʾ is the broken one. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19108,"(19102) Al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Aḥmad ibn Shawdhb in Wāsiṭ narrated to us — Shuʿayb ibn Ayyūb narrated to us — and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us — Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim informed us — ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsá informed us — Isrāʾīl narrated from Abī Isḥāq — from Sharīḥ ibn al-Nuʿmān — from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalaihi wa ālihi wa sallam) commanded us to examine the eye and the ear, and not to sacrifice the muqābalah, the mudābarah, the sharqāʾ, or the kharqāʾ.

He said: The muqābalah is that whose ear-tip has been cut off; the mudābarah is that whose ear has been cut from the side; the sharqāʾ is the one with a split ear; and the kharqāʾ is the one with a pierced ear. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19109,"(19103) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah informed us — Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Aḥmad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Sarrāj narrated to us — Abū Shuʿayb al-Ḥarrānī informed me — Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Wāqid al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us — Zuhayr narrated to us — Abū Isḥāq narrated from Sharīḥ ibn al-Nuʿmān. Abū Isḥāq said: And he was a truthful man — from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), and he mentioned it similarly, adding: and not to sacrifice the one-eyed. Zuhayr said: I said to Abū Isḥāq — and he mentioned ʿaḍbāʾ — I said: What is the muqābalah? He said: The two tips of the ear are cut off. I said: What is the mudābarah? He said: The back of the ear is cut off. I said: What is the sharqāʾ? He said: The ear is split. I said: What is the kharqāʾ? He said: A hole is pierced in her ear. [Ḍaʿīf]"
19110,"(19104) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us — Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Shuʿbah narrated from Qatādah — from Jurayy ibn Kulayb — who heard ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) forbade sacrificing an animal with a cut ear or a cut horn.

Qatādah said: And I asked Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib about the ʿaḍb (severed), and he said: Half, or more. [Ḍaʿīf]"
19111,"(19105) Abū Bakr informed us, ʿAbdullāh informed us, Yūnus narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, from Abū ʿAwanah, from Jābir, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Nujayy, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) prohibited [the sacrifice of an animal with] half the ear or horn cut off. This is how it appears in these two narrations; the first of the two is more reliable, and the second is weaker.

It has also been narrated from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) as a mawqūf [stopped] narration contradicting this regarding the horn. [Weak]"
19112,"(19106) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Aḥmad ibn Shawdhab narrated to us, Shuʿayb ibn Ayyūb narrated to us, Qabīṣah narrated to us, Sufyān narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Ḥujayyah ibn ʿAdī, who said — Ḥujayyah said: We were with ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) when a man came to him and asked: ""[How many partners in] a cow?"" He replied: ""From seven."" He asked: ""The horn?"" He said: ""It does not harm you."" He asked: ""The lame?"" He said: ""When it reaches the place of sacrifice, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) commanded us to examine the eye and the ear carefully."" [Weak]"
19113,"(19107) Abū ʿAlī informed us, Ibn Shawdhab narrated to us, Shuʿayb narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, from Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us, Salamah ibn Kuhayl narrated to us, from Ḥujayyah ibn ʿAdī, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), that he was asked about the cow and said: ""From seven."" He was asked about a broken horn and said: ""It does not harm you."" He was asked about lameness and said: ""When it reaches the place of sacrifice, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) commanded us to examine the eye and the ear carefully.""

This indicates that what is meant by the first [narration], if the prohibition regarding the horn is authentically established, [is as stated].

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: There is no deficiency in the horn — meaning that its loss or absence does not constitute a deficiency in the flesh. [Weak]"
19114,"(19108) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan al-Kārizī narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us, from Abū ʿUbayd narrated to us, Hushaym informed us, Abū Jamrah informed us, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), that he saw no harm in sacrificing an animal with small ears (al-ṣamʿāʾ). Abū ʿUbayd said: Al-Aṣmaʿī said: Al-Ṣamʿāʾ is the one with small ears. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19115,"(19109) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Abū Muslim Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated to us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Zubayd, from al-Shaʿbī, from al-Barāʾ (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) addressed us on the Day of Sacrifice and said: ""The first thing we begin with on this day of ours is that we pray, then we return and then sacrifice. Whoever does so has followed our Sunnah, and whoever slaughters before praying, it is merely meat he has hastened for his family — it is not sacrifice (nusuk) at all."" Thereupon my uncle, Abū Burdah ibn Niyār, stood up and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I slaughtered before I prayed, and I have a jadhʿah [young animal] that is better than a musinnah [mature animal]."" He said: ""Sacrifice it in its place,"" or he said, ""Slaughter it, but it will not suffice for anyone after you."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19116,"(19110) ʿAlī informed us, Aḥmad narrated to us, Abū Muslim narrated to us, Ḥajjāj ibn Minhāl narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated to us, and he mentioned it with its chain, similar to it, and said: ""Sacrifice it in its place, and it will not suffice,"" or ""it will not compensate for anyone after you.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb and Ḥajjāj ibn Minhāl, and Muslim narrated it from another route from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19117,"(19111) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn ʿAlī ibn Mukram al-Bazzāz narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ghālib narrated to us, Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, Abū ʿAwanah informed us, Firās informed us, from ʿĀmir, from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) prayed one day and said: ""Whoever prays our prayer and faces our qiblah, let him not slaughter until he has [finished the prayer and] departed."" Thereupon Abū Burdah ibn Niyār stood up and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have already done so."" He said: ""That is something you hastened."" He said: ""I have a jadhʿah that is better than two sheep of meat — shall I slaughter it?"" He said: ""Yes, but it will not suffice for any person after you.""

ʿĀmir said: ""It is the best of two sacrifices.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19118,"(19112) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Faqīh narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated to us, Manṣūr ibn al-Muʿtamir narrated to us, from al-Shaʿbī, from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) addressed us on the Day of Sacrifice after the prayer and said: ""Whoever prays our prayer and performs our rite has indeed achieved the sacrifice (nusuk), and whoever performs the sacrifice before the prayer, that is merely a sheep of meat."" Thereupon Abū Burdah ibn Niyār stood up and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, by Allah, I performed the sacrifice before I came out to the prayer, knowing that today is a day of eating and drinking, so I hastened and ate and fed my family and my neighbors."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""That is a sheep of meat.""

He said: ""I have a young she-goat (ʿanāq) that is better than two sheep of meat — will it suffice for me?"" He said: ""Yes, but it will not suffice for anyone after you.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah and Hunnād from Abī al-Aḥwaṣ.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
19119,"(19113) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdad informed us, Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn Ibrāhīm al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us, Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind informed us, from ʿĀmir, from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Let no one slaughter before he prays."" Thereupon my uncle stood up and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, today there is much meat, and I have already sacrificed my offering so that my family and neighbors may eat, and I have a young she-goat that is better than a sheep of meat — shall I slaughter it?"" He said: ""Yes, but a jadhʿah will not suffice for anyone after you, and it is the best of your two sacrifices.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from two other routes from Dāwūd, and al-Bukhārī cited it as corroborating evidence. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19120,"(19114) Muṭarrif said, from ʿĀmir al-Shaʿbī, from al-Barāʾ (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Whoever sacrifices before the prayer has slaughtered only for himself, and whoever slaughters after the prayer has completed his sacrifice (nusuk) and has followed the way of the Muslims."" Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of it; he said: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Khālid ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated to us, from Muṭarrif, and he mentioned it.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from Musaddad from Khālid. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
19121,"(19115) Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us — Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — Shu'bah narrated from Salamat ibn Kuhayl, who said: I heard Abu Juwayfah narrating from al-Bara' ibn 'Azid (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: Abu Burdah slaughtered before the Salah, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: ""Replace it."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have only a jadh'ah, which is better than a musinnah."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: ""Use it in its place, but it will not suffice or fulfill the obligation for anyone after you.""

Al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from the hadith of Shu'bah. [Sahih]"
19122,"(19116) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us — Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn Sakhawayh narrated to us — Tamim ibn Muhammad narrated to us — Muhammad, that is, ibn 'Ubayd ibn Hisab, narrated to us — Hammad narrated to us — Ayyub and Hisham narrated from Muhammad, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) prayed, then addressed the people, and ordered whoever had slaughtered before the Salah to slaughter again. A man from the Ansar stood and said: ""My neighbors are in need"" — or he said: ""in hardship"" — ""so I slaughtered before the Salah, and I have a young goat that I love more than two sheep of meat."" So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) granted him a concession. If it was a concession for him, then so it was; otherwise, I have no knowledge of it. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) turned to two spotted rams and slaughtered them, and the people went to their small livestock and slaughtered them.

Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd ibn Hisab, and al-Bukhari narrated it from Hamid ibn 'Umar from Hammad ibn Zayd up to his words ""he granted him a concession."" [Sahih]"
19123,"(19117) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf al-Isbahaninformed us — Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi informed us — and Abu al-Husayn ibn Burhan informed us — Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us — both said: Sa'dan ibn Nasr narrated to us — Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated from al-Aswad ibn Qays, who said: I heard Jundub ibn Sufyan (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: I was present on the Day of al-Adha with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), when a man stood and said: ""Some people have already slaughtered before the Salah."" He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: ""Whoever among you has slaughtered before the Salah, let him repeat his slaughter, and whoever has not, let him slaughter in the name of Allah.""

This is the wording of the hadith of Ibn al-A'rabi. In the narration of al-Saffar, it reads: ""He came to know that some people had slaughtered, and he said: 'Let each of you repeat his udhiyah, and whoever has not yet done so, let him slaughter in the name of Allah.'""

Muslim narrated it in the Sahih from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim and Ibn Abi 'Umar from Sufyan.

In these reports there is evidence that whoever slaughtered before the Salah of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) has not fulfilled the rite of sacrifice in any way. [Sahih]"
19124,"(19118) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us — Abu Dawud narrated to us — Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us — Abu al-Mughirah narrated to us — Safwan narrated to us — Yazid ibn Khumayr al-Rahabi, who said: 'Abd Allah ibn Busr, a companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), went out with the people on the Day of 'Id al-Fitr or al-Adha, and he disapproved of the Imam's delay, saying: ""We would have finished by this time, that is, during the time of al-tasbih.""

And it has been narrated from al-Hasan al-Basri that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) used to go out to al-Adha and al-Fitr when the sun had risen and its rising was complete. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) used to pray the Salah of 'Id at the earliest time. So whoever slaughtered before the Salah of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) and ate and fed his family and neighbors — as was narrated in the hadith of Abu Burdah ibn Niyar — his slaughter occurred before its time became permissible, and that is not permissible. That is why he ordered a repetition. So whoever sacrifices after the time in which the Salah becomes permissible has elapsed, and the duration of the Salah of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) and his two khutbahs has passed, his udhiyah is valid, if Allah wills. [Sahih]"
19125,"(19119) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us — 'Ubayd ibn Sharik narrated to us — Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us — al-Layth narrated to me — Kathir ibn Farqad narrated from Nafi', from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with both of them): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) used to slaughter and offer his sacrifice at the musalla.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Yahya ibn Bukayr. [Sahih. Bukhari 5552]"
19126,"(19120) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us — Abu Hamid ibn Bilal narrated to us — Abu al-Azhar narrated to us — Abu Usamah narrated to us — Usamah ibn Zayd narrated from —

(h) And Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affan al-'Amiri narrated to us — Abu Usamah narrated from Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi, who said: Nafi' narrated to us from Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) used to slaughter his udhiyah at the musalla. Nafi' said: And Ibn 'Umar used to do the same. This is the wording of the hadith of al-'Amiri. In the hadith of Abu al-Azhar, it reads: ""The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) used to…"" [Sahih]"
19127,"(19121) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me — Abu al-Nadr al-Faqih narrated to us — Ibrahim ibn Isma'il al-'Anbari narrated to us — Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us — Khalid ibn al-Harith al-Hujaymi narrated to us — 'Ubayd Allah narrated from Nafi', who said: 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with both of them) used to offer his sacrifice at the place of slaughtering. 'Ubayd Allah said: ""The place of slaughtering of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family)."" 'Ubayd Allah said: ""And al-Qasim used to offer his sacrifice at his home.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami and Ishaq ibn Ibrahim from Khalid ibn al-Harith, without mentioning the action of al-Qasim. [Sahih]"
19128,"(19122) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq and Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us — Ibn Wahb informed me — I heard Sufyan ibn Sa'id narrating from Ayyub ibn Abi Tamimah al-Sakhtiyani, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: ""The tadhkiyah (slaughter) is in the throat and the labbah (the base of the neck).""

[Sahih]"
19129,"(19123) Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Ardastani informed us — Abu Nasr al-'Iraqi narrated to us — Sufyan ibn Muhammad al-Jawhari narrated to us — 'Ali ibn al-Hasan narrated to us — 'Abd Allah ibn al-Walid narrated to us — Sufyan narrated from Ayyub, from 'Abd Allah ibn Sa'id, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: ""The tadhkiyah is in the throat and the labbah.""

[Sahih]"
19130,"(19124) And with his isnad, Sufyan narrated from Ayyub, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Furafisah al-Hanafi, from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""The tadhkiyah is in the throat and the labbah, and do not hasten the souls before they expire.""

This has been narrated from another weak chain as a marfu' report, and it is not established. [Da'if]"
19131,"(19125) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Tamtām narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Muqātil al-Marwazī narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿUthmān Saʿīd ibn Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdān al-Naysābūrī informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Kārizī informed us orally: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Zayd al-Ṣāʾigh narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Muqātil narrated to us: Ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Maʿmar, from ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās and Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with both of them, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Do not eat the sharīṭah, for it is the slaughter of Satan."" [Weak]"
19132,"(19126) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿĪsā, the client of Ibn al-Mubārak, narrated from Ibn al-Mubārak through this chain of narration, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, forbade the sharīṭah of Satan, which is an animal that is slaughtered by cutting the skin without severing the jugular veins, and then it is left until it dies. [Weak]"
19133,"(19127) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb informed me from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Zaḥr, from al-Qāsim, the client of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Abī Umāmah al-Bāhilī, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Whatever has had its jugular veins severed is permissible, so long as it was not killed by the gnawing of a canine tooth or the cutting of a claw.""

Abū al-ʿAbbās said: In my book, it is not from ʿAlī ibn Yazīd.

The Shaykh, may Allah have mercy upon him, said: And in this chain of narration there is weakness. [Weak]"
19134,"(19128) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Faqīh al-Isfarāyīnī narrated to us there: Abū Sahl Bishr ibn Aḥmad al-Isfarāyīnī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī informed us: Zuhayr informed us: Abū al-Zubayr narrated from Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Do not slaughter except a musinnah (a mature animal with two teeth), unless it is difficult for you, in which case you may slaughter a jadhaʿah (a young sheep) from the sheep.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus from Zuhayh. [Authentic. Muslim 1963]"
19135,"(19129) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū ʿUthmān Saʿīd ibn Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdān al-Naysābūrī both said: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed us: Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Malik narrated from ʬAṭāʾ, from Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We used to perform tamattuʿ (enjoyment) with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and we would slaughter a cow on behalf of seven persons.

Muslim narrated it through another chain from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Sulaymān. [Authentic. Agreed upon]"
19136,"(19130) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUllawayh narrated to us: Hārūn ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: ʿAbadah narrated from Hishām, from Fāṭimah bint al-Mundhir, from Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr, may Allah be pleased with both of them, who said: We slaughtered a horse during the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, while we were in Madīnah, and we ate it.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq from ʿAbadah ibn Sulaymān. [Authentic]"
19137,"(19131) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Shuʿbah and Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated — and the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUyaynah is more complete — from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Ṣuḥayb, the client of Ibn ʿĀmir, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, who said: ""Whoever kills a sparrow without its right, Allah will ask him about it on the Day of Resurrection."" It was said: ""And what is its right?"" He said: ""He should slaughter it and eat it, and not cut off its head and throw it away."" [Weak]"
19138,"(19132) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ narrated to us: al-Sarī ibn Khuzaymah narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Wuhayb narrated from Ayyūb, from Abī Qilābah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned the ḥadīth regarding the iḥrām and said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sacrificed seven camels standing with his own hand, and he slaughtered in Madīnah two black-and-white horned rams.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl. [Authentic. Agreed upon]"
19139,"(19133) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to us: ʿImrān — that is, Ibn Mūsā — narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Jarīr and ʿAbadah ibn Sulaymān narrated.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAmr informed us: Abū Bakr informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Ibn Numayr narrated to us: My father, Ḥafṣ, and Wakīʿ narrated — all of them from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from Fāṭimah bint al-Mundhir, from Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr, may Allah be pleased with both of them, who said: We slaughtered (naḥarnā) a horse during the time of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and we ate it. And ʿAbadah said: We slaughtered (dabaḥnā).

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah from Jarīr, and he said: Wakīʿ and Ibn ʿUyaynah followed him from Hishām regarding nahr (slaughtering). And he narrated it from the ḥadīth of al-Thawrī from Hishām regarding nahr, and from Isḥāq from ʿAbadah regarding dhabḥ (slaughter). And Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr from all three of them regarding nahr.

And it has already been mentioned in the Book of Ḥajj from ʿAmrah from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, in the story of the Ḥajj, who said: On the Day of Nahr, beef was brought to us, and I said: ""What is this?"" They said: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sacrificed on behalf of his wives."" And in another narration: ""He slaughtered (dabaḥa).""

Similarly, the narrations differ regarding this from Abū al-Zubayr from Jābir. In one narration: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, sacrificed (naḥara) a cow on behalf of his wives. And in another narration: He slaughtered (dabaḥa) a cow on behalf of ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her. [Authentic. Agreed upon]"
19140,"(19134) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated, and he said: Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy upon him, said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, forbade nakhʿ (breaking the neck) and hastening the soul's departure before it is properly dispatched.

Al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy upon him, said: Nakhʿ is that a sheep is slaughtered and then its neck is broken at the place of slaughter for the purpose of nakhʿ and the breaking at that spot, or it is struck to hasten the cessation of its movement. I disapprove of this, and he said: This does not render it impermissible, because it is properly slaughtered (dhakiyyah). [Authentic]"
19141,"(19135) Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami informed us; Abu al-Hasan al-Karazi narrated to us; 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us from Abu 'Ubaid; Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us from Hisham al-Dastawa'i and Hajjaj ibn Abu 'Uthman from Yahya ibn Abu Kathir from al-Ma'rur al-Kalbi from 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, that he forbade al-fars in the slaughtered animal.

Abu 'Ubaid said: Abu 'Ubaidah said: al-fars is al-nakha'. It is said: ""farastu al-shaata"" (I performed fars on the sheep) and ""nakha'tuha"" (I performed nakha' on it). This means that the slaughtering is carried out until it reaches the nakha', which is a bone in the neck. It is also said that it is rather that which is located in the vertebrae of the spinal column, resembling marrow, and is connected to the nape. He forbade that the slaughtering be carried out to that extent.

Abu 'Ubaid said: As for al-nakha', it is as Abu 'Ubaidah described it. As for al-fars, there has been disagreement regarding it. It is said that it refers to breaking (al-kasr). What was forbidden is that the neck of the slaughtered animal be broken before it has cooled. What clarifies this is that in the hadith it states: ""And do not hasten the souls until they expire."" [Sahih]"
19142,"(19136) Abu Sa'd al-Malini informed us; Abu Ahmad ibn 'Adi al-Hafiz narrated to us; Isma'il ibn Musa al-Hasib narrated to us; Jubarah narrated to me; 'Abd al-Hamid ibn Bahram narrated to me; Shahr, who is ibn Hawshab, narrated to me from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with both of them: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, forbade that the slaughtered animal be subjected to fars before it dies.

This chain of narration contains weakness. [Da'if]"
19143,"(19137) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq narrated to us; Isma'il ibn Qutaybah narrated to us.

(Additionally) al-Ustadh Abu Ishaq — Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Isfarayini — informed us; Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn Rizmawayh narrated to us; Abu Zakariyya — Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Ghalib al-Nasawi — narrated to us. Both said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us; Hushaym narrated to us from Khalid al-Hadha' from Abu Qilabah from Abu al-Ash'ath al-San'ani from Shaddad ibn Aws, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I preserved from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, two qualities. He said: ""Indeed, Allah has prescribed excellence (al-ihsan) in all things. So when you kill, kill well, and when you slaughter, slaughter well. Let one of you sharpen his blade and let him give ease to his slaughtered animal.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya. [Sahih. Muslim 1955]"
19144,"(19138) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-Fadl ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Abd al-Majid narrated to us; Khalid al-Hadha' narrated to us from Abu Qilabah from Abu al-Ash'ath al-San'ani from Shaddad ibn Aws, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, who said: ""Indeed, Allah is al-Muhsin (the Bestower of Good). He has prescribed excellence in all things. So when you kill, kill well, and when one of you slaughters, let him slaughter well. Let one of you sharpen his blade and let him give ease to his slaughtered animal.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Ishaq al-Hanzhali.

And we have narrated in the hadith of 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, when the ram was brought to him for sacrifice: ""O 'A'ishah, bring me the knife."" Then he said: ""Sharpen it with a stone."" [Sahih]"
19145,"(19139) Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan — and Abu Sa'id ibn Abu 'Amr both said: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us; Abu al-Aswad — al-Nadr ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar — narrated to us; Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us from 'Uqayl from Ibn Shihab from Salim ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, commanded that the blades be sharpened and that they be concealed from the animals. Then he said: ""When one of you slaughters, let him dispatch (the animal) quickly.""

This is how Ibn Lahi'ah narrated it, connected and well-transmitted. [Munkar]"
19146,"(19140) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abu Ishaq and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan both narrated to us; they said: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Qurrah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Ma'afiri narrated to me from Ibn Shihab that 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with both of them, said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, commanded that the blades be sharpened and that they be concealed from the animals. And he said: ""When one of you slaughters, let him dispatch (the animal) quickly."" [Da'if]"
19147,"(19141) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far al-Farisi narrated to us; Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to me; Yusuf ibn 'Adi narrated to me; 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Sulayman narrated to me from 'Asim al-Ahwal from 'Ikrimah from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with both of them, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, passed by a man who had placed his foot on the side of a sheep while he sharpening his blade, and the sheep was looking at him with her eyes. He said: ""Why not before this? Do you want to cause her to die multiple deaths?""

Hammad ibn Zayd followed him in narrating from 'Asim, and he said: ""Do you want to cause her to die multiple deaths?"" Ma'mar narrated from 'Asim but sent it as a mursal narration, not mentioning Ibn 'Abbas in it. [Hasan]"
19148,"(19142) Abu Ahmad al-Mihrajani informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Ja'far al-Muzakki narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-'Abdi narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Malik narrated to us from 'Asim ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Asim ibn 'Umar ibn al-Khattab that a man sharpened a blade and took a sheep to slaughter it. 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, struck him with the whip and said: ""Do you torture the soul? Why did you not do this before you took it?"" [Da'if]"
19149,"(19143) Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah informed us; Abu 'Amr ibn Najid informed us; Abu Muslim narrated to us; 'Abd al-Rahmad ibn Hammad narrated to us; Ibn 'Awn narrated to us from Muhammad ibn Sirin that 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saw a man dragging a sheep to slaughter it. He struck him with the whip and said: ""Lead it to death, may you have no mother, with a gentle leading."" [Da'if]"
19150,"(19144) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz, Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi — and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abu Ishaq all said: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us; Ibn Wahb informed me; Sufyan ibn Sa'id narrated to us from his father from 'Abayah from Rafi' ibn Khadij, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him: ""We hope or we fear that we will encounter the enemy and we do not have any knives with us. Shall we slaughter with reeds?"" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""That which causes the blood to flow and upon which the name of Allah has been mentioned, eat from it, except for the tooth and the nail.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Qabisah from Sufyan, and both he and Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Yahya al-Qattan from Sufyan. [Sahih]"
19151,"(19145) Abū Bakr ibn Furak informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from Simāk ibn Ḥarb, who said: I heard Murriyy ibn Qaṭariyy say: I heard ʿAdīy ibn Ḥātim (may Allah be pleased with him) relate that he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family): ""O Messenger of Allah, I catch game but I find nothing to slaughter it with except a stone or a staff."" He said: ""Let the blood flow with whatever you wish, and mention the name of Allah."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19152,"(19146) And ʿAlīyy ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʢUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Abū Muslim Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdullāh informed us, Ḥajjāj, who is Ibn Minhāl, narrated to us, Ḥammād, who is Ibn Salamah, narrated from Simāk ibn Ḥarb, from Murriyy ibn Qaṭariyy, from ʿAdīy ibn Ḥātim (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, when one of us catches game and has no knife with him, may he slaughter it with a stone or a split piece of staff?"" He said: ""Let the blood flow with whatever you wish, and mention the name of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19153,"(19147) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us, Ibn Wahb narrated from Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbdullāh, from Abū al-Zinād, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah al-ʿAdawiyy, from ʿAdīy ibn Ḥātim (may Allah be pleased with him), that he said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, one of us catches game and has nothing with which to slaughter it except a stone or a split piece of staff."" He said: ""Let the blood flow with whatever you wish, and mention the name of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19154,"(19148) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīliyy informed us, al-Qāsim ibn Zakariyyā narrated to us, Ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā narrated to us, al-Muʿtamir ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, who said: I heard ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar, from Nāfiʿ, that he heard Ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik inform ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him), that he informed him: A slave-girl of theirs was grazing at Salʿ when she saw one of their sheep in the throes of death. She broke a stone and slaughtered it with it. He said to his family: ""Do not eat from it until I come to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and ask him."" So he sent someone to ask him, and he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and asked him about that, or his messenger did. He said: ""O Prophet of Allah, a slave-girl of ours was grazing at Salʿ and she saw one of our sheep in the throes of death, so she broke a stone and slaughtered it with it."" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) commanded him to eat it.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr, from al-Muʿtamir ibn Sulaymān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 2304]"
19155,"(19149) Abū ʿAlīyy al-Rūdhabāriyy informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, Yaʿqūb narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār, from a man of Banī Ḥārithah: He was shepherding a she-camel in a ravine of the ravines of Uḥud when death overtook it. He found nothing with which to slaughter it, so he took a tent-peg and struck it into its breast until its blood was shed. Then he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and informed him of that, and he commanded him to eat it. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19156,"(19150) And Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim narrated it, saying: I heard Zayd ibn Aslam say: ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār narrated to me, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudriyy (may Allah be pleased with him): A she-camel belonging to a man of the Anṣār was in the direction of Uḥud. Death overtook it, so he slaughtered it with a tent-peg. He asked the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) about eating it, and he commanded him to eat it.

Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us of it, Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ghālib narrated to us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us, Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim narrated to us, and he mentioned it. And Ḥibbān ibn Hilāl narrated it from Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim, adding: I said to him: ""Iron?"" He said: ""No, rather wood"" — meaning the tent-peg. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19157,"(19151) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamiyy informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan al-Kāriziyy informed us, ʿAlīyy ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us, from Abū ʿUbayd, Ibn ʿUlayyah narrated from Ayyūb, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both): He was asked about slaughtering with a piece of wood. He said: ""Anything that tears the jugular veins, other than a mu-tharrid.""

Abū ʿUbayd said: Abū Ziyād al-Kilābiyy said: al-Tharīd is that the animal is slaughtered with something that has no sharp edge, so it does not cause the blood to flow or shed it. Abū ʿUbayd said: His statement ""anything that tears the jugular veins"" means that which splits them and causes the blood to flow from them. Abū ʿUbayd said: Some people have interpreted this hadith as meaning his statement ""eat"" from eating, and this is an error. If he had meant eating, the meaning would apply to the knife, for it is the knife that tears. Rather, the meaning of the hadith is that anything that tears the jugular veins, whether wood or stone, after it has torn them, it is considered properly slaughtered (dhakiyy). [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19158,"(19152) Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Ṭarāʾifiyy narrated to us, ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimiyy narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ, from ʿAlīyy ibn Abī Ṭalḥah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: ""Their food is their slaughtered animals.""

And it has been narrated to us from Mujāhid and Makḥūl. [Ḍaʿīf]"
19159,"(19153) Abū ʿAlīyy al-Rūdhabāriyy informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Thābit al-Marwaziyy narrated to me, ʿAlīyy ibn Ḥusayn narrated from his father, from Yazīd al-Naḥawiyy, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: Regarding the verses { ""So eat of that upon which the name of Allah has been mentioned"" } [al-Anʿām 118] and { ""And do not eat of that upon which the name of Allah has not been mentioned"" } [al-Anʿām 121], this was abrogated and an exception was made from it, as He said: { ""The food of those who were given the Law is lawful for you, and your food is lawful for them"" } [al-Māʾidah 5].

[Ḍaʿīf]"
19160,"(19154) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed us, Tamīm ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, Shaybān ibn Farrūkh narrated to us, Sulaymān narrated to us, Ḥumayd, who is Ibn Hilāl, narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Mughaffal (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I acquired a sack of fat on the day of Khaybar. I clung to it and said: ""I will not give anyone anything of this today."" I turned around, and there was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), smiling.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Shaybān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon]"
19161,"Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affan narrated to us: Yahya ibn Fasil narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn Salih narrated from Simak from 'Ikrimah from Ibn 'Abas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The slaughtered animals of the Jews and the Christians were made lawful for them only because they believed in the Torah and the Gospel."
19162,"Abu 'Amr al-Adib informed us: Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed us: al-Hasan ibn Sufyan informed me: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim informed us: 'Abdah narrated to us: 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar narrated from Nafi' from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, that a woman slaughtered a sheep with a stone. This was mentioned to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and he saw no harm in it."
19163,"Abu Ahmad al-Mihranjani informed us: Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Muzakk narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-'Abdi narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Malik narrated from Nafi', the freedman of 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, from a man among the Ansar from Mu'adh ibn Sa'd or Sa'd ibn Mu'adh, who informed him that a slave-girl of Ka'b ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, used to graze his sheep at al-Sil'. One of them was on the verge of death, so she caught up with it and slaughtered it with a stone. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, was asked about it, and he said: ""There is no harm in it; eat it."""
19164,"Abu Sa'd al-Malini informed us: Abu Ahmad ibn 'Adi al-Hafiz narrated to us: Sadaqah ibn Mansur narrated to us: Abu Ma'mar narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Mu'adh narrated — Abu Ahmad said: and al-Hasan ibn Sufyan informed me: Abu Ma'mar narrated from 'Abd Allah ibn Mu'adh — from Ma'mar from Jabir from al-Sha'bi from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, granted a concession regarding the slaughter of a woman or a boy or a young man, provided they mention the name of Allah."
19165,"'Abd al-Khaliq ibn 'Ali ibn 'Abd al-Khaliq al-Mu'adhdhin informed us: Bakr Muhammad al-Sayrafi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn al-Faraj narrated to us: al-Waqidi narrated to us: Ma'mar narrated from Jabir al-Ju'fi from 'Amir from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, commanded that the slaughter of a young man be eaten provided he pronounces the name of Allah.

It has been narrated from Mujahid that he said: There is no harm in the slaughter of a boy or a woman from among the Muslims and the People of the Book."
19166,"Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Muhammad ibn al-Qasim al-'Ataki narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Sawwar narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us: Abu 'Awanah narrated from Qatadah from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sacrificed two horned, speckled rams. He slaughtered them with his own hand, pronounced the name of Allah and said the Takbir, and placed his flank upon their sides."
19167,"Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf al-Isbahani informed us: Abu Sa'id Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'faran narrated to us: Yazid ibn Harun informed us: Sa'id ibn Zayd narrated to us: 'Amr ibn Khalid narrated from Muhammad ibn 'Ali from his fathers from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said to Fatimah: ""O Fatimah, rise and be present at your sacrifice. Indeed, for the first drop of its blood that drips, you will be forgiven for every sin. Indeed, it will be brought on the Day of Resurrection with its flesh and its blood, multiplied seventy times, until it is placed in your scale."" Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""O Messenger of Allah, is this specifically for the family of Muhammad, for they are deserving of the good with which they have been distinguished, or for the family of Muhammad and the people in general?"" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Rather, it is for the family of Muhammad and the people in general."""
19168,"Abu al-Hasan ibn 'Abdan informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah, Abu Muslim, narrated to us: Ma'qil ibn Malik narrated to us: al-Nadr ibn Isma'il narrated from Abu Hamzah al-Thumali from Sa'id ibn Jubayr from 'Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""O Fatimah, rise and be present at your sacrifice, for indeed you will be forgiven for every sin you have committed with the first drop of its blood that drips, and say: 'Indeed, my prayer, my sacrifice, my living, and my death are for Allah, Lord of the worlds.'"" [Al-An'am 162–163] I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, is this for you and your household specifically, or for the Muslims in general?"" He said: ""Rather, it is for the Muslims in general.""

It is also narrated from 'Amr ibn Qays al-Mula'i from 'Ata' from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said to Fatimah, and he mentioned its meaning.

It is narrated from Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, that he commanded his daughters to sacrifice with their own hands."
19169,"Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Malik ibn Anas informed from Ja'far ibn Muhammad from his father from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sacrificed some of his sacrificial animals with his own hand, and others were sacrificed by someone else."
19170,"Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran al-'Adl in Baghdad informed us: Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Amr al-Razzaz narrated to us: Sa'dan ibn Nasr narrated to us: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Qasim from his father from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, sacrificed cattle on behalf of his wives.

Al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy upon him, said: He was given sacrificial animals as gifts, and the one who gave them as gifts sacrificed them along with him."
19171,"(19165) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed me that Abu 'Amr ibn Abi Ja'far informed me — that 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad narrated to us — that Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Qata'i narrated to us — that 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us — that Sa'id narrated from Qatada — from Sinan ibn Salama — from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, that Dhuyayb, Abu Qabisah, narrated to him: that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to send sacrificial camels with him and then say: ""If any of them becomes exhausted and you fear its death, then slaughter it, immerse its sandal in its blood, and then strike its side with it. Neither you nor any of your companions should eat from it.""

Muslim recorded it in his Sahih from Abu Ghassan from 'Abd al-A'la.

Al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""However, I dislike that a polytheist should slaughter any of the sacrificial animals."" [Sahih. Muslim 1326]"
19172,"(19166) Abu Bakr al-Ardashtani informed us — that Abu Nasr al-'Iraqi informed us — that Sufyan ibn Muhammad al-Jawhari narrated to us — that 'Ali ibn al-Hasan narrated to us — that 'Abd Allah ibn al-Walid narrated to us — that Sufyan narrated to me — that Ja'far narrated from his father — from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: ""Let not a Jew or a Christian slaughter the sacrificial offering of a Muslim."" [Da'if]"
19173,"(19167) And with his isnad, Sufyan narrated to me — that Qabus narrated from Abu Dhubyan — from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: that he disliked that a Jew or a Christian should slaughter the sacrificial offering of a Muslim. [Da'if]"
19174,"(19168) And Abu Nasr ibn Qatada informed us — that Abu al-Fadl ibn Khumayruwayh informed us — that Ahmad ibn Najda narrated to us — that Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us — that Zuhayr narrated to us — that Qabus ibn Abi Dhubyan narrated, that his father narrated to him, that he said: Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: ""Let no one slaughter your udhiyah except a Muslim. And when you slaughter it, say: 'Bismillah, Allahumma minka wa laka, Allahumma taqabbal min fulan' (In the name of Allah, O Allah, from You and for You, O Allah, accept from so-and-so).""

Al-Shafi'i said: ""If a polytheist slaughters it, its slaughter is valid and suffices, though I consider it disliked.""

The Shaykh said: ""This is because of what has previously been established regarding the permissibility of their slaughtered animals.""

And it has been narrated from 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah that he saw no harm in it. [Da'if]"
19175,"(19169) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan both said: Al-'Abbas al-Asamm informed us — that al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us — that al-Shafi'i informed us — that Ibrahim ibn Abi Yahya narrated to us — from 'Abd Allah ibn Dinar — from Sa'd al-Falja, the freedman of 'Umar, or the son of Sa'd al-Falja: that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""The Christians of the Arabs are not People of the Book, their slaughtered animals are not lawful for us, and I will not leave them until they embrace Islam or until I strike their necks."" [Da'if. Previously referenced as no. 18798]"
19176,"(19170) And Abu Zakariyya and Abu Bakr both said: Al-'Abbas informed us — that al-Rabi' informed us — that al-Shafi'i informed us — that al-Thaqafi narrated from Ayyub — from Ibn Sirin — from 'Ubayda al-Salmani — from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: ""Do not eat the slaughtered animals of the Christians of Banu Taghlib, for they have held onto nothing of their religion except the drinking of wine."" [Sahih]"
19177,"(19171) Abu Nasr ibn Qatada and Abu Bakr al-Mashshat both said: Abu 'Amr ibn Matar narrated to us — that Ibrahim ibn 'Ali al-Dhuhli narrated to us — that Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us — that Waki' narrated from Sufyan — from Qays ibn Muslim — from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hanafiyya, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, wrote to the Magians of Hajr, offering Islam to them. Whoever accepted Islam would be accepted, and who refused would have the jizyah imposed upon them, with the condition that their slaughtered animals would not be eaten and their women would not be married.

This is a mursal hadith, and the consensus of the majority of the Ummah upon it serves to corroborate it. [Da'if]"
19178,"(19172) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Asbahani informed us — that Abu Muhammad ibn Hayyan informed us — that 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Hatim narrated to us — that Ahmad ibn Sinan narrated to us — that 'Abd Allah ibn Numayr narrated to us — from Yahya ibn Salama ibn Kuhayl — from his father — from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Khalil al-Hadrami — from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""There is no harm in the food of the Magians; it is only their slaughtered animals that have been prohibited.""

Ibn Khuzaymah recorded it from Yusuf ibn Musa from Ibn Numayr, and from Muhammad ibn Maymun al-Makki from Abu Sa'id, the freedman of Banu Hashim, from Yahya ibn Salama, citing it as evidence. However, Yahya ibn Salama is considered weak in this regard.

And it has also been reported from him — from his father — from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Khalil — from his father — from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him.

And it has been narrated from Qays ibn al-Rabi' from Salama ibn Kuhayl — from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi al-Khalil al-Hadrami — from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. [Da'if]"
19179,"(19173) Abu Bakr al-Ardashtani informed us — that Abu Nasr al-'Iraqi informed us — that Sufyan al-Jawhari narrated to us — that 'Ali ibn al-Hasan narrated to us — that 'Abd Allah ibn al-Walid narrated to us — that Sufyan narrated from Ibn Jurayj — from Nafi' — from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both: that he used to like to face the qiblah when slaughtering.

Others also narrated from Ibn Jurayj, and in the hadith they said: ""He used to face the qiblah with his slaughtered animal."" And a marfu' narration has also been reported in this regard from Ghalib al-Jazari from 'Ata' from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, though its chain of narration is weak. [Da'if]"
19180,"(19174) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed me — that Abu Ahmad, the hafiz, informed us — that Abu 'Urwah informed us — that Bundar and Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna and Yahya ibn Hakim all said: Ibn Abi 'Adi narrated to us — from Sa'id — from Qatada — from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Prophet, peace and blessings of Allah be upon him, used to sacrifice two rams, placing his foot upon their sides, slaughtering them with his own hand, saying: ""Bismillah, Allahu Akbar (In the name of Allah, Allah is the Greatest).""

Muslim recorded it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna. [Sahih. Muttafaq 'alayh]"
19181,"(19175) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us that al-'Abbas al-Aṣamm informed him that al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman said: al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy upon him, said: The tasmiyah (invocation of Allah's name) upon the animal to be slaughtered is ""Bismillah."" If one adds anything further after that in remembrance of Allah, then such addition is good, and I do not dislike it. Along with the tasmiyah upon the animal to be slaughtered, I do not dislike one saying ""ṣallallāhu 'alā rasūlillāh"" (may the peace and blessings of Allah be upon the Messenger of Allah). Rather, I love it for him, and I love for him to increase in sending ṣalawāt (blessings) upon him. So may the peace and blessings of Allah be upon him in all circumstances, for the remembrance of Allah and the sending of ṣalawāt upon him constitute faith in Allah and worship of Him. Whoever says them shall be rewarded for them, if Allah wills. And 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn 'Awf mentioned that he was with the Prophet (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19182,"(19176) He related the meaning of what Abū al-Ḥasan 'Alī ibn Aḥmad ibn 'Abdān informed us, that Aḥmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, that Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Milḥān informed us, that Ibn Bukayr informed us, that al-Layth informed us from Ibn al-Hādī, from 'Amr — who is Ibn Abi 'Amr — from 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥuwayrith, from Muḥammad ibn Jubayr, from 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn 'Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I entered the mosque and saw the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) coming out of the mosque. I followed him, walking behind him, and he was unaware of me. He entered among the palm trees and faced the qiblah and prostrated, prolonging the prostration. I was behind him until I thought that Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, had taken his soul. I came forward, walking until I reached him, and I lowered my head to look at his face. He raised his head and said: ""What is the matter with you, O 'Abd al-Raḥmān?"" I said to him: ""When you prolonged the prostration, O Messenger of Allah, I feared that Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, had taken your soul, so I came to look."" He said: ""When I entered among the palm trees, I met Jibrīl, peace be upon him, and he said: 'I give you glad tidings that Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, says: Whoever sends salām upon you, I send salām upon him, and whoever sends ṣalawāt upon you, I send ṣalawāt upon him.'""

And that was also related from Ibn Abi Sandar al-Aslamī, from a mawlā of 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn 'Awf, from 'Abd al-Raḥmān, may Allah be pleased with him.

Al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy upon him, said: And the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""Whoever forgets to send ṣalawāt upon me, the path of Paradise is missed by him."" [Ḍa'īf]"
19183,"(19177) Abū Sahl Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Mihrānī informed him that Abū Muḥammad 'Abdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn Ka'b al-Tājir informed us that Muḥammad ibn Sulayman informed us that 'Umar ibn Ḥafṣ ibn Ghayyāth informed me, from my father, from Muḥammad ibn 'Amr, from Abī Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""Whoever forgets to send ṣalawāt upon me, the path of Paradise is missed by him."" [Ḍa'īf]"
19184,"(19178) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abi Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan and others besides them informed us, saying: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us that al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us that al-Shafi'i informed us that Ibn 'Uyaynah informed us from Ibn Abi Najīḥ, from Mujāhid, regarding His statement {"
19185,"(19179) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us that Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān informed us that Ja'far, meaning Ibn Hāshim, informed us that Sahl ibn 'Uthmān informed me that Yaḥyā ibn Abi Zā'idah informed me that al-Mubārak informed me from al-Ḥasan regarding {"
19186,"(19180) As for the ḥadīth that Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, that Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq ibn Ayyūb al-Faqīh informed us that Ismā'īl ibn Qutaybah informed us that Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us that Sulaymān ibn 'Īsā informed me that 'Abd al-Raḥīm ibn Zayd al-'Ammī informed me from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""Do not mention me in three situations: at the tasmiyah of food, at the time of slaughter, and at the time of sneezing."" This ḥadīth is munqaṭi' (disconnected). Both 'Abd al-Raḥīm and his father are weak (ḍa'īf). Sulaymān ibn 'Īsā al-Sijzī is counted among those who fabricate ḥadīth. Had Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā known his condition, he would not have permitted narration from him. This is what our shaykh, Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, may Allah have mercy upon him, mentioned. And Abū Aḥmad ibn 'Adī al-Ḥāfiẓ also attributed him to ḥadīth fabrication, as informed by Abū Sa'd al-Mālinī from him. And Abū Sa'd al-Mālinī informed us that Abū Aḥmad ibn 'Adī informed us, saying: I heard Muḥammad ibn Ḥammād saying: Al-Sa'dī — who is Ibrāhīm ibn Ya'qūb al-Jawzajānī — said: ""Sulaymān ibn 'Īsā, who narrates the ādāb of Sufyān, is an outright liar."" [Ḍa'īf jiddan]"
19187,"(19181) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Ismā'īl ibn Aḥmad informed us that Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Qutaybah informed us that Ḥarmalah informed us that Ibn Wahb informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū 'Abdillāh informed us that Aḥmad ibn Ja'far informed us that 'Abdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal informed me, my father informed me, that Hārūn ibn Ma'rūf informed us that Ibn Wahb said: Ḥaywah informed me that Abū Ṣakhr informed me from Yazīd ibn Qusayṭ, from 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from 'Ā'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) ordered a horned ram — black-footed, black-bellied, and black-eyed — to be brought. It was brought to him so that he could sacrifice it. He said: ""O 'Ā'ishah, bring the knife and sharpen it with a stone."" She did so. Then he took it and took the ram, laid it down, and slaughtered it. Then he said: ""Bismillah. O Allah, accept from Muḥammad and the family of Muḥammad, and from the Ummah of Muḥammad."" Then he sacrificed it.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Hārūn ibn Ma'rūf. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19188,"(19182) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us that Muḥammad ibn 'Abd al-Ḥakam al-Qiṭrī informed us that Sa'īd ibn Manṣūur informed us that Ya'qūb ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān informed us from 'Amr ibn Abi 'Amr, from al-Muṭṭalib, from Jābir ibn 'Abdillāh, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I witnessed the sacrifice with the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) at the muṣallā (prayer ground). When he had delivered his sermon and descended from his pulpit, a ram was brought to him. He slaughtered it and said: ""Bismillah, and Allah is the greatest. This is on my behalf and on behalf of those among my Ummah who have not sacrificed."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]"
19189,"(19183) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us that Abū Zur'ah al-Dimashqī informed us that Aḥmad ibn Khālid al-Wahbī informed us from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq.

(Ḥ) And Abū 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us that Abū Dāwūd informed us that Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā al-Rāzī informed us that 'Īsā informed us that Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb, from Abī 'Ayyāsh, from Jābir ibn 'Abdillāh, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) slaughtered on the Day of Slaughter two horned, black-and-white, castrated rams. When he turned them (toward the qiblah), he said: ""Verily, I have directed my face toward Him who created the heavens and the earth, upon the religion of Ibrāhīm, as a ḥanīf, and I am not among the polytheists. Verily, my prayer, my sacrifice, my life, and my death are for Allah, the Lord of the worlds. He has no partner, and by that I am commanded, and I am among the Muslims. O Allah, from You and for You, on behalf of Muḥammad and his Ummah. Bismillah, and Allah is the greatest."" Then he slaughtered them.

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of 'Īsā ibn Yūnus. And in the narration of al-Wahbī: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) slaughtered two rams on the Day of 'Īd. When he turned them, he said the supplication, then said: ""O Allah, from You and for You, on behalf of Muḥammad and his Ummah."" He mentioned the name (of Allah) and slaughtered.

And Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, and said in the ḥadīth: He turned them toward the qiblah when he slaughtered. And it is also related from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq from Yazīd ibn Khālid ibn Abī 'Imrān, from Abī 'Ayyāsh, from Jābir, may Allah be pleased with him.

Al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy upon him, said: And it has been narrated from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) through a chain that is not established like it, that he sacrificed two rams. In one of them, after mentioning Allah, he said: ""O Allah, on behalf of Muḥammad and the family of Muḥammad."" And in the other: ""O Allah, on behalf of Muḥammad and the Ummah of Muḥammad."" [Ḍa'īf. Previously referenced under no. 19033]"
19190,"(19184) The shaykh said: He intended by this what Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Burhān informed us in Baghdad, that Abū al-Ḥasan 'Alī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī informed me, that Jāmi' ibn Sāwādah informed us, that Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥusayn ibn Dīnār informed us, that Sufyān informed us.

(Ḥ) And 'Alī ibn Aḥmad ibn 'Abdān informed us that Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Lakhmī informed us that Ibn Abi Maryam informed us that al-Firyābī informed us, from Sufyān, from 'Abdullāh, from Muḥammad ibn 'Aqīl, from Abī Salamah, from 'Ā'ishah, or from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with both of them, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) used to sacrifice two horned, castrated rams. He would begin with one of them and say: ""Bismillah, and Allah is the greatest. O Allah, from You and for You, on behalf of Muḥammad and his Ummah — those who bear witness to Your Oneness and bear witness to my conveyance (of the Message)."" Then he would slaughter the other and say: ""Bismillah, and Allah is the greatest. O Allah, from You and for You, on behalf of Muḥammad and the family of Muḥammad.""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Ibn Burhān. And in the narration of Ibn 'Abdān: The Prophet (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), when he would sacrifice, would purchase two fat, horned, black-and-white, castrated rams. He would slaughter one of them on behalf of his Ummah — those who bear witness to His Oneness and bear witness to his conveyance (of the Message) — and the other on behalf of Muḥammad and the family of Muḥammad. Thus narrated a group from Sufyān al-Thawrī.

And Zuhayr ibn Muḥammad narrated it from 'Abdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn 'Aqīl, from 'Alī ibn al-Ḥusayn, from Abī Rāfi', may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). And Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated it from 'Abdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn 'Aqīl, from 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn Jābir, from his father, from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). Al-Bukhārī said: Perhaps he heard from these (narrators).

The shaykh said: And in what we have mentioned before his ḥadīth is sufficient. [Ḍa'īf]"
19191,"(19185) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Zakariyya al-'Anbari narrated to us — Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Salam narrated to us — Ishaq informed us — Jarir narrated from al-A'mash and Mansur from Abu Zubayyan from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: I said to him regarding the words of Allah, the Most High: **""And the camels and cattle We have appointed for you as among the symbols of Allah; for you therein is good. So mention the name of Allah upon them when lined up""** [al-Hajj 36] — he said: When you intend to sacrifice the she-camel, then set it upright and say: **Allahu Akbar, Allahu Akbar. Allahumma minka wa laka.** Then invoke the name and then sacrifice it. He (Ibn 'Abbas) said: I said: And I say that regarding the udhiyah (sacrificial animal)? He said: And the udhiyah. [Da'if — previously referenced as no. 14009]"
19192,"(19186) Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Ardastani informed us — Abu Nasr al-'Iraqi narrated to us — Sufyan ibn Muhammad narrated to us — 'Ali ibn al-Hasan narrated to us — 'Abd Allah ibn al-Walid narrated to us — Sufyan narrated to me — Abu Bakr al-Zubaydi narrated from 'Asim ibn Shurayb, who said: 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, was brought a ram on the Day of Sacrifice, and he slaughtered it saying: **Bismillah, Allahumma minka wa laka wa min Muhammadin laka.** Then he was commanded to give it in charity, so he did. Then another ram was brought to him, and he slaughtered it saying: **Bismillah, Allahumma minka wa laka wa min 'Aliyyin laka.** He then said: Bring me a platter from it and give the rest in charity. [Sahih]"
19193,"(19187) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Dawri narrated to us — Malik ibn Isma'il al-Nahdi narrated to us — Sharik narrated from Abu al-Hasan from al-Hakam ibn 'Utayba from Hanish ibn al-Harith, who said: 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, used to sacrifice a ram on behalf of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, and a ram on his own behalf. We said to him: O Commander of the Faithful, do you sacrifice on behalf of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him? He said: Indeed, the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, commanded me to sacrifice on his behalf forever, so I sacrifice on his behalf forever. Abu Dawud narrated it from 'Uthman ibn Abi Shayba from Sharik — Sharik ibn 'Abd Allah was alone in narrating it with this chain. And if it is established, it indicates the permissibility of sacrificing on behalf of a Muslim who has departed from the abode of this world. As for sacrificing on behalf of what is in the womb, al-Shafi'i said: No sacrifice is offered for what is in the belly. [Da'if]"
19194,(19188) Abu Ahmad al-Mihrajani informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Ja'far al-Muzakki narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-'Abdi narrated to us — Ibn Bukayr narrated to us — Malik narrated from Nafi': that 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar would not sacrifice on behalf of what was in a woman's womb. [Da'if]
19195,"(19189) Abu Ahmad al-Mihrajani informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Ja'far al-Muzakki narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us — Ibn Bukayr narrated to us — Malik narrated from Nafi': that 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, once offered a sacrifice in Madinah. Nafi' said: He commanded me to buy him a horned ram. So I bought one that was horned, and he ordered me to slaughter it on the Day of Sacrifice in the prayer ground of the people. Nafi' said: I did so, and the ram was carried to 'Abd Allah, and he shaved his head when the ram was slaughtered, for he was ill and did not attend the 'Eid with the people. Nafi' said: 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to say: Shaving the head is not obligatory upon one who sacrifices, provided he is not performing Hajj — and yet 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar himself did it. [Sahih]"
19196,"(19190) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Asbahani informed us — Abu Muhammad ibn Hayyan, Abu al-Shaykh, informed us — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us — 'Ali ibn 'Isa al-Alghaf al-Makhrami narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Salama narrated from Abu 'Abd al-Rahim from al-Jahm ibn Jarud from Salim ibn 'Abd Allah from his father: that 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, had been gifted a she-camel for which he had been given three hundred dinars, and he wished to sell it and purchase sacrificial animals with its price. He asked the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, about that, and he commanded him to sacrifice it and not to sell it. Thus he said: a she-camel. [Sahih]"
19197,"(19191) Abu Bakr al-Ardastani narrated to us — Abu Nasr al-'Iraqi narrated to us — Sufyan ibn Muhammad narrated to us — 'Ali ibn al-Hasan narrated to us — 'Abd Allah ibn al-Walid narrated to us — Sufyan narrated to us — Zuhayr ibn Abi Thabit narrated from Mughira ibn Hadhf al-'Absi, who said: We were with 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, in al-Rahba, when a man from Hamdan came driving a cow along with its calf. He said: I purchased it to sacrifice with it, and it has given birth. He ('Ali) said: Do not drink of its milk save what is surplus to the needs of its calf. And when the Day of Sacrifice arrives, sacrifice it — it and its calf — on behalf of seven people."
19198,"(19192) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us — Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us — Muhammad ibn 'Ali al-Warraq narrated to us — 'Ubayd Allah ibn Musa narrated to us — Sufyan narrated from Jabir.

(And) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan both informed us — they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Khalid ibn Khal narrated to us — Ahmad ibn Khalid al-Wahbi narrated to us — Isra'il narrated from Jabir from Muhammad, who is ibn Qaraza, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I purchased a sheep to sacrifice with it, but a wolf took its hindquarter. I asked the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, and he said: **Sacrifice with it.**

In the narration of Sufyan: We purchased a ram to sacrifice with it, but a wolf severed its hindquarter or a part of its hindquarter. I asked the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, and he commanded me to sacrifice with it.

And its meaning was narrated by Shu'ba ibn al-Hajjaj and Sharik ibn 'Abd Allah from Jabir al-Ju'fi — except that Jabir is not relied upon as an authority. [Hasan]"
19199,"(19193) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran al-'Adl in Baghdad informed us — Abu Ja'far al-Razzaz and Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar said — Sa'dan ibn Nasr narrated to us — Abu Mu'awiya narrated to us — al-Hajjaj ibn Arta'a narrated from a shaykh from the people of Madinah from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: **There is no harm in sacrificing an animal whose tail has been cut off.** And this is an abridged version of the first hadith. For Hammad ibn Salama narrated it from al-Hajjaj from 'Atiyya from Abu Sa'id: that a man asked the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, about a sheep whose tail had been cut off by a wolf — could he sacrifice with it? He said: **Sacrifice with it.** [Da'if]"
19200,"(19194) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakki narrated to us — Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us — Ja'far ibn 'Awn informed us — Mis'ar narrated from Abu Husayn: that Ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, saw among his sacrificial animals a she-camel that was one-eyed. He said: If the defect occurred after you purchased it, then proceed with it (as a sacrifice); but if it was defective before you purchased it, then exchange it. [Da'if]"
19201,"(19195) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham narrated to us; Abū al-Yamān informed us; Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah narrated, saying: Nāfiʿ said that Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) used to say: ""Any man who offers a sacrificial animal and it goes lost — if it was a vowed (nadhr) sacrifice, he must replace it; and if it was a voluntary (taṭawwuʿ) offering, then if he wishes he may replace it, and if he wishes he may leave it."" This is how Mālik narrated it from Nāfiʿ as a mawqūf (stopped) narration, while ʿAbdullāh ibn ʀmir al-Aslamī narrated it from Nāfiʿ as a marfūʿ (raised) narration, and the correct view is that it is mawqūf. [Ḥasan]"
19202,"(19196) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-ʿAdl informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; ʿUbaydullāh ibn Muʿādh narrated to us; my father narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from Tamīm ibn Ḥuwayṣ, meaning the Egyptian, who said: ""I purchased a sheep in Minā for sacrifice, and it went lost. So I asked Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) about that, and he said: 'It will not harm you.'""

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""However, if he finds it after having obligated it (as a sacrifice), he should slaughter it, even if the days of slaughter have passed, just as is done with the badanah (camels) of hady (pilgrimage sacrifice)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19203,"(19197) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us; Ibn Nājiyah narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Shuʿayb narrated to us; Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us; Saʿd ibn Saʿīd narrated from al-Qāsim, meaning Ibn Muḥammad, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she sent forth two sacrificial camels and they went lost. So Ibn al-Zubayr sent to her two sacrificial camels in their place, and she slaughtered them. Then she found the first two and slaughtered them as well. She then said: ""This is the Sunnah regarding sacrificial camels (al-budn)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19204,"(19198) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us; Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us — and he mentioned it (i.e., the same hadith as above)."
19205,"(19199) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad from his father, from ʿAlī ibn Ḥusayn, that he said to his steward who had gathered his palm produce by night: ""Did you not know that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) prohibited the gathering of palm produce by night, and the ṣawm (fasting) of the night"" — or he said, ""and the ḥaṣād (reaping) of the night."" Sufyān said: ""It is said: so that it may be done during the day and the poor may be present."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19206,"(19200) And Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Isfarāʾīnī informed us with it; Bishr ibn Aḥmad informed us; Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Naṣr narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated it with its meaning, but he did not mention al-ṣirām (gathering of fruit) nor al-ḥaṣād (reaping). Sufyān said: ""They asked Jaʿfar about performing the sacrifice (al-uḍḥiyah) by night, and he said: 'No.'"" Sufyān said: ""This is regarding the situation of the poor (al-masākīn)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19207,"(19201) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us; Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated from Ashʿath ibn ʿAbd al-Malik from al-Ḥasan, who said: ""The gathering of palm produce by night, the reaping by night, and the sacrifice by night were all prohibited. That was only due to the severity of the people's condition — a man would do it at night, so he was prohibited from it. Then permission was granted for it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19208,"(19202) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from al-Zuhrī from Abī ʿUbayd, the freedman of Ibn Azhar, who said: ""I witnessed the ʿĪd with ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him), and I heard him say: 'None of you should eat from his sacrificial offering after three days.'"" This is how al-Shāfiʿī narrated it from Sufyān as a mawqūf narration, while it is also narrated from the ḥadīth of Maʿmar as marfūʿ. And the ḥadīth is narrated by others from Sufyān as marfūʿ. [Ḥasan]"
19209,"(19203) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Jabbār ibn al-ʿAlāʾ al-Makkī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from al-Zuhrī from Abī ʿUbayd, who said: ""I witnessed the ʿĪd with ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him). He began with the prayer before the sermon and said: 'Indeed, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) prohibited us from eating from the meat of our sacrificial offerings after three days.'""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd al-Jabbār ibn al-ʿAlāʾ, and al-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it from the ḥadīth of Yūnus ibn Yazīd and others from al-Zuhrī as marfūʿ. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19210,"(19204) And Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us by dictation; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī from Abī ʿUbayd, the freedman of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, that he heard ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) say on the day of al-Aḍḥā: ""O people, indeed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) has prohibited you from eating from your sacrificial offerings after three days, so do not eat thereof.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd ibn Ḥumayd from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
19211,"(19205) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Ahmad ibn Ja'far informed us; 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad narrated to us; my father narrated to us; 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us; Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with both of them), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade the meat of the sacrificial animals to be eaten after three days. Salim said: Ibn 'Umar used not to eat the meat of the sacrificial animals beyond three days.

It was narrated by Muslim in his Sahih from Ibn Abi 'Umar and 'Abd ibn Humayd, from 'Abd al-Razzaq. Al-Bukhari also narrated it through another chain from al-Zuhri. [Sahih]"
19212,"(19206) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; Malik informed us.

(And) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abdullah ibn Ya'qub informed us; Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Salam narrated to us; Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us. He said: I read to Malik, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), that he forbade eating the meat of the sacrificial animals after three days. Then he said afterwards: ""Eat, take provisions, and store.""

It was narrated by Muslim in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya. [Sahih]"
19213,"(19207) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Ka'bi informed me; Muhammad ibn Ayyub informed us; Musaddad narrated to us; Yahya narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj narrated to us; 'Ata' informed us that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) say: We used not to eat from the meat of our sacrificial animals beyond three days. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) granted us permission and said: ""Eat and take provisions."" So we ate and took provisions. I said to 'Ata': Did Jabir say, ""until we came to Madinah""? He said: No.

It was narrated by al-Bukhari in his Sahih from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Muhammad ibn Hatim, from Yahya al-Qattan, and he said: ""Yes,"" instead of his saying: ""No.""

It was also narrated by Ahmad ibn Hanbal from Yahya, as Musaddad narrated it. [Sahih, agreed upon]"
19214,"(19208) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us; Abu Hamid Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Bilal informed us; Yahya ibn al-Rabi' al-Makki narrated to us; Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Amr, from 'Ata', from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: We used to take provisions from the meat of the sacrificial animals in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) to Madinah.

It was narrated by al-Bukhari from 'Ali and others, and Muslim narrated it from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, all of them from Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah. The taking of provisions to Madinah was preserved by 'Amr ibn Dinar from 'Ata', and it was also preserved by 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman from 'Ata', and it was preserved by Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiya from Abu al-Zubayr from Jabir. [Sahih, agreed upon]"
19215,"(19209) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-Fadl ibn Ibrahim informed us; Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Ishaq ibn Ibrahim informed us; 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us; Mu'awiyah ibn Salih narrated to us, from Abu al-Zahiriyyah, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from Thawban, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) slaughtered his sacrificial animal and said: ""O Thawban, prepare this sheep for us and set it right."" He said: I kept feeding him from it until we reached Madinah.

It was narrated by Muslim in his Sahih from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim. [Sahih, agreed upon]"
19216,"(19210) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr al-Qadi both informed us; they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani informed us; Abu Muharrar narrated to us; Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to me; al-Zubaydi narrated to me, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, that he narrated to him from his father, from Thawban, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family).

(And) Abu Muhammad 'Abdullah ibn Yahya al-Sukkari informed us in Baghdad; Isma'il al-Saffar informed us; al-'Abbas al-Tarqafi informed us; Muhammad ibn al-Mubarak narrated to me; Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated to me, from al-Zubaydi Muhammad ibn al-Walid, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, that he narrated to me. He said: My father narrated to us; Thawban narrated to us. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said to me: ""Set right this meat."" So I set it right. He said: He continued eating from it until he reached Madinah. Abu Muharrar added in his narration: ""During the Farewell Pilgrimage.""

It was narrated by Muslim in his Sahih from Ishaq ibn Mansur, from Abu Muharrar, and he said in it: ""During the Farewell Pilgrimage,"" and I do not consider this wording preserved. He also narrated it from 'Abdullah al-Darimi, from Muhammad ibn al-Mubarak, without this wording. [Sahih, narrated by Muslim]"
19217,"(19211) Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Dawud al-'Alawi (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us by dictation; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Khalil al-Qattan informed us; Abu al-Azhar al-Saliti informed us; Muhammad ibn Yusuf narrated to us. He said: Sufyan mentioned, from 'Alqamah ibn Murthad, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), who said: ""I had forbidden you from eating the meat of the sacrificial animals beyond three days. I only intended by that so that those of means might provide for those who have no means. So eat as you wish, and store."" [Sahih]"
19218,"(19212) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran informed us in Baghdad; Abu al-Hasan al-Misri informed us; Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us; al-Firyabi narrated to us; Sufyan narrated to us, from 'Alqamah ibn Murthad, from Sulayman ibn Buraydah, from his father, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), with its like.

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Hajjaj ibn al-Sha'ir, from Abu 'Asim, from Sufyan, as previously mentioned in the Book of Drinks."
19219,"(19213) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us; Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us; Khallad ibn Yahya narrated to us; Ma'rif narrated to me; Muharib ibn Dithar narrated to me, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""I forbade you from three things, and I now command you regarding them. I forbade you from visiting graves, so visit them, for in their visitation is a reminder. I forbade you from drinks that they are drunk in vessels of hide, so drink in every vessel, provided that you do not drink an intoxicant. And I forbade you from the meat of the sacrificial animals, that you should eat it after three days, so eat it and benefit from it in your journeys.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih through another chain from Ma'rif ibn Wasil. And this Ibn Buraydah is 'Abdullah ibn Buraydah. Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Abu Sinan Dirr ibn Murrah, from Muharib ibn Dithar, from 'Abdullah ibn Buraydah, from his father.

[Sahih, as previously mentioned]"
19220,"(19214) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us; Ibn Milhan narrated to us; Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Layth, i.e., ibn Sa'd, narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Ibn Khabbab, that Abu Sa'id ibn Malik al-Khudri returned from a journey and the meat of the sacrificial animals was presented to him. He said: ""I shall not eat it until I ask."" So he went to his brother from his mother's side, who was a Badri companion, Qatadah ibn al-Nu'man, and asked him about that. He said to him: ""A new matter has occurred after you, abrogating what was previously forbidden regarding eating the meat of the sacrificial animals after three days.""

It was narrated by al-Bukhari in his Sahih from 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf, from al-Layth. [Sahih, as previously mentioned]"
19221,"(19215) Abū al-Ḥasan — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī, may Allah have mercy on him — informed us: Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān — narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn al-Azhar al-ʿAbdī narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated to us: my father narrated to me, from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Ḥusayn — Abū Jaʿfar — and my father Isḥāq ibn Yasār narrated to me, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Khabbāb, the freedman of Banī ʿAdī, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with him, who said:

The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) had forbidden us from eating the meat of our sacrificial animals beyond three days. I went on a journey and then returned to my family. She (my wife) said: ""People have been granted a concession after that."" I did not believe her until I sent word to my brother Qatādah ibn al-Nuʿmān, who was among the people of Badr, asking him about it. He sent back to me: ""Eat your food, for she has spoken the truth. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) has granted the Muslims a concession in this matter.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 3997]"
19222,"(19216) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Isḥāq al-Baghawī al-ʿAdl in Baghdād narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us: Saʿīd ibn Iyās al-Jurayrī narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us — and the wording is his — Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Abū Mūsā Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Aʿlā narrated to us: Saʿīd, meaning al-Jurayrī, narrated to us, from Abū Naḍrah, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, may Allah be pleased with him, who said:

The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""O people of Madīnah, do not eat the meat of the sacrificial animals beyond three days."" They complained to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) that they had dependents, relatives, and servants. He said: ""Eat, feed others, set aside, and store.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19223,"(19217) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Makhlad narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Abī ʿUbayd narrated to us, from Salamah ibn al-Akwaʿ, may Allah be pleased with him, who said:

The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said on the Day of Sacrifice: ""Whoever among you has offered a sacrifice, let nothing from his sacrificial animal remain in his house after the third day."" When the following year came, they said: ""O Messenger of Allah, shall we do this year as we did last year?"" He said: ""No. Eat, feed others, and store, for that year there was hardship — or a word to that effect — and I wanted you to distribute among the people.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī ʿĀṣim al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Makhlad, and Muslim narrated it from Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr from Abī ʿĀṣim, and he said: ""and I wanted it to spread among them."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
19224,"(19218) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Zuraʿī narrated to us: Khālid al-Ḥadhāʾ narrated to us, from Abū al-Malīḥ, from Nubayshah, who said:

The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""We had forbidden you from eating the meat of your sacrificial animals beyond three days, in order that it might suffice you. Now Allah has granted abundance. So eat, store, and seek reward. Indeed, these are days of eating, drinking, and remembrance of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic.""

His word ""seek reward"" (ittajirū) — its original form is ""iʾtajirū"" — and it is on the pattern iftaʿalū. It refers to charity (ṣadaqah) through which reward is sought, and it is not from the domain of commerce (tijārah). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
19225,"(19219) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Ziyād narrated to us: Ibn Abī Uways narrated to us: my brother narrated to me, from Sulaymān, from Yaḥyā, from ʿAmrah, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said:

We used to salt the meat of the sacrificial animal and present it to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) in Madīnah. He said: ""Do not eat from it except for three days."" But it was not a strict prohibition; rather he intended that they should feed others from it. And Allah knows best.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19226,"(19220) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik narrated to us, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Wāqid ibn ʿAbdullāh, who said:

The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade eating the meat of sacrificial animals beyond three days.

ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr said: I mentioned this to ʿAmrah, and she said: ""He has spoken the truth. I heard ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, say: 'A group of Bedouin people arrived at the time of sacrifice during the era of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Store the three (days' portion) and give in charity what remains.""' She said: 'Then after that, it was said: ""O Messenger of Allah, the people have been benefiting from their sacrificial animals — they render the fat from them and make waterskins from them."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""What is that?"" or as he said. They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, you forbade eating the meat of sacrificial animals beyond three days."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""I only forbade you on account of the group that arrived at the time of sacrifice. So eat, give in charity, and store.""'""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Rūḥ from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5570]"
19227,"(19221) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān in Baghdād informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī narrated to us: Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us: al-Firyābī narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿĀbis, who informed me: my father narrated to me, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her. He said: I asked her: ""Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbid…""

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqrīʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿĀbis ibn Rabīʿah narrated to us, from his father ʿĀbis ibn Rabīʿah, who said:

It was said to ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her: ""Did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbid eating the meat of sacrificial animals beyond three days?"" She said: ""He only forbade it once, in a year when the people were hungry, so that the wealthy might feed the poor. We used to take out the shank after fifteen days and eat it."" I said: ""Why did you do that?"" She laughed and said: ""The family of Muḥammad (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) never ate their fill of wheat bread with condiment for three days until he met Allah, the Mighty and Majestic.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Kathīr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1971]"
19228,"(19222) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, informed us: al-Rabīʿ said: al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, said:

When ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, narrated that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade it on account of the arriving group, and then he said: ""Eat, give in charity, and store"" — and Jābir narrated what we have mentioned — it is obligatory upon the one who knows both commands to say: the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade it for a specific reason. When a similar situation arises, it is forbidden; and when such a situation does not exist, it is not forbidden. Or one should say: the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade it at a certain time, and then granted a concession in it afterward, and the latter command abrogates the former.

He also said: al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, said in another place: It is as though the Prophet's (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) prohibition of retaining the meat of sacrificial animals beyond three days, when the group arrived, was by way of recommendation and not by way of obligation, in light of the words of Allah, the Most High, regarding the sacrificial camels: ""Then when their sides fall down (after slaughter), eat from them and feed"" [al-Ḥajj 36]. This verse pertains to the sacrificial camels that their owners offer voluntarily.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī 5438]"
19229,"(19223) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān in Baghdād informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Wakīʿ ibn al-Jarrāḥ narrated to us: Ṭalḥah ibn ʿAmr narrated to us, from ʿAṭāʾ, regarding the words of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: ""And feed the wretched poor"" [al-Ḥajj 28], he said: ""That is the one who asks of you."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19230,"(19224) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us: Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Saʿdān narrated to us: Wakīʿ narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us, from Furāt al-Qazzāz, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, who said:

""Al-Qāniʿ"" [al-Ḥajj 28] means the one who asks (al-sāʾil), and ""al-Muʿtarr"" [al-Ḥajj 28] means the one who comes to you seeking you out but does not ask you. [Ḍaʿīf]"
19231,"(19225) And through his chain of narration from Sufyan, from Mansur, from Ibrahim and Mujahid, regarding His words {al-qāniʿa} [al-Hajj 28]: ""The one who sits in his house,"" {wa'l-muʿtarr} [al-Hajj 28]: ""The one who comes to you (seeking)."" [Sahih]"
19232,"(19226) Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah informed us; al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Nadriyy informed us; Ahmad ibn Najdah narrated to us; Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us; Hushaym narrated to us; Yunus and Mansur informed us, on the authority of al-Hasan, regarding His words {al-qāniʿa wa'l-muʿtarr} [al-Hajj 36]. He said: ""Al-Qāniʿ is the one who asks a man (for his need), and al-Muʿtarr is the one who presents himself (exposing his need) but does not ask."" [Sahih]"
19233,"(19227) He said: And Sa'id narrated to us; Hushaym narrated to us; Mughirah informed us, from Ibrahim, who said: ""One of them is the passerby, and the other is the one who asks."" [Sahih]"
19234,"(19228) He said: And Sa'id narrated to us; Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, who said: {Al-qāniʿa} [al-Hajj 28] means ""the one who asks (the beggar)."" [Sahih]"
19235,"(19229) Abu al-Hasan Ali ibn Muhammad ibn Ali ibn al-Saqqa' al-Isfarayini informed us; Abu Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Battah al-Isbahani narrated to us; Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Zakariyya narrated to us; Sa'id ibn Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Umawi narrated to us; Muslim ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, regarding His words {Fa'kulu minha wa't'imu al-ba'isa al-faqir} [al-Hajj 28]. He said: ""Al-Ba'is is the one who asks with his hand when he asks. And al-Qāniʿ is the covetous one who covets your sacrificial meat from among your neighbors."" He said: {Wa'l-muʿtarr} [al-Hajj 36] is ""the one who comes to you by himself and does not ask you, but presents himself before you."" And something similar has been narrated from Ibn Abbas. [Sahih]"
19236,"(19230) Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah informed us; Abu al-Fadl ibn Khamirawayh al-Harawi informed us; Ahmad ibn Najdah narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us; Zuhayr narrated to us; Qabus ibn Abi Zabyan narrated to us, that his father narrated to him. He said: We said to Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both: ""What do you say about al-Qāniʿ and al-Muʿtarr? What is al-Qāniʿ and what is al-Muʿtarr?"" He said: ""As for {al-qāniʿa} [al-Hajj 28], it is the one who is content with what you send to him in his house. And {wa'l-muʿtarr} [al-Hajj 28] is the one who comes to you (exposing his need)."" [Da'if]"
19237,"(19231) Abu Ja'far Kamil ibn Ahmad al-Mustamli informed us; Bishr ibn Ahmad al-Isfarayini narrated to us; Dawud ibn al-Husayn al-Bayhaqi narrated to us; Yahya ibn Yahya informed us; Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim.

(And) Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Musa narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ayyub informed us; Musaddad and Abdullah ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us; they said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Karim al-Jazari, from Mujahid, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, commanded me to stand over his sacrificial camels and to divide their skins and their coverings (saddlecloths). And he commanded me not to give the slaughterer anything from them, and he said: 'We shall give him (his wages) from our own resources.'""

In the narration of Abu Khaythamah: ""And that I give in charity their meat, their skins, and their saddlecloths, and that I not give the slaughterer's wages from them."" He said: ""We shall give him from our own resources.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya and from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah and others. And al-Bukhari extracted it through another chain from Abd al-Karim. [Da'if]"
19238,"(19232) Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Muhammad Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Adl in Baghdad narrated to us; Yahya ibn Ja'far ibn al-Zibriqan narrated to us; Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us; Abdullah ibn Ayyash ibn Abbas narrated to us; Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj narrated to us, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Whoever sells the skin of his sacrificial animal, he has no sacrifice (accepted from him)."" [Sahih. Agreed upon]"
19239,"(19233) Abu Uthman Sa'id ibn Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn Abdan al-Naysaburi informed us; Abu Abdullah ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Hamid ibn Abi Hamid narrated to us; Ishaq ibn Sulayman narrated to us; Malik ibn Anas narrated to us.

(And) Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Ja'far ibn Sawwar narrated to us; Qutaybah narrated to us, from Malik, from al-Zubayr, from Jabir ibn Abdullah al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""We sacrificed with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, at al-Hudaybiyyah: a camel on behalf of seven persons, and a cow on behalf of seven persons.""

The wording is that of the hadith of Qutaybah. Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya and Qutaybah ibn Sa'id. [Da'if]"
19240,"(19234) Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Ayyub informed us; al-Hasan ibn Ali ibn Ziyad informed us; Ahmad ibn Yunus narrated to us; Zuhayr narrated to us; Abu al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""We went out with the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, entering into ihram for Hajj. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, commanded us to share in the camels and the cattle, every seven of us in one sacrificial camel (badanah).""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Ahmad ibn Yunus. [Sahih. Muslim 1318]"
19241,"(19235) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — that is, al-Shaybani — informed us; Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Yahya narrated from Ibn Jurayj; Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) during Hajj and 'Umrah, and we shared in a camel sacrifice among seven persons. A man asked him: ""Can a cow be shared in?"" He replied: ""It is nothing but from the badan (camels)."" And Jabir was present at Hudaybiyyah. He said: ""We shared, every seven of us, in one badanah (sacrificial camel), and on that day we slaughtered seventy badanah.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn Hatim from Yahya ibn Sa'id. [Sahih]"
19242,"(19236) Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi — informed us; Abu Sahl ibn Ziyad al-Qattan informed us; Ishaq ibn al-Hasan ibn Maymun al-Harbi narrated to us; 'Affan narrated to us; Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Qays ibn Sa'd narrated from 'Ata', from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""A cow is for seven, and a badanah is for seven.""

And the consensus of these Imams from Abu al-Zubayr from Jabir, and then the narration of 'Ata' from Jabir that the badanah is for seven is more deserving of consideration than the narration of al-Thawri from Abu al-Zubayr from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding the badanah being for ten.

And it has been narrated from 'Ali, Hudhayfah, Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari, and 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with them all) that they said: ""A cow is for seven."" [Sahih]"
19243,"(19237) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed me; Abu Bakr ibn 'Abdullah informed me; al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us; Abu Bakr ibn Abaybah narrated to us.

(And) Abu al-Walid informed me; 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Rafi' narrated to us — both saying: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us from Mu'awiyah ibn Salih; Abu al-Zahiriyyah — Hudhayr ibn Kurayb — narrated to us from Jubayr ibn Nufayr ibn Malik al-Hadrami from Thawban (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) sacrificed his udhiyah during a journey and then said: ""O Thawban, prepare its meat."" So I continued to prepare it until we arrived in Madinah.

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abaybah and Muhammad ibn Rafi'. [Sahih]"
19244,"(19238) Abu Hamid — Ahmad ibn 'Ali ibn Ahmad al-Hafiz al-Isfara'ini — narrated to us there; Abu 'Ali — Zahir ibn Ahmad — informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Ziyad al-Naysaburi narrated to us; Abu al-Azhar narrated to us; Abu al-Mughirah narrated to us; Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated to us; Sulayman ibn Musa narrated to me from Jubayr ibn Mut'im (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), who said: ""All of 'Arafat is a place of standing, and hasten past 'Urayniyyat. And all of Muzdalifah is a place of standing, and hasten past Muhassir. And all the paths of Mina are places of sacrifice, and all the days of al-Tashriq are days of slaughter."""
19245,"(19239) He said: And Abu Bakr narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Yusuf narrated to us; Abu al-Yaman narrated to us; Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated it with its like. This is the sahih version, and it is mursal."
19246,"(19240) And it has been narrated as Abu Sa'id al-Malini informed us; Abu Ahmad ibn 'Adi al-Hafiz informed us; Ahmad ibn al-Hasan ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Sufi narrated to us; Abu Nasr al-Tammar narrated to us; Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated from Sulayman ibn Musa from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Husayn from Jubayr ibn Mut'im (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""'Arafat is a place of standing, and hasten past 'Uranah. And all of Muzdalifah is a place of standing, and hasten past Muhassir. And all the paths of Mina are places of sacrifice, and in all the days of al-Tashriq there is slaughter.""

And it was narrated by Suwayd ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz — and he is da'if (weak) according to some of the scholars of transmission — from Sa'id."
19247,"(19241) As Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Isbahani informed us; 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz informed us; Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us. And Abu Hamid al-Hafiz informed us; Zahir ibn Ahmad informed us; Abu Bakr al-Naysaburi narrated to us — both saying: Ahmad ibn Mansur narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Bukayr al-Hadrami narrated to us; Suwayd ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated from Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz al-Tanukhi from Sulayman ibn Musa from Nafi' ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im from his father (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""All the days of al-Tashriq are days of slaughter."" [Da'if]"
19248,"(19242) And it has been narrated from another route from Sulayman, as Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith informed us; 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Bakr al-Naysaburi narrated to us; Ahmad ibn 'Isa al-Khashshab narrated to us; 'Amr ibn Abi Salamah narrated to us; Abu Mu'ayd narrated to us from Sulayman ibn Musa that 'Amr ibn Dinar narrated to him from Jubayr ibn Mut'im (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""All the days of al-Tashriq are days of slaughter."" [Da'if]"
19249,"(19243) And 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd informed us; al-Harith ibn Abi Usamah narrated to us; Ruh ibn 'Ubadah narrated from Ibn Jurayj; 'Amr ibn Dinar informed me that Nafi' ibn Jubayr ibn Mut'im (may Allah be pleased with him) informed him from a man among the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) — Nafi' named him, but I have forgotten his name — that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said to a man from Ghifar: ""Stand and announce that none shall enter Paradise except a believer, and that these are days of eating and drinking"" — the days of Mina. Sulayman ibn Musa added: ""and of slaughter."" That is, he said: ""days of slaughter."" Ibn Jurayj says it. [Da'if]"
19250,"(19244) And Mu'awiyah ibn Yahya al-Sadafi narrated it from al-Zuhri from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, once from Abu Sa'id and once from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with them both), from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family): ""All the days of al-Tashriq are days of slaughter.""

Abu Sa'id al-Malini informed us it; Abu Ahmad ibn 'Adi al-Hafiz informed us; 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Salm narrated to us; Duhaym narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Shu'ayb narrated to us; Mu'awiyah ibn Yahya narrated it and said: from Abu Sa'id. [Sahih]"
19251,"(19245) And Abū Saʿd informed us — Abū Aḥmad informed us — Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us — Ḥumayd narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Shuʿayb narrated from al-Ṣadafī, who mentioned it and said: from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him.

Abū Aḥmad said: ""And this is erroneous — 'from al-Zuhrī, from Saʿīd, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him' — and it is erroneous — 'from al-Zuhrī, from Ibn al-Musayyib, from Abū Saʿīd' — both are not preserved; no one narrates them other than al-Ṣadafī.""

The shaykh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: ""And al-Ṣadafī is weak (ḍaʿīf) and cannot be used as evidence."""
19252,"(19246) Abū Ḥāmid informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Zāhir ibn Aḥmad narrated to us — Abū Bakr ibn Ziyād al-Naysābūrī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated from Ṭalḥah ibn ʿAmr al-Ḥaḍramī, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: ""The sacrifice (al-aḍḥā) is for three days after the Day of Sacrifice (yawm al-naḥr)."" [Weak (ḍaʿīf)]"
19253,"He said: And Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us — Rūḥ informed us — Ḥammād narrated from Maṭar that al-Ḥasan and ʿAṭāʾ both said: ""The sacrifice may be offered until the last of the Days of Tashrīq."" [Weak (ḍaʿīf)]"
19254,"He said: And Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, that is al-Ṣaghānī, narrated to us — Rūḥ narrated — Ibn Jurayj said: ʿAṭāʾ said: ""The sacrifice is offered during the Days of Tashrīq."" [Authentic (ṣaḥīḥ)]"
19255,"He said: And Muḥammad narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Shuʿbah narrated from Qatādah, from al-Ḥasan, who said: ""The sacrifice (al-aḍḥā) is for three days after the Day of Sacrifice (yawm al-naḥr)."" [Authentic (ṣaḥīḥ)]"
19256,"He said: And Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, that is al-Ṣaghānī, narrated to us — Rūḥ narrated — Ibn Jurayj said: ʿAṭāʾ said: ""The sacrifice is offered during all the Days of Minā and on the Day of the Final Departure (yawm al-nafar al-ākhir)."" [Authentic (ṣaḥīḥ)]"
19257,"He said: And Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us — Haysh ibn Khārijah narrated to us — Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated from ʿAmr ibn Muhājir that ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz said: ""The sacrifice (al-aḍḥā) is on the Day of Sacrifice (yawm al-naḥr) and the three days after it."" [Authentic (ṣaḥīḥ)]"
19258,"He said: And Ibrāhīm ibn Hānīʾ narrated to me — al-Ḥakam ibn Mūsā narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn Ḥamzah narrated from al-Nuʿmān, from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā, that he said: ""The sacrifice (al-naḥr) is for four days."" Then Makhūl said: ""He spoke the truth."" [Authentic (ṣaḥīḥ)]"
19259,"Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Burshān al-ʿAdl in Baghdad informed us — Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us — ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham narrated to us — Abū al-Yamān informed us — Shuʿayb said: Nāfiʾ said: Abū Salamah asked ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, one day after the Day of Sacrifice: ""It has occurred to me that I should offer a sacrifice."" Ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: ""Whoever wishes may offer the sacrifice today, and tomorrow if Allah wills."" [Authentic (ṣaḥīḥ)]"
19260,"And Abū Aḥmad — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Mihrajānī — informed us — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us — Ibn Bukayr narrated to us — Mālik narrated from Nāfiʾ that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to say: ""The sacrifice (al-aḍḥā) is for two days after the Day of Sacrifice (yawm al-aḍḥā).""

He said: And Mālik narrated that it reached him that ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, used to say: ""The sacrifice (al-aḍḥā) is for two days after the Day of Sacrifice (yawm al-aḍḥā)."" [Authentic (ṣaḥīḥ)]"
19261,"Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah informed us; Abu 'Amr ibn Nujayd informed us; Abu Muslim informed us; 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Hammad narrated to us; Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated from Qatadah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The slaughtering after the sacrifice is for two days. [Sahih]"
19262,"Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Isbahani informed us; 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz informed us — and Abu Hamid Ahmad ibn 'Ali al-Hafiz informed us; Zahir ibn Ahmad informed us — both saying: Abu Bakr al-Naysaburi narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Sa'id ibn Sakhr narrated to us; Hibban ibn Hilal narrated to us; Aban ibn Yazid narrated to us; Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim; Abu Salamah and Sulayman ibn Yasar narrated to me that it reached them that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: The sacrificial animals are until the end of the month for whoever wishes to delay that.

The wording is that of the narration of al-Isbahani. And in the narration of Abu Hamid: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: The sacrificial animals are until the crescent of al-Muharram for whoever wishes to delay that.

Abu Dawud narrated it in al-Marasil from Musa ibn Isma'il from Aban. [Da'if]"
19263,"Abu Hamid Ahmad ibn 'Ali al-Hafiz informed us; Zahir ibn Ahmad informed us; Abu Bakr al-Naysaburi narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Muslim narrated to us; Mu'alla ibn Mansur narrated to us; 'Abbad ibn al-'Awwam narrated to us; Yahya ibn Sa'id said: I heard Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf say: Indeed, the Muslims would purchase the udhiyah, fatten it, and slaughter it after al-Adha at the end of Dhu al-Hijjah.

The hadith of Abu Salamah and Sulayman is mursal, and the hadith of Abu Umamah is a report from unnamed persons.

Abu Ishaq al-Marwazi (may Allah have mercy upon him) said in al-Sharh: It has been narrated in some reports that the udhiyah is until the beginning of al-Muharram. If this is authentic, then the matter extends to the first of al-Muharram. If it is not authentic, then the authentic report is: The days of Mina are the days of sacrifice. Upon this, al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy upon him) based his ruling. The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: In both of them there is consideration — this due to its being mursal, and what has preceded due to the difference of narrators in it from Sulayman ibn Musa. The hadith of Sulayman ibn Musa — the foremost of them is that it should be acted upon. And Allah knows best. [Sahih]"
19264,"Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abd Allah al-Saffar informed us; Isma'il ibn Ishaq narrated to us; Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us; Hammad ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyub, from Muhammad, from a man called Salaman, who raised it (to the Prophet), saying: With the boy is his 'aqiqah, so shed blood on his behalf and remove the harm from him.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from 'Arim from Hammad ibn Zayd, and he did not say ""he raised it."" He said: And Hajjaj said: Hammad — meaning Ibn Salamah — narrated to us; Ayyub, Qatadah, Hisham, and Habib narrated from Ibn Sirin from Salaman (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family)."
19265,"Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abd Allah al-Saffar informed us; Isma'il ibn Ishaq narrated to us; Hajjaj ibn Minhal narrated to us; Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Ayyub, Qatadah, and Habib narrated from Muhammad.

(And) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us; Khalaf ibn 'Amr al-'Ukbari narrated to us; 'Abd al-A'la ibn Hammad narrated to us; Hammad narrated from Yunus, Ayyub, Hisham, Habib, Qatadah, and others, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Salaman ibn 'Amir al-Dabbi (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: With the boy is his 'aqiqah, so shed blood on his behalf and remove the harm from him.

Al-Faqih (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: It has been narrated from al-Thawri from Ayyub likewise, in a well-authenticated form. [Sahih. Bukhari 5471]"
19266,"Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Dawud al-'Alawi (may Allah have mercy upon him) informed us orally; Abu Hamid ibn al-Sharqi narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Yusuf al-Sulami narrated to us; Abu Hufayfah narrated to us; Sufyan narrated from Ayyub, from Ibn Sirin, from Salaman ibn 'Amir al-Dabbi (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: With the boy is his 'aqiqah, so shed blood on his behalf and remove the harm from him.

Al-Bukhari also cited as evidence the narration of Jarir ibn Hazim from Ayyub likewise, in a well-authenticated form. Al-Bukhari said: And Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated from Ibn Sirin from Salaman — his own statement. [Sahih. Preceded before it.]"
19267,"Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri informed us; al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us; Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us; Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to us, who said: Salaman said: The 'aqiqah is with the child, so shed blood on his behalf and remove the harm from him.

Muhammad said: I was eager to know what ""remove the harm from him"" meant, but I could not find anyone to inform me. [Sahih]"
19268,"Al-Faqih (may Allah have mercy upon him) said: Hisham narrated from al-Hasan al-Basri that he used to say: Removing the harm is shaving the head.

Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri informed us; al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us; Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us; Yazid ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Sirin narrated to us, who said: Salaman said: The 'aqiqah is with the child, so shed blood on his behalf and remove the harm from him.

Muhammad said: I was eager to know what ""remove the harm from him"" meant, but I could not find anyone to inform me. [Sahih]

Abu 'Ali al-Rawdhbari informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Yahya ibn Khalaf narrated to us; 'Abd al-A'la narrated to us; Hisham narrated from al-Hasan, and he mentioned it. Al-Bukhari said: And more than one narrated from 'Asim and Hisham from Hafsah bint Sirin from al-Rabab from Salaman (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). [Sahih]"
19269,"Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Sukkari informed us in Baghdad; Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us; Ahmad ibn Mansur informed us; 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us; Hisham ibn Hassan narrated from Hafsah bint Sirin, from al-Rabab, from Salaman ibn 'Amir (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: With the boy is his 'aqiqah, so shed blood on his behalf and remove the harm from him. [Sahih]"
19270,"Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi informed us; Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Duhaym al-Shaybani informed us; Ibrahim ibn Ishaq al-Qadi narrated to us; Ja'far ibn 'Awn narrated from Sa'id, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Samurah ibn Jundub (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: Every boy is held in pledge for his 'aqiqah; it is slaughtered for him on his seventh day, his head is shaved, and he is named. [Sahih]"
19271,"(19265) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Kāmil al-Qāḍī Abū Bakr narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad al-Raqāshī narrated to us: Quraysh ibn Anas narrated to us: Ḥabīb ibn al-Shahīd narrated to us. He said: Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn said to me: ""Ask al-Ḥasan from whom he heard the hadith of the ʿAqīqah."" So I asked him, and he said: ""From Samurah ibn Jundub, may Allah be pleased with him."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī al-Aswad from Quraysh."
19272,"(19266) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Aḥmad al-Fārisī narrated to us: Abū Ḥātim narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Sharḥīl narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ḥamzah narrated to us. He said: I said to ʿAṭāʾ al-Khurāsānī: ""What is the meaning of one who leaves his ʿAqīqah as a pledge (mortgaged)?"" He said: ""He is deprived of the intercession of his child."" al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, said: It was narrated from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, that he performed the ʿAqīqah for al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn, and had their heads shaved, and Fāṭimah, may Allah be pleased with her, gave in charity silver equal to the weight of their hair. [Ḥasan]"
19273,"(19267) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmrūyah narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: Abū Maʿmar ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr al-Minqarī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated to us: Ayyūb narrated from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with them both: that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, performed the ʿAqīqah for al-Ḥasan with a ram and for al-Ḥusayn with a ram. Abū Dāwūd narrated it in Kitāb al-Sunan from Abū Maʿmar. [Munkar]"
19274,"(19268) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū ʿUthmān ibn ʿAbdān, and Abū Ṣādiq Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-ʿAṭṭār informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated from Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim from Qatādah from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, performed the ʿAqīqah for al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn with two rams. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19275,"(19269) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Mālik narrated from Rabīʿah ibn Abī ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Ḥusayn, who said: Fāṭimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, weighed the hair of al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn and gave in charity silver equal to its weight. [Ḍaʿīf]"
19276,"(19270) He said: And Mālik narrated to us from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd: that the ʿAqīqah was performed on behalf of al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn, the two sons of ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with them. And it was narrated from Rabīʿah from Anas, but it is not preserved (as authentic). [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19277,"(19271) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, Abū ʿUthmān ibn ʿAbdān, and Abū Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqriʾ informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Sinān al-Qazzāz narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bukayr narrated to me: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Lahīʿah narrated to me: ʿUmārah ibn Ghazīyah narrated from Rabīʿah ibn Abī ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from Anas ibn Mālik, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, ordered that the heads of al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn, the two sons of ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with them, be shaved on the seventh day. So their heads were shaved, and he gave in charity silver equal to its weight, and he did not find—or did not renew—a slaughtering. And it was narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd from ʿAmrah from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, but it is not preserved (as authentic). [Ḍaʿīf]"
19278,"(19272) Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed me by way of ijāzah: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān and Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥārith ibn Miskīn informed us: My father informed me: Ibn Wahb informed me: Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr, meaning al-Yāfiʿī, narrated from Ibn Jurayj from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd from ʿAmrah from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, performed the ʿAqīqah for al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn on the seventh day, named them, and ordered that the harm (dirt) be removed from their heads. Abū Aḥmad said: ""I do not know of anyone who narrated it from Ibn Jurayj with this isnād other than Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Yāfiʿī and ʿAbd al-Majīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Rāwād."" The Faqīh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: And ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥarrir narrated a munkar hadith regarding the ʿAqīqah of the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, on his own behalf. [Ḍaʿīf]"
19279,"(19273) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī, may Allah have mercy on him, informed us: Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad ibn Sufyān al-Ṭūsī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ḥammād al-Abīwardī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥarrir narrated from Qatādah from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, performed the ʿAqīqah on his own behalf after Prophethood. ʿAbd al-Razzāq said: ""The only reason they abandoned ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥarrir was because of this hadith."" The Faqīh, may Allah have mercy on him, said: And it has been narrated from another route from Qatādah, and from another route from Anas, but it is nothing (i.e., not established). [Ḍaʿīf]"
19280,"(19274) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhabārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us: Dāwūd ibn Qays narrated from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb: that the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said—(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān al-Anbārī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿAmr narrated from Dāwūd from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb from his father, who traced it back to his grandfather, who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, was asked about the ʿAqīqah, so he said: ""Allah does not love al-ʿUqūq (disobedience/ingratitude)."" It is as though he disliked the name. And he said: ""Whoever is born a child and wishes to perform a sacrifice (nasuk) on his behalf, let him perform for a boy two equivalent ewes, and for a girl one ewe."" [Ḥasan]"
19281,"(19275) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrānī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzzakkī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam, from a man of Banī Ḍamrah, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was asked about the ʿAqīqah. He said: ""I do not like ʿUqūq (disobedience)."" It was as though he disliked the name (due to its resemblance to the word for disobedience). Then he said: ""Whoever is born a child and wishes to perform a sacrifice on behalf of his child, let him do so."" The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""This, when joined with the first narration, becomes strong, and in both of them this is conditioned upon his love (for it).""

[Ṣaḥīh li-ghayrihi]"
19282,"(19276) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Bashshār narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Yazīd narrated from his father, from Sibāʿ ibn Thābit, from Umm Kurz al-Khuzāʿiyyah — who is also called al-Kaʿbiyyah (may Allah be pleased with her) — she said: I heard the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) say regarding the ʿAqīqah: ""For the male child, two equivalent sheep, and for the female child, one sheep. It does not harm you whether they are male or female."" Thus said Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah from his father, and the mention of his father in it is an error. [Ṣaḥīh]"
19283,"(19277) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Yazīd, from Sibāʿ ibn Thābit, from Umm Kurz (may Allah be pleased with her). She said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""For the male child, two equivalent sheep, and for the female child, one sheep.""

Abū Dāwūd said: ""This is the ḥadīth, and the ḥadīth of Sufyān is an error."" The learned jurist (may Allah have mercy on him) said: ""Al-Muzanī narrated it in al-Mukhtaṣar from al-Shāfiʿī, from Sufyān, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Yazīd, from Sibāʿ ibn Wahb, from Umm Kurz — and al-Muzanī was mistaken in it in two respects. The first is that all the other narrators narrated it from Ibn ʿUyaynah, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Yazīd, from his father, from Sibāʿ. The second is that they said: 'Sibāʿ ibn Thābit.' And al-Ṭaḥāwī narrated it from al-Muzanī in Kitāb al-Sunan in one of the two places correctly, as the rest of the people narrated it from Sufyān. And with Allah is the success.""

It was also narrated by Ibn Jurayj from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Yazīd, from Sibāʿ ibn Thābit, that Muḥammad ibn Thābit ibn Sibāʿ informed him that Umm Kurz informed him. And this was narrated from another route from Umm Kurz. [Ṣaḥīh]"
19284,"(19278) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us orally; Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī narrated to us in Makkah; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ḥabībah bint Maysarah, from Umm Kurz (may Allah be pleased with her), that she heard the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) say: ""For the male child, two equivalent sheep, and for the female child, one sheep."" [Ṣaḥīh]"
19285,"(19279) Abū Muḥammad al-Sukkārī informed us in Baghdād; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Ibn Jurayj informed me; ʿAṭāʾ informed me with this ḥadīth. I said — meaning to ʿAṭāʾ — ""What are 'two equivalent (sheep)'?"" He said: ""Two alike. And the ewe is more beloved to him than the goat, and the male of them is more beloved to him than the female"" — this was his personal opinion. A man said to ʿAṭāʾ: ""What if I were to slaughter a camel in their place?"" He said: ""Begin with what was named, then slaughter afterwards whatever you wish."" I asked him: ""And the Sunnah?"" He said: ""And the Sunnah."" [Ṣaḥīh]"
19286,"(19280) Abū Jaʿfar Kāmil ibn Aḥmad al-Mustamlī (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us; Bishr ibn Aḥmad al-Isfarāyīnī informed us; Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Bayhaqī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Jabbār ibn Ward narrated. He said: I heard Ibn Abī Mulaykah say: ""A male child was born to ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr, so it was said to ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her): 'O Mother of the Believers, perform the ʿAqīqah on his behalf' — or he said — 'slaughter a camel on his behalf.' She said: 'I seek refuge in Allah! Rather, what the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said is: ""Two equivalent sheep.""'"" [Ṣaḥīh. Without the story]"
19287,"(19281) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād; Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to me; my father narrated to us; Bishr ibn al-Mufaḍḍal, Abū Ismāʿīl, narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUthmān ibn Khuthaym, from Yūsuf ibn Māhak. He said: We visited Ḥafṣah bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, and she informed us that ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) had informed her that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""For the male child, two equivalent sheep, and for the female child, one sheep."" [Ṣaḥīh]"
19288,"(19282) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us in Baghdād; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Jaʿfar informed us; al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn Makhlad narrated to us; Abū Ḥafṣ Sālim ibn Tamīm narrated from his father, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Aʿraj, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Indeed, the Jews perform the ʿAqīqah for the male child but do not perform it for the female child. So perform the ʿAqīqah for the male child with two sheep, and for the female child with one sheep."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
19289,"(19283) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Tamtām narrated to me; Abū Maʿmar ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr al-Hudhalī al-Muqaʿid narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn al-ʿAzīz ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar informed us; Abū Khalīfah informed us; Abū Maʿmar narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated to us; Ayyūb narrated from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) performed the ʿAqīqah for al-Ḥasan with a ram and for al-Ḥusayn with a ram. [Munkar. Previously mentioned under no. 19267]"
19290,"(19284) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrānī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzzakkī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik narrated from Nāfiʿ, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), that no one among his children would ask him about the ʿAqīqah but that he would give it to them. And he used to perform the ʿAqīqah for his children — one sheep for each, whether male or female. [Ṣaḥīh]"
19291,"(19285) He said: And Malik narrated to us from Hisham ibn Urwah, that his father, Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, used to perform the ʿaqīqah for his children, males and females, with one sheep each. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19292,"(19286) Abū Dāwūd narrated in al-Marāsīl from Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAlāʾ from Ḥafṣ from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad from his father, that the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said regarding the ʿaqīqah that Fāṭimah performed on behalf of al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn (ʿalayhim al-salām): ""Send a leg from it to the midwife, and eat and give charity, but do not break any bone from it.""

Abū Bakr informed us: Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad informed us, Abū al-Ḥusayn al-Dāwūdī narrated to us, Abū ʿAlī al-Luʾluʾī narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, and he mentioned it. [Ḍaʿīf]"
19293,"(19287) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us, ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated to us from ʿĀmir al-Aḥwal from ʿAṭāʾ from Umm Kurz (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhā), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""For the male child, two equivalent sheep, and for the female child, one sheep.""

He said: And ʿAṭāʾ used to say: ""It is cut into pieces but no bone is broken from it."" — I think he said — ""and it is cooked."" And ʿAṭāʾ said: ""When you slaughter, say: 'Bismillāh, Allāhu Akbar, this is the ʿaqīqah of so-and-so.'"" And in the narration of Ibn Jurayj from ʿAṭāʾ, he said regarding the ʿaqīqah: ""It is cut joint by joint and cooked with water and salt, and it is given as a gift to the neighbours.""

And something to that effect was also narrated from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh as his own statement. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19294,"(19288) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Thābit narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us, my father narrated to me, ʿAbdullāh ibn Buraydah narrated to me: I heard my father, Buraydah (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), saying: ""We, in the days of Jāhiliyyah, when a male child was born to one of us, would slaughter a sheep and smear its blood upon his head. Then when Allah brought Islam, we would slaughter a sheep, shave his head, and smear it with saffron.""

The Shaykh (raḥimahullāh) said: And in the ḥadīth of Ayyūb ibn Mūsā from Yazīd ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Muzanī from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""In camels there is a farʿ, and in sheep there is a farʿ, and the ʿaqīqah is performed for the male child, but his head is not to be touched with blood."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19295,"(19289) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Ziyād informed us, Abū Ḥumaydah — Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf — informed us, Abū Qurrah informed us from Ibn Jurayj, a ḥadīth he mentioned from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd.

And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us, Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Rustah narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Bakkār al-Ṣayrafī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Majīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us from Ibn Jurayj from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Anṣārī from ʿAmrah from ʿĀʾishah (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhā) in the ḥadīth of the ʿaqīqah, she said: ""The people of Jāhiliyyah used to place a piece of cotton in the blood of the ʿaqīqah and place it upon the head of the infant. So the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) ordered that perfume (khulūq) be placed in place of the blood.""

Al-Faqīh (raḥimahullāh) said: And in the ḥadīth of Salmān ibn ʿĀmir, his statement ""Amīṭū ʿanhu al-adhā"" (remove the harm from it) may be intended to mean shaving the head and the prohibition against touching his head with its blood. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19296,"(19290) As for the ḥadīth which Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Burshān informed us — Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz narrated to us, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Shākir narrated to us, ʿAffān narrated to us, Hammām narrated to us, Qatādah narrated to us.

And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Muḥammad ibn Jabalah informed us, Abū ʿUmar — Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar, the companion of the pool (al-Ḥawḍ) — narrated to us, Hammām narrated to us from Qatādah from al-Ḥasan from Samurah (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""Every boy is held as a ransom by his ʿaqīqah; it is slaughtered for him on the seventh day, his head is shaved, and blood is applied to it."" Al-Ḥawḍī added in his narration: He said: ""And Qatādah, when asked about the blood — how it should be dealt with — said: 'When the ʿaqīqah is slaughtered, a piece of wool is taken from it, and its jugular veins are wiped with it, then it is placed upon the crown of the infant's head until it flows like a thread. Then his head is washed and shaved afterwards.'""

[Ṣaḥīḥ, excluding his statement ""and blood is applied to it""]"
19297,"(19291) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, he said: Abū Dāwūd said: ""This is a mistake from Hammām — 'yudammā' (blood is applied).""

Abū ʿAlī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us, Ibn Abī ʿAdī narrated to us from Saʿīd — he is Ibn Abī ʿArūbah — from Qatādah, and he mentioned it and said: ""On the seventh day, his head is shaved and he is named."" Abū Dāwūd said: ""And he is named"" — this is more correct. Salām ibn Abī Muṭīʿ said, meaning from Qatādah, and Iyās ibn Daghfal and Ashʿath from al-Ḥasan. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19298,"(19292) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us, Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated to us from Hishām from Ḥafṣah from al-Rabāb from Salmān ibn ʿĀmir (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who raised it, saying: ""The boy is held as a ransom by his ʿaqīqah; the harm is removed from him and blood is shed for him on the seventh day.""

And the ḥadīth of Ibn Abī ʿArūbah from Qatādah has already passed in this regard. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19299,"(19293) Abū al-Fatḥ — Hilāl ibn Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Ḥaffār in Baghdad — informed us, al-Ḥusayn ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAyyāsh al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ narrated to us from Ismāʿīl ibn Muslim from Qatādah from ʿAbdullāh ibn Buraydah from his father from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), who said: ""The ʿaqīqah is slaughtered on the seventh, the fourteenth, and the twenty-first."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
19300,"(19294) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Ziyād informed us, Abū Ḥumaydah — Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf — informed us, Abū Qurrah informed us from Ibn Jurayj, a ḥadīth he mentioned from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd.

And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us, Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Rustah narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Bakkār al-Ṣayrafī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Majīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us from Ibn Jurayj from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Anṣārī from ʿAmrah from ʿĀʾishah (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhā), from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), who said: ""The ʿaqīqah is performed for the male child with two equivalent sheep, and for the female child with one sheep."" She said: ""And the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) performed the ʿaqīqah for al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn with two sheep on the seventh day, and he ordered that the harm be removed from his head."" And he said: ""Slaughter in his name and say: 'Bismillāh, Allāhu Akbar, Allahumma laka wa ilayka hādhihi ʿaqīqatu fulān' (In the name of Allah, Allah is the Greatest, O Allah, this is for You and to You, this is the ʿaqīqah of so-and-so).""

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Majīd. And in the narration of Abī Qurrah from al-Ḥasan: ""Two sheep and for al-Ḥusayn two sheep, he slaughtered them on the seventh day and named them."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19301,"(19295) Abū Muḥammad al-Sukkarī informed us in Baghdād: Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār informed us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad narrated from his father, from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) that he named al-Ḥasan on the seventh day, and that he derived the name Ḥusayn from Ḥasan, and that there was between them only the interval of pregnancy. [Weak]"
19302,"(19296) Abū Aḥmad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Mihrajānī informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Mālik narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī, from his father, that he said: Fāṭimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), weighed the hair of al-Ḥasan, al-Ḥusayn, Zaynab, and Umm Kulthūm, and she gave in charity the equivalent weight of that in silver.

And we narrated it from Rābiʿah, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Ḥusayn, regarding al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn (ʿalayhimā al-salām). [Weak]"
19303,"(19297) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us: Mūsā ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us: al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, from his father, from his grandfather, that Fāṭimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), sacrificed a sheep for al-Ḥasan and al-Ḥusayn when she gave birth to them, shaved their heads, and then gave in charity the weight of their hair in silver. [Weak]"
19304,"(19298) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Ṣāʿid narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Makhzūmī narrated to us: Ḥusayn ibn Zayd narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, from his father, from his grandfather, from ʿAlī (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), that the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) commanded Fāṭimah (ʿalayhā al-salām), saying: ""Weigh the hair of al-Ḥusayn and give in charity its weight in silver, and give the midwife the leg of the ʿaqīqah animal.""

Thus is this narration.

And al-Ḥumaydī narrated from Ḥusayn ibn Zayd, from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, from his father, that ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) gave the midwife the leg of the ʿaqīqah. And Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated it from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, from his father, from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) in a mursal narration, that they should send a leg of it to the midwife. And in the narration of Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Ḥusayn, from ʿAlī (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), he said: The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) performed the ʿaqīqah for al-Ḥasan with a sheep and said: ""O Fāṭimah, shave his head and give in charity the weight of his hair in silver."" So we weighed it, and its weight was a dirham or part of a dirham.

This narration is also munqaṭiʿ. And it has been said in its chain: from Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Ḥusayn, from his father, from his grandfather, from ʿAlī (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), and I do not know whether it is preserved or not. [Weak]"
19305,"(19299) The noble Abū al-Fatḥ al-ʿUmarī informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Shurayḥ informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn al-Jaʿd informed us: Sharīk narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAqīl, from Ibn al-Ḥusayn, from Abū Rāfiʿ, who said: When Fāṭimah gave birth to al-Ḥasan (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā), she said: ""O Messenger of Allāh, shall I not perform the ʿaqīqah for my son with blood?"" He said: ""No, but shave his hair and give in charity its weight in silver to the Ahl al-Ṣuffah or to the poor.""

ʿAlī said: Sharīk meant by ""al-Awfāḍ"" the people of the Ṣuffah. So she did that. And when she gave birth to al-Ḥusayn (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), she did the same. [Weak]"
19306,"(19300) Abū Saʿīd al-Ṣayrafī informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ṣaffār informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ghālib narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Ashʿath narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Salamah — and he is Ibn Abī al-Ḥusām — narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad narrated from ʿAlī ibn Ḥusayn, from Abū Rāfiʿ, that when al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī (ʿalayhimā al-salām) was born, his mother wished to perform the ʿaqīqah for him with a large ram, so she came to the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), and he said to her: ""Do not perform the ʿaqīqah for him with anything, but shave the hair of his head, then give in charity its weight in silver in the way of Allāh, the Mighty and Majestic, or to the wayfarer."" And she gave birth to al-Ḥusayn the following year, and she did the same. This was narrated uniquely by Ibn ʿAqīl.

And if it is authentic, it is as though he (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) intended to perform the ʿaqīqah for them himself, so he commanded her with something else, which is giving in charity the weight of their hair in silver. And in Allāh is the success. [Weak]"
19307,"(19301) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAmr ʿUthmān ibn Aḥmad al-Daqqāq narrated to us in Baghdād: Yaḥyā ibn Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us: Shujāʿ ibn al-Walīd narrated to us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Yaḥyā — and he is Ibn Saʿīd — narrated to us: ʿUbaydullāh narrated to us: ʿUmar ibn Nāfiʿ narrated from his father Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā), who said: The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) forbade al-qazaʿ.

And al-qazaʿ is that part of a child's head is shaved while part of it is left.

The wording is from the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb, from Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn Jurayj, from ʿUbaydullāh. [Authentic. Agreed upon.]"
19308,"(19302) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us: Shabābah ibn Sawwār narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār, from Ibn ʿUmar (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā), who said: The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) forbade al-qazaʿ. [Authentic]"
19309,"(19303) Abū Manṣūr al-Ẓafar ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-ʿAlawī (raḥimahullāh) informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym informed us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim ibn Abī Gharazah informed us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī informed us in Baghdād: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī informed us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us. They both said: Sufyān al-Thawrī informed us: ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿĀṣim narrated from ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Rāfiʿ, from his father, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) give the adhān in the ear of al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu) with the call to prayer when Fāṭimah (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhā) gave birth to him. [Weak]"
19310,"(19304) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed me: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Imām, Tamīm ibn Muḥammad, and al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us. They said: ʿAbd al-Aʿlā ibn Ḥammād al-Narsī narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us: Thābit al-Bunānī narrated from Anas ibn Mālik (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said: I took ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Ṭalḥah al-Anṣārī to the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) when he was born, and the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) was with his camels tending them. He said: ""Do you have dates with you?"" I said: ""Yes."" So I gave him some dates, which he took and chewed in his mouth, then he opened the mouth of the infant and placed it therein. The infant began to lick it. The Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""The Anṣār love dates."" And he named him ʿAbdullāh.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd al-Aʿlā, and they both narrated it from the ḥadīth of Anas ibn Sīrīn, from Anas ibn Mālik. [Authentic. Agreed upon.]"
19311,"Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Abū Usāmah narrated to us: Burayd ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated from Abū Burdah, from Abū Mūsā (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""A son was born to me, so I brought him to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). He named him Ibrāhīm and performed taḥnīk for him with a date.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Naṣr, from Abū Usāmah, and he added in it: ""He supplicated for blessings for him and handed him back to me. He was the eldest son of Abū Mūsā."" Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others, from Abū Usāmah.

[Sound. Agreed upon.]"
19312,"Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Faqīh narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī and ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Baghawī narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī in Naysābūr, and Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Firās in Makkah narrated to us, saying: Abū Ḥafṣ ʿUmar ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Jumaḥī narrated to us: Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us, saying: Ibrāhīm ibn Ziyād al-Baghdādī, who is called Sablān, narrated to us: ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbbād narrated to us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar and his brother ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us in Makkah in the year one hundred and forty-four, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Indeed, the most beloved of your names to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, are ʿAbd Allāh and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān.""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAbd Allāh. In the ḥadīth of the others, the one called Sablān and the date are not mentioned. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibrāhīm ibn Ziyād.

[Sound. Muslim 2138.]"
19313,"Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us: My father narrated to us: Hishām, meaning Ibn Saʿīd al-Ṭālaqānī, narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Muhājir narrated to me: ʿAqīl ibn Shabīb narrated from Abū Wahb al-Jushamī (may Allah be pleased with him), who had been a Companion, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Name after the names of the Prophets. The most beloved of names to Allah are ʿAbd Allāh and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān. The most truthful of them are Ḥārith and Hammām, and the most repugnant of them are Ḥarb and Murrah.""

[Weak.]"
19314,"Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Faḍl ibn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Isfarāʾinī narrated to us: Abū Sahl ibn Aḥmad narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlī al-Dhuhlī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Hushaym narrated to us: Dāwūd ibn ʿAmr narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Zakariyyā al-Khuzāʿī narrated from Abū al-Dardāʾ (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Indeed, you will be called on the Day of Resurrection by your names and the names of your fathers, so make your names good.""

This is a mursal ḥadīth. Ibn Abī Zakariyyā did not hear from Abū al-Dardāʾ.

[Weak.]"
19315,"Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Faḍl ibn ʿAlī narrated to us: Bishr ibn Aḥmad narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlī al-Dhuhlī narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, saying: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Muʿtamir ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, saying: I heard al-Rukayn ibn al-Rabīʿ narrating from his father, from Samurah ibn Jundub (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade us from naming our slaves with four names: Aflaḥ, Rabbāḥ, Yassār, and Nāfiʿ.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā.

[Sound. Muslim 2136.]"
19316,"Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Shaybānī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us: Zuḥayr narrated to us: Manṣūr narrated to us: Hilāl ibn Yassāf narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn ʿUmaylah narrated to us, from Samurah ibn Jundub (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""The most beloved of speech to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, are four phrases: Lā ilāha illā Allāh, Allāhu Akbar, Subḥān Allāh, and al-ḥamdu lillāh. It will not harm you with which of them you begin. Do not name your slave Yassār, nor Rabbāḥ, nor Najīḥ, nor Aflaḥ, for you will say: 'Is he there?' and he will not be there, so you will say: 'No.' They are only four, so do not add to them.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Yūnus.

[Sound.]"
19317,"Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Hārūn ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbadah narrated to us: Ibn Jurayj narrated to me: Abū al-Zubayr narrated to me that he heard Jābir (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: ""The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) intended to forbid naming with Yaʿlā, Barakah, Aflaḥ, Yassār, Nāfiʿ, and the like, but then I saw him remain silent thereafter and say nothing. Then he passed away without forbidding that. Then ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) intended to forbid that, but then he left it.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī Khalaf, from Rūḥ.

[Sound.]"
19318,"Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Rajāʾ narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, saying: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us: Abū al-Zinād narrated to us: al-Aʿraj narrated to us, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who reported it as reaching the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family): ""The most ignominious name before Allah on the Day of Resurrection is that of a man who is named 'King of Kings.'""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Aḥmad. Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah added in his narration: ""There is no owner except Allah.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from Ibn ʿUyaynah. Muslim narrated it from Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal and Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal added: ""I asked Abū ʿAmr about 'akhnaʿ,' and he said: 'The most lowly.'""

[Sound. Agreed upon.]"
19319,"Abū ʿUthmān Saʿīd ibn Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdān al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated to us: Nāfiʿ narrated to us, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) changed the name of ʿĀṣiyah, saying: ""You are Jamīlah.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal and others.

[Sound. Muslim 2139.]"
19320,"Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī narrated in Ṭūs: Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Faqīh narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us: Abū Ghassān narrated to me: Abū Ḥāzim narrated to me, from Sahl ibn Saʿd, who said: ""Al-Mundhir ibn Abī Usayd was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) when he was born. He placed him on his thigh while Abū Usayd was sitting. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) became occupied with something before him, so Abū Usayd was told to take his son. He was lifted from the Prophet's (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) thigh and turned away. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: 'Where is the boy?' Abū Usayd said: 'We turned him away, O Messenger of Allah.' He said: 'What is his name?' He said: 'So-and-so.' He said: 'No, rather his name is al-Mundhir.' So he named him al-Mundhir that day.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam. Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Sahl and others, from Saʿīd.

[Sound. Agreed upon.]"
19321,"(19315) Abu Muhammad — 'Abd Allah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Sukkari — informed us in Baghdad: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar — informed us: Ahmad ibn Mansur al-Ramadi — narrated to us: 'Abd al-Razzaq — informed us: Ma'mar — from al-Zuhri — from Ibn al-Musayyib — from his father — from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said to me: ""What is your name?"" He said: I said: ""Hazn"" (meaning 'hardship'). He said: ""Nay, you are Sahl"" (meaning 'ease'). He said: ""I shall not change the name by which my father named me."" Ibn al-Musayyib said: So that hardship (al-huzunah) remained among us thereafter.

Narrated by al-Bukhari in al-Sahih from 'Ali ibn 'Abd Allah and others from 'Abd al-Razzaq. [Sahih. Bukhari 1690–6193]"
19322,"(19316) Abu Bakr ibn Furak — informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far — narrated to us: Yunus ibn Habib — narrated to us: Abu Dawud — narrated to us: Shu'bah —

(And) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz — informed us: Abu al-Fadl ibn Ibrahim — narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Salamah — narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Bashshar — narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ja'far — narrated to us: Shu'bah — from 'Ata' ibn Abi Maymunah, who said: I heard Abu Rafi' narrating from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that Zaynab's name was Barrah, and it was said: ""Do you purify yourself (tuzakki nafsaki)?"" So the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) named her Zaynab.

The wording is that of the hadith of Muhammad ibn Ja'far. Narrated by al-Bukhari in al-Sahih from Sadaqah ibn al-Fadl from Muhammad, and narrated by Muslim from Ibn Bashshar and others. [Sahih. Agreed upon (muttafaq 'alayh)]"
19323,"(19317) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr — both said: narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us: Abu Ja'far Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Hamid al-Harithi — narrated to us: Abu Usamah — from al-Walid ibn Kathir — narrated to me: Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn 'Ata', who said: narrated to me Zaynab bint Umm Salamah, who said: My name was Barrah, so the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) named me Zaynab. And Zaynab bint Jahsh came to him, and her name was also Barrah, so he named her Zaynab.

Narrated by Muslim in al-Sahih from Abu Kuraib from Abu Usamah. [Sahih. Muslim 2142]"
19324,"(19318) Abu Tahir al-Faqih — informed us: Abu al-Hasan al-Tara'ifi — narrated to us: 'Uthman ibn Sa'id al-Darimi — narrated to us: Yahya ibn Bukayr — narrated to me: al-Layth — from Yazid ibn Abi Habib — from 'Abd Allah ibn al-Harith ibn Jaz' al-Zubaydi, who said: A companion of mine died as a stranger, and we were at his grave — I, 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, and 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr ibn al-'As. My name was al-'As, and the name of Ibn 'Umar was al-'As, and the name of Ibn 'Amr was al-'As. The Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said to us: ""Go down and bury him, for you are the slaves of Allah ('ibad Allah)."" He said: So we went down and buried our brother, and we ascended from the grave, and our names had been changed.

And in this chapter there are many reports, for he changed the name of al-'As ibn al-Aswad to Muti', and Asram to Zur'ah, and Shihib to Hasham, and Harb to Salm, and al-Mutajatti' to al-Munba'ith, and others besides that which would be too lengthy to mention in this book. [Da'if]"
19325,"(19319) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi — by recitation — and Abu 'Abd al-Rahman Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Sulami — by dictation — all said: narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us: Abu Yahya Zakariyya ibn Yahya ibn Asad — narrated to us: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah — from Ayyub — from Muhammad ibn Sirin, who said: I heard Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: Abu al-Qasim (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Name yourselves by my name, but do not take my kunya (teknonym) as your kunya.""

Narrated by al-Bukhari in al-Sahih from 'Ali ibn 'Abd Allah, and narrated by Muslim from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah and others from Sufyan. [Sahih. Agreed upon (muttafaq 'alayh)]"
19326,"(19320) Abu Bakr ibn Furak — informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far — narrated to us: Yunus ibn Habib — narrated to us: Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi — narrated to us: Shu'bah and Abu 'Awanah — from Abi Hasin — from Abi Salih — from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Name yourselves by my name, but do not take my kunya as your kunya.""

Narrated by al-Bukhari in al-Sahih from Musa ibn Isma'il from Abu 'Awanah. [Sahih]"
19327,"(19321) Abu al-Husayn ibn Burhan al-'Adl in Baghdad — informed us: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar — narrated to us: Abu Yahya Zakariyya ibn Yahya ibn Asad — narrated to us: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah — from Ibn al-Munkadir — who heard Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: A boy was born to a man among us, and he named him al-Qasim. We said: ""We shall not give you the kunya of Abu al-Qasim, and it shall not be pleasing to the eye."" So we came to the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and mentioned that to him. He said: ""Name your son 'Abd al-Rahman.""

Both al-Bukhari and Muslim recorded it in their Sahihs from the hadith of Ibn 'Uyaynah. [Sahih]"
19328,"(19322) Abu 'Ali al-Husayn ibn Muhammad al-Rudhbari — narrated to us: Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Mahmuyah al-'Askari — narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Qalanisi — narrated to us: Adam ibn Abi Iyas — narrated to us: Shu'bah — from Husayn ibn 'Abd al-Rahman — from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd — from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Name yourselves by my name, but do not take my kunya as your kunya, for I am merely a Qasim (distributor); I have been sent to distribute among you.""

Narrated by al-Bukhari in al-Sahih from Adam, and Muslim recorded it from the hadith of Ghundar from Shu'bah. [Sahih. Agreed upon (muttafaq 'alayh)]"
19329,"(19323) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz — informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq — informed us: Abu al-Muthanna — narrated to us: Musaddad — narrated to us: Khalid, i.e., 'Abd Allah — narrated to us: Husayn — from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd — from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: A boy was born to a man among us, and he named him with the name of the Prophet (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family). They said: ""We shall not give him a kunya until we ask the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family)."" He (Jabir) said: He (the Prophet) said: ""Name yourselves by my name, but do not take my kunya as your kunya.""

Narrated by al-Bukhari in al-Sahih from Musaddad, and by Muslim from Rifa'ah ibn al-Haytham from Khalid. And with this meaning, 'Abthar narrated from Husayn. [Sahih. Agreed upon (muttafaq 'alayh)]"
19330,"(19324) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz — informed us: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn Raja' — narrated to us: 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah — narrated to us: Jarir —

(And) he said: Abu al-Fadl ibn Ibrahim — informed us: Ahmad ibn Salamah — narrated to us: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim — informed us: Jarir — from Mansur — from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd — from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: A boy was born to a man among us, and he named him Muhammad. His people said to him: ""We shall not allow you to name him with the name of the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family)."" So he set out carrying his son on his back and brought him to the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, a boy has been born to me, and I named him Muhammad, but my people said to me: 'We shall not allow you to name him with the name of the Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family).'"" The Messenger of Allah (may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Name yourselves by my name, but do not take my kunya as your kunya, for I am merely a Qasim (distributor); I distribute among you.""

Narrated by Muslim in al-Sahih from 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah and Ishaq ibn Ibrahim, and both recorded it from the hadith of Shu'bah from Mansur. [Sahih. Agreed upon (muttafaq 'alayh)]"
19331,"(19325) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Hishām ibn Milās al-Numayrī narrated to us: Marwān ibn Muʿāwiya al-Fazārī narrated to us: Ḥumayd narrated to us, saying: Anas said:

A man called out in al-Baqīʿ, ""Yā Abā al-Qāsim!"" So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) turned toward him. The man said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I did not mean you; I meant so-and-so."" He (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Take my name, but do not take my kunyah.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar and Abū Kurayb, from Marwān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh.]"
19332,"(19326) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Maḥmūyah al-ʿAskarī narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Ādam narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl, from Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him), who said:

The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was in the marketplace when a man said, ""Yā Abā al-Qāsim!"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) turned toward him. The man said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I was only calling this one (i.e., someone else)."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Take my name, but do not take my kunyah.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam. [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
19333,"(19327) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: I heard Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb say: I heard al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān say: I heard al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) say:

It is not permissible for anyone to take the kunyah Abū al-Qāsim, whether his name is Muḥammad or otherwise.

Al-Faqīh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: The meaning of this has also been narrated to us from Ṭāwūs al-Yamānī (may Allah have mercy on him). [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
19334,"(19328) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us: Hishām narrated from—

(Ḥ) And ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq and Abū Muslim said: Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Hishām narrated to us: Abū al-Zubayr narrated to us, from Jābir (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said:

""Whoever takes my name, let him not take my kunyah; and whoever takes my kunyah, let him not take my name.""

This has also been narrated through another chain from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), though there is disagreement regarding it. The narrations prohibiting it absolutely are more numerous and have stronger chains. And Allah knows best. [Munkar.]"
19335,"(19329) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: ʿUthmān and Abū Bakr, the two sons of Abī Shaybah, said: Abū Usāmah narrated to us, from Fiṭr, from Mundhir, from Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥanafiyyah, who said: ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) said:

I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, if a child is born to me after you, may I give him your name and your kunyah?"" He said: ""Yes.""

Abū Bakr did not say ""I said""; rather, he said: ""ʿAlī said to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
19336,"(19330) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Sarī al-Tamīmī al-Ḥāfiẓ in Kūfah narrated to us: Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Jaʿfar al-Ṣayrafī narrated to us: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us: Fiṭr, who is ibn Khalīfah, narrated to us, from Mundhir al-Thawrī, who said: I heard Ibn al-Ḥanafiyyah say:

It was a special concession for ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him). He said, ""O Messenger of Allah, if a child is born to me after you, may I give him your name and your kunyah?"" He said: ""Yes.""

It has also been narrated through another weak chain from Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥanafiyyah, and the hadith differs regarding its being connected (mawṣūl). [Ḍaʿīf.]"
19337,"(19331) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: al-Nufaylī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿImrān al-Ḥajabī narrated to us, from his grandmother Ṣafiyyah bint Shaybah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said:

A woman came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I have given birth to a boy and named him Muḥammad and given him the kunyah Abū al-Qāsim. I have been told that you dislike that."" He said: ""What is it that has made my name permissible and my kunyah impermissible? Or what is it that has made my kunyah impermissible and my name permissible?""

Al-Faqīh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: The narrations prohibiting absolutely the taking of the kunyah Abū al-Qāsim are more authentic than this hadith of al-Ḥajabī and more numerous, so the ruling is based upon them, not upon it. The hadith of ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) indicates that he was aware of a prohibition, so he asked for a special concession for himself alone. As for the hadith of ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), if its chain is authentic, it may be that his prohibition initially occurred as a matter of dislike (karāhah) and discouragement (tanzīh), not as an absolute prohibition (taḥrīm). When the woman understood it to be an absolute prohibition, he clarified that it was not so. The first interpretation is more apparent, and Allah knows best.

Ḥumayd ibn Zanjawayh said in his book al-Adab: I asked Ibn Abī Uways what Mālik's position was regarding a man who combines the name of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and his kunyah. He gestured toward a shaykh sitting with us and said, ""This is Muḥammad ibn Mālik; he named him Muḥammad and gave him the kunyah Abū al-Qāsim."" And he used to say: ""The prohibition of that was only during the lifetime of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), out of dislike that someone would be called by his name or his kunyah and the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) would turn toward him. But today there is nothing wrong with that.""

Ḥumayd ibn Zanjawayh said: He only disliked someone being called by his kunyah during his lifetime, but he did not dislike someone being called by his name, because hardly anyone would call by his name. When he passed away, that [concern] ceased. Do you not see that he permitted ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), if a son was born to him after him, to combine the name and the kunyah for him? And a number of the sons of the eminent Companions combined the two, among them Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr, Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Abī Ṭālib, Muḥammad ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, Muḥammad ibn Ḥāṭib, and Muḥammad ibn al-Muntashir.

Al-Shaykh said: This specification [of the prohibition] to his lifetime, and the evidence drawn from those who combined the two after his death, is of the type that al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) used to speak of: ""There is no proof in anyone's statement alongside the statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family)."" And Allah knows best."
19338,"(19332) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Hārūn ibn Zayd ibn Abī al-Zarqāʾ narrated to us: My father narrated to us: Hishām ibn Saʿd narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) struck his son who was known by the kunyah Abū ʿĪsā, and that al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah was known by the kunyah Abū ʿĪsā. So ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to him, ""Is it not sufficient for you to be known by the kunyah Abū ʿAbd Allāh?"" He said, ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) gave me this kunyah."" He said, ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) has been forgiven his earlier and later sins, and we are in our [mortal] state."" So he continued to be known by the kunyah Abū ʿAbd Allāh until he died. [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
19339,"(19333) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to me: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Shaybān ibn Farrūkh and Jaʿfar ibn Mihrān said: ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated to us, from Abū al-Tayyāḥ, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), who said:

The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was the best of people in character. I had a brother who was called Abū ʿUmayr—I think he said, ""He was a young child (faṭīm)."" When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) came and saw him, he would say, ""Abā ʿUmayr, what did the little bird (nughayr) do?"" He used to play with it.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Shaybān ibn Farrūkh and from Abū al-Rabīʿ. [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
19340,"(19334) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: Ibn Abī Qumāsh narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn ʿAwn narrated to us, from Ḥammād ibn Zayd, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said:

I said, ""O Messenger of Allah, all your wives have kunyahs except me."" He said, ""Take the kunyah after your son ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr."" So she was known by the kunyah Umm ʿAbd Allāh until she died. [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
19341,"(19335) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us, saying: Abū Muʿāwiya narrated to us from Hishām. And Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī from the original of his hearing, and Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Fāmī among others, all said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān al-ʿĀmirī al-Kūfī narrated to us, saying: Abū Usāma narrated to us from Hishām ibn ʿUrwa from ʿAbbād ibn Ḥamza ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn al-Zubayr from ʿĀ'isha (may Allah be pleased with her) that she said: ""O Messenger of Allah, will you not give me a kunya, for all of your wives have a kunyas?"" He said: ""Indeed, take the kunya after your son ʿAbdillāh."" And so she was given the kunya Umm ʿAbdillāh. This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Abū Usāma. (T) It was followed by Ḥammād ibn Salama and Maslama ibn Qaʿnab from Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19342,"(19336) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Shaybān al-Ramlī narrated to us, saying: Sufyān ibn ʿUyayna narrated to us from ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Yazīd from his father from Sibāʿ ibn Thābit, who heard it from Umm Kurz al-Kaʿbiyya (may Allah be pleased with her), who narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) that he said: ""For the male child, two sheep, and for the female child, one sheep — it does not matter to you whether they are male or female."" And I also heard him say: ""Leave the birds upon their perches."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19343,"(19337) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Abū Yaḥyā Zakariyyā ibn Yaḥyā ibn Asad narrated to us, saying: Sufyān narrated to us from ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Yazīd from his father from Sibāʿ ibn Thābit, who heard Umm Kurz al-Kaʿbiyya (may Allah be pleased with her) say that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Leave the birds upon their perches."" And someone other than him narrated from Sufyān: ""Upon their nests (makīnātihā)."" And it is also with a fatḥā on the kāf, being the plural of makān, as has reached me. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19344,"(19338) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh narrated to us, saying: Ibrāhīm ibn Maḥmūd narrated to us, saying: A man asked Yūnus ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā about the meaning of the statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him): ""Leave the birds upon their nests."" He said: ""Indeed, Allah loves truth. Indeed, al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah be pleased with him) was the master of this. I heard him say regarding the interpretation of the statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him): 'Leave the birds upon their nests': A man in the Jāhiliyya, when he had an errand, would come to a bird in its nest and startle it. If it flew to the right, he would proceed with his errand, and if it flew to the left, he would turn back. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade that."" He said: ""And al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) was unparalleled in these meanings."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19345,"(19339) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, saying: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, saying: Musaddad and Naṣr ibn ʿAlī narrated to us from Bishr ibn al-Mufaḍḍal, the meaning being: Khālid al-Ḥadhāʾ narrated to us from Abū Qilāba from Abī al-Malīḥ, who said: Nabīsha said: A man called out to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""We used to slaughter an ʿAtīra in the Jāhilyya during Rajab. What do you command us?"" He said: ""Slaughter for Allah in whatever month it may be, and do good to Allah and feed people."" He said: ""We used to slaughter a Farʿa in the Jāhiliyya. What do you command us?"" He said: ""In every flock of grazing animals there is a Farʿa. Let your livestock feed it until, when it has reached full maturity, you slaughter it and give its meat in charity."" Khālid said: ""I think he said: 'Upon the wayfarer, for that is better.'"" Khālid said: ""I said to Abū Qilāba: 'How many constitutes a grazing flock?' He said: 'One hundred.' Thus did Abū Qilāba say it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19346,"(19340) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī narrated to us in Baghdād, saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us, saying: Ibn Jurayj narrated to us from Ibn Khuthaym from Yūsuf ibn Māḥak from Ḥafṣa bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from ʿĀ'isha (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded that a Farʿa be given from every fifty sheep, one sheep."" Thus it is in my book. And in the narration of Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad and others from Ibn Jurayj: ""From every five, one."" (T) And Ḥammād ibn Salama narrated it from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUthmān ibn Khuthaym, and he said: ""From every fifty sheep, one sheep."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
19347,"(19341) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, saying: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, saying: al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us, saying: Dāwūd ibn Qays narrated to us from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said — (Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān al-Anbārī narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿAmr narrated to us from Dāwūd from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb from his father, and he traced it to his grandfather, who said: ""The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) was asked about the ʿAqīqa, and he mentioned it. And he was asked about the Farʿ, and he said: 'And the Farʿ is a right. And that you leave it until it becomes a mature young camel — a two-year-old or a one-year-old — and then give it to a widow or use it as a mount in the cause of Allah is better than slaughtering it while its meat clings to its hair, and you overturn your vessel and abandon your she-camel.'"" [Ḥasan]"
19348,"(19342) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Shaybān narrated to us, saying: Sufyān narrated to us, saying: Zayd ibn Aslam narrated to us from a man, from his father or his paternal uncle, who said: ""I was present with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) at ʿArafa, and he was asked about the ʿAqīqa, and he said: 'I do not like ʿUqūq (disobedience). Whoever is born a child and wishes to perform a sacrifice on his behalf, let him do so.' And he was asked about the ʿAtīra, and he said: 'It is a right.' And he was asked about the Farʿ, and he said: 'It is a right. But it is not that you slaughter it at the very beginning of its youth. Rather, you allow it to grow among your livestock until it becomes a one-year-old or a two-year-old, fully mature, and then you slaughter it. That is better than that you overturn your vessel and abandon your she-camel, and slaughter it while its meat is mixed with its hair.'"" And ʿAbd al-Jabbār ibn al-ʿAlāʾ narrated it from Sufyān, and he said in the ḥadīth: ""And that you leave it with its mother until it becomes a one-year-old or a two-year-old."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
19349,"(19343) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā ibn Abī Qumāsh narrated to us, saying: Abū Maʿmar ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr narrated to us from ʿAbd al-Wārith ibn Saʿīd from ʿUtba ibn ʿAbd al-Malik al-Sahmī, who said: Zurāra ibn Karīm ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to me that al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAmr narrated to him, saying: ""I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) at ʿArafa — or he said: at Minā — and the people had surrounded him."" He mentioned the ḥadīth and said in it: ""A man asked him about the ʿAtīra, and he said: 'Whoever wishes may perform the ʿAtira, and whoever wishes may not. And whoever wishes may perform the Farʿ, and whoever wishes may not.' And regarding sheep, he said: 'Its sacrifice (uḍḥiyya).'"" And Abū Maʿmar described it to us and indicated with his index finger: one sheep. [Ḍaʿīf]"
19350,"(19344) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, saying: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Mihrān al-Dīnawarī narrated to us, saying: Khalaf ibn Hishām narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAwāna narrated to us from Yaʿlā ibn ʿAṭāʾ from Wakīʿ ibn ʿUdas, who said: ""My paternal uncle Abū Razīn informed me that he said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we used to slaughter sacrifices in the Jāhiliyya, from which we would eat and feed whoever came to us.' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'There is nothing wrong with that.'"" Wakīʿ said: ""I will never abandon it."" (T) And someone other than him narrated it from Abū ʿAwāna and said: ""We slaughtered in Rajab."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
19351,"(19345) Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdān informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us — al-Hārith ibn Abī Usāmah narrated to us — Rūḥ narrated to us — Ibn Awn narrated to us — Abū Ramlah narrated from Mikhnaf ibn Sulaym al-Ghāmidī, who said: We were standing with the Prophet (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) at 'Arafāt, and I heard him saying: ""O people, upon every household in every year is an udhiyah and an 'atīrah."" Do you know what the 'atīrah is? It is that which is called the Rajabiyyah. [Weak]"
19352,"(19346) Abū 'Abdillāh the Hāfiẓ informed us — Abū 'Abdillāh ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us — Ja'far ibn Muḥammad narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us — Sufyān ibn 'Uyaynah narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Sa'īd, from Abū Hurayrah (raḍiyallāhu 'anhu), from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), who said: ""There is no fara'ah and no 'atīrah."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from 'Alī ibn 'Abdillāh from Sufyān, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Authentic]"
19353,"(19347) Abū 'Amr — Muḥammad ibn 'Abdillāh al-Adīb informed us — Abū Bakr al-Ismā'īlī informed me — al-Ḥasan, that is Ibn Sufyān, narrated to us — Ḥibbān informed us — 'Abdillāh narrated from Ma'mar — al-Zuhrī narrated to me from Sa'īd, from Abū Hurayrah (raḍiyallāhu 'anhu), from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), who said: ""There is no fara' and no 'atīrah."" He said: ""The fara' is the first offspring that was produced; they would slaughter it for their idols, and the 'atīrah is in Rajab."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from 'Abdān from 'Abdillāh ibn al-Mubārak. [Authentic]"
19354,"(19348) The jurist Abū Isḥāq — Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad al-Armawī (raḥimahullāh) informed us — Shāfi'ī ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī 'Awānah informed us — Abū Ja'far al-Ṭaḥāwī narrated to us — al-Muzanī narrated to me — al-Shāfi'ī, I heard him saying: ""It is something the people of the Jāhiliyyah used to seek through it blessings in their wealth. One of them would slaughter the first-born of his she-camel or his sheep, then would not feed it, hoping for blessing in what would come after it. They asked the Prophet (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) about it, and he said: 'Perform the fara' if you wish' — meaning, slaughter if you wish. And they used to ask him about what they had been doing in the Jāhiliyyah, fearing that it might be disliked in Islam. So he informed them that there was no dislike upon them in it, and he commanded them by choice to feed it and then carry upon it in the cause of Allah.""

Imam al-Shāfi'ī (raḥimahullāh) said: ""One who heard Zayd ibn Aslam narrate informed me, from a man from Banī Ḍamrah, from his father, that the Prophet (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) was asked about the fara'ah, and he said: 'The fara'ah is a right. And if you feed it until it becomes a fully grown she-camel (ibn labūn) of thick body, and you give it to a widow or carry upon it in the cause of Allah, that is better than that you empty your cooking pots and wean your she-camel and eat it while its flesh clings to its hair.'""

Imam al-Shāfi'ī (raḥimahullāh) said: ""His statement 'The fara'ah is a right' means that it is not void (bāṭil), but rather it is an Arabic expression in response to the questioner. And it has also been narrated from him, upon him be peace: 'There is no fara'ah and no 'atīrah.' This is not a contradiction in the narration; rather, the first means there is no obligatory fara'ah and no obligatory 'atīrah, and the other hadith indicates the meaning that he permitted the slaughter for him and preferred that he give it to a widow or carry upon it in the cause of Allah.""

""As for the 'atīrah, it is the Rajabiyyah — a slaughter that the people of the Jāhiliyyah would perform as an act of devotion in Rajab. The Prophet (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: 'There is no 'atīrah,' meaning no binding 'atīrah. And his statement, upon him be peace, when asked about the 'atīrah: 'Slaughter for Allah in whatever month it may be' — meaning, slaughter if you wish and make the slaughter for Allah, not for other than Him, in whatever month it may be — not that it is exclusively in Rajab to the exclusion of other months."" [Authentic]"
19355,"(19349) Abū 'Abd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us — Abū al-Ḥasan al-Kārīzī narrated to us — 'Alī ibn 'Abd al-'Azīz narrated to us, he said: Abū 'Ubayd said: ""The fara' is the first thing a she-camel produces; they would slaughter it when it was born, and he disliked that and said: 'Leave it until it becomes a she-camel of one or two years of age (ibn makhāḍ or ibn labūn).' Then it becomes fit for eating. And 'al-zukhzūb' is one whose body has become thick and whose flesh has become firm. His statement 'better than that you empty your cooking pots' means: when you slaughter it when its mother gives birth, the mother is left without a young to nurse, and her milk ceases. So if you do that, you have emptied your cooking pots and wasted it. His statement 'and wean your she-camel' means: you slaughter her young, and every female that loses her young is wālih (distraught)."" [Authentic]"
19356,"(19350) Abū 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Hārūn ibn 'Abdillāh narrated to us — Ḥammād ibn Mas'adah narrated from 'Awf, from Abū Rayḥānah, from Ibn 'Abbās (raḍiyallāhu 'anhu), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) forbade the mu'āqarah of the Bedouins.""

Abū Dāwūd said: ""Ghundar stopped it at Ibn 'Abbās."" Abū Dāwūd said: ""The name of Abū Rayḥānah is 'Abdillāh ibn Maṭar."" [Weak]"
19357,"(19351) Abū Ḥāmid — Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd al-Zawzanī informed us — Abū al-Qāsim al-Ṭabarānī narrated to us — Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Dabarī narrated to us — 'Abd al-Razzāq — (ḥ) and Abū 'Abdillāh the Hāfiẓ narrated to us — Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn Ma'īn narrated to us — 'Abd al-Razzāq narrated from Ma'mar, from Thābit, from Anas (raḍiyallāhu 'anhu) that the Prophet (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""There is no 'aqr in Islam.""

Abū Zakariyyā said: ""Al-'aqr means the Bedouins at the water — one would slaughter and the other would slaughter, and they would eat for other than Allah and His Messenger."" And Abū Sulaymān al-Khaṭṭābī said, as it has reached me: ""The mu'āqarah of the Bedouins is that two men compete, each one arguing with his companion, and this one slaughters a number of his camels and his companion slaughters a number — whichever of them slaughters more defeats his companion, and they dislike its flesh lest it be from what has been dedicated to other than Allah."" [Authentic]"
19358,"(19352) Abū 'Abd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us — Abū al-Ḥasan al-Kārīzī informed us — 'Alī ibn 'Abd al-'Azīz narrated from Abū 'Ubayd — 'Umar ibn Hārūn narrated to me — Yūnus ibn Yazīd al-Aylī narrated from al-Zuhrī, raising the hadith: ""That he was forbidden from the slaughter of the jinn."" He said: ""The slaughter of the jinn is that a house is purchased, or a spring is extracted, or something similar, and a slaughter is performed for it (the jinn) for the sake of good omen (ṭīrah).""

Abū 'Ubayd said: ""This is the explanation of the hadith, and its meaning is that they would have recourse to this act out of fear that if they did not slaughter and feed, something from the jinn might befall them and harm them. So the Prophet (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) invalidated this and forbade it."" [Weak]"
19359,"(19353) Abū 'Abdillāh the Hāfiẓ and Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī — said: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn 'Abdillāh ibn 'Abd al-Ḥakam informed us — Ibn Wahb informed me — Mālik ibn Anas and Ibn Abī Dhīb and 'Amr ibn al-Ḥārith and Yūnus ibn Yazīd and others — that Ibn Shihāb narrated to them from Abū Idrīs al-Khawlānī from Abū Tha'labah al-Khushanī (raḍiyallāhu 'anhu) that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) forbade the eating of every fanged beast of prey.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from 'Abdillāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik, and Yūnus and a group followed him — and Muslim narrated it from Abū al-Ṭāhir from Ibn Wahb from Mālik and Ibn Abī Dhīb and Yūnus, and from Hārūn al-Aylī from Ibn Wahb from 'Amr. [Authentic]"
19360,"(19354) And Abū 'Abdillāh the Hāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us — they said: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us — al-Rabī' ibn Sulaymān informed us — al-Shāfi'ī informed us — Ibn 'Uyaynah narrated from Ibn Shihāb from Abū Idrīs al-Khawlānī from Abū Tha'labah al-Khushanī — (ḥ) and Abū 'Abdillāh the Hāfiẓ narrated to us — Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us — Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us — al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us — Sufyān said: I heard al-Zuhrī saying: ""Abū Idrīs al-Khawlānī narrated to me from Abū Tha'labah al-Khushanī (raḍiyallāhu 'anhu) that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu 'alayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) forbade the eating of every fanged beast of prey.""

In the narration of al-Ḥumaydī: ""Every fanged beast (sabu').""

Al-Zuhrī said: ""I did not hear this hadith until I came to Syria.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from 'Abdillāh ibn Muḥammad, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others, all from Ibn 'Uyaynah. And he also extracted it from the hadith of Ma'mar, Yūsuf al-Mājishūn, and Ṣāliḥ ibn Kaysān from al-Zuhrī. [Authentic]"
19361,"(19355) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik informed us, from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Ḥakīm, from ʿUbaydah ibn Sufyān al-Ḥaḍramī, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), who said: ""The consumption of every predatory animal with fangs is forbidden."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1933]"
19362,"(19356) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī informed us: Mālik informed us, narrating it with his chain of narration, except that he said: ""Every predatory animal with fangs — its consumption is forbidden.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19363,"(19357) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Abū ʿAwanah informed us, from al-Ḥakam and Abī Bishr, from Maymūn ibn Mihrān, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) forbade every predatory animal with fangs and every bird with talons.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal, from Abū Dāwūd, and he also narrated it from the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah, from al-Ḥakam, thus in a marfūʿ form. And from the ḥadīth of Hushaym, from Abī Bishr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1934]"
19364,"(19358) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Hushaym informed us, from Abī Bishr, from Maymūn ibn Mihrān, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: ""The consumption of every predatory animal with fangs was forbidden, and every bird with talons.""

Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. And ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥakam al-Bunānī narrated it from Maymūn ibn Mihrān, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19365,"(19359) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdad: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz informed us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr informed us: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah informed us: Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah informed us, from ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥakam, from Maymūn ibn Mihrān, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) forbade every predatory animal with fangs and every bird with talons.

And similarly, Ibn Abī ʿAdī narrated it from Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19366,"(19360) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb informed us: Mālik ibn Anas and others informed me that Nāfiʿ informed them, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: ""There are five creatures for which there is no sin upon the one in iḥrām for killing them: the crow, the kite, the mouse, the scorpion, and the dog.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf, from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19367,"(19361) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb informed us: Mālik ibn Anas informed us, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dīnār, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), a similar narration.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf, from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar, from ʿAbd Allāh."
19368,"(19362) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdad: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz informed us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr informed us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah informed us, from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim, from his father, reaching the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), who said: ""There are five creatures for which there is no sin in killing them, whether in the ḥill or the ḥaram: the crow, the mouse, the kite, the scorpion, and the rabid dog.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb and others, from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19369,"(19363) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Minhāl informed us: Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ informed us: Maʿmar informed us, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: ""There are five vicious creatures that may be killed within the ḥaram: the scorpion, the kite, the piebald crow, the mouse, and the rabid dog.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, from Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ, and Muslim narrated it from al-Qawārīrī, from Yazīd, except that the two of them did not mention ""al-abqaʿ"" (the piebald). [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19370,"(19364) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Sinān al-Zāhid informed me: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzaymah informed us: Bundār and Abū Mūsā both said: Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Shuʿbah informed us. He said: I heard Qatādah narrating from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), that he said: ""There are five vicious creatures that may be killed in the ḥill and the ḥaram: the snake, the piebald crow, the mouse, the rabid dog, and the kite.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Bundār and Abū Mūsā, and he mentioned in it ""al-abqaʿ"" (the piebald). [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19371,"(19365) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdad; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn al-Khalīl al-Burjulānī narrated to us; Abū al-Naḍr Hāshim ibn al-Qāsim narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Masʿūdī narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad, from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""The snake is a transgressor (fāsiq), the scorpion is a transgressor, the mouse is a transgressor, and the crow is a transgressor."" A man said to al-Qāsim: ""Is the crow eaten?"" He said: ""Who would eat the crow after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) called it a transgressor?"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19372,"(19366) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us; Hushaym narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Abī Ziyād narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Nuʿm al-Bajalī narrated from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was asked about what the muḥrim (pilgrim in iḥrām) may kill. He said: ""The snake, the scorpion, the mouse, and drive away the crow but do not kill it, the rabid dog, the kite (ḥidʾah), and the predatory beast that attacks.""

And we have narrated in the chapter on Ḥajj the ḥadīth of Ibn al-Musayyab from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) regarding the killing of the snake and the wolf, and we have narrated the ḥadīth of Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ and others regarding the killing of the lizard (waẓagh). [Ḍaʿīf]"
19373,"(19367) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf narrated to us by dictation; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us; ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Hilālī narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj reported from ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd ibn Jubayr ibn Shaybah, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from Umm Sharīk (may Allah be pleased with her) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) commanded the killing of lizards (awzāgh) and said: ""It used to blow upon Ibrāhīm (peace be upon him) [in the fire].""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā, or from a man from ʿUbayd Allāh, and Muslim narrated it through other chains from Ibn Jurayj. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
19374,"(19368) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Maḥbūr al-Dahhān narrated to us; Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz narrated to us; Abū al-Azhar narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Sahl al-Mihrānī informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us; Abū al-Walīd ibn Burd al-Anṭākī narrated to us. Both said: al-Haytham ibn Jamīl narrated to us; Sharīk narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: ""Who would eat the crow when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) called it a transgressor (fāsiq)? By Allah, it is not among the good things (ṭayyibāt)."" It fell from my copy from al-Dahhān from his father, though it is in it. [Ḍaʿīf]"
19375,"(19369) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh al-Fārisī narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān al-Fārisī narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways narrated to me; my father narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from ʿAmrah bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, and from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she said: ""I am astonished at one who eats the crow when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) permitted its killing for the muḥrim and called it a transgressor (fāsiq). By Allah, it is not among the good things (ṭayyibāt)."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
19376,"(19370) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us; Hishām narrated from his father, who said: He was asked: ""Is the crow among the good things (ṭayyibāt)?"" He said: ""How can it be among the good things when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) called it 'the transgressor' (fāsiq)?"" ʿUrwah did not permit it. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19377,"(19371) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd al-Ṣayrafī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Hāshimī narrated in Ḥalab; Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us; Shuʿbah said: I asked al-Ḥakam about eating crows. He said: ""As for these large black ones, I dislike eating them. But as for those small ones which are called zāgh, there is no harm in eating them."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
19378,"(19372) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Sayyārī narrated to us; Abū al-Muwajjah narrated to us; Ṣadaqah ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us; ʿUmar ibn Zayd, a man from the people of Ṣanʿāʾ, narrated from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade the eating of cat meat and the eating of its price. [Ḍaʿīf]"
19379,"(19373) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Ṣanʿānī narrated to us in Makkah; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Dabarī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade the killing of four creatures among the crawling animals: the ant, the bee, the hoopoe (hudhud), and the shrike (ṣurad). [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19380,"(19374) Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed me; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl al-Shaʿrānī narrated to us; my grandfather narrated to me; Abū Thābit Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd narrated from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with both of them), from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), a similar narration."
19381,"Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakkī informed us; Abu al-Abbas, that is al-Aṣamm, narrated to us; Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Ibn Wahb narrated to us; he said: and I heard Ibn Jurayj from one who narrated to him, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbāṣ (may Allah be pleased with them both), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Four creatures shall not be killed: the ant, the bee, the hoopoe, and the shrike."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19382,"Abu al-Ḥasan ʿAḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj; he said: I was narrated to from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from Ibn ʿAbbāṣ (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade killing the ant, the bee, the shrike, and the hoopoe.""

Yaḥyā said: ""And I saw in the book of Sufyān, from Ibn Jurayj, from Ibn Abī Labīd, from al-Zuhrī, meaning this hadith."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was mentioned before it.]"
19383,"And Abu Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us; Abu Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Ibn Qutaybah and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Ramlī both said: Wāriṭh ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us; Khalaf ibn Ayyūb narrated to us; Khārijah, that is Ibn Muṣʿab, narrated to us, from ʿAbd al-Majīd ibn Suhayl, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbāṣ (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade eating the vulture (al-rahmah)."" I did not record it except through this chain of narration, and it is not strong. [Ḍaʿīf]"
19384,"Abu ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abu Bakr al-Qāḍī both said: Abu al-Abbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us; ʿAlī ibn Baḥr, that is al-Qaṭṭān, informed us; ʿAbd al-Muhaymin ibn ʿAbbās ibn Sahl ibn Saʿd al-Sāʿidī said: ""I heard my father narrate from my grandfather, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), that he forbade killing five creatures: the ant, the bee, the frog, the shrike, and the hoopoe."" ʿAbd al-Muhaymin ibn ʿAbbās is the only one who narrated it, and he is weak. And the hadith of ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) is the strongest narration that came regarding this topic.

And the strongest narration that came regarding the frog: [Ḍaʿīf]"
19385,"What Abu al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Abu ʿĀṣim al-Ḍaḥḥākh ibn Makhlad narrated to us, from Ibn Abī Dhiʾb, from Saʿīd ibn Khālid, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUthmān, a man from Banī Taym, who said: ""The frog was mentioned before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) for medicinal purposes, and he forbade killing it."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
19386,"Abu al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdad; Abu ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk narrated to us; Ḥanbal ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Abū Uways narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muʿāwiyah, Abū al-Ḥuwayrith al-Murādī, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), that he forbade killing the swallows (al-khaṭāṭīf) and said: ""Do not kill these refuge-seeking creatures, for they seek refuge with you from others.""

Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān narrated it from ʿAbbād ibn Isḥāq, from his father, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade killing the swallows that are the refuges of houses."" Both of these chains are interrupted. And Ḥamzah al-Naṣībī narrated a musnad hadith regarding it, except that he was accused of fabrication. [Ḍaʿīf]"
19387,"Abu ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abu Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abu al-Abbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed us; Ḥanẓalah ibn Abī Sufyān narrated to us, from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she said: ""The lizards (al-awzāgh) on the day the House of al-Maqdis (Jerusalem) were burned were blowing at the fire with their mouths, and the bat (al-waṭwāṭ) was extinguishing it with its wings.""

Abū Naṣr, meaning ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ, said: ""It is the bat (al-khaffāsh)."" [Ḥasan]"
19388,"And Abu ʿAbd Allāh and Abu Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abu al-Abbās narrated to us; Yaḥyā narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Hishām al-Dastawāʾī narrated to us, from Qatādah, from Zurārah ibn Awfā, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: ""Do not kill frogs, for their croaking is glorification (tasbīḥ), and do not kill the bat, for when the House of al-Maqdis (Jerusalem) was destroyed, it said: 'O Lord, grant me power over the sea so that I may drown them.'"" These two narrations are mawqūf (stopped) regarding the bat, and their chains of narration are sound.

As for that which is commanded to be killed in both the Ḥill (non-sacred territory) and the Ḥaram (sacred territory), its consumption is forbidden, for if it were permissible (ḥalāl), He would not have commanded its killing in the Ḥaram or during Iḥrām. And Allah has forbidden the killing of game during Iḥrām. And that which is forbidden to be killed, its consumption is forbidden, for if it were permissible, He would have commanded its slaughter and would not have forbidden its killing, just as He did not forbid the killing of that whose slaughter and consumption is permissible. And Allah knows best. [Ḥasan]"
19389,"Abu Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated among others; they said: Abu al-Abbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam narrated to us; Ibn Wahb informed us; Ibn Jurayj informed us, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr al-Laythī, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī ʿAmmār, who said: ""I said to Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh (may Allah be pleased with him): 'Do I eat the porcupine (al-ḍabʿ)?' He said: 'Yes.' I said: 'Is it game (ṣayd)?' He said: 'Yes.' I said: 'Did you hear that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family)?' He said: 'Yes.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19390,"And Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq, and Abu Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan, and Abu Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr all said: Abu al-Abbās, that is al-Aṣamm, narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Muslim, ʿAbd al-Majīd, and ʿUbayd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith informed us, from Ibn Jurayj, and he mentioned it with its meaning. Abu Saʿīd added in his narration that al-Shāfiʿī said: ""And the meat of porcupines (al-ḍibāʿ) was sold in Makkah only between al-Ṣafā and al-Marwah."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19391,"(19385) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī informed us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Saʿīd ibn ʿUfayr narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us: al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb narrated to me from Ibn Jurayj — he informed him. (H) And ʿAlī informed us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥammād narrated to us: Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Umayyah, Ibn Jurayj, and Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim narrated to me that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr narrated to them: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī ʿAmmār informed me that he asked Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh (may Allah be pleased with him) about the hyena, and he mentioned it in a similar manner. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19392,"(19386) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr informed us: Ḥassān ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Ibrāhīm al-Ṣāʾigh narrated from ʿAṭāʾ, from Jābir (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), who said: ""The hyena is game, and its recompense is a mature sheep, and it may be eaten."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19393,"(19387) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us: Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Abī Jaʿfar informed us: Abū Muḥammad narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Maʿqil al-Sulamī, the companion of al-Dathniyyah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what do you say about the hyena?"" He said: ""I do not eat it, and I do not forbid it."" He said: I said: ""Since you do not forbid it, then I shall eat it."" He said: I said: ""O Prophet of Allah, what do you say about the gecko?"" He said: ""I do not eat it, and I do not forbid it."" He said: I said: ""Since you do not forbid it, then I shall eat it."" He said: I said: ""O Prophet of Allah, what do you say about the hare?"" He said: ""I do not eat it, and I do not declare it forbidden."" He said: I said: ""Since you do not declare it forbidden, then I shall eat it."" He said: I said: ""O Prophet of Allah, what do you say about the wolf?"" He said: ""Would anyone eat that?"" So I said: ""O Prophet of Allah, what do you say about the fox?"" He said: ""Would anyone eat that?""

It is narrated from ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn Abī al-Mukhāriq, from Ḥibbān ibn Jazʾ, from his brother Khuzaymah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family)…"" and he mentioned the hadith, agreeing with al-Sulamī in some of his hadith and differing in some of it. Both chains of narration contain weakness.

And it is narrated in our book of Ḥajj from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, and ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them) that they ruled that for the hyena, a sheep is to be given as recompense if a muḥrim kills it. [Ḍaʿīf]"
19394,"(19388) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā informed us: Abū al-Minḥāl Naṣr ibn Aws al-Ṭāʾī, a trustworthy Kūfan, narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Zayd, who said: ""I asked Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) about the young of the hyena, and he said: 'That is the farʿul — it is like a young sheep.'"" [Ḥasan]"
19395,"(19389) And Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan al-Kārizī informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us from Abī ʿUbayd, who said: Muḥammad ibn Rabīʿah al-Ruʾāsī narrated to us from Naṣr ibn Aws, from his uncle, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that he was asked about the hyena, and he said: ""The farʿul — that is like a sheep."" Abū ʿUbayd said: ""The farʿul, according to the Arabs, is the young of the hyena. What is intended by this hadith is his statement: 'It is like a sheep,' meaning that it is permissible, equivalent in status to sheep."" [Ḥasan]"
19396,"(19390) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥbūb informed us: ʿAbd al-Majīd ibn Ibrāhīm informed us: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ziyād narrated from Shuʿbah, from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Mayṣarah, from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said: ""A letter from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) reached them while they were on one of the military expeditions: 'It has reached me that you are in a land where you eat a food called cheese — so examine what of it is permissible and what is forbidden. And you wear furs — so examine what is properly slaughtered and what is carrion.'"" [Ḥasan]"
19397,"(19391) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Qāsim ibn al-Qāsim al-Sayyārī informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Saʿd al-Dashtakī informed us: My father narrated to me: My father narrated to me: Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān narrated to me: Yūnus ibn Khabbāb narrated to me: Abū ʿUbayd Allāh narrated from Salmān (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) was asked about cheese, clarified butter, and furs. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: ""The permissible is that which Allah has made permissible in the Qurʾān, and the forbidden is that which Allah has forbidden in the Qurʾān, and that concerning which He has remained silent — He has pardoned it.""

Sīf ibn Hārūn narrated it from al-Taymī, from Abū ʿUthmān, from Salmān, as a marfūʿ hadith, except that he said in his book: ""And that is to be taken back, if Allah wills."" [Munkar]"
19398,"(19392) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh, Abū Muslim, informed us: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb informed us: Shuʿbah narrated from Hishām ibn Zayd ibn Anas, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""We started a hare at Marr al-Ẓahrān. The people pursued it and grew exhausted. I caught it, took it, and brought it to Abū Ṭalḥah. He slaughtered it and sent its haunch and thigh to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family)."" He said: ""Its thigh — I have no doubt about that — and he accepted it."" I said: ""Did he eat from it?"" He said: ""He ate from it."" Then he said afterwards: ""He accepted it.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon]"
19399,"(19393) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed me: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed me: Abū al-Walīd informed us: Shuʿbah narrated from Hishām ibn Zayd, who said: ""I heard Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) say: 'We started a hare while we were at Marr al-Ẓahrān. The people pursued it and grew exhausted. I took it and brought it to Abū Ṭalḥah. He slaughtered it and sent its two haunches and two thighs to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), and he accepted them.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Walīd, and Muslim narrated it through other chains from Shuʿbah, similar to the hadith of Abū al-Walīd. And ʿAffān narrated from Shuʿbah, who said in it: ""I said: 'Did he eat it?' He said: 'He accepted it.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19400,"(19394) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Marwān informed us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us: ʿĀṣim narrated from al-Shaʿbī, from Ṣafwān ibn Muḥammad or Muḥammad ibn Ṣafwān (may Allah be pleased with him) that he hunted two hares and could not find an iron instrument with which to slaughter them, so he slaughtered them with a stone. He then came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) and mentioned that to him, and he commanded him to eat them. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19401,"(19395) Abū Bakr ibn Furak informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from ʿĀṣim, who said: I heard al-Shaʿbī narrating from Muḥammad ibn Ṣafwān (may Allah be pleased with him) that he caught a hare and slaughtered it with a sharp stone, then he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and mentioned that to him, whereupon he commanded him to eat it.

It was corroborated by Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind from al-Shaʿbī from Muḥammad ibn Ṣafwān. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19402,"(19396) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed him, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Usāmah narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us, Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind narrated from al-Shaʿbī, from Muḥammad ibn Ṣafwān (may Allah be pleased with him), that he passed by the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) with two hares hanging, and he said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I hunted these two hares but could not find any iron instrument with which to slaughter them, so I slaughtered them with a sharp stone. May I eat them?"" He said: ""Eat.""

It was also narrated from al-Shaʿbī from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, but the ḥadīth of Ibn Ṣafwān is more authentic, as stated by al-Bukhārī. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19403,"(19397) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed him — by recitation — and Abū Muḥammad ʿUbayd ibn Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Mahdī al-Qushayrī verbally, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn Aṭāʾ informed us, Saʿīd — who is Ibn Abī ʿArūbah — narrated from Qatādah, from al-Shaʿbī, from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh (may Allah be pleased with him), that a young man from his people caught a hare and slaughtered it with a sharp stone, then hung it up. He asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) about eating it, and he commanded him to eat it.

It is also narrated from ʿUmar ibn ʿĀmir from Qatādah with a similar meaning, and Hammām sent it as a mursal narration from Qatādah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19404,"(19398) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, al-ʿAbbās al-Dūrī narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us, Sufyān narrated from Jābir, from al-Shaʿbī, from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: A young man from Banū Hāshim brought a hare to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), dragging it, and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I entered Mount Uḥud and hunted this hare, but I could not find anything with which to slaughter it, so I slaughtered it with a sharp stone."" He said: ""Eat it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19405,"(19399) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān al-ʿĀmirī narrated to us, Abū Yaḥyā al-Ḥimmānī narrated from Abū Ḥanīfah, Mūsā ibn Ṭalḥah narrated to me from Ibn al-Hawtakiyyah, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) was asked about the hare. He said: ""Were it not that I dislike adding to or subtracting from this ḥadīth, I would narrate it to you. But rather, I will send for one who witnessed it."" So he sent for ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (may Allah be pleased with him) and said to him: ""Narrate to these people the ḥadīth of the hare."" ʿAmmār said: ""A Bedouin presented a roasted hare to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), and he commanded us to eat it, though he himself did not eat. A man held back and did not eat. He said to him: 'What is the matter with you?' He said: 'I am fasting.' He said: 'What fast is it?' He said: 'I fast three days of every month.' The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: 'Why do you not make them the white days?' The Bedouin said: 'I saw blood on it.' The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: 'It is nothing.'"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
19406,"(19400) He said: And Abū Yaḥyā narrated to us from Ṭalḥah ibn Yaḥyā, from Mūsā, the same, except that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""Why do you not make them the white days — the thirteenth, the fourteenth, and the fifteenth?"" [Ḍaʿīf]"
19407,"(19401) Abū Bakr ibn Furak informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, al-Masʿūdī narrated from Ḥakīm ibn Jubayr, from Mūsā ibn Ṭalḥah, from Ibn al-Hawtakiyyah, who said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) was brought a hare. He mentioned the meaning of this story but did not mention the question from anyone other than ʿAmmār. [Ḍaʿīf]"
19408,"(19402) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī narrated from Zāʾidah ibn Qudāmah, from Ḥakīm ibn Jubayr, from Mūsā ibn Ṭalḥah, who said: ʿUmar said to Abū Dharr, ʿAmmār, and Abū al-Dardāʾ (may Allah be pleased with them): ""Do you remember the day when we were with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) at such and such a place, and a Bedouin came to him with a hare and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I saw blood on it.' He commanded us to eat it, though he himself did not eat."" They said: ""Yes."" Then he said to him: ""Come closer and eat."" He said: ""I am fasting."" Ibn al-Hawtakiyyah was not mentioned in its isnād. [Ḍaʿīf]"
19409,"(19403) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Khalaf narrated to us, Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Khālid narrated, who said: I heard my father — Khālid ibn al-Ḥuwayrith — say: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr (may Allah be pleased with them both) was at al-Ṣafā, a place in Makkah, and a man came to us with a hare he had hunted. He said: ""O ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, what do you say?"" He said: ""It was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) while I was sitting there. He neither ate it nor forbade its consumption, and he believed that it menstruates."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
19410,"(19404) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Jarīr narrated from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Rufayʿ, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Qatādah, who said: Abū Qatādah was among a group of people in the state of iḥrām when a wild ass appeared to them. They did not alert him until he himself saw it. He snatched a whip from one of them, charged at it, and felled it, then brought it to them and they ate it. They met the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and asked him. He said: ""Did any one of you point it out to him with anything?"" They said: ""No."" He said: ""Then eat.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through various chains from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Qatādah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19411,"(19405) It was narrated by 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Qatadah, from Abu 'Amr ibn Najid, from Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-'Abdiyy, from Ibn Bukayr, from Malik, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, who said: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Harith al-Taymi informed me, from 'Isa ibn Talhah ibn 'Ubayd Allah, from 'Umayr ibn Salamah al-Damri, who reported on the authority of al-Bahzi: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) set out intending Makkah while he was in the state of ihram. When he arrived at al-Rawha', a wild ass that was wounded was brought to his attention. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Leave it, for its owner will soon come."" Then al-Bahzi, who was its owner, came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, do with this ass as you wish."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) instructed Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), and he divided it among the companions. [Sahih]"
19412,"(19406) It was narrated by Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz, Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq, and Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan, who said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Hakam informed us, Ibn Wahb informed us, Ibn Jurayj informed me that Abu al-Zubayr al-Makki informed him that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""We ate horses and wild asses at the time of Khaybar, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) prohibited the domestic ass."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu al-Tahir, from Ibn Wahb. [Sahih]"
19413,"(19407) It was narrated by Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf al-Isbahani, from Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi, from Sa'dan ibn Nasr, from Sufyan, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Zahdam al-Jarmi, that Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) eating chicken."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Ibn Abi 'Umar, from Ibn 'Uyaynah, and al-Bukhari narrated it through another chain from Ayyub. [Sahih, agreed upon]"
19414,"(19408) It was narrated by 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan, from Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar, from Tamtam, from Sulayman ibn Dawud al-Minqari, from Ibn Abi Fudayk, from Burayh ibn 'Umar ibn Safinah — and it was narrated by Abu Tahir al-Husayn ibn 'Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Salamah al-Hamadhani, from 'Umar ibn Nuh, from Abu Muhammad Babawayh ibn Khalid ibn Babawayh, from al-Nadir ibn Tahir Abu al-Hajjaj, from Burayh ibn 'Umar ibn Safinah, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: ""I ate bustard (hubara) with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him)."" Narrations have been transmitted from the Companions and the Tabi'un regarding the compensation (jaza') for the game animals we have mentioned, as well as for the hyena, the jerboa, and others. [Da'if]"
19415,"(19409) It was narrated by Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Ishaq Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Mu'awiyah al-'Attar, who said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us, al-Shafi'i informed us, Malik narrated from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with both of them), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was asked about the lizard (al-dabb), and he said: ""I do not eat it, nor do I declare it forbidden."" Muslim narrated it from the hadith of al-Layth, 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar, Ayyub, and others, from Nafi'. [Sahih]"
19416,"(19410) It was narrated by Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz, from Abu al-'Abbas, from al-Rabi', from al-Shafi'i, from Sufyan, from 'Abd Allah ibn Dinar — and it was narrated by Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz, from Bukayr ibn Muhammad al-Haddad in Makkah, from Ya'qub ibn Ishaq al-Bayhasi, from 'Affan, from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muslim, from 'Abd Allah ibn Dinar, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was asked about the lizard, and he said: 'I do not eat it, nor do I declare it forbidden.'"" Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Musa ibn Isma'il, from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muslim, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from 'Abd Allah. [Sahih, agreed upon]"
19417,"(19411) It was narrated by Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzaki in Naysabur, who said: Abu al-Husayn Ahmad ibn 'Uthman narrated to us, Abu Qilabah 'Abd al-Malik ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Wahb ibn Jarir narrated from Shu'bah, from Tawbah al-'Anbari, who said: al-Sha'bi said to me: ""What do you think of al-Hasan when he narrates from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him)? I sat with Ibn 'Umar for nearly two years and I did not hear him narrate from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) except that one day he said: 'Some of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) were eating lizard in his presence, among them Sa'd ibn Malik, when a woman from the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) called out: ""It is a lizard!"" So the people stopped. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Eat, for it is not forbidden, and there is nothing wrong with it, but it is not the food of my people.""'"" In the narration of Abu Zakariyya: ""or there is nothing wrong with it."" Al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it in their two Sahihs from Ghundar and others, from Shu'bah. [Sahih, agreed upon]"
19418,"(19412) It was narrated by Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz, from Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub, from al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman, from al-Shafi'i, from Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with both of them). Al-Shafi'i said: I am uncertain whether Malik said it from Ibn 'Abbas on the authority of Khalid ibn al-Walid, or from Ibn 'Abbas and Khalid ibn al-Walid (may Allah be pleased with them both): that they entered the house of Maymunah with the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and a roasted lizard was brought to him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) reached out for it with his hand, but some of the women in Maymunah's house said: ""Inform the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) of what he intends to eat."" They said: ""It is a lizard, O Messenger of Allah."" So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) withdrew his hand. I said: ""Is it forbidden?"" He said: ""No, but it is not found in the land of my people, so I find myself averse to it."" Khalid said: ""So I pulled it towards me and ate it while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was watching."" [Sahih, agreed upon]"
19419,"(19413) It was narrated by Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan, from Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar, from Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Qadi, from 'Abd Allah ibn Maslamah, i.e., al-Qa'nabi, from Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with both of them), from Khalid ibn al-Walid (may Allah be pleased with him): that he entered the house of Maymunah with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and mentioned the hadith with its like. Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from al-Qa'nabi. Isma'il ibn Abi Uways similarly narrated it from Malik, as al-Qa'nabi narrated it. [Sahih]"
19420,"(19414) It was narrated by Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz, from Abu al-Nadr al-Faqih, from Abu Musa Harun ibn Musa ibn Kathir ibn Mihran, from Yahya ibn Yahya, who said: I read to Malik, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: ""I and Khalid ibn al-Walid entered the house of Maymunah bint al-Harith with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)"" and he mentioned the hadith with its like. Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya. Similarly, Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated it from Malik, and it is as though Malik was uncertain about it. The correct narration is that of al-Qa'nabi and those who followed him. Yunus ibn Yazid narrated it, and Ma'mar in the narration of Hisham ibn Yusuf from him, and Salih ibn Kaysan narrated from al-Zuhri, in a manner similar to the narration of al-Qa'nabi from Malik. [Sahih]"
19421,"(19415) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Bishr ibn Aḥmad al-Isfarāyinī informed us that Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAqīl, al-Khusrūjirdī, narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Shuʿayb ibn al-Layth narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, that Khālid ibn Yazīd narrated to me from Saʿīd ibn Abī Hilāl, from Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir, that Abū Umāmah informed him from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was in the house of Maymūnah, and Khālid ibn al-Walīd was with him. Lizard meat was brought to them. Maymūnah said: ""Inform the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) what it is."" When he was informed, he left it. Khālid said: ""O Messenger of Allah, is it forbidden?"" He said: ""No, but I find it repugnant."" So Khālid took it and began gnawing on its bones.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Shuʿayb. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19422,"(19416) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Ṣādiq ibn Abī al-Fawāris both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us that Asbāṭ ibn Muḥammad narrated to us from al-Shaybānī, from Yazīd ibn al-Aṣamm, who said: We were invited to a wedding feast in Madīnah. Food was brought to us and we ate. Then thirteen lizards were brought to us—some ate and some left them. When morning came, I went to Ibn ʿAbbās and said: ""So-and-so was married, and food was brought to us which we ate, then thirteen lizards were brought to us—some ate and some left them."" Some of those present with Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with both of them) said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: 'I do not eat it, nor do I declare it forbidden, nor do I command it, nor do I prohibit it.'"" Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""What a bad thing you say! The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) was only sent as one who permits and forbids. Lizard meat was brought to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), and he extended his hand to eat it, but Maymūnah said to him: 'O Messenger of Allah, it is lizard meat.' So he withdrew his hand and said: 'This is meat I have never eaten before—so eat.'"" He said: So al-Faḍl ibn ʿAbbās, Khālid ibn al-Walīd, and a woman who were with them ate. Maymūnah said: ""I will not eat from food from which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) did not eat.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah, from ʿAlī ibn Mushir, from al-Shaybānī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
19423,"(19417) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Maḥmūyah al-ʿAskarī narrated to us that Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī narrated to us that Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us that Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us that Ādam narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated to us that Jaʿfar ibn Iyās narrated to us, saying: I heard Saʿīd ibn Jubayr narrating from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: Umm Ḥufayd, the maternal aunt of Ibn ʿAbbās, sent as a gift to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) dried yogurt, ghee, and lizards. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) ate from the dried yogurt and the ghee, but left the lizards out of aversion. Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""It was eaten at the table of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). Had it been forbidden, it would not have been eaten at his table.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ghundar from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muttafaq ʿalayh]"
19424,"(19418) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us that Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us that Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ narrated to us that Ḥabīb al-Muʿallim narrated to us from ʿAṭāʾ, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: A plate containing lizards was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family). He said: ""Eat, for I find it repugnant."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19425,"(19419) As for the ḥadīth that Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me of—Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbdillāh informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us that Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us that ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us from Ibn Jurayj, who informed me that Abū al-Zubayr said: I heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: A lizard was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), and he refused to eat it, saying: ""I do not know—perhaps it is from the early generations that were transformed."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm.

This is similar to the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUmar and Ibn ʿAbbās regarding his abstaining from eating it, but he added to them another reason for abstaining besides aversion, and he added to it that which indicates permissibility. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1949]"
19426,"(19420) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb and Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar both said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad narrated to us that Salamah ibn Shabīb narrated to us that al-Ḥasan ibn Aʿyan narrated to us that Maʿqil narrated to us from Abī al-Zubayr, who said: I asked Jābir (may Allah be pleased with him) about the lizard. He said: ""Do not eat it,"" and he found it repulsive. He said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) did not declare it forbidden. Allah, the Most High, benefits more than one person through it, for it is the food of the majority of the herders. Had it been with me, I would have eaten it."" Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Salamah ibn Shabīb.

Likewise, Sulaymān al-Yashkurī narrated it from Jābir, from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him).

And upon this is the ḥadīth of Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1950]"
19427,"(19421) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Burshān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us that Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz narrated to us that ʿAlī ibn Ibrāhīm al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us that Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us that Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind informed us from Abū Naḍrah, from Abū Saʿīd (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: A man came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) and said: ""We are in a land abundant with lizards—what do you command us?"" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""It has reached me that a nation from the Children of Israel was transformed into beasts, and I do not know which of the beasts it is."" So he neither commanded him nor forbade him.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned]"
19428,"(19422) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Shaybānī informed me that my father informed us that Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us that Ibn Abī ʿAdī narrated to us from Dāwūd, from Abū Naḍrah, from Abū Saʿīd, with the meaning of this ḥadīth. Abū Saʿīd said: After that, ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) stood up and said: ""Allah benefits more than one person through it, and it is the food of the majority of these herders. Had it been with me, I would have eaten it. It was only the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) who found it repugnant.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1951]"
19429,"(19423) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us that Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that Shuʿbah narrated to us that Abū ʿAqīl Bashīr ibn ʿUqbah narrated to us that Abū Naḍrah narrated to us from Abū Saʿīd (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: A Bedouin asked the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), saying: ""O Messenger of Allah, I am in a walled garden abundant with lizards, and it is the staple food of my family."" He remained silent, so we said: ""Repeat your question."" He repeated it, and he remained silent. We said again: ""Repeat your question."" He repeated it a third time, and he said: ""O Bedouin, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, was angry with two tribes of the Children of Israel and transformed them into beasts that crawl upon the earth. I do not know—perhaps it is some of them. I am neither forbidding you from it nor commanding you with it.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from another chain from Abū ʿAqīl, and he said: ""I neither eat it nor forbid it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19430,"(19424) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Burshān in Baghdād informed us that Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us that Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us that al-Aʿmash narrated to us from Zayd ibn Wahb, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥasanah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: We were on a journey and were overcome by hunger. We stopped at a place abundant with lizards. While the cooking pots were boiling with them, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) said: ""A nation from the Children of Israel was transformed, and I fear that these may be among them."" So we overturned the cooking pots.

Thus narrated al-Aʿmash from Zayd. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19431,"(19425) Al-Hakam ibn Utaybah narrated from Zayd, as Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz, Abu Bakr al-Qadi, and Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Sub'iyy informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas — al-Asamm — narrated to us: al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affan narrated to us: 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us.

(Ch) And Abu Sa'id — al-Khalil ibn Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Qadi al-Bustiyy — informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Ahmad ibn al-Muzaffar al-Bakri informed us: Ibn Abi Khaythamah narrated to us: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated from al-Hakam from Zayd ibn Wahb from al-Bara' ibn 'Azib (may Allah be pleased with him) from Thabit ibn Wadi'ah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) was brought a lizard, and he said: ""It is from among a nation that was transformed — and Allah knows best."" In the narration of 'Ubaydullah: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) was brought a lizard, and he said: ""Indeed, a nation was transformed — and Allah knows best.""

Thus said al-Hakam. And Husayn narrated it from Zayd ibn Wahb from Thabit ibn Wadi'ah, and it was said: Thabit ibn Yazid al-Ansari, Yazid being his father and Wadi'ah his mother. It is in the meaning of the hadiths of those before it, and there is no prohibition in it — and Allah knows best. Al-Bukhari said: The hadith of Thabit ibn Wadi'ah is more authentic, and there is room for consideration within the hadith itself. [Sahih]"
19432,"(19426) Abu Bakr ibn Furak informed us: 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated from Hammad from Ibrahim from al-Aswad from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: A lizard was gifted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), but he did not eat it. I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, shall we not feed it to the poor?"" He said: ""Do not feed them what you yourselves do not eat.""

This was narrated uniquely by Hammad ibn Abi Sulayman in a connected (mawsul) chain. And it was also reported from him: from Ibrahim from 'A'ishah, in a disconnected (mursal) chain. [Munkar]"
19433,"(19427) Ibn Bishran informed it: Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Amr narrated to us: Ahmad ibn al-Walid al-Fahham narrated to us: Abu Ahmad al-Zubayri narrated to us: Sufyan narrated from Hammad from Ibrahim from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: A lizard was gifted to us, and I presented it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), but he did not eat from it. I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, shall we not feed it to the beggars?"" He said: ""Indeed, we do not feed them what we ourselves do not eat.""

And if it is established, it is in the meaning of what preceded regarding his abstention from eating it. And in it is that he preferred not to feed the poor what he himself did not eat — and with Allah lies success. [Da'if]"
19434,"(19428) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us: 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far ibn Durustawayh narrated to us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us: Abu al-Yaman narrated to us: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated from Damdam ibn Zur'ah from Shurayh ibn 'Ubayd from Abu Rashid al-Hubrani from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Shibl that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) forbade the eating of [it]. This is narrated uniquely by Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash, and he is not a decisive authority (hujjah). What has preceded regarding its permissibility is more authentic than it — and Allah knows best. [Da'if]"
19435,"(19429) Abu Tahir al-Faqih and Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi both informed us, saying: Hajib ibn Ahmad al-Tusi narrated to us: 'Abd al-Rahim ibn Munib narrated to us: al-Fadl ibn Musa narrated to us: al-Husayn ibn Waqid narrated from Ayyub from Nafi' from Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said: ""I wish we had a white loaf of wheat, moistened with ghee and milk."" A man from among the people stood up, prepared it, and brought it. He asked: ""In what was this stored?"" He said: ""In a lizard-skin pouch."" He said: ""Take it away.""

Abu Dawud narrated it in the Sunan and said: This is a munkar hadith. [Da'if]"
19436,"(19430) Abu Ishaq said: I was with 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdillah ibn Mas'ud when his son — al-Qasim, as I think — came. He said: ""Did you obtain something of what you needed today?"" Some of the people said: ""What did he need?"" He said: ""I have not seen a boy more eager to eat a lizard than him."" Some of the people said: ""Is it not haram?"" He asked: ""Who made it haram?"" They said: ""Did not the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) dislike it?"" He said: ""A man may dislike something without it being haram."" 'Abdullah said: ""Whoever declares halal to be haram is like whoever declares haram to be halal."" [Sahih]"
19437,"(19431) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Abu Thawr Ibrahim ibn Khalid al-Kalbi narrated to us: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated from 'Isa ibn Numaylah from his father, who said: I was with Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) when he was asked about eating the hedgehog. He recited: **""Say: I do not find in what has been revealed to me anything forbidden""** (the verse). An elder who was with him said: ""I heard Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) say: It was mentioned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), and he said: 'It is among the foul things (khaba'ith).'"" Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) said: ""If the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) said this, then it is as he said."" This hadith was not narrated except through this chain, and it is a chain containing weakness. [Da'if]"
19438,"(19432) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran in Baghdad informed us: Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Amr narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Zuhayr narrated to us: Hawdah ibn Khalifah narrated to us: 'Awf narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Abi Wahshiyyah narrated from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, who said: Umm Hufayd brought a lizard and a hedgehog to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), and he placed it before him, but he pushed it away and did not eat it. This is a mursal narration.

(T) And we have narrated it from the hadith of Shu'bah, from Ja'far — Abu Bishr — in a connected (mawsul) chain, without mention of the hedgehog. Similarly, Abu 'Awanah narrated it from Abu Bishr in a connected chain, without mention of the hedgehog. And even if this is authentic, it does not indicate prohibition; rather, it is as though he found it repugnant, just as he found the lizard repugnant. [Da'if]"
19439,"(19433) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Musa ibn Isma'il narrated to us: Ghalib ibn Hajrah narrated to me: Miqlam ibn Talib narrated from his father, who said: I accompanied the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family), and I did not hear any prohibition of the creatures of the earth. And even if this is authentic, it does not indicate permissibility. What he did not hear, others did hear, and the ruling belongs to the one who heard rather than to him. And we have narrated from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) what indicates the prohibition of the scorpion and the snake, and likewise anything that shares in their meaning among the things that the Arabs find repulsive and do not eat except out of necessity — and Allah knows best. [Da'if]"
19440,"(19434) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us: Isma'il al-Qadi narrated to us: Sulayman ibn Harb and Musaddad both narrated to us: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated from 'Amr ibn Dinar from Muhammad ibn 'Ali from Jabir ibn 'Abdillah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) forbade, on the day of Khaybar, the meat of domestic donkeys, and he permitted the meat of horses. Sulayman did not mention ""domestic"" in his hadith, and Musaddad said in his hadith: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him and his family) forbade us.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Sulayman ibn Harb and Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Yahya ibn Yahya and others from Hammad ibn Zayd. [Sahih. Agreed upon (Muttafaq 'alayh)]"
19441,"(19435) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; Ḥammād narrated from Abī al-Zubayr, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: ""We slaughtered horses, mules, and donkeys on the day of Khaybar. The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) prohibited us from [eating] mules and donkeys, but he did not prohibit us from [eating] horses."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19442,"(19436) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī and Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bashrān both said: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Wakīʿ narrated from Sufyān, from ʿAbd al-Karīm, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Jābir (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said: ""We used to eat the meat of horses."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19443,"(19437) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ṣāʿid narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Bukayr al-Ḥaḍramī narrated to us; Sharīk narrated from ʿAbd al-Karīm, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Jābir (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu), who said: ""We travelled—that is, with the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam)—and we used to eat the meat of horses and drink their milk."" [Ṣaḥīḥ, without the mention of drinking the milk]"
19444,"(19438) Abū Bakr informed us; ʿAlī narrated to us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ṣāʿid narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ḥakīm, Abū Saʿīd, narrated to us; Kathīr ibn Hishām narrated to us; Furāt ibn Salmān narrated from ʿAbd al-Karīm al-Jazarī, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ, from Jābir (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu): ""They used to eat the meat of horses during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19445,"(19439) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān al-ʿĀmirī narrated to us; Abū Usāmah narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from Fāṭimah bint al-Mundhir, from Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā), who said: ""We ate the meat of a horse during the time of the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam).""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Kurayb, from Abī Usāmah, and al-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it through other chains from Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ, agreed upon]"
19446,"(19440) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī both said: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Farrāʾ informed us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us; Hishām narrated it with the same wording as the ḥadīth of Abī Usāmah, and added therein: ""And we were in Madīnah."" ʿAbdah ibn Sulaymān also narrated it from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah."
19447,"(19441) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās, al-Aṣamm, informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān said: al-Shāfiʿī said: Sufyān informed us from Hishām, from Fāṭimah, from Asmāʾ (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhā), who said: ""We slaughtered a horse during the time of the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and ate it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ, agreed upon]"
19448,"(19442) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us; al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us; Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated from Fāṭimah bint al-Mundhir, from her grandmother Asmāʾ (raḍiyallāhu ʿanhā), who said: ""We slaughtered a horse during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and ate it.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥumaydī, and they both [al-Bukhārī and Muslim] narrated it through other chains from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19449,"(19443) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās, al-Aṣamm, informed us; al-Rabīʿ said: al-Shāfiʿī said: Sufyān informed us from ʿAbd al-Karīm, Abī Umayyah, who said: ""I ate horse meat during the time of Ibn al-Zubayr and found it to be sweet [and pleasant]."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
19450,"(19444) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bashrān informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Wakīʿ narrated from Shuʿbah, from Yūnus, from al-Ḥasan, who said: ""There is no harm in [eating] the meat of a horse."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19451,"(19445) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us, Ibn Fuḍayl narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Muslim from al-Ḥasan, who said: We went on a military expedition with ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Samurah to Sijistān, and he mentioned the hadith, saying: And we used to eat the flesh of horses during this expedition of ours.

And it has been narrated to us from Ibrāhīm from al-Aswad that he ate the flesh of a horse. [Weak]"
19452,"(19446) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn al-Muṣaffá narrated to us, Baqiyyah narrated to me, Thawr ibn Yazīd narrated from Ṣāliḥ ibn Yaḥyā ibn al-Miqdām from his father from his grandfather from Khālid ibn al-Walīd (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade the flesh of horses, mules, and donkeys, and every beast of prey possessing a fang. [Weak]"
19453,"(19447) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us, saying: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Ibn Mubashshir narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Sinān al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUmar al-Wāqidī narrated to us, Thawr ibn Yazīd narrated it with its chain, similar to it, except that he said: He forbade it on the day of Khaybar. And Muḥammad ibn Ḥumayr narrated it from Thawr from Ṣāliḥ that he heard his grandfather al-Miqdām (narrate it). And ʿUmar ibn Hārūn al-Balkhī narrated it from Thawr from Yaḥyā ibn al-Miqdām from his father from Khālid. Thus, this chain is confused (muḍṭarib), and in addition to its confusion, it contradicts the narration of the trustworthy narrators. [Weak]"
19454,"(19448) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fārisī informed us, Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh informed us, Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān ibn Fāris narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī said: Ṣāliḥ ibn Yaḥyā ibn al-Miqdām ibn Maʿdīkarib al-Kindī al-Shāmī — from his father; Thawr and Sulaymān ibn Sulaym narrated from him. There is some question regarding him."
19455,"(19449) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us, saying: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād narrated to us, saying: I heard Mūsā ibn Hārūn say: Ṣāliḥ ibn Yaḥyā and his father are not known except through his grandfather, and this is weak. And al-Wāqidī claimed that Khālid ibn al-Walīd accepted Islam after the conquest of Khaybar."
19456,"(19450) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsá narrated to us, Abū Yaḥyā al-Khaffāf, Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlī, and Mūsā ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, saying: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, saying: I read to Mālik from Ibn Shihāb from ʿAbdullāh and al-Ḥasan, the two sons of Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī, from their father, from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade mutʿah (temporary marriage) with women on the day of Khaybar, and the flesh of domestic donkeys.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf and others from Mālik. [Sound — agreed upon]"
19457,"(19451) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿUqbah al-Shaybānī informed us in al-Kūfah, Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Abī al-ʿAnbas al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated from ʿUbaydullāh from Nāfiʿ and Sālim from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade the eating of the flesh of domestic donkeys.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Naṣr from Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd, and Muslim narrated it through another route from ʿUbaydullāh. [Sound — agreed upon]"
19458,"(19452) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, al-Ḥasan — that is, Ibn Sufyān — informed us, Ibn Numayr narrated to us, my father narrated to us, ʿUbaydullāh narrated.

(Ḥ) And al-Ḥasan informed me, Muṣarrif ibn ʿAmr al-Yāmī narrated to us, ʿAbdah narrated to us, ʿUbaydullāh narrated from Nāfiʿ and Sālim from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade the flesh of domestic donkeys. ʿAbdah added: ""On the day of Khaybar."" And Ibn Numayr said: Nāfiʿ and Sālim narrated to me.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ṣadaqah ibn al-Faḍl from ʿAbdah ibn Sulaymān, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Numayr. [Sound — agreed upon]"
19459,"(19453) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us, Ismāʿīl al-Qāḍī informed us, ʿĀrim ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ said: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār from Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī from Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) forbade on the day of Khaybar the flesh of domestic donkeys and permitted the flesh of horses.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad from Ḥammād, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and others. [Sound — agreed upon]"
19460,"(19454) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, Abū Muslim narrated to us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb and ʿAmr ibn Marzūq narrated to us — and the wording is that of Sulaymān — saying: Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAdīy ibn Thābit from al-Barāʾ (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) when we came upon some donkeys and cooked them. Then he ordered a caller to announce — or he said: he announced — ""Turn over the cooking pots."" [Sound — agreed upon]"
19461,"(19455) He said: And Abū Muslim narrated to us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAdīy ibn Thābit from Ibn Abī Awfá (may Allah be pleased with him), a similar narration. Al-Bukhārī and Muslim recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains from Shuʿbah."
19462,"(19456) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salmān the jurist informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Salām and Jaʿfar the coppersmith narrated to us, ʿAffān narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAdīy ibn Thābit and Abū Isḥāq from al-Barāʾ and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Awfá (may Allah be pleased with them both): that they came upon domestic donkeys on the day of Khaybar and cooked them, whereupon the announcer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) proclaimed: ""Turn them over (i.e., pour out their contents)."" Muslim recorded it from the ḥadīth of Ghundar from Shuʿbah from Abū Isḥāq from al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
19463,"(19457) Abū ʿAmr al-Bisṭāmī informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, ʿImrān ibn Mūsá narrated to us, ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, Jarīr narrated from ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal from ʿĀmir from al-Barāʾ (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) commanded us to discard the meat of domestic donkeys, both raw and cooked, and thereafter he did not command us to eat it. Muslim recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb from Jarīr, and al-Bukhārī recorded it through another chain from ʿĀṣim. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
19464,"(19458) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to me, my father narrated to us, Ṣafwān ibn ʿĪsá narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us, Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Ḥammād ibn Masʿadah and Ṣafwān ibn ʿĪsá narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī ʿUbayd from Salamah al-Akwāʿ (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: When we arrived at Khaybar, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) saw fires burning and said: ""What are these fires being lit for?"" They said: ""For the meat of domestic donkeys."" He said: ""Break the pots and pour out what is in them."" A man from the people said: ""O Messenger of Allah, shall we pour out what is in them and wash them?"" He said: ""Or do that."" The wording is from the ḥadīth of Ibn Ḥanbal. Muslim recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm, and al-Bukhārī recorded it through another chain from Yazīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
19465,"(19459) As for the ḥadīth which Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us of: ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh the upright informed us, Bishr ibn Mūsá al-Asadī narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, who said: I said to Jābir ibn Zayd: ""They claim that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) prohibited the meat of domestic donkeys at the time of Khaybar."" He said: ""Al-Ḥakam ibn ʿAmr used to say that from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), but the Sea (i.e., Ibn ʿAbbās) rejected that and recited: 'Say: I do not find in what has been revealed to me anything forbidden for one who eats it' [al-Anʿām 145] — the verse. The people of the Jāhiliyyah used to abandon certain things out of disgust, so Allah, the Most High, revealed His Book and made clear its lawful and its unlawful. What He made lawful is lawful, what He made unlawful is unlawful, and what He remained silent about is pardonable."" Then he recited this verse: 'Say: I do not find in what has been revealed to me anything forbidden for one who eats it, unless it be a dead animal, or blood poured forth, or the flesh of swine' [al-Anʿām 145]. Al-Bukhārī recorded the beginning of it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī from Sufyān. Had Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both) known that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) had made it absolutely unlawful, he would not have turned to anything else — except that he did not know it. [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
19466,"(19460) And Abū Ṭāhir the jurist informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us, ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated to me, my father narrated from ʿĀṣim from ʿĀmir from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: ""I do not know whether the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) prohibited it because it was the beast of burden of the people and he disliked that their means of transport be destroyed, or whether he made unlawful the meat of domestic donkeys on the day of Khaybar."" Al-Bukhārī recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Ḥusayn from ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ, and Muslim recorded it from Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Azdī. [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
19467,"(19461) And in a similar ḥadīth, Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar informed us, ʿImrān ibn Mūsá informed us, Abū Kāmil narrated to us. They both said: ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād narrated to us, Sulaymān al-Shaybānī narrated to us. He said: I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Awfá (may Allah be pleased with him) say: ""We were afflicted with famine during the nights of Khaybar. On the day of Khaybar we came upon domestic donkeys and slaughtered them. When the pots came to a boil with them, the announcer of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) proclaimed: 'Turn over the pots and do not eat anything of the meat of the donkeys.' Some people said: 'The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) only prohibited it because it had not been subjected to the one-fifth (khums).' Others said: 'He prohibited it absolutely (al-battah).'"" The wording is from the ḥadīth of Abū Kāmil. In the narration of Ibn Abī Bukayr it is said: ""Some people said he made it absolutely unlawful (ḥarramahā al-battah)."" Al-Bukhārī recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl from ʿAbd al-Wāḥid, and Muslim recorded it from Abū Kāmil. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
19468,"(19462) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, Maḥmūd ibn Muḥammad al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us, Wahb narrated from Khālid from al-Shaybānī from Ibn Abī Awfá (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""We were afflicted with famine on the day of Khaybar."" Then he mentioned the ḥadīth. Al-Shaybānī said: ""I met Saʿīd ibn Jubayr and mentioned that to him, and he said: 'The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) prohibited it absolutely (al-battah) because it used to eat filth (al-adhiraah).'"" Al-Bukhārī recorded it from the ḥadīth of ʿAbbād ibn al-ʿAwwām from al-Shaybānī. A group of the Companions (may Allah be pleased with them) knew that the prohibition in this regard amounted to absolute unlawfulness (taḥrīm). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
19469,"(19463) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us, Yaḥyá — that is Ibn Bukayr — narrated to us, al-Layth narrated from ʿUqayl from Ibn Shihāb from Abū Idrīs from Abū Thaʿlabah al-Khushanī (may Allah be pleased with him), a Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him and his family) made unlawful the meat of donkeys and the meat of every predatory animal with fangs."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
19470,"(19464) And Abū al-Ḥasan informed us, Aḥmad informed us, Ibn Milḥān narrated to us, Yaḥyá narrated it with its chain, similar to it, except that he said: ""From Abū Idrīs al-Khawlānī,"" and he said ""meats"" (luḥūm) in both places. Al-Bukhārī recorded it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ṣāliḥ ibn Kaysān from Ibn Shihāb, then he said: ""Al-Zubaydī and ʿUqayl followed him, narrating from Ibn Shihāb."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned.]"
19471,"(19465) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabīb ibn ʿArabī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī narrated from Ayyūb, from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) was approached by a person who said, ""The donkeys have been eaten."" Then another person came to him and said, ""The donkeys have been eaten."" Then another person came to him and said, ""The donkeys have been exhausted."" So a crier called out among the people: ""Indeed, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and His Messenger forbid you from the meat of domestic donkeys, for they are impure."" He said: ""So the cooking pots were overturned while they were still boiling with meat.""

It was narrated by al-Bukhārī in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Salām and others, from ʿAbd al-Wahhāb. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon]"
19472,"(19466) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn Nuʿaym and Aḥmad ibn Sahl said: Ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from Ayyūb, from Muḥammad, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""When the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) conquered Khaybar, we came upon donkeys outside the village and cooked them. Then the crier of the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) called out: 'Indeed, Allah and His Messenger forbid you from them, for they are an abomination from the work of Satan.' So the cooking pots were overturned along with what was in them, while they were still boiling with their contents.""

It was narrated by Muslim in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar, and he also narrated it from the ḥadīth of Hishām ibn Ḥassān from Muḥammad, with the wording of the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Wahhāb, except that he said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) ordered Abū Ṭalḥah (may Allah be pleased with him), so he called out."" The reason cited in it indicates prohibition. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ — agreed upon]"
19473,"(19467) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Ḥārithī narrated to us, Ḥusayn al-Juʿfī narrated from Zāʾidah, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Laythī, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) forbade, on the day of Khaybar, every predator possessing a fang, the mujaththamah (animal killed by being tied and shot), and the domestic donkey. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19474,"(19468) Abū al-Qāsim ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Iyādī al-Mālikī in Baghdād narrated to us, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Sulamī narrated to us, Abū Ṣāliḥ narrated to me, Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to me, Ibn Jābir narrated that he heard Miqdām, the companion of the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), say: ""The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) forbade certain things on the day of Khaybar, among them the domestic donkey."" He (the narrator) said: ""And he (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: 'It is imminent that a man, reclining upon his couch, will be narrated a ḥadīth of mine and will say: ""Between us and you is the Book of Allah; whatever we find in it to be lawful, we shall deem lawful, and whatever we find in it to be forbidden, we shall deem forbidden."" Beware! Indeed, what the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) has forbidden is like what Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has forbidden.'""

This Ibn Jābir is al-Ḥasan ibn Jābir. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated it from Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19475,"(19469) And its witness is what Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī in Baghdād informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Tarqafī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn al-Mubārak narrated to me, Yaḥyā ibn Ḥamzah narrated to me, Muḥammad ibn al-Walīd al-Zubaydī narrated from Marwān ibn Ruʾbah, who narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī ʿAwf al-Jurashī, from al-Miqdām ibn Maʿdīkarib al-Kindī, from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam), who said: ""I was given the Book and what is equal to it"" — meaning the Sunnah. ""It is imminent that a man, satiated upon his couch, will say: 'Between us and you is this Book; whatever is in it of lawful things, we shall deem lawful, and whatever is in it of forbidden things, we shall deem forbidden.' But it is not so. Beware! It is not lawful to eat any predator possessing a fang, nor the domestic donkey, nor the found property of a person under a covenant of protection, unless one has no need of it. And any man who is hosted by a people and they do not honor him, it is his right to take from them the equivalent of his hospitality."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19476,"(19470) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū Ṭalḥah Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Karīm narrated to us, Bundār narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to us, Isrāʾīl narrated from Majzaʾah ibn Zāhir, from his father, who said — and he had pledged allegiance to the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) beneath the tree — that he fell ill and it was prescribed for him to use the milk and broth of female donkeys, but he (the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) disliked that. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19477,"(19471) As for the ḥadīth which Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Ziyād narrated to us, ʿUbaydullāh narrated from Isrāʾīl, from Manṣūr, from ʿUbayd Abī al-Ḥasan, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān — that is, Ibn Maʿqil — from Ghālib ibn Abjar, who said: ""A year of famine befell us, and there was nothing in my possession with which to feed my family except some donkeys. The Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) had forbidden the meat of domestic donkeys. So I came to the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, a year of famine has befallen us, and there is nothing in my possession with which to feed my family except fat donkeys, and you have forbidden the meat of domestic donkeys.' He said: 'Feed your family from your fat donkeys, for I only forbade them on account of the animals that roam about the villages.'""

This is a ḥadīth whose chain of narration differs. Shuʿbah narrated it in one of the two versions from ʿUbayd, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Maʿqil, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr, from some men of Muzaynah, that Abjar or Ibn Abjar asked the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam). And in another version from him, from ʿUbaydullāh, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Maʿqil, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Bishr.

(T) And it was narrated from Misʿar, from ʿUbayd, from Ibn Maʿqil, from two men of Muzaynah, one of them from the other: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿĀmir ibn Luʾayy and Ghālib ibn Abjar. Misʿar said: ""I think it was Ghālib ibn Abjar who asked the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam)."" And it was narrated from Abī al-ʿUmayṣ, from ʿUbayd ibn al-Ḥasan, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Maʿqil, from Ghālib ibn Abjar.

A ḥadīth such as this cannot be used to contradict the authentic ḥadīths that have preceded, which explicitly declare the prohibition of the meat of domestic donkeys. And success is through Allah. [Ḍaʿīf]"
19478,"(19472) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, ʿAbdah narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, from Ibn Abī Najīḥ, from Mujāhid, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) forbade the eating of the jalālah and its milk.""

Shārik contradicted him, narrating from Layth ibn Abī Sulaym. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19479,"(19473) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaydalānī narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Naṣr narrated to us, Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us, Shārik narrated from Layth, from Mujāhid, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) forbade, on the day of the conquest of Makkah, the meat of the jalālah and the taking of plunder (nuhbah).""

It was also narrated through another chain from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with both of them). [Ḍaʿīf]"
19480,"(19474) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Nuʿaym narrated to us. They said: Aḥmad ibn Abī Surayj al-Rāzī narrated to me, ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Jahm narrated to me, ʿAmr ibn Abī Qays narrated from Ayyūb al-Sakhtiyānī, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with both of them), who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) forbade (riding) the jalālah among camels and (drinking) their milk."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19481,"(19475) 'Abd al-Warith narrated it from Ayyub from Nafi' from Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with both of them) who said: He (the Prophet, peace be upon him and his family) prohibited riding al-jallalah. Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; 'Abd al-Warith narrated it.

Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi — informed us; Abu Sahl ibn Ziyad al-Qattan informed us; Yahya ibn Ja'far ibn al-Zubayqan narrated to us; Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi narrated to us; Hisham ibn Abi 'Abd Allah narrated from Qatadah from 'Ikrimah from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with both of them) that the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) prohibited al-mujaththamah, the milk of al-jallalah, and drinking from the mouth of a waterskin. This was corroborated by Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah, Hammad ibn Salamah, and 'Umar ibn 'Amir, all from Qatadah.

However, Hammad ibn Salamah said: ""And riding al-jallalah"" — he did not mention the milk. Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us; 'Affan narrated to us; Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Qatadah narrated it with the same meaning, and he said: ""riding al-jallalah."" It has also been narrated from 'Ikrimah from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him). [Sahih]"
19482,"(19476) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubaid informed us; Isma'il al-Qadi narrated to us; Hajjaj narrated to us; Hammad narrated from Ayyub from 'Ikrimah from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) prohibited drinking from the mouth of a waterskin, al-mujaththamah, and al-jallalah. [Sahih]"
19483,(19477) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both said: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us; 'Ubayd Allah ibn Sa'id ibn Kathir ibn 'Ufayr al-Misri narrated to us; my father narrated to me; Ibn Lahi'ah narrated from Abu al-Zubayr from Tawus from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with both of them) that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) prohibited eating the meat of al-jallalah and drinking their milk. And 'Ata' ibn Abi Rabah used to prohibit the meat of al-jallalah among camels and sheep from being eaten. [Sahih]
19484,"(19478) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Bakr ibn Muhammad al-Sayrafi in Marw informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd Allah ibn Idris informed us; Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Hadrami narrated to us; Wuhaib narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Tawus narrated from 'Amr ibn Shu'aib from his father from his grandfather, 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) prohibited, on the day of Khaybar, the meat of domestic donkeys, and prohibited riding al-jallalah and eating their meat. Abu Dawud narrated it in the Sunan from Sahl ibn Bakkar from Wuhaib. [Sahih]"
19485,"(19479) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us; Muhammad ibn Sinan al-Qazzaz informed us; Abu 'Ali — 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Majid al-Hanafi — informed us; Isma'il ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhajir said: I heard my father narrate from 'Abd Allah ibn Babah from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amr (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) prohibited al-jallalah — that their meat be eaten or their milk be drunk, and that they be carried upon. I think he said: ""except upon a saddle,"" and that people should not ride them until they have been fed (clean fodder) for forty nights. This is not strong (in chain).

Al-Shafi'i alluded to it and claimed that he intended thereby the change of their disliked natures to natures that are not disliked, which is the natural disposition of animals, so that the essence of filth is not found in their sweat and meat. [Hasan]"
19486,"(19480) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us; Abu Bakr al-Qattan informed us; Ahmad ibn Yusuf al-Sulami informed us; Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Firyabi said: Sufyan mentioned.

(And) Abu al-Hasan — 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan — informed us; Abu al-Qasim — Sulayman ibn Ahmad al-Tabrani — informed us; Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us; al-Firyabi narrated to us; Sufyan narrated from Ayyub from Abu Qilabah from Zahdam who said: I saw Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him) eating chicken, so he invited me. I said: ""I saw it eating something foul."" He said: ""Come close and eat, for I saw the Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) eating it.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Waki' from Sufyan, and they both narrated it through other chains from Ayyub. [Da'if]"
19487,"(19481) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Abu al-Walid al-Tayalisi informed us; Shu'bah narrated from Hisham ibn Zaid who said: I entered with Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) upon al-Hakam ibn Ayyub, and he saw young boys or servants who had tied up a chicken and were throwing (arrows) at it. So Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) prohibited that animals be made targets (for killing). Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Abu al-Walid, and Muslim narrated it through other chains from Shu'bah. [Sahih — agreed upon]"
19488,"(19482) Abu Bakr ibn Furak (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us; 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us; Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us; Abu Dawud al-Tayalisi narrated to us; Abu 'Awanah and Hushaym narrated from Abu Bishr from Sa'id ibn Jubayr who said: I was with Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with both of them) when a bird or a chicken was being targeted (by arrows). When they saw Ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with both of them), they dispersed. He said: ""May Allah curse the one who did this! Indeed, the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) cursed the one who did this.""

Al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Abu 'Awanah, and Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Hushaym. [Sahih]"
19489,"(19483) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz, Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi — Abu Muhammad — 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ahmad ibn Ibrahim al-Muqri' — and Abu Sadiq — Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-'Attar — all said: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us; al-Hasan ibn Mukram Abu 'Ali informed us; Abu al-Nadr narrated to us; Ishaq ibn Sa'id ibn 'Amr ibn Sa'id ibn al-'As narrated from his father who said: 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with both of them) came to Yahya ibn Sa'id — who was the son of al-'As — and one of his sons had tied up a chicken and was throwing (arrows) at it. So he walked to the chicken and untied it, then came with it and the boy and said to Yahya: ""Restrain your boy from making this bird a target for killing, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him and his family) prohibit that an animal be made a target. And if you wish to slaughter it, then slaughter it.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in the Sahih from Ahmad ibn Ya'qub from Ishaq ibn Sa'id. [Sahih]"
19490,"(19484) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Saghani narrated to us; Hajjaj ibn Muhammad narrated from Ibn Jurayj; Abu al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah (may Allah be pleased with both of them) say: The Prophet (peace be upon him and his family) prohibited that any animal be killed by being made a target.

Muslim narrated it from Harun ibn 'Abd Allah from Hajjaj ibn Muhammad. [Sahih]"
19491,"(19485) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us — al-Hasan ibn Ya'qub al-'Adl narrated to us — Yahya ibn Abi Talib informed us — 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata' informed us — Sa'id ibn Abi 'Arubah narrated from Qatadah, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, forbade the milk of al-Jallalah, the eating of al-Mujaththamah, and drinking from the mouth of the water-skin."
19492,"(19486) Abu Sa'id — 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn Shubah al-Shahid in Hamadan — informed us — Abu al-Qasim 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Hasan al-Asadi narrated to us — Ibrahim ibn al-Husayn narrated to us — 'Abd Allah ibn Maslamah al-Qa'nabi narrated to us — Abu Uways narrated to us — al-Zuhri narrated from Abu Idris al-Khawlani, from Abu Tha'labah al-Khushani, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, forbade al-Khatafah (snatched prey), al-Nuhbah (plundered prey), al-Mujaththamah (an animal tied up and killed), and eating any fanged beast of prey. Abu 'Ubayd said: Al-Mujaththamah is also al-Masburah (the detained one), but it applies only to birds, rabbits, and the like — those animals that crouch upon the ground and remain fixed to it."
19493,"(19487) Abu al-Hasan — 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan — informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us — Tamam, Ibn Abi Qumash, and Ibn Zawran said — al-Hasan ibn Bishr ibn Salm al-Bajali narrated to us — Zuhayr narrated from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: The slaughter of the fetus is included in the slaughter of its mother. And 'Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Ziyad al-Qaddah al-Makki narrated it from Abu al-Zubayr through this chain, and Abu Dawud extracted it in Kitab al-Sunna. And Hammad ibn Shu'ayb and Ibn Abi Layla also narrated it from Abu al-Zubayr."
19494,"(19488) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us — Tamam narrated to us — 'Abd Allah ibn Maslamah narrated to us — Ibn al-Mubarak narrated from Mujalid ibn Sa'id, from Abu al-Waddak, from Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, about the fetus, and he said: Eat it, if you wish. Abu Dawud narrated it in Kitab al-Sunna from 'Abd Allah ibn Maslamah al-Qa'nabi."
19495,"(19489) Abu al-Hasan — 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' — informed us — al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us — Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi narrated to us — Musaddad narrated to us — Hushaym narrated from Mujalid, from Abu al-Waddak, from Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We said: O Messenger of Allah, one of us slaughters a she-camel, or a cow, or a sheep, and there is a fetus in its belly — should we discard it or eat it? He said: Eat it, if you wish, for its slaughter is included in the slaughter of its mother. Abu Dawud narrated it in al-Sunna from Musaddad."
19496,"(19490) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us — 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz narrated to us — Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim al-Bazzaz narrated to us — 'Ali ibn Muslim narrated to us — Abu Yusuf al-Qadi narrated to us — Mujalid ibn Sa'id narrated from Abu al-Waddak, from Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, that he was asked about a she-camel and a cow in whose belly a fetus is found, and he said: If you have invoked the name over the slaughter of the mother, then its slaughter is included in the slaughter of its mother. And that was also narrated from Abu 'Ubaydah al-Haddad from Yunus, from Abu al-Waddak, in an abridged form."
19497,"(19491) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me through what Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Muzakki narrated to them — Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-'Abdi narrated to us — Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to me — 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Wasil, Abu 'Ubaydah al-Haddad, narrated to us — Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated from Abu al-Waddak, Jabr ibn Nauf, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: The slaughter of the fetus is included in the slaughter of its mother. And in this chapter there are narrations from 'Ali, 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas, Abu Ayyub, Abu Huraytah, Abu al-Darda', Abu Umamah, and al-Bara' ibn 'Azib, may Allah be pleased with them all, as marfu' narrations. And in the hadith of al-Zuhri from Ibn Ka'b ibn Malik, he used to say: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, used to say regarding the fetus: When its hair has grown, its slaughter is included in the slaughter of its mother."
19498,"(19492) Abu Ahmad — 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Mihrajani — informed us — Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Muzakki narrated to us — Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-'Abdi narrated to us — Ibn Bukayr narrated to us — Malik narrated from Nafi', from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he used to say: — (H) And Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq informed us — they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Hakam informed us — Ibn Wahb informed me — 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar and Malik ibn Anas and others informed me that Nafi' narrated to them that 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, used to say: When a she-camel is slaughtered, the slaughter of what is in its belly is included in its slaughter, provided its creation has been completed and its hair has grown. But if it emerges alive from its belly, it should be slaughtered until blood flows from within it. This is the wording of the hadith of Ibn Bukayr. And in the narration of Ibn Wahb it reads: ""with its slaughter"" — and the remainder is the same. This is the correct version, and it is mawquf (stopping at the Companion's statement)."
19499,"(19493) Abu al-Hasan — Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Dawud al-'Alawi, may Allah have mercy on him — informed us — Muhammad ibn Hamdawayh ibn Sahl al-Marwazi al-Mutawwi'i narrated to us — Abu Shahab — Ma'mar ibn Muhammad ibn Ma'mar al-'Awfi — narrated to us — 'Asam ibn Yusuf narrated to us — al-Mubarak ibn Mujahid narrated from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said regarding the fetus: Its slaughter is included in the slaughter of its mother, whether its hair has grown or not. Abu al-Hasan — 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Daraqutni — narrated it in his book from Muhammad ibn Hamdawayh al-Marwazi, as did 'Ali ibn al-Fadl ibn Tahir."
19500,"(19494) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Isfahani informed us of that — 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz narrated to us, and he mentioned it. And it has been narrated through other routes from Ibn 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, as a marfu' narration, but its being raised (to the Prophet) is weak, and the correct version is mawquf. And in the hadith of al-Harith from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, he said regarding the slaughter of the fetus: Its slaughter is included in the slaughter of its mother."
19501,"(19495) Abū al-Qāsim — ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥurfī — in Baghdād, narrated to us. Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us, dictating, Abū Yaḥyā Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, meaning al-Rāzī, narrated to us. Sahl ibn ʿUthmān narrated to us. Yaḥyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah narrated from Idrīs, from ʬAṭiyyah, from Ibn ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with them both), who said: ""The beast of cattle has been made lawful for you, and its slaughter is the slaughter of its mother.""

And in the ḥadīth of Muḥammad ibn Muslim, Abū Thumāmah al-Baṣrī, who heard Ḥanẓalah, Abū Khaldah, say: ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir said: ""O Ḥanẓalah, the beast of cattle has been made lawful for you. It was only revealed concerning that in which the womb was obscure — when its creation is complete and its hair has grown, then its slaughter is the slaughter of its mother.""

[Munkar]"
19502,"(19496) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fārisī informed us. Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed us. Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān ibn Fāris narrated to us. Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rajāʾ said, from Muḥammad ibn Muslim.

[Similar to the preceding ḥadīth]"
19503,"(19497) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us. Abū Manṣūr al-Naḍrawī informed us. Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us. Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us. Jarīr narrated from Manṣūr, from Qābūs, who said: ""A cow was slaughtered while alive, and we found a fetus in its belly. We roasted it and brought it to Abū Ẓabyān. He took a morsel of it and said: 'This is that which Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allāh be pleased with them both) narrated to us — that it is from the beast of cattle.'""

It was also narrated by Ṭāwūs from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allāh be pleased with them both). And it has been narrated to us from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allāh be pleased with them both), that he said regarding the beast of cattle: ""It is the fetus — its slaughter is the slaughter of its mother.""

[Ḍaʿīf]"
19504,"(19498) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl informed us. ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us. Yaʿqūb narrated to us. Ādam narrated to us. Shuʿbah narrated from al-Mughīrah, from Ibrāhīm, who said: ""The fetus — its slaughter is the slaughter of its mother.""

[Ḍaʿīf]"
19505,"(19499) He said: And Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us. Sufyān narrated from al-Zubayr ibn ʿAdī, from Ibrāhīm, who said: ""Its slaughter is the slaughter of its mother.""

[Ḥasan]"
19506,"(19500) He said: And Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us. Sufyān narrated from al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUbayd Allāh, from Ibrāhīm, who said: ""It used to be said: it is but a limb of its limbs — its slaughter is the slaughter of its mother.""

[Ḥasan]"
19507,"(19501) He said: And Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us. Sufyān narrated from Manṣūr, from Ibrāhīm, who said: ""All of it, whether it has hair or has not yet grown hair — provided it is not repulsive"" — meaning the fetus.

Yaʿqūb said: And it has been narrated from Ḥammād, from Ibrāhīm, who said: ""The slaughter of one soul is not the slaughter of two souls.""

[Ḥasan]"
19508,"(19502) Yaʿqūb said: And some of our companions narrated to us from ʿUthmān ibn ʿUthmān. Al-Battī informed us, saying: ""Ḥammād, when he spoke by his own opinion, was correct; and when he said 'Ibrāhīm said,' he was mistaken.""

And it has been narrated to us from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad, al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī, ʿĀmir al-Shaʿbī, ʬAṭāʾ, Ṭāwūs, Mujāhid, Nāfiʿ, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā, ʿIkrimah, and ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, something to the effect of our statement."
19509,"(19503) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us. Abū Bakr ibn Maḥmūyah narrated to us. Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad narrated to us. Ādam narrated to us. Shuʿbah narrated to us. ʿAwn ibn Abī Ḥuṣayfah narrated, saying: ""My father purchased a cupping slave. He ordered his cupping instruments, and they were broken. He said: 'Indeed, the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) prohibited the price of a dog, the earnings of a prostitute, and the price of blood. He cursed the tattooer and the one tattooed, the consumer of ribā and the one who feeds it, and the image-maker.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam.

[Ṣaḥīḥ]"
19510,"(19504) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū ʿAbd Allāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us. Al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Yazīd informed us. My father informed me, saying: I heard al-Awzāʿī say: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to me. Ibrāhīm ibn Qāriẓ narrated to us. Al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd narrated to me. Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj (may Allāh be pleased with him) narrated to me that the Messenger of Allāh (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam) said: ""The earnings of the cupper are wicked, the fee of a prostitute is wicked, and the price of a dog is wicked.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of al-Walīd ibn Muslim, from al-Awzāʿī.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim 1568]"
19511,"(19505) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-Nadr al-Faqih informed me: Muhammad ibn Nsar al-Imam narrated to us: Abu Qudamah narrated to us: Yahya al-Qattan narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Yusuf, client of 'Amr ibn 'Uthman al-Madani, narrated to us: Al-Sa'ib ibn Yazid narrated from Rafi' ibn Khadij, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, who said: The worst of earnings are the fee of the prostitute, the price of a dog, and the earnings of the cupper (hajjam). Muslim related it from Muhammad ibn Hatim from Yahya ibn Sa'id. [Authentic. Previously referenced.]"
19512,"(19506) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq and Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan — both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us: al-Shafi'i informed us: Sufyan narrated from al-Zuhri from Haram ibn Sa'd ibn Muhayyisah: that Muhayyisah, may Allah be pleased with him, asked the Prophet, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, about the earnings of the cupper, so he prohibited it for him. He continued to speak with him until he said: Feed it to your slave and give it as fodder to your camel. [Authentic.]"
19513,"(19507) Abu Zakariyya and Abu Bakr both said: Abu al-'Abbas informed us: al-Rabi' informed us: al-Shafi'i informed us: Malik — (cross-reference) — and Abu Ahmad al-Mihrajani informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Ja'far al-Muzakka narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Malik narrated from Ibn Shihab from Ibn Muhayyisah, one of the sons of Harithah, from his father: that he sought permission from the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, regarding the cupper's fee, so he prohibited it for him. He continued to ask him until he said: Give it as fodder to your camel and feed it to your slave. [Authentic. Previously referenced.]"
19514,"(19508) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us: Ibn Milhan narrated to us: Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us: al-Layth narrated from Yazid ibn Abi Habib from Abi 'Ufayr al-Ansari from Muhammad ibn Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah from Muhayyisah ibn Mas'ud al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him: that he had a cupper slave named Nafi', who went to the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, asking about his earnings. He said: Do not go near it. So he was turned back to the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, and he said: Use it to feed your camel and consider it among your companions. [Weak.]"
19515,"(19509) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us: Abu Tahir Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Muhammadabadi informed us: Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah al-Sa'di informed us: Yazid ibn Harun informed us: Humayd al-Tawil narrated from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him: that Abu Taybah cupped the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, so he ordered that two sa's of food be given to him. He spoke to his masters, and they reduced his tax. He said: The best of remedies you may use are cupping (hijamah) and costus al-bahri (sea costus). And do not torment your children by pressing for punishment of the throat. Al-Bukhari and Muslim both recorded it in the Sahih from various routes from Humayd. [Authentic. Agreed upon.]"
19516,"(19510) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us: al-Shafi'i informed us: Malik narrated from Humayd from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Abu Taybah cupped the Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, so he ordered that one sa' of dates be given to him, and he ordered his family to reduce his tax. Al-Bukhari recorded it in the Sahih from 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf from Malik. [Authentic. Previously referenced.]"
19517,"(19511) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Mahmuyah al-'Askari narrated to us: Ja'far ibn Muhammad al-Qalanisi narrated to us: Adam ibn Abi Iyas narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated from Humayd al-Tawil, who said: I heard Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: The Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, called for a slave and had him cupped, then ordered that one or two sa's, or one or two mudd, be given to him. He spoke concerning it, and his tax was reduced. Al-Bukhari recorded it in the Sahih from Adam, and Muslim also recorded it from another route from Shu'bah. [Authentic. Previously referenced.]"
19518,"(19512) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari and Abu al-Husayn ibn Bashran both said: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us: Sa'dan ibn Nsar narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd narrated from Mis'ar from 'Amr ibn 'Amir from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, would undergo cupping and would not wrong anyone of his wages. Muslim recorded it in the Sahih from another route from Mis'ar ibn Kidam. [Authentic. Previously referenced.]"
19519,"(19513) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq al-Faqih and Abu Bakr ibn Balwayh both said: Ishaq ibn al-Hasan informed us: 'Affan — (cross-reference) — and Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me: Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Balwayh narrated to us: Musa ibn al-Hasan ibn 'Abbad narrated to us: Mu'alla ibn Asad al-'Ammi — both said: Wahb narrated from 'Abd Allah ibn Tawus from his father from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with both of them: that the Prophet, may Allah's peace and blessings be upon him and his family, was cupped and gave the cupper his wages, and he sought medical treatment (istatta'a). Al-Bukhari related it from Mu'alla ibn Asad, and Muslim related it from Abi Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah from 'Affan. [Authentic. Agreed upon.]"
